window.YUGA = {"count":54,"research":[{"id":"puranic-classical","title":"Classical Puranic model: 4,320,000-year Mahayuga, 4:3:2:1 ratio, divine years x360","category":"Traditional scriptural","proponents":["Traditional Hindu commentators and almanac (panchang) makers","Aryabhata (Aryabhatiya, 499 CE) as the earliest datable astronomer to fix the Kali epoch backward","Modern historians of Indian science, e.g. Kim Plofker (Mathematics in India, 2009) on how the mahayuga figure functions in Siddhantic astronomy","Orientalist translators such as H.H. Wilson (Vishnu Purana, 1840) and F.E. Pargiter (Ancient Indian Historical Tradition, 1922) who documented the scheme"],"primarySources":["Manusmriti, Book 1 (roughly c. 200 BCE to 200 CE), which gives the four yugas as 4800, 3600, 2400 and 1200 divine years and equates a divine year with 360 human years","Mahabharata, Vana Parva (Markandeya episode), which gives the same yuga lengths in divine years and the 4:3:2:1 proportion","Vishnu Purana, Book 1, chapters 3 and related passages (text generally placed c. 300 to 500 CE in its present form), which gives the 12,000 divine-year mahayuga and the 4,000/3,000/2,000/1,000 core periods with dawn and dusk (sandhya) margins","Aryabhatiya (499 CE), which uses a 4,320,000-year mahayuga as a mean-motion framework and sets the Kali epoch at 3102 BCE","Surya Siddhanta (c. 5th century CE, with later revisions), which codifies the 4,320,000-year mahayuga and its subdivisions"],"period":"Yuga scheme in Manusmriti (c. 200 BCE to 200 CE) and Mahabharata; systematized in Vishnu Purana (c. 300 to 500 CE) and Siddhantic astronomy (c. 400 to 600 CE)","totalCycleYears":"4,320,000 human years (12,000 divine years x 360)","durations":{"satya":"1,728,000 human years (4,800 divine years including dawn and dusk; core 4,000 divine years)","treta":"1,296,000 human years (3,600 divine years including margins; core 3,000)","dvapara":"864,000 human years (2,400 divine years including margins; core 2,000)","kali":"432,000 human years (1,200 divine years including margins; core 1,000)"},"kaliStart":"3102 BCE (midnight, 17/18 February, the conventional Kali epoch fixed by Aryabhata and the Siddhantas; tradition links it to the death of Krishna)","kaliEnd":"Approximately 428,900 CE (432,000 years after the 3102 BCE epoch)","currentPosition":"In 2026 CE the Kali Yuga is in its 5,127th year (Kali Samvat), about 1.19 percent of its 432,000-year span. Roughly 426,870 years remain on the scheme. The scheme is read as a continuing decline from Satya through Kali, so the present is treated as the darkest phase.","summary":"The classical Puranic model divides a Mahayuga of 12,000 divine years into four yugas in a 4:3:2:1 ratio: Satya, Treta, Dvapara and Kali. Each divine year is taken as 360 human years, giving 4,320,000 human years in total and 432,000 years for the Kali Yuga. The scheme is presented as moral decline, with dharma shrinking across the ages. Its text-historical anchor is the Kali epoch of 3102 BCE, which Aryabhata and the Siddhanta tradition back-calculated and which is not supported by any independent dated event. Within astronomy the 4,320,000-year figure functioned as a computational mean-motion framework rather than an observed cosmic cycle.","keyClaims":["The four yugas last 4,800, 3,600, 2,400 and 1,200 divine years including dawn and dusk, in a 4:3:2:1 ratio","One divine year equals 360 human years, so the Mahayuga is 4,320,000 human years","The Kali Yuga began in 3102 BCE and lasts 432,000 years","Dharma and human lifespan decline across the yugas, so the present age is the most degenerate","The cycle repeats indefinitely within a Kalpa of 1,000 Mahayugas (4.32 billion years), as stated in later texts"],"evidence":["Internal consistency: the 4:3:2:1 ratio and the x360 conversion are applied uniformly across Manusmriti, Mahabharata, Vishnu Purana and Siddhantic works","The Kali epoch of 3102 BCE is given in Aryabhatiya and later Siddhantic texts, and Aryabhata states that 3,600 Kali years had elapsed when he wrote in 499 CE, which fixes the start by back-calculation","Siddhantic astronomers used the 4,320,000 figure as a mean-motion period, where it yields integer counts of planetary revolutions, so the number has real computational function","The scheme is textually attested across several genres over more than a millennium, so it was a stable tradition rather than a single invention"],"criticisms":["No independent evidence: the scheme is not tied to any archaeological, epigraphic or documentary sequence, and no dated event corresponds to the start of Kali","The 3102 BCE epoch is a retrospective calculation from astronomical and ritual convenience. It was not recorded by contemporaries","The 4,320,000-year figure is a computational convenience. Siddhantic astronomers used it for mean-motion arithmetic, which does not make it a physical cycle of the cosmos","The x360 conversion of divine years is a mythic-numerical device and has no observational basis","Texts disagree on details such as whether dawn and dusk margins are included and how the yugas map onto human lifespan, which weakens any literal reading","Scholars such as Kim Plofker and earlier Indologists treat the scheme as theology and calendrics, not historiography. Literal date claims derived from it are generally rejected by historians of India","The Vishnu Purana's present form is post-Gupta, so it cannot be a primary source for ancient chronology","The scheme's moral-decline framing is ideological and has been used to justify social hierarchy, which critics argue makes it an unreliable guide to real history"],"plausibility":1,"plausibilityReason":"As a literal timeline it fails. No independent evidence supports a 4.32-million-year cycle, a 3102 BCE start of a Kali age, or a steady moral decline across ages. The one real anchor is the Aryabhata-era epoch, which is a calendrical convention and not a recorded event. Its value lies in the history of ideas and computational astronomy, not in dating reality.","references":["Wilson, H.H. (1840) translation of the Vishnu Purana","Pargiter, F.E. (1922) Ancient Indian Historical Tradition, Oxford University Press","Plofker, Kim (2009) Mathematics in India, Princeton University Press","Olivelle, Patrick (2004) Manu's Code of Law, Oxford University Press (translation of Manusmriti 1.67 to 1.86 on the yugas)","Aryabhatiya of Aryabhata (499 CE), with Kali epoch discussion in its commentaries","Doniger, Wendy (ed. and trans.) Hindu Myths (Penguin, 1975), for Puranic yuga passages in translation"]},{"id":"manusmriti","title":"Manusmriti Chapter 1: Yugas in Divine Years (12,000 divine years)","category":"Traditional scriptural","proponents":["Traditional Hindu commentators (e.g., Kulluka Bhatta, c. 15th century, commentary on Manusmriti)","Modern Indological translators who present the cosmology as the text's own (e.g., Georg Bühler, Wendy Doniger and Brian K. Smith, Patrick Olivelle)"],"primarySources":["Manusmriti (Manava Dharmashastra), chapter 1, roughly verses 1.64-1.86 (numbering varies by edition), composed c. 2nd century BCE to 2nd century CE per Olivelle's dating","Mahabharata, Vana Parva (yuga passages, e.g., Markandeya narrative; numbering to be verified)","Later Puranas (e.g., Vishnu Purana, Bhagavata Purana) repeat and elaborate the scheme"],"period":"Originates in the early centuries CE (Manusmriti composition c. 2nd century BCE to 2nd century CE); the 4.32 million-year mahayuga figure is standard in later Puranic and astronomical tradition. The explicit divine-year framing is traditionally read as the text's own.","totalCycleYears":"4,320,000 human years (mahayuga = 12,000 divine years x 360). Manu 1.71 frames this as 12,000 divine years; the 360 multiplier comes from Manu 1.67 (a human year equals one day and night of the gods).","durations":{"satya":"4,800 divine years = 1,728,000 human years (4,000 proper + 400 dawn + 400 dusk)","treta":"3,600 divine years = 1,296,000 human years (3,000 proper + 300 + 300)","dvapara":"2,400 divine years = 864,000 human years (2,000 proper + 200 + 200)","kali":"1,200 divine years = 432,000 human years (1,000 proper + 100 + 100)"},"kaliStart":"No epoch given in Manusmriti. The traditional start of Kali (3102 BCE) comes from later astronomical tradition (widely cited from Aryabhata's Aryabhatiya, c. 499 CE), not from Manu.","kaliEnd":"If the traditional 3102 BCE start is applied to the full 432,000-year Kali (including twilights), the end falls around 428,898 CE. This is arithmetic on a traditional epoch, not a text-given date.","currentPosition":"Under this scheme, 2026 CE corresponds to roughly 5,128 years into Kali (about 1.2% of its 432,000-year length). Kali is still in its early phase. This only holds if the 3102 BCE epoch is accepted.","summary":"Manusmriti chapter 1 describes four yugas (Krita/Satya, Treta, Dvapara, Kali) with proportions of 4:3:2:1, each with dawn and dusk twilight periods, totaling 12,000 divine years. Verse 1.67 equates one human year with one day and night of the gods, so a divine year is 360 human years, which gives a 4.32 million-year mahayuga. The text also says a thousand mahayugas make a day of Brahma. The cycle is presented as a cosmological and moral-decline framework, with the Kali age as the present degenerate era. Whether this 360-year conversion is part of the original composition or a later clarification is a debated question. The numbers are internally consistent but have no external chronological anchor.","keyClaims":["The four yugas total 12,000 divine years, with dawn and dusk periods included in each yuga's total.","The yuga proportions are 4:3:2:1 (4800, 3600, 2400, 1200 divine years including twilights).","One human year equals one day and night of the gods (Manu 1.67), so a divine year is 360 human years.","The mahayuga is 4,320,000 human years when converted using the 360 multiplier.","The Kali yuga is the present, most degenerate age, in which dharma is reduced to one quarter."],"evidence":["Internal consistency: the four yuga totals (4800+3600+2400+1200) sum to 12,000 divine years, and the 4:3:2:1 ratio is clear in the text.","Manu 1.67 explicitly gives the day-of-gods equivalence, which supports the 360 multiplier as a textual statement rather than purely a later commentary.","The same 12,000 divine-year and 360-multiplier logic recurs in the Mahabharata and Puranas, suggesting a shared, circulating scheme in the early centuries CE.","Ancient Indian cosmological numbers are often built from ratios and multiples of 360 (days in a year), consistent with the system being internally motivated."],"criticisms":["Manusmriti is not a chronology. It gives no epoch, no historical events, and no method for linking the cycle to dated history, so it cannot be tested against evidence.","The 4.32 million-year figure is an arithmetic product of the 360 multiplier and the 12,000 divine-year total, not an empirical measurement.","Manusmriti is a composite, layered text with many later accretions. Scholars debate how much of the chapter 1 cosmology (including the yuga passages) belongs to the earliest layer, so attributing the scheme to a single author or date is uncertain.","The 3102 BCE Kali epoch often attached to this scheme does not come from Manusmriti. It comes from later astronomical tradition, so linking Manu to that date mixes sources.","The decline-of-dharma moral narrative is normative and theological. It was not designed as a prediction and has no empirical test.","Modern astronomy and geology show no cosmic-scale cycle matching 4.32 million years in a way that correlates with the yuga proportions. Claims that the scheme 'matches' astronomical or geological cycles are generally post-hoc."],"plausibility":1,"plausibilityReason":"As a real-world timeline, the scheme has no verifiable anchor. It is a symbolic and theological cosmology with no dated events, and its numbers were not designed to be checked against history or science. It is internally coherent as a text, which is why it is a useful reference point, but it scores very low on real-world accuracy. The 3102 BCE start, often paired with it, is a later tradition and is not verifiable from Manusmriti alone.","references":["Olivelle, Patrick (trans.), Manu's Code of Law: A Critical Edition and Translation of the Mānava-Dharmaśāstra, Oxford University Press, 2005","Bühler, Georg (trans.), The Laws of Manu, Sacred Books of the East vol. 25, 1886","Doniger, Wendy and Brian K. Smith (trans.), The Laws of Manu, Penguin Classics, 1991","Kane, P. V., History of Dharmaśāstra, vol. 2, Bhandarkar Oriental Research Institute (1930-1962) (on the dating and composition of Manusmriti)","Aryabhatiya of Aryabhata (c. 499 CE), source of the commonly cited Kali epoch of 3102 BCE (secondary attribution, verify before citing)","Note: this entry was written from knowledge, not live web lookup. Verse numbers, dates and secondary attributions (Kulluka Bhatta, the 3102 BCE epoch, Mahabharata passage numbers) should be checked against the primary editions before publication."]},{"id":"surya-siddhanta","title":"Surya Siddhanta astronomical yuga (mahayuga, planetary revolutions, Kali epoch conjunction)","category":"Astronomical/Siddhantic","proponents":["Anonymous author(s) of the Surya Siddhanta (tradition attributes it to a divine source)","Aryabhata I (adopted the Kali epoch framework, 499 CE)","Varahamihira (summarized it in Panchasiddhantika, c. 505 CE)","Modern translators and historians: Ebenezer Burgess (1860 translation), W.D. Whitney (editing and notes), David Pingree (Census of the Exact Sciences in Sanskrit), Kim Plofker (Mathematics in India)","Traditional panchanga (almanac) makers in parts of India, including some Tamil and other regional calendar traditions, which still compute with Surya Siddhanta-derived parameters"],"primarySources":["Surya Siddhanta (anonymous; core text usually dated to roughly the 4th-5th century CE, with later revisions and recensions)","Aryabhatiya of Aryabhata I (499 CE), uses the Kali epoch framework","Panchasiddhantika of Varahamihira (c. 505 CE), summarizes the Surya Siddhanta and rival siddhantas","Brahmasphutasiddhanta of Brahmagupta (628 CE), critiques and revises earlier siddhantas","Bhaskara I commentary on Aryabhatiya (629 CE)","Siddhanta Shiromani of Bhaskara II (1150 CE), later critique and reform"],"period":"Core text c. 4th-5th century CE (scholarly estimate; some later revision). The 3102 BCE Kali epoch is a retrospective computational reference point, not a dated historical event. The mahayuga framework itself is older Indian cosmological material, but the specific parameters here are Siddhantic.","totalCycleYears":"4,320,000 solar years per mahayuga (1,000 mahayugas make one kalpa of 4.32 billion years, described as a day of Brahma). Divine years (360 solar years each) are used in the text: 12,000 divine years per mahayuga.","durations":{"satya":"1,728,000 years (4,800 divine years)","treta":"1,296,000 years (3,600 divine years)","dvapara":"864,000 years (2,400 divine years)","kali":"432,000 years (1,200 divine years)"},"kaliStart":"Midnight at the start of Friday, around 18 February 3102 BCE (Julian proleptic reckoning), taken as the epoch when all mean planetary longitudes are zero at the start of Aries (mesha). This is a computed epoch, not an observed event.","kaliEnd":"About 428,899 CE, if the 432,000-year Kali yuga is counted in full from 3102 BCE.","currentPosition":"In 2026 CE, the Kali yuga is at roughly year 5,127 (about 5,127 years elapsed out of 432,000, or about 1.19 percent). Some traditions also add sandhya (transition) periods, which shift the exact boundaries slightly.","summary":"The Surya Siddhanta is a Sanskrit astronomical manual that defines a mahayuga of 4,320,000 solar years, split into four yugas in a 4:3:2:1 ratio (Satya, Treta, Dvapara, Kali). It gives mean sidereal revolution counts for the Sun (4,320,000), Moon (57,753,336), Mars (2,296,832), Mercury (17,937,060), Jupiter (364,220), Venus (7,022,388), and Saturn (146,568) per mahayuga, plus the Moon's apogee and node. These counts are used to compute planetary mean positions from a fixed epoch. The text places the start of Kali at 3102 BCE, a point where all mean longitudes are conventionally zero, and uses that epoch as the reference for all calculations. The framework is primarily a computational and calendrical system for predicting eclipses, planetary positions, and festival dates, and it is not a historical timeline of past events.","keyClaims":["A mahayuga is 4,320,000 solar years, divided into Satya (1,728,000), Treta (1,296,000), Dvapara (864,000), and Kali (432,000) yugas.","Kali yuga began at midnight at the start of Friday, 3102 BCE (Julian reckoning).","At the Kali epoch, all planets had mean longitudes of zero, meaning they were in conjunction at the start of Aries.","Planetary revolution counts per mahayuga (Sun 4,320,000; Moon 57,753,336; Mars 2,296,832; Mercury 17,937,060; Jupiter 364,220; Venus 7,022,388; Saturn 146,568) produce accurate mean motions for calendrical use.","The sidereal year is given as about 365.2587565 days, which is close to modern values."],"evidence":["The internal consistency of the mahayuga arithmetic (the 4:3:2:1 ratios and the 1,000-mahayuga kalpa) is clear in the text.","The planetary revolution counts are internally consistent with the text's sine tables and computational procedures, and the algorithms yield usable predictions for eclipses and conjunctions in the medieval period.","The sidereal year value of 365.2587565 days is within a few thousandths of a day of modern sidereal year values, which is a real achievement for its era.","Historically, the Kali epoch of 3102 BCE was widely adopted in Indian astronomy (including by Aryabhata), which shows the framework was used as a shared reference point.","The Surya Siddhanta-based panchanga remain in use in parts of India, so the system has practical continuity."],"criticisms":["The 3102 BCE epoch and the all-planets-in-conjunction starting condition are retrospective, computed reference points. Actual planetary positions around 3102 BCE do not match this configuration, so the claim is a mathematical convention, not an observed event.","Mahayuga and kalpa durations are cosmological constructs, not measurements. Attempts to tie them to real history or geology are not supported by evidence.","The text's planetary models have known inaccuracies. For example, the Moon's and planets' motions and some epicycle structures were later refined by Aryabhata, Brahmagupta, and Bhaskara II, and modern ephemerides are far more precise.","The date 3102 BCE is not confirmed by archaeology, textual evidence from that period, or any independent dating method. The Surya Siddhanta itself dates to roughly 4th-5th century CE, so the Kali start is a later back-projection.","Different siddhantas and later traditions use different epochs and parameters, which shows that these numbers are conventions rather than discovered constants.","Some popular and fringe writers treat the yuga durations as literal historical or prophetic timelines. Scholars such as Pingree and Plofker treat them as computational devices, and this reading is not supported by the text itself."],"plausibility":2,"plausibilityReason":"As a calendrical and astronomical computation system, the Surya Siddhanta is a strong historical achievement and has useful, roughly accurate parameters. As a literal real-world timeline, its claim that Kali began in 3102 BCE with all planets in conjunction, and that the world is in year ~5,127 of a 432,000-year Kali, has no independent support. Archaeology, astronomy, and textual history do not corroborate a 3102 BCE start. The plausibility score reflects the literal timeline claim, not the value of the astronomy.","references":["Burgess, Ebenezer (trans.), 'Translation of the Surya Siddhanta', 1860, edited and annotated by W.D. Whitney (Journal of the American Oriental Society). Standard English translation; no URL verified in this session.","Pingree, David, Census of the Exact Sciences in Sanskrit, American Philosophical Society, 1970-1994 (series, Series A). Bibliographic reference; no URL verified.","Plofker, Kim, Mathematics in India, Princeton University Press, 2009. Bibliographic reference; no URL verified.","Aryabhatiya of Aryabhata (499 CE), with Kali epoch discussion; translated by Walter Eugene Clark (1930). Bibliographic reference; no URL verified.","Note: web search and fetch were not used in this run; the figures above come from established scholarly knowledge and should be checked against the cited editions before publication."]},{"id":"aryabhata","title":"Aryabhata's Aryabhatiya: equal quarter-yugas and the Kali epoch","category":"Astronomical/Siddhantic","proponents":["Aryabhata (author, Kusumapura/Pataliputra, Aryabhatiya c. 499 CE)","Bhaskara I (commentary on Aryabhatiya, c. 629 CE)","Somayaji (later commentator, c. 1500s)","Modern historians: B. Datta and A. N. Singh, Kim Plofker, David Pingree, K. S. Shukla (editor/translator)"],"primarySources":["Aryabhatiya (499 CE), esp. Gitikapada and Kalakriyapada","Dasagitika (related Aryabhata text, lost in full form)","Bhaskara I's commentary on the Aryabhatiya (629 CE)"],"period":"Originated c. 499 CE (Aryabhatiya). The Kali epoch of 3102 BCE was used by later Indian astronomers; Aryabhata's own use of it is inferred by commentators and modern scholars rather than stated as an explicit historical claim.","totalCycleYears":"4,320,000 years (mahayuga), split into four equal quarter-yugas of 1,080,000 years each under the usual reading of Aryabhata. This differs from the 4:3:2:1 unequal scheme (1,728,000 / 1,296,000 / 864,000 / 432,000; total 4,320,000) found in the Surya Siddhanta tradition.","durations":{"satya":"1,080,000 years under the equal-quarter reading (Surya Siddhanta tradition would give 1,728,000)","treta":"1,080,000 years under the equal-quarter reading (Surya Siddhanta tradition would give 1,296,000)","dvapara":"1,080,000 years under the equal-quarter reading (Surya Siddhanta tradition would give 864,000)","kali":"1,080,000 years under the equal-quarter reading (Surya Siddhanta tradition would give 432,000). The exact status of Kali as a quarter in Aryabhata is not fully explicit in the text and is debated."},"kaliStart":"3102 BCE (Kali epoch, midnight at the start of Friday, 17/18 February 3102 BCE in the proleptic Julian calendar). This is the standard traditional epoch, used in Aryabhata's computational framework through the ahargana (day count from epoch).","kaliEnd":"Not explicitly stated by Aryabhata. Under the equal-quarter reading the Kali quarter would run to about 1,077,000 CE. Commentators did not treat this as a predicted end date, and it is not a common interpretation.","currentPosition":"In 2026 CE, the Kali era count is about year 5127 completed (5128 by the common count). Under Aryabhata's framework we are still in the first Kali quarter-yuga, which is about 1,080,000 years long on the equal reading, so roughly 0.5 percent has elapsed.","summary":"Aryabhata's Aryabhatiya (499 CE) gives a mahayuga of 4,320,000 years, the same total as the Siddhanta tradition, but under the usual reading it splits the four yugas into equal quarters of 1,080,000 years each. This departs from the 4:3:2:1 scheme of later Siddhantas, where the golden age is longest and the Kali age shortest. Aryabhata's astronomy relies on a day count from a Kali epoch of 3102 BCE, but he does not offer a historical narrative of decline or a prediction of when Kali ends. The Gitikapada verse 3 is often read as saying that 3,600 years plus three quarters of a yuga had passed at his time, which is consistent with the 3102 BCE epoch. Most scholars treat the epoch as a computational convention for astronomical tables, not a historical claim about a real event in 3102 BCE. Ideas about yuga decline that appear in popular Hindu thought are mostly not part of Aryabhata's own text.","keyClaims":["Mahayuga is 4,320,000 years, consistent with the Siddhanta tradition.","The four quarter-yugas are treated as equal (1,080,000 years each), unlike the 4:3:2:1 scheme in later Siddhantas. This is the usual reading, though some scholars note the text is compressed on this point.","Kali yuga is dated from 3102 BCE, which is consistent with the traditional epoch.","Gitikapada 3 is read as placing Aryabhata's own time about 3,600 years plus part of a quarter-yuga into the Kali age, so about 3600 years of Kali had passed at his birth (about 476 CE if his birth is taken as 23 years earlier than 499 CE).","Aryabhata's yuga framework is primarily for calculating planetary positions via the ahargana, not for describing moral or civilizational decline."],"evidence":["The Aryabhatiya's stated mahayuga of 4,320,000 years and kalpa-based day counts, which are internally consistent with the parameters attributed to him.","Gitikapada 3 verse, which is often cited (with varying interpretations) as giving his age and the elapsed Kali years at the time of composition.","Bhaskara I's commentary (629 CE), which explicitly works with the Kali epoch and the four quarters.","Agreement among modern scholars (Datta and Singh, Plofker, Pingree) that Aryabhata's calendar is anchored to Kali."],"criticisms":["The verse-level reading of Gitikapada 3 is contested. Some scholars read the elapsed time as counted from a different reference point, so the 3102 BCE link is an inference, not an explicit statement.","Aryabhata's text does not clearly state the equal 1,080,000-year quarters. The claim rests on reconstruction and on later commentators, so presenting it as settled overstates the evidence.","The 3102 BCE epoch is not historically verifiable. It is a convention, and there is no archaeological or documentary event matching it.","Aryabhata did not claim that the yugas describe actual historical ages. Presenting his scheme as a historical timeline misreads his purpose, which was computational astronomy.","Some popular accounts attribute to Aryabhata a prediction of Kali's end or a specific civilizational timeline. Neither appears in the surviving text."],"plausibility":2,"plausibilityReason":"As a historical timeline, the scheme does not survive scrutiny. The 4.32 million-year mahayuga and the 1,080,000-year quarters are computational constructs, not measured history. The 3102 BCE epoch is a convention and cannot be matched to a real event. Aryabhata's astronomical parameters are impressive for their era, but the yuga durations are not empirically testable, so the plausibility score reflects the historical claim, not the quality of his astronomy.","references":["Aryabhatiya text and translation: K. S. Shukla and K. V. Sarma, Aryabhatiya of Aryabhata (INSA, 1976)","B. Datta and A. N. Singh, History of Hindu Mathematics (1935, 1938)","Kim Plofker, Mathematics in India (Princeton, 2009)","David Pingree, Census of the Exact Sciences in Sanskrit (American Philosophical Society)","Bhaskara I, commentary on the Aryabhatiya (629 CE)"]},{"id":"brahmagupta","title":"Brahmagupta's critique of Aryabhata's yuga scheme (Brahmasphutasiddhanta, 628 CE)","category":"Academic/historical","proponents":["Brahmagupta (598-c.668 CE), as author of the Brahmasphutasiddhanta","Later commentators and historians who read BSS as a corrective to Aryabhata: H.T. Colebrooke (1817 translation), Otto Neugebauer, David Pingree, Kim Plofker"],"primarySources":["Aryabhatiya (Aryabhata I, 499 CE): the yuga scheme being critiqued (mahayuga of 4,320,000 years; Kali epoch at 3102 BCE)","Brahmasphutasiddhanta (Brahmagupta, 628 CE, Bhinmal/Bhillamala region): critical revision of Aryabhata's planetary parameters and methods","Khandakhadyaka (Brahmagupta, 665 CE): simplified practical handbook that departs from BSS and partly follows Aryabhata's school"],"period":"7th century CE (BSS composed 628 CE; Khandakhadyaka 665 CE). The critique targets Aryabhata's 499 CE system.","totalCycleYears":"4,320,000 years (mahayuga). Brahmagupta retains the same total as Aryabhata; his dispute is over planetary revolution counts and computational methods, not the yuga length.","durations":{"satya":"1,728,000 years (4,800 divine years)","treta":"1,296,000 years (3,600 divine years)","dvapara":"864,000 years (2,400 divine years)","kali":"432,000 years (1,200 divine years)"},"kaliStart":"3102 BCE (conventional epoch, midnight at Ujjain, used by Aryabhata and retained in BSS-based reckoning; a computed back-projection, not a recorded event)","kaliEnd":"About 428,900 CE, roughly 432,000 years after the 3102 BCE epoch","currentPosition":"In 2026 CE we are about 5,127 years into the Kali yuga (Kali year about 5,127 to 5,128 depending on the convention used). This is roughly 1.2% of its 432,000-year span, leaving about 426,900 years. Brahmagupta's system does not change this figure.","summary":"Brahmagupta's Brahmasphutasiddhanta (628 CE) accepts the same four-yuga framework and 4,320,000-year mahayuga as Aryabhata, so it is not an alternative yuga timeline. Its critique is aimed at the planetary revolution counts, mean-motion parameters, and computational procedures that Aryabhata's school used to convert yuga cycles into planetary positions. Brahmagupta argued that several of these parameters and methods gave results that disagreed with observation, and he proposed corrected values and rules. He also criticized Aryabhata's proposal of a diurnally rotating earth, which he rejected on physical grounds. The Kali epoch of 3102 BCE was retained in BSS-based reckoning, so the cosmic timeline itself was left intact. Brahmagupta's later Khandakhadyaka simplified some of his own astronomy for practical use.","keyClaims":["Brahmagupta kept the 4,320,000-year mahayuga and the 1:3:2:1 yuga ratios of Aryabhata's framework.","The critique is about the accuracy of planetary revolution numbers and mean motions, not about whether the yugas are real.","Brahmagupta rejected Aryabhata's daily rotation of the earth, arguing it was physically untenable.","BSS provides corrected astronomical procedures (for example for planetary longitudes and eclipses) that were more accurate in some respects than Aryabhata's rules.","The Kali yuga epoch of 3102 BCE survived into BSS-based reckoning and was later used by Indian astronomers."],"evidence":["Textual continuity: BSS and Aryabhatiya share the same mahayuga length and yuga ratios, which can be checked directly in the texts.","BSS explicitly disputes Aryabhata's methods and parameters, as documented in Colebrooke's 1817 translation and in later scholarship (Neugebauer 1975, Plofker 2009).","Brahmagupta's rejection of earth rotation is recorded in BSS and discussed by historians of Indian astronomy.","The Khandakhadyaka (665 CE) shows Brahmagupta working out simpler practical methods, which is consistent with his own revision of earlier rules."],"criticisms":["Brahmagupta's critique does not actually challenge the yuga durations. Critics therefore see it as a refinement inside a mythic cosmology, not a move toward empirical chronology.","Parts of BSS's correction are sometimes exaggerated in popular accounts. Scholars differ on how much of BSS's astronomy was genuinely more accurate versus merely different.","Brahmagupta's rejection of earth rotation was based on physical arguments that were reasonable for his time but missed the point that the earth's motion would not necessarily be felt in the way he assumed. Historians debate how fair his criticism was.","The 3102 BCE Kali epoch is a back-projection from medieval astronomical reckoning. No independent historical evidence supports it as a real event.","Since the mahayuga figures are computed, not measured, Brahmagupta's accuracy claims are only meaningful within the astronomical calculation system, not within the cosmic timeline itself.","Some attributions of specific corrections to Brahmagupta (versus earlier or later sources) are contested in the secondary literature. Treat specific claims with caution until checked against the Sanskrit text."],"plausibility":2,"plausibilityReason":"The historical record of Brahmagupta's dispute with Aryabhata is well supported, and the texts are datable. But the yuga durations and the 3102 BCE epoch are not empirically grounded and cannot be matched to physical or archaeological reality. Brahmagupta's corrections improved computational accuracy within a mythic framework and did not move the chronology toward reality. The rating reflects the timeline claims, not the quality of his astronomy.","references":["Brahmasphutasiddhanta, Brahmagupta, 628 CE (translation by H.T. Colebrooke, 1817, chapters 12 and 18 in Algebra with Arithmetic and Mensuration)","Khandakhadyaka, Brahmagupta, 665 CE (translation and study by Bina Chatterjee, INSA, 1970)","Aryabhatiya, Aryabhata I, 499 CE (translation by Kripa Shankar Shukla, 1976)","Neugebauer, Otto. A History of Ancient Mathematical Astronomy. Springer, 1975","Plofker, Kim. Mathematics in India. Princeton University Press, 2009","Pingree, David. Census of the Exact Sciences in Sanskrit. American Philosophical Society, 1970-1994","Note: the author did not run live web searches for this entry. Citation details and dates should be verified before publication."]},{"id":"kali-3102","title":"Kali Yuga epoch of 17/18 Feb 3102 BCE as a real planetary conjunction","category":"Academic/historical","proponents":["Aryabhata (Aryabhatiya, 499 CE, the earliest surviving text to use the Kali epoch arithmetic)","Authors and redactors of the Surya Siddhanta tradition","Brahmagupta (Brahmasphutasiddhanta, 628 CE), who works in the same epoch framework","Sri Yukteswar Giri (The Holy Science, 1894), who popularised a cosmic-cycle reading of the yugas","Later popular writers who present the 3102 BCE date as a verified 'Great Conjunction' of all seven planets"],"primarySources":["Aryabhatiya (Aryabhata, 499 CE): gives the Kali epoch arithmetic (about 3600 Kali years elapsed at the time of composition)","Surya Siddhanta (anonymous, core redaction usually dated to roughly the 4th-5th century CE): defines the mahayuga of 4,320,000 years and a midnight Kali epoch","Brahmasphutasiddhanta (Brahmagupta, 628 CE): uses the same epoch framework","Vishnu Purana and other Puranas: yuga durations in divine years, a different ancient layer of the same cosmology"],"period":"The epoch arithmetic originates in the 5th century CE (Aryabhata, 499 CE). The 3102 BCE date was not an observed event recorded in any early text. The 'planetary conjunction' claim is a modern popular reading, mostly 19th and 20th century.","totalCycleYears":"4,320,000 years (mahayuga). Kali is 432,000 years of that. In human years, the Surya Siddhanta-style model uses 360 mortal years per divine year.","durations":{"satya":"1,728,000 years","treta":"1,296,000 years","dvapara":"864,000 years","kali":"432,000 years"},"kaliStart":"17/18 February 3102 BCE (proleptic Julian; midnight at Ujjain or Lanka in the Siddhanta reckoning, so the exact day varies by convention)","kaliEnd":"About 428,899 CE, after 432,000 years","currentPosition":"In 2026 CE the Kali year count is about 5128, so we are in Kali year 5128 (about 5,127 completed years).","summary":"The 17/18 February 3102 BCE date is the epoch (zero point) of the Kali Yuga in the Siddhanta-based Indian astronomical tradition. Indian astronomers set it by back-calculating from the mean motions of the Sun, Moon and planets, so the epoch is a mathematical anchor and not a recorded event. The popular claim that all seven planets were in conjunction at 0 degrees Aries on that date comes from the model's own construction: Siddhanta texts start the mahayuga with all mean longitudes at zero Aries. Modern ephemerides (JPL, Swiss Ephemeris) do not show a real seven-planet conjunction on that date. So the conjunction is best read as a feature of the model, not an observed sky event.","keyClaims":["The 3102 BCE epoch is an astronomical zero point in Siddhanta arithmetic, not a dated historical event.","The 'all planets at 0 Aries' condition at the mahayuga epoch is a model assumption built into the mean-motion parameters, so it does not count as independent evidence.","The Kali epoch was first explicitly used in the Aryabhatiya arithmetic (499 CE) and was not attested as a recorded starting date before that.","Modern ephemeris computations for 3102 BCE do not show a real conjunction of the seven planets within a tight arc.","The claim that the 3102 BCE conjunction is historically verified is a 19th-20th century popular claim, not a finding of peer-reviewed astronomy or Indology."],"evidence":["Surya Siddhanta and Aryabhatiya arithmetic define the epoch and mean motions, so the epoch is derived from the model.","Ebenezer Burgess's 1860 translation of the Surya Siddhanta presents the text's epoch and mean-motion parameters, which supports reading the epoch as a model zero point.","David Pingree (1973, 1978 and later) argued that Indian astronomy drew on Greek and Babylonian sources and that epochs were fixed by computation, not by observation.","Kim Plofker (Mathematics in India, 2009) summarises the Siddhanta epoch construction and the role of mean-motion parameters.","Computed positions from modern ephemerides for 3102 BCE do not reproduce a tight seven-planet conjunction; this is a checkable claim that anyone can run with JPL HORIZONS or Swiss Ephemeris, though I did not run it in this session.","The 4,320,000-year mahayuga and its quarter-yugas are internally consistent arithmetic, which shows the cycle was designed as a computational system."],"criticisms":["No contemporary record from 3102 BCE mentions a Kali epoch or a planetary conjunction. Records of the epoch start only in the 5th century CE.","The 'all planets at 0 Aries' condition is a property of the Siddhanta model, so it cannot also serve as evidence for the model.","Modern ephemeris checks do not show the claimed seven-planet alignment on that date.","Pingree and others argue the epoch was chosen for computational convenience (round numbers and mean-motion zeroes), not measured from the sky.","Popular sources (including some Western and Hindu revivalist writers) present the conjunction as a verified historical fact. Scholars of Indian astronomy do not accept that framing.","Different Siddhantas and different reckoning conventions (Ujjain vs Lanka midnight, Julian vs astronomical dating) give slightly different dates, which undermines the idea of one precise 'real' event.","Sri Yukteswar's reinterpretation (cycles of about 24,000 years with yuga shifts) is a separate theory and does not confirm the Siddhanta epoch."],"plausibility":2,"plausibilityReason":"As a claim that a real seven-planet conjunction happened on 17/18 Feb 3102 BCE, the plausibility is very low. The epoch is a computed zero point of the Siddhanta mean-motion model, and no independent record or modern ephemeris check supports a real conjunction. The model may have been built on real Babylonian and Greek astronomical data, so the arithmetic has a historical basis, but that does not make the specific 3102 BCE event real. This rating is from scholarly knowledge and was not verified with live web searches in this session.","references":["Burgess, Ebenezer (1860), Translation of the Surya Siddhanta (with notes; edited by Phanindralal Gangooly, 1935)","Pingree, David (1973), 'The Mesopotamian Origin of Early Indian Mathematical Astronomy', Journal for the History of Astronomy 4","Pingree, David (1978), 'History of Mathematical Astronomy in India', Dictionary of Scientific Biography, vol. 15","Plofker, Kim (2009), Mathematics in India, Princeton University Press","Sri Yukteswar Giri (1894), The Holy Science"]},{"id":"yukteswar","title":"Sri Yukteswar's 24,000-year cycle (The Holy Science, 1894)","category":"Modern spiritual/esoteric","proponents":["Sri Yukteswar Giri (1855-1936)","Paramahansa Yogananda (popularized it in Autobiography of a Yogi, 1946)","Self-Realization Fellowship and Kriya Yoga lineage followers"],"primarySources":["Sri Yukteswar Giri, The Holy Science (Kaivalya Darshanam), 1894","Paramahansa Yogananda, Autobiography of a Yogi, 1946 (secondary popularization)"],"period":"Originated 1894 (The Holy Science); widely disseminated from the 1940s onward via Yogananda","totalCycleYears":"24,000 years (a full ascending plus descending cycle, two arcs of 12,000 years each)","durations":{"satya":"4,800 years per arc (9,600 across both arcs)","treta":"3,600 years per arc (7,200 across both arcs)","dvapara":"2,400 years per arc (4,800 across both arcs)","kali":"1,200 years per arc (2,400 across both arcs)"},"kaliStart":"Commonly summarized as about 499 CE for the descending Kali Yuga (unverified in this run; treat as secondary-source reading)","kaliEnd":"About 1699 CE, when the scheme says Dvapara begins","currentPosition":"In 2026 the scheme places us in the Dvapara Yuga (about 1699 to 4099 CE by this reading), which is the ascending arc's second age. Note this is the claim as relayed in the task and in secondary summaries; the primary-text dating was not re-verified in this run.","summary":"Sri Yukteswar's The Holy Science (1894) reinterprets the yuga scheme of the Puranas as a 24,000-year cycle of the equinoxes, which he tied to a larger solar motion around a central point he called Vishnunabhi. He divides the cycle into an ascending arc (Kali, Dvapara, Treta, Satya, each lengthening from 1,200 up to 4,800 years) and a descending arc (Satya down to Kali). Each arc is 12,000 years, and he argues the Puranic 4.32-million-year figures come from a misreading of the astronomical units. On his reading the world is in an ascending phase of spiritual and intellectual progress. Yogananda popularized the system, and it is most influential in Yogananda-derived and Kriya Yoga circles. The main criticism is that the astronomical premises do not match modern astronomy or the Siddhantic tradition.","keyClaims":["The precession of the equinoxes (or a related solar cycle) takes about 24,000 years, not the modern ~25,772 years.","The yuga durations (4800/3600/2400/1200 years) apply to each arc, with the total cycle being 24,000 years.","The Puranic yuga figures (4.32 million years per mahayuga) are a misreading of the astronomical units, and the correct reading is the shorter 24,000-year cycle.","Humanity is currently in an ascending phase, with Dvapara beginning around 1699 CE.","The Sun's motion around a central point (Vishnunabhi) underlies the cycle; Holy Science treats this as an astronomical fact."],"evidence":["Internal astronomical arguments in The Holy Science (author's reinterpretation of Puranic texts)","Claimed correspondence with the precession of the equinoxes, which is real but has a ~25,772-year period, not 24,000","Yogananda's account of his guru's teaching (Autobiography of a Yogi), which is testimony, not independent evidence","Historical observation of Indian scientific and social progress since the 19th century, used as confirmation after the fact"],"criticisms":["The 24,000-year precession figure does not match the observed precession period of about 25,772 years, which is the standard modern value.","No independent astronomical evidence supports a sun-centred cycle around Vishnunabhi or a 24,000-year solar orbit as described.","The reinterpretation of Puranic yuga numbers as reduced astronomical units is an ad hoc move, not an accepted reading in Sanskrit or Siddhantic scholarship.","Mainstream Indologists and historians of astronomy do not treat the Holy Science as a historical or scientific source. Its dating of Dvapara (1699 CE) and of the Kali Yuga has no external anchor.","Unfalsifiable framing: the scheme is retrofitted to observed history, and the 1699 date is not derived from independent evidence.","The Holy Science is a theological and philosophical text on Kriya yoga and Christian-Hindu synthesis, so reading it as chronology overreaches its purpose."],"plausibility":2,"plausibilityReason":"The core astronomical premise (a 24,000-year precession) conflicts with the measured precession period of ~25,772 years, and the solar-orbit claim has no observational support. The yuga scheme is a philosophical reinterpretation rather than a testable chronology. Some of the cyclical idea (alternating ages) has an analogue in the Puranic tradition, which gives it modest cultural weight but no empirical plausibility.","references":["Sri Yukteswar Giri, The Holy Science (1894), Kaivalya Darshanam; the full text is available from Self-Realization Fellowship and various public-domain archives","Paramahansa Yogananda, Autobiography of a Yogi (1946), chapter on Sri Yukteswar","Precession of the equinoxes: standard modern value ~25,772 years, as in standard astronomy references","NOT VERIFIED IN THIS RUN: specific page numbers and the exact 499 CE / 1699 CE dating in the primary text should be checked against the original before citing"]},{"id":"yogananda","title":"Yogananda and SRF extensions of Sri Yukteswar's 24,000-year yuga theory","category":"Modern spiritual/esoteric","proponents":["Sri Yukteswar Giri (originator of the model)","Paramahansa Yogananda (popularized and extended it)","Self-Realization Fellowship (SRF) and Yogoda Satsanga successors who teach it"],"primarySources":["Sri Yukteswar, The Holy Science (Kaivalya Darshana), commonly dated 1894","Paramahansa Yogananda, Autobiography of a Yogi (1946), which recounts Yukteswar's teaching and Yogananda's own gloss","Paramahansa Yogananda, The Second Coming of Christ (SRF compilation of lectures and commentary, published posthumously, 1979 onward)","SRF lesson and commentary materials (no single canonical yuga chart; the 24,000-year model is presented in summary form)"],"period":"Yukteswar's model c. 1894; Yogananda's popularization and extensions c. 1920s to 1946, with further SRF teaching later in the 20th century","totalCycleYears":"24,000 (two arcs of 12,000 years each: descending and ascending)","durations":{"satya":"4,800 years in each arc (9,600 total with transitional periods, per Yukteswar's ratio scheme)","treta":"3,600 years in each arc","dvapara":"2,400 years in each arc","kali":"1,200 years in each arc"},"kaliStart":"Approximately 499 CE, at the start of the ascending Kali arc, as commonly reported from Yukteswar's scheme (exact transitional dating varies between secondary accounts; verify against The Holy Science)","kaliEnd":"Approximately 1699 CE, which Yukteswar's scheme marks as the end of the ascending Kali and the start of the ascending Dvapara","currentPosition":"Per this view, 2026 falls in the ascending Dvapara Yuga, about 327 years into its 2,400-year span, which would end around 4099 CE. The model treats the world as in a gradual upswing of spiritual awareness.","summary":"Sri Yukteswar's The Holy Science proposes that the equinox precession cycle of about 24,000 years is the true yuga cycle, split into descending and ascending halves of 12,000 years, each divided into Kali, Dvapara, Treta, and Satya periods in the ratio 1:2:3:4. He reinterpreted the Puranic figures of millions of years as symbolic and tied the cycle to the sun's supposed motion around a central point. Yogananda adopted this framework in the Autobiography of a Yogi and later SRF teaching, presenting the current era as an ascending Dvapara that began around 1699 CE. He also linked the scheme to the spiritual role of his guru lineage, including the claim that Mahavatar Babaji and the Kriya Yoga revival fit a larger cosmic plan. Critics note that the cycle's astronomy is inexact and that the model is a modern synthesis rather than a traditional Hindu reckoning.","keyClaims":["The yuga cycle is approximately 24,000 years, tied to the precession of the equinoxes, with descending and ascending arcs of 12,000 years each.","Yukteswar's ratio scheme (1:2:3:4) assigns Kali 1,200, Dvapara 2,400, Treta 3,600, and Satya 4,800 years per arc.","The traditional Puranic figure of 4.32 million years per mahayuga is a misreading or symbolic inflation of the shorter astronomical cycle.","Humanity is in the ascending Dvapara Yuga, which began around 1699 CE, implying a long gradual rise in consciousness.","Yogananda's guru lineage (Babaji, Lahiri Mahasaya, Yukteswar) is part of a cosmic plan to revive yoga and spiritual awareness in this period."],"evidence":["Precession of the equinoxes is a real, measurable phenomenon, roughly 25,772 years for one tropical cycle, so the idea of a long astronomical cycle has a genuine basis.","The 1:2:3:4 ratio of yuga lengths is traditional in Hindu texts, so Yukteswar's proportions have textual precedent even if the absolute durations are re-scaled.","Yukteswar's writing and Yogananda's Autobiography are primary testimony of the teaching as delivered within SRF lineage.","SRF adherents report subjective spiritual experience and practice-based validation, which is not evidence about cosmic chronology."],"criticisms":["The precession period is about 25,772 years, not exactly 24,000, so the 24,000 figure is a rounded or adjusted number rather than a measured one.","The claim that the sun moves around a central star on a 24,000-year orbit has no observational support; the sun's actual galactic orbit takes roughly 225 to 250 million years.","Yukteswar's re-scaling of the Puranic figures is a reinterpretation that the texts do not clearly support; the Puranas and Manusmriti describe the mahayuga as 4.32 million years.","The 1699 CE transition and the 1200/2400/3600/4800 breakdown are not attested in any pre-modern Hindu source and cannot be checked against independent historical evidence.","Yogananda's extensions tie the model to his own lineage and mission, which makes the scheme hard to test independently and exposes it to confirmation bias.","Academic study of Yukteswar's yuga scheme is thin; it is usually treated as a modern esoteric synthesis rather than a continuation of classical Hindu chronology."],"plausibility":2,"plausibilityReason":"The underlying idea of a long precession-based cycle is real astronomy, but the specific 24,000-year sun-orbit claim, the 1699 transition, and the claimed match with traditional yuga ratios lack independent evidence and conflict with established astronomy. The model is internally coherent within SRF teaching but does not survive external scrutiny.","references":["Sri Yukteswar, The Holy Science (1894); check the SRF edition for exact dating and wording of the cycle chart","Paramahansa Yogananda, Autobiography of a Yogi (1946), SRF edition","Paramahansa Yogananda, The Second Coming of Christ (SRF, 1979 onward)","SRF official website, yogananda-srf.org (verify current teaching materials)","Precession of the equinoxes: standard astronomy references giving about 25,772 years for a tropical cycle"]},{"id":"precession","title":"Yugas as a precession cycle (Yukteswar's 24,000-year Great Year model)","category":"Modern spiritual/esoteric","proponents":["Sri Yukteswar Giri (The Holy Science, 1894)","Paramahansa Yogananda (popularized the scheme in Autobiography of a Yogi, 1946)","Later Kriya Yoga and New Age writers who repeat the 24,000-year scheme"],"primarySources":["Manusmriti 1.68-71 (c. 2nd c. BCE to 2nd c. CE): gives yuga lengths in divine years, not linked to precession","Vishnu Purana Book 1 (c. 3rd-5th c. CE): yuga durations, no precession link","Surya Siddhanta (c. 4th-5th c. CE, later revised): treats precession (ayana-chalana) as an oscillation, with yugas computed separately","Aryabhatiya (499 CE): gives the Kali epoch used for chronology","Sri Yukteswar Giri, The Holy Science (1894): proposes a 24,000-year cycle of ascending and descending yugas tied to equinox precession"],"period":"Yukteswar's formulation: 1894 CE. The idea of a Great Year is much older in Hellenistic and Babylonian thought (c. 2nd c. BCE onward), and Hipparchus identified precession c. 150 BCE.","totalCycleYears":"24,000 years for Yukteswar's yuga cycle (ascending plus descending halves of 12,000 years each). Classical Puranic mahayuga is 4,320,000 years. Precession period itself is about 25,772 years (modern value), and the Greek Great Year is 25,920 years.","durations":{"satya":"4,800 years (Yukteswar, literal solar years). Classical texts: 1,728,000 years (4,800 divine years x 360)","treta":"3,600 years (Yukteswar). Classical: 1,296,000 years","dvapara":"2,400 years (Yukteswar). Classical: 864,000 years","kali":"1,200 years (Yukteswar). Classical: 432,000 years"},"kaliStart":"c. 499 CE (start of the descending Kali phase in Yukteswar's scheme, as commonly summarized; verify against the text)","kaliEnd":"c. 1699 CE (end of the descending Kali phase, per the same summaries). The scheme then places the ascending Dvapara after this point.","currentPosition":"In 2026, under Yukteswar's scheme, the world is in an ascending Dvapara Yuga that began around 1699 CE, roughly 327 years in. Under classical chronology, we are still in Kali Yuga, about 5,127 years after its traditional start in 3102 BCE. These two positions cannot both be right.","summary":"This view says the yuga sequence (Satya, Treta, Dvapara, Kali) is not a purely numerological or ritual count but a description of the equinox precession cycle. Yukteswar proposed a 24,000-year cycle, with a 12,000-year descending arc and a 12,000-year ascending arc, each divided into 1:2:3:4 yuga proportions. He read the phases as changes in human consciousness or knowledge tied to the position of the equinoxes against the stars. The classical Puranic yuga lengths are much larger (4.32 million years for a mahayuga) and do not match this 24,000-year cycle. The scheme also does not match the real precession period of about 25,772 years, which is a separate and well-measured quantity.","keyClaims":["The yuga cycle equals a precession-driven Great Year of about 24,000 years (Yukteswar)","Humanity moves through ascending and descending halves of the cycle, with consciousness rising or falling","The current era is an ascending Dvapara that began around 1699 CE","Classical Indian texts encode the precession cycle in the yuga durations","Ancient Indian astronomers knew precession and used it to date the yugas"],"evidence":["The 1:2:3:4 ratio of yuga lengths matches the Puranic proportions, which Yukteswar used as an internal consistency argument","Precession is real and well measured (about 50.3 arcseconds per year, ~25,772-year period), and Indian astronomy did discuss precession (ayanamsa, ayana-chalana)","Yukteswar's scheme is internally consistent with its own 24,000-year cycle and gives a tidy 12,000-year ascending and descending structure","Proponents cite the lack of a mahayuga-precession link in Indian texts as evidence that the link was esoteric and not written down"],"criticisms":["Classical sources (Manusmriti, Vishnu Purana, Mahabharata) give yuga lengths in divine years (x360), with no reference to precession. Yukteswar's 24,000-year cycle is a modern reinterpretation.","The real precession period is about 25,772 years, not 24,000. Using 24,000 is not a measurement and does not fit observed precession.","Precession does not produce four phases of unequal length in any natural way. Its effect on the equinox position is smooth and cyclical, so the 1:2:3:4 structure has no astronomical basis.","Indian astronomers (Surya Siddhanta, Brahmagupta, Bhaskara II) treated precession as an oscillation of limited amplitude, not a linear 24,000-year cycle that drives human history.","The Kali epoch (3102 BCE) is a computed astronomical epoch from Aryabhata's system, not a precession marker. Its claimed alignment of planets is a retrospective calculation.","The scheme's predictions about consciousness phases are not testable and have no historical evidence behind them.","Two different Indian positions (classical chronology vs Yukteswar) give incompatible present dates, which undermines any claim that the scheme reflects a single known fact."],"plausibility":2,"plausibilityReason":"Precession is real and well measured, so the physical premise is sound. The link to yuga durations is not supported: classical yuga numbers are not precession-based, and Yukteswar's 24,000-year cycle does not match the real ~25,772-year period. Nothing in the timeline can be checked against observation, and the claimed phase changes in history are not documented. The most that can be said is that the scheme is a modern, internally consistent reinterpretation.","references":["Sri Yukteswar Giri, The Holy Science (1894), Yogoda Satsanga publications","Paramahansa Yogananda, Autobiography of a Yogi (1946)","Burgess, E. (trans.), Surya Siddhanta (1860), Journal of the American Oriental Society","Manusmriti, chapter 1 (verses on yuga durations)","Vishnu Purana, Book 1 (yuga and kalpa durations)","Pingree, David, works on Sanskrit astronomy and precession (general reference; check specific titles before citing)","Hipparchus, precession discovery, as reported in Ptolemy's Almagest (c. 150 CE)","IAU and IERS documentation for the lunisolar precession period (~25,772 years)"]},{"id":"kalpa","title":"Kalpa / Day of Brahma: 4.32 billion-year days and a 311-trillion-year lifespan","category":"Traditional scriptural","proponents":["Traditional Hindu pandits and almanac (panchanga) makers","Puranic commentators","Modern Hindu apologists who cite the Kalpa figure as evidence of ancient knowledge (e.g., some writers associated with Vedic-science movements)"],"primarySources":["Manusmriti Ch. 1 (roughly 2nd c. BCE to 2nd c. CE), gives 1,000 mahayugas = 1 kalpa and 12,000 divine years = 4 yugas","Vishnu Purana Book 1 (c. 3rd-6th c. CE) on kalpas, manvantaras and Brahma's life","Bhagavata Purana Book 3 (c. 9th-10th c. CE) on Brahma's 100-year life","Surya Siddhanta (c. 4th-5th c. CE, with later revisions) gives 4,320,000 solar years per mahayuga and the Kali start epoch","Aryabhatiya (499 CE) uses the 4.32 million-year mahayuga","Brahmasphutasiddhanta of Brahmagupta (628 CE) reworks the same framework"],"period":"Framework attested from the late Vedic/epic period (Mahabharata and Manusmriti); numerical scheme fixed in Siddhantic texts c. 4th-6th c. CE and in Puranas through the medieval period. Modern reinterpretations are 20th-21st c.","totalCycleYears":"Kalpa = 4,320,000,000 years (1,000 mahayugas of 4,320,000 years each). Brahma's full life = 100 Brahma-years of 360 Brahma-days each = 311,040,000,000,000 years (about 311 trillion).","durations":{"satya":"1,728,000 human years (4,800 divine years)","treta":"1,296,000 human years (3,600 divine years)","dvapara":"864,000 human years (2,400 divine years)","kali":"432,000 human years (1,200 divine years); one divine year = 360 human years"},"kaliStart":"3102 BCE (traditional epoch, 18 February 3102 BCE in the Siddhantic reckoning; Aryabhata and Surya Siddhanta-based almanacs)","kaliEnd":"About 428,899 CE (432,000 years after the traditional start)","currentPosition":"Per the traditional scheme, we are in the first kalpa of the 51st year of Brahma (Shvetavaraha Kalpa, the first kalpa of the 2nd half/parardha of Brahma's life), in the 7th of 14 manvantaras (Vaivasvata Manvantara), in the 28th mahayuga, in Kali Yuga. About 5,127 years of Kali Yuga have elapsed as of 2026 CE. The sankalpa count of elapsed creation time is about 1.97 billion years (the exact figure varies by almanac and year).","summary":"The Kalpa is the Puranic and Siddhantic unit of cosmic time: 1,000 mahayugas, or 4.32 billion years, forming one day of Brahma. Brahma's life of 100 years, each of 360 such days, gives about 311 trillion years. Within each kalpa are 14 manvantaras, each with 71 mahayugas, plus transition periods. The traditional almanac (panchanga) places us in the 51st year of Brahma, in the Vaivasvata manvantara and Kali Yuga. Whatever the cosmological intent, the numbers are a symbolic-computational scheme. They were fixed by Siddhantic astronomers and mythic chronologists, not by any observation or measurement. The 4.32 billion-year kalpa is often noted as roughly matching the Earth's age (about 4.54 Gyr), but that resemblance is not evidence of anything.","keyClaims":["A kalpa is 4.32 billion years, equal to 1,000 mahayugas, and is one day of Brahma.","Brahma lives 100 years of 360 days each, about 311.04 trillion years in total.","We are in the first kalpa of the 51st Brahma-year, in the 7th manvantara (Vaivasvata), 28th mahayuga, Kali Yuga.","Kali Yuga began in 3102 BCE and lasts 432,000 years.","The scheme is internally consistent across Puranic and Siddhantic sources."],"evidence":["Manusmriti 1.64-86 gives the 12,000 divine-year mahayuga (4.32 million human years) and the 1,000-mahayuga kalpa, and says the kalpa is one day of Brahma.","Surya Siddhanta and Aryabhatiya (499 CE) both use the 4,320,000-year mahayuga, and the Kali epoch of 3102 BCE is a standard Siddhantic reckoning point.","Brahmagupta's Brahmasphutasiddhanta (628 CE) and later almanacs retain the 4.32 billion-year kalpa, so the figure is attested across many centuries.","David Pingree's work on Sanskrit astronomical texts (Census of the Exact Sciences in Sanskrit, 1970s-1990s) documents the Siddhantic mahayuga and kalpa arithmetic and its internal consistency.","Almanac sankalpa formulas recite elapsed kalpa, manvantara and yuga counts, showing continuous ritual use of the scheme."],"criticisms":["The numbers have no empirical or observational basis. They are computed from ratios and mythic values, not measured.","The kalpa-to-Earth-age resemblance (about 4.32 Gyr vs. about 4.54 Gyr) is coincidental. The Earth's age comes from radiometric dating of meteorites and rocks, and the Kalpa figure cannot be mapped onto it without circular reasoning.","Brahma's 311-trillion-year lifespan exceeds the age of the observable universe (about 13.8 Gyr) by a factor of roughly 22,000. Nothing physical corresponds to it.","Textual chronology conflicts across sources: Puranas, Manusmriti and Siddhantas differ on several parameters, and some counts (e.g., the elapsed-kalpa figure in sankalpa) are arithmetic outputs rather than historical records.","Kali Yuga's 3102 BCE epoch is a Siddhantic convention. It does not correspond to any dated historical event, and there is no independent archaeological or astronomical check.","Claims that the scheme encodes ancient astronomical knowledge (precession, planetary periods) are contested; Siddhantic astronomy did use real observations for planetary parameters, but the cosmic-cycle numbers were not derived from them.","Scholars such as Pingree and others note the mahayuga arithmetic is closer to a symbolic ratio system than a chronology."],"plausibility":1,"plausibilityReason":"As a literal account of cosmic or human history, the Kalpa scheme has essentially no support. Its numbers are internally consistent arithmetic, but they conflict with radiometric dating, the cosmological age of the universe, and archaeology. The only partial overlap is the rough match of kalpa length to Earth's age, which is coincidental. The scheme is best treated as a religious and mathematical cosmology.","references":["Manusmriti, Ch. 1 (Olivelle translation, Oxford, 2005)","Vishnu Purana, Book 1 (H.H. Wilson translation)","Bhagavata Purana, Book 3 (A.C. Bhaktivedanta translation, or Sanskrit critical edition)","Surya Siddhanta (Burgess translation, 1860)","Aryabhatiya, Ch. 1 (Clark translation, 1930)","Brahmasphutasiddhanta of Brahmagupta (Colebrooke translation, 1817)","David Pingree, Census of the Exact Sciences in Sanskrit (American Philosophical Society, 1970-1994)","David Pingree, 'History of Mathematical Astronomy in India', Dictionary of Scientific Biography, vol. 15 (1978)","Note: no live web lookups were performed for this entry; dates and numbers are from prior knowledge and should be spot-checked against the sources above."]},{"id":"manvantara","title":"Manvantara system: 14 Manus, 71 mahayugas each, sandhis","category":"Traditional scriptural","proponents":["Traditional Puranic and Smriti exegesis (Vishnu Purana, Bhagavata Purana, Vayu/Brahmanda Puranas)","Kullūka Bhaṭṭa (commentary on Manusmriti, 15th c.)","Modern traditionalist expositors who present the Manvantara scheme as literal cosmology"],"primarySources":["Manusmriti (Manava Dharmashastra), chapter 1 (roughly 2nd c. BCE to 2nd c. CE), gives manvantara and yuga arithmetic","Vishnu Purana Book 1 and Book 3 (approx. 3rd to 5th c. CE), describes 14 Manus, 71 mahayugas per manvantara, and sandhis","Bhagavata Purana Book 8 (approx. 9th to 10th c. CE), Manvantara narratives","Surya Siddhanta (approx. 5th c. CE, revised later), gives mahayuga 4,320,000 solar years and yuga subdivisions","Aryabhatiya (499 CE), Kali epoch and mahayuga arithmetic"],"period":"Manusmriti and Puranic texts, roughly 2nd c. BCE to 10th c. CE. Standardized in Siddhantic astronomy from about the 5th c. CE onward.","totalCycleYears":"One mahayuga is 4,320,000 years. One manvantara is 71 mahayugas, or 306,720,000 years. One kalpa is 1,000 mahayugas, or 4.32 billion years, containing 14 manvantaras plus 15 sandhis.","durations":{"satya":"1,728,000 years (sandhya and sandhyamsa add 144,000 each side, for a total of 2,016,000 with dawn and dusk in some reckonings)","treta":"1,296,000 years","dvapara":"864,000 years","kali":"432,000 years"},"kaliStart":"3102 BCE (traditional epoch, roughly 18 February 3102 BCE in the Hindu calendar; Aryabhata's astronomical back-calculation)","kaliEnd":"About 428,899 CE (432,000 years after the 3102 BCE epoch, counting astronomical years with no year zero)","currentPosition":"In 2026 CE we are in Kali year roughly 5,127 to 5,128, which is about 1.2% of the 432,000-year Kali Yuga. Within the Manvantara scheme this is the Kali Yuga that ends the 28th mahayuga of the 7th (Vaivasvata) manvantara. Puranic counts usually say 27 mahayugas of this manvantara have elapsed, with the 28th current. Kalpa-level counts usually place us roughly 1.97 billion years after the start of the current kalpa, a figure cited in Hindu calendrical tradition.","summary":"The Manvantara system is a Puranic and Smriti cosmology. A kalpa (day of Brahma) is divided into 14 manvantaras, each ruled by a Manu, and each manvantara is 71 mahayugas long. Between manvantaras, and at the start of the kalpa, there are 15 sandhi periods, each equal to one Krita (Satya) Yuga of 1,728,000 years. The arithmetic is internally consistent: 14 times 71 gives 994 mahayugas, and 15 sandhis of 0.4 mahayugas each give 6 more, for 1,000 per kalpa. We are in the 7th manvantara, the Vaivasvata Manvantara, ruled by Manu Vaivasvata (son of Vivasvat, the Sun). The current mahayuga is the 28th of that manvantara, and its Kali Yuga began in 3102 BCE. The scheme is cosmological and mythic in scale. It is not an historical chronology, and its numbers serve theological and ritual purposes.","keyClaims":["A kalpa of 4.32 billion years contains 14 manvantaras and 15 sandhis, and is equal to 1,000 mahayugas.","A manvantara is 71 mahayugas (306,720,000 years), and each sandhi is one Satya Yuga (1,728,000 years).","The 14 Manus are Svayambhuva, Svarocisha, Uttama, Tamasa, Raivata, Chakshusha, Vaivasvata, Savarni, Daksha-savarni, Brahma-savarni, Dharma-savarni, Rudra-savarni, Deva-savarni (Raucya), and Indra-savarni (Bhautya).","The current manvantara is the 7th (Vaivasvata), and we are in its 28th mahayuga.","The Kali Yuga began in 3102 BCE, so Kali started about 5,127 years ago.","The Manusmriti and some Puranic passages state the yuga figures in divine years of 360 human years each, which multiplies the figures by 360. The Surya Siddhanta and most Puranic presentations treat them as human solar years."],"evidence":["Internal arithmetic: 14 x 71 = 994, plus 15 sandhis of 0.4 mahayugas = 6, giving 1,000 mahayugas per kalpa. The scheme is self-consistent.","Manusmriti 1.79 and Vishnu Purana 1.3 and 3.3 describe the manvantara structure with the same numbers, which shows textual agreement across traditions.","The Surya Siddhanta and Aryabhatiya give the mahayuga as 4,320,000 years and the Kali epoch as 3102 BCE, so the astronomical texts and the Puranic arithmetic are linked.","Calendrical use: Hindu almanacs (panchanga) record Kali Yuga years and the Vikram and Shaka eras in ways that depend on this epoch."],"criticisms":["No physical, geological, archaeological, or astronomical evidence supports a manvantara of about 306.7 million years, or a kalpa of 4.32 billion years. Its scale is mythic, not measured.","The Manusmriti and some Puranic passages use divine-year multipliers (1 divine year = 360 human years). Texts disagree about whether figures are human or divine years, which makes the chronology internally inconsistent across sources.","The Kali epoch of 3102 BCE is not a historical event. Scholars such as Pingree and Plofker treat it as a retrospective astronomical construct, probably chosen by Aryabhata-era and Siddhantic astronomers for computational convenience.","The Vaivasvata manvantara is placed at roughly 120,000 years in some reckonings and at about 1.95 to 1.97 billion years since kalpa start in others. The mahayuga count (27 or 28 elapsed) is itself disputed among Puranas.","Modern historians of science (Burgess 1860 translation of the Surya Siddhanta, Pingree 1981 Census of the Exact Sciences in Sanskrit, Plofker 2009 Mathematics in India) show that the yuga system was formalized in Siddhantic astronomy after the Puranic cosmology, and that its numbers were partly fixed by ritual and calendrical needs.","Kali Yuga's start date is conventionally tied to the Mahabharata war and to Parikshit's reign in some traditions. Those historical anchors are not reconciled with 3102 BCE and are used selectively."],"plausibility":1,"plausibilityReason":"As a literal historical or cosmological timeline the Manvantara scheme has no empirical support. The arithmetic is consistent, and the scheme is a useful record of how Hindu cosmology structured time, but its numbers describe no measured or dated events. Plausibility as a statement about the real age of the universe or the Earth is effectively zero, since the scientific age of the universe is about 13.8 billion years and the Earth about 4.54 billion years.","references":["Burgess, E. (1860). Surya Siddhanta: A Textbook of Hindu Astronomy. (Translation with notes.)","Pingree, D. (1981). Census of the Exact Sciences in Sanskrit, Series A, Vol. 1. American Philosophical Society.","Plofker, K. (2009). Mathematics in India. Princeton University Press.","Doniger, W. and Smith, B. K. (1991). The Laws of Manu. Penguin Classics. (Translation of Manusmriti, including chapter 1 on yugas and manvantaras.)","Wilson, H. H. (1840). The Vishnu Purana. (Translation, Book 1 and Book 3.)","Aryabhatiya of Aryabhata (499 CE), mahayuga and Kali epoch arithmetic."]},{"id":"mahabharata","title":"Mahabharata accounts of the yugas (Markandeya, Bhishma, Hanuman-Bhima)","category":"Traditional scriptural","proponents":["Traditional Hindu exegesis that treats the Mahabharata as authoritative (smriti/itihasa)","Narrative frame speakers within the text: Markandeya (Vana Parva), Bhishma (Shanti Parva), Hanuman (Vana Parva, Bhima episode)"],"primarySources":["Mahabharata, Vana Parva: Markandeya's discourse to Yudhishthira on the four yugas and the coming decline of dharma (approx. ch. 149-150 and 188-190 in Ganguli numbering; numbering varies by edition), including the Kalki prophecy (approx. ch. 190)","Mahabharata, Vana Parva: Hanuman-Bhima episode near the Gandhamadana/Saugandhika lotus material (approx. ch. 146-150 in Ganguli numbering), where Hanuman speaks of the ages and his own Treta-age role","Mahabharata, Shanti Parva: Bhishma's instruction on rajadharma and the decline of dharma across the ages (approx. ch. 231-232 in Ganguli numbering)","Mahabharata, Mausala and Mahaprasthanika Parvas: portents after Krishna's death, later read by commentators as the onset of Kali","Composition: the Mahabharata is a layered text, generally dated c. 400 BCE-400 CE in its main redaction, with later additions; the yuga material belongs mostly to the later didactic layers"],"period":"Composed in layers c. 400 BCE-400 CE; the yuga passages are generally treated by scholars as later didactic additions (Biardeau and Hiltebeitel discuss the layering)","totalCycleYears":"4,320,000 human years (12,000 divine years x 360). The Mahabharata passages use the same 4:3:2:1 proportions as Manusmriti 1.67-71, with twilights (sandhya and sandhyamsha) added to each age.","durations":{"satya":"4000 + 400 + 400 = 4800 divine years = 1,728,000 human years","treta":"3000 + 300 + 300 = 3600 divine years = 1,296,000 human years","dvapara":"2000 + 200 + 200 = 2400 divine years = 864,000 human years","kali":"1000 + 100 + 100 = 1200 divine years = 432,000 human years"},"kaliStart":"No calendar date in the Mahabharata. Mausala Parva portents after Krishna's death are later read as Kali's onset. The 3102 BCE epoch is from later Indian astronomical and Puranic reckoning (e.g., Aryabhata's Kali epoch, 499 CE), not from the Mahabharata itself.","kaliEnd":"About 428,899 CE if the 3102 BCE start and the full 432,000-year duration are used. The Mahabharata gives no end date.","currentPosition":"Within this scheme, about year 5,128 of Kali (2026 CE with a 3102 BCE start), with roughly 426,872 years remaining. This is only meaningful if the scheme itself is accepted.","summary":"The Mahabharata presents the four yugas (Krita/Satya, Treta, Dvapara, Kali) as a declining sequence of dharma, with lifespans and virtue shrinking from age to age. Markandeya's discourse in the Vana Parva gives the 4:3:2:1 divine-year durations with twilights, which sum to 12,000 divine years, equal to 4.32 million human years when a divine year is taken as 360 human years. Bhishma's teaching in the Shanti Parva and the Hanuman-Bhima episode in the Vana Parva discuss the ages mainly in moral and ethical terms, not as a precise chronology. The Mahabharata does not supply a calendar date for the start of Kali; the 3102 BCE date that later tradition attaches to Kali comes from outside the epic.","keyClaims":["The four yugas last 4800, 3600, 2400, and 1200 divine years including twilights, for a total of 12,000 divine years.","A divine year is equivalent to 360 human years, giving a mahayuga of 4,320,000 human years.","Dharma declines across the ages; in Kali, human lifespans and virtue are sharply reduced.","Kalki (Vishnuyashas) is prophesied as a future restorer of dharma at the end of Kali (Vana Parva prophecy).","Kali's arrival is linked in tradition to the end of the Yadava line and Krishna's departure, though the epic does not state a date."],"evidence":["Markandeya's Vana Parva discourse explicitly lays out the 4000/3000/2000/1000 proportions with 400/300/200/100 twilight periods on each side.","Manusmriti 1.67-71 gives the parallel 4800/3600/2400/1200 divine-year structure, and the Mahabharata passages match it closely, which suggests a shared textual tradition rather than independent observation.","The Kalki prophecy in the Vana Parva places the restoration of dharma at the close of Kali.","Mausala Parva omens after Krishna's death are the epic's own marker of a decisive turn toward Kali, which later commentators use as the start of the age."],"criticisms":["The durations are mythic and symbolic; no independent evidence (archaeological, astronomical, or textual dating) supports a 4.32 million-year cosmic cycle.","The yuga passages are didactic and mostly appear in later layers of a text that grew over many centuries, so they cannot be read as contemporary chronicle.","Different parts of the Mahabharata do not give a single consistent scheme; the Hanuman material places him in Treta while other parts stress moral rather than numerical decline.","The Mahabharata gives no date for Kali's start or for the Kurukshetra war in calendar terms, so the 3102 BCE epoch is an inference from later tradition, not from the epic.","Scholars such as Biardeau and Hiltebeitel stress that the yuga material is a theological and ethical frame, not a historical calendar.","Reading the 360-to-1 divine-year conversion as stated in the text is itself an interpretive step; the epic does not always spell out the conversion in the same place."],"plausibility":1,"plausibilityReason":"As a literal timeline, the figures have no support: there is no independent dating for a 4.32 million-year cycle, and the Mahabharata itself offers no datable anchor for Kali's start. The Mahabharata's own internal structure is coherent, and the proportions match Manusmriti, so the text is internally consistent as a theological scheme. Its value for a real-world timeline is minimal.","references":["Ganguli, K.M. (trans.) The Mahabharata, Vana Parva and Shanti Parva, 1883-1896 (sacred-texts.com: https://www.sacred-texts.com/hin/m03/index.htm and https://www.sacred-texts.com/hin/m12/index.htm)","van Buitenen, J.A.B. (trans.) The Mahabharata, University of Chicago Press, 1973-1978 (Books 2-5 cover much of the Vana material)","Sukthankar, V.S. et al. (eds.) The Critical Edition of the Mahabharata, Bhandarkar Oriental Research Institute, 1933-1966","Biardeau, Madeleine. Le Mahabharata: un recit fondateur du bresil ... (Biardeau's work on the Mahabharata's ethical and cosmological frame; cite as Biardeau 1976-1978 and later works)","Hiltebeitel, Alf. The Ritual of Battle: Krishna in the Mahabharata, Cornell University Press, 1976","Manusmriti 1.67-71 (Olivelle, Patrick, trans. Manu's Code of Law, Oxford University Press, 2005)"]},{"id":"bhagavata","title":"Bhagavata Purana: Yuga durations and the Kali Yuga decline (Skandha 12)","category":"Traditional scriptural","proponents":["Traditional Vaishnava (Gaudiya and other Bhagavata-tradition) commentators","ISKCON (A.C. Bhaktivedanta Swami Prabhupada, Srimad-Bhagavatam translation and purport, 1972-1977)","Ganesh Vasudeva Tagare (English translation of the Bhagavata Purana, 1976-1978)"],"primarySources":["Bhagavata Purana (Srimad-Bhagavatam), usually dated by scholars to roughly the 6th-10th century CE, with a South Indian origin commonly proposed; the traditional attribution to Vyasa is not accepted by most historians","Bhagavata 1.10-1.19 (Parikshit's reign, Kali's appearance, Krishna's departure as the onset of Kali)","Bhagavata 1.17.23-24 (Dharma as a bull standing on four, three, two, then one leg across the yugas)","Bhagavata 3.11 (Krita-yuga characteristics in the Uddhava-Vidura dialogue)","Bhagavata 11.5.19-21 (yuga colors and modes of worship: white, red, dark, black)","Bhagavata 12.2 (Kali-yuga characteristics, yuga durations via divine years, Kalki prophecy)","Bhagavata 12.3 (Kali-yuga decline and the saving merit of chanting the name of Krishna, 12.3.51-52)"],"period":"Composed roughly 6th-10th century CE (scholarly consensus range; far later than the ancient setting its narrative claims). The four-yuga scheme is older and is shared across Puranas and the Mahabharata (Vana Parva 149-150 is an early statement of the sequence).","totalCycleYears":"4,320,000 human years (a mahayuga of 12,000 divine years, each divine year equal to 360 human years, per Bhagavata 12.2.29-32 approx.)","durations":{"satya":"1,728,000 human years (4,800 divine years)","treta":"1,296,000 human years (3,600 divine years)","dvapara":"864,000 human years (2,400 divine years)","kali":"432,000 human years (1,200 divine years)"},"kaliStart":"Not stated as a calendar date in the Bhagavata itself. The narrative ties the onset of Kali to Krishna's departure from the earth (Bhagavata 1.15-1.16 and surrounding chapters). The 3102 BCE epoch comes from later Indian astronomical tradition (e.g., Aryabhata, 499 CE, and the Surya Siddhanta tradition), not from the Bhagavata.","kaliEnd":"If the traditional epoch of 3102 BCE is combined with the 432,000-year duration, Kali ends around 428,900 CE. The Bhagavata gives no separate end date; its Kalki prophecy is not dated.","currentPosition":"In 2026 CE, roughly 5,127 years of Kali have elapsed on the traditional reckoning, about 1.2 percent of its 432,000-year span. Under this view we are deep in the decline phase, with Dharma reduced to one quarter (one leg) per Bhagavata 1.17.24 and 12.2.","summary":"The Bhagavata Purana gives the standard four-yuga scheme with durations in the ratio 4:3:2:1, expressed as divine years multiplied by 360, so Kali is 432,000 human years and a full mahayuga is 4.32 million. Skandha 12, chapters 2 and 3, describes Kali as an age of moral decline in which wealth replaces birth as the measure of status, rulers become exploitative, and dharma is weakened. It also prophesies that Kalki, son of the brahmin Vishnuyasha at Shambhala, will restore dharma at the end of the age. The text offers a devotional escape within the decline: chanting the name of Hari (12.3.51-52) is presented as the great merit of Kali. Within the Bhagavata this is a moral and theological frame, not a dated forecast.","keyClaims":["Four yugas with durations of 4800, 3600, 2400 and 1200 divine years (1,728,000 / 1,296,000 / 864,000 / 432,000 human years).","Dharma diminishes by one leg per yuga, from four legs in Krita to one leg in Kali (1.17.24).","Kali is characterized by greed, falsehood, weakened brahmins, wealth as the basis of status, and declining bodily and moral life-spans (12.2-12.3).","Kalki, born to Vishnuyasha in Shambhala, will restore dharma at the end of Kali (12.2).","Kali has one great merit: liberation through the chanting of Krishna's name (12.3.51-52).","Kali began with Krishna's departure from the earth (1.15-1.16 narrative)."],"evidence":["Internal consistency of the yuga arithmetic (4:3:2:1 ratio and the 360-fold divine-year multiplier), which matches the Vishnu Purana and Manu-smriti framework.","Many shared Puranic statements about the 432,000-year Kali, suggesting a common tradition rather than a single empirical observation.","The Bhagavata's own link of Kali's onset to Krishna's departure, which ties chronology to narrative rather than astronomy.","Devotional commentaries within the Bhagavata tradition (ISKCON, Gaudiya Vaishnava) that treat the figures as literal and often cite the 3102 BCE epoch as settled."],"criticisms":["The Bhagavata is a late text (roughly 6th-10th century CE by most scholarly estimates). Its Kali-decline passages reflect contemporary moral commentary and possibly a retrospective (ex eventu) framing, not genuine foreknowledge.","The 432,000-year figure is a mythic number. It is not derived from any observed astronomical or historical cycle, and the divine-year multiplier is a construction, not an empirical method.","The 3102 BCE epoch, often treated as the Kali start, is not in the Bhagavata. It comes from later astronomical reckoning and is projected back onto the narrative by commentators.","Kalki's prophecy is future-tense and undated, so it cannot be tested or falsified. It has also served various sectarian and political agendas over the centuries.","Scholars such as Wendy Doniger and Ludo Rocher note that Puranic chronology is not a systematic history; yuga figures functioned more as cosmological and ritual frameworks than as predictions.","Other Puranas and the Mahabharata give different details about yuga characteristics and transitions, so the Bhagavata is not the sole or definitive account."],"plausibility":1,"plausibilityReason":"As a literal chronological claim, the Bhagavata's yuga timeline has no empirical or historical support. The 432,000-year Kali is a mythic cosmological figure, and the 3102 BCE start is an astronomical convention added later. The moral diagnosis of Kali decline is cultural commentary whose accuracy is a value judgment, not a testable forecast. Plausibility is rated 1 for the timeline; the text's social observations can be read as historically interesting without being predictive.","references":["Bhagavata Purana, Skandhas 1, 3, 11, 12 (verse numbers cited from memory; check against a critical edition or the Tagare/Prabhupada translations before quoting; not web-verified in this run)","Ganesh Vasudeva Tagare, The Bhagavata Purana, Ancient Indian Tradition and Mythology series (1976-1978)","A.C. Bhaktivedanta Swami Prabhupada, Srimad-Bhagavatam (ISKCON, 1972-1977)","Edwin F. Bryant, Krishna: A Sourcebook (Oxford University Press, 2007)","Wendy Doniger (ed.), Purana Perennis: Reciprocity and Transformation in Hindu and Jaina Texts (SUNY Press, 1993)","Ludo Rocher, The Puranas, in Jan Gonda (ed.), A History of Indian Literature, vol. 2, fasc. 3 (Harrassowitz, 1986)","Vana Parva of the Mahabharata (early statement of the four-age sequence)"]},{"id":"vayu-brahmanda-linga","title":"Vayu, Brahmanda and Linga Purana yuga accounts and their textual variants","category":"Traditional scriptural","proponents":["Traditional Puranic commentators","P. V. Kane (History of Dharmasastra, discusses yuga reckoning)","F. E. Pargiter (Ancient Indian Historical Tradition, 1922)","Ludo Rocher (Puranic textual studies)","R. C. Hazra (Studies in the Upapuranas)"],"primarySources":["Vayu Purana, yuga-description chapters (around ch. 57-58 in common numbering; numbering varies by edition), c. 4th-6th century CE core, later additions possible","Brahmanda Purana, Purva-bhaga yuga passage (closely parallel to Vayu; the two share a large common block), c. 5th-7th century CE","Linga Purana, Purva-bhaga yuga passages, c. 7th-10th century CE","Manusmriti 1.69-71 (4800/3600/2400/1200 divine years), c. 2nd century BCE to 2nd century CE","Mahabharata Vana Parva 3.149 and 3.188 (yuga durations in divine years), c. 4th century BCE to 4th century CE"],"period":"Composed roughly 4th-10th century CE (Puranic redaction); the 12,000 divine-year scheme itself is older, appearing in Manu and the Mahabharata","totalCycleYears":"4,320,000 human years (Mahayuga = 12,000 divine years x 360 human years per divine year)","durations":{"satya":"4,800 divine years = 1,728,000 human years (4,000 + 800 sandhya/sandhyamsa)","treta":"3,600 divine years = 1,296,000 human years (3,000 + 600)","dvapara":"2,400 divine years = 864,000 human years (2,000 + 400)","kali":"1,200 divine years = 432,000 human years (1,000 + 200)"},"kaliStart":"3102 BCE (traditional astronomical epoch, later associated with Aryabhata and Indian astronomy; the Vayu/Brahmanda/Linga passages themselves give no calendar date and do not tie Kali to any historical event)","kaliEnd":"About 428,898 CE (3102 BCE plus 432,000 years)","currentPosition":"In 2026 CE about 5,128 years have elapsed in Kali Yuga, roughly 1.2% of its 432,000-year span. Under this view we are early in Kali, and the text describes the decline of dharma as progressive across the whole yuga.","summary":"The Vayu, Brahmanda and Linga Puranas describe the four yugas (Krita, Treta, Dvapara, Kali) with the same core scheme found in Manusmriti and the Mahabharata: 12,000 divine years total, each divine year equal to 360 human years, giving a 4.32 million-year mahayuga. Each yuga has a leading and trailing sandhya period, and dharma declines by one quarter per yuga. The Vayu and Brahmanda passages are nearly parallel, which suggests a shared recension, while the Linga version differs in wording and some details. These texts give the numbers but do not anchor them to any historical date, so the 3102 BCE start of Kali comes from later astronomical tradition rather than from the Puranas themselves.","keyClaims":["The yuga durations are 4800, 3600, 2400 and 1200 divine years including sandhyas, totaling 12,000 divine years.","One divine year is 360 human years, so a mahayuga is 4,320,000 human years.","Vayu and Brahmanda share a closely parallel yuga passage, indicating a common textual source.","The Linga Purana repeats the scheme with variant wording and some manuscript differences.","Kali Yuga is currently ongoing and began in 3102 BCE by traditional reckoning."],"evidence":["Internal consistency: the four figures and the 360 multiplier reproduce the 4.32 million total exactly, which Kane and others treat as a textual convention rather than observation.","Parallel passages in Vayu and Brahmanda, and their overlap with Manusmriti 1.69-71 and Mahabharata 3.188, indicate a shared tradition that predates the Puranic redaction.","The 3102 BCE epoch is widely attested in Indian astronomical texts (for example Aryabhatiya-based computation), not in these Puranic chapters.","Pargiter and Hazra discuss the Puranic king-lists and yuga material as a mix of genealogical memory and schematic chronology."],"criticisms":["The numbers are schematic: a 360-to-1 divine-year ratio and 12,000-year cycles are arithmetic conventions, not an observed or measured chronology.","No Puranic passage ties the Kali start to a dated event, so 3102 BCE is a later astronomical construct, not a historical record.","Textual variants (differences in sandhya figures and wording across Vayu, Brahmanda and Linga manuscripts) show the numbers were not fixed in the Puranic tradition, which weakens any claim to exact precision.","Dating the Puranic redactions to c. 4th-10th century CE means the yuga account reflects a much later period than the Vedic texts it claims to describe.","Some scholars argue that the 12,000-year structure was shaped by Mesopotamian or Greek astronomical cycles, though this remains contested and the evidence is indirect.","The text is prescriptive and cosmological, so treating it as a historical timeline misreads its genre."],"plausibility":2,"plausibilityReason":"As a literal chronology of the cosmos, the scheme has no support: the figures are cosmological conventions, the Kali start is a later astronomical epoch, and no archaeological or astronomical evidence corresponds to a 4.32 million-year cycle. The texts are a reliable witness to what Puranic authors believed about time and to their textual history, but not to real elapsed time. Note: this entry was written from prior knowledge without live web verification, so specific chapter numbers and dates should be checked against a critical edition before publication.","references":["Pargiter, F. E. (1922). Ancient Indian Historical Tradition. London: Oxford University Press.","Kane, P. V. (1930-1962). History of Dharmasastra, vol. II and IV (yuga and chronology discussions).","Hazra, R. C. (1958). Studies in the Upapuranas, vol. 1. Calcutta.","Bentley, J. (1825). A Historical View of the Hindu Astronomy. London.","Pingree, D. (1981). Jyotihsastra: Astral and Mathematical Literature. Wiesbaden: Harrassowitz.","Manusmriti 1.69-71 and Mahabharata 3.149, 3.188 (yuga durations in divine years)."]},{"id":"jain","title":"Jain time cycle: Utsarpiṇī/Avasarpiṇī and the six aras","category":"Traditional scriptural","proponents":["Digambar Jain tradition (e.g., Yati Vṛṣabha's Tiloyapaṇṇatti)","Śvetāmbara Jain tradition (canonical and commentarial texts)","Modern expositors such as Padmanabh S. Jaini and Paul Dundas"],"primarySources":["Sthānāṅga Sūtra (Śvetāmbara canon; core material considered early, roughly c. 3rd century BCE to early CE, with later redaction)","Jambudvīpaprajñapti (Śvetāmbara upāṅga; detailed ara tables; date debated)","Tattvārthasūtra of Umāsvāti (c. 2nd–5th century CE; short statement of the cycle)","Tiloyapaṇṇatti of Yati Vṛṣabha (Digambar cosmography; c. 6th century CE)","Ādipurāṇa of Jinasena (Digambar; c. 9th century CE)","Triṣaṣṭiśalākāpuruṣacarita of Hemacandra (Śvetāmbara; c. 12th century CE)"],"period":"Core doctrine appears in canonical and early commentarial Jain texts (roughly the early centuries CE, with earlier oral roots); fully elaborated numerical tables in medieval Digambar and Śvetāmbara cosmographies (6th–12th centuries CE).","totalCycleYears":"Not fixed in years. One full cycle (one Utsarpiṇī plus one Avasarpiṇī) is 20 koṭākoṭi sāgaropama, each half-cycle being 10 koṭākoṭi sāgaropama. The sāgaropama is a palya-based unit so large that it is not meaningfully expressed as a year count.","durations":{"satya":"Analogue: the first two aras (Suṣama-suṣamā, 4 koṭākoṭi sāgara; Suṣamā, 3 koṭākoṭi sāgara) in the descending half. These are not equal to Satya in the Hindu sense, which is a fixed 4,800 divine years (1,728,000 human years). Mapping is approximate.","treta":"Analogue: ara 3 (Suṣama-duṣamā), about 2 koṭākoṭi sāgara less 42,000 years. Again only a rough structural correspondence.","dvapara":"Analogue: ara 4 (Duṣama-suṣamā), about 1 koṭākoṭi sāgara less 42,000 years. This era contains the 24 Tīrthaṅkaras, including Ṛṣabha and Mahāvīra.","kali":"Analogue: aras 5 (Duṣamā, 21,000 years) and 6 (Duṣama-duṣamā, 21,000 years), so 42,000 years in total per descending half-cycle. Hindu Kali is 432,000 human years, so the Jain Duṣamā period is one-tenth that length."},"kaliStart":"Traditionally about 523 BCE, about 3 years and 8.5 months after Mahāvīra's nirvāṇa (Śvetāmbara dating of nirvāṇa to 527 BCE). Digambar sources date nirvāṇa earlier (about 605 BCE), which shifts the anchor. Some Western scholars (e.g., Hermann Jacobi) placed Mahāvīra's death near 468 BCE, which would shift this anchor later as well.","kaliEnd":"Under the traditional anchor, the fifth ara (21,000 years) ends around 20,478 CE. The sixth ara (another 21,000 years) would end around 41,478 CE, after which the Utsarpiṇī ascent begins.","currentPosition":"In 2026 CE we are in the fifth ara (Duṣamā) of the current descending Avasarpiṇī, roughly 2,549 years into its 21,000-year span, with about 18,450 years remaining under the traditional anchor. Jain texts describe this era as one of moral and spiritual decline with no Tīrthaṅkaras and no liberation possible for those born in Bharata now; the intervening Jain reading is that the descent is gradual rather than catastrophic.","summary":"Jain cosmology has no creation or dissolution. Time is a beginningless and endless wheel divided into two half-cycles: Avasarpiṇī (descending, decline of lifespan, body size, and virtue) and Utsarpiṇī (ascending). Each half-cycle has six aras (spokes or periods) that run in mirror order. Within the descending half, the first three aras are progressively less blissful and the fourth contains the Tīrthaṅkaras. The fifth and sixth are the Duṣamā (dark) periods of 21,000 years each. The cycle is a moral and spiritual frame, not a predictive or astronomical one. It is structurally similar to the Hindu four-yuga decline (4:3:2:1 ratios), but its numbers, durations, and purpose differ substantially.","keyClaims":["Time consists of Utsarpiṇī and Avasarpiṇī, each lasting 10 koṭākoṭi sāgaropama, with six aras each.","Descending aras have durations 4, 3, 2, 1 (koṭākoṭi sāgara), then 21,000 and 21,000 years, with a 42,000-year adjustment making the total exact.","We are currently in the fifth ara (Duṣamā), which started about 2,500 years ago after Mahāvīra's nirvāṇa.","Each ara has characteristic lifespans, body sizes, and human conditions; the fourth ara includes the 24 Tīrthaṅkaras and the 63 śalākāpuruṣas.","Videha, a region of Jambudvīpa, is outside the ara cycle and is permanently in a state comparable to the third or fourth ara.","Kāla is beginningless and eternal, with no creator God."],"evidence":["Internal consistency: the 4:3:2:1 scheme and the 42,000-year correction are given explicitly in the canon and the medieval cosmographies.","Textual continuity across Śvetāmbara and Digambar traditions on the basic structure, though numbers of Tīrthaṅkaras and certain details differ.","Historical kernel: the Buddhist Pāli Nikāyas mention Nigaṇṭha Nātaputta (Mahāvīra) as a contemporary teacher, supporting a real historical figure of roughly the 6th–5th century BCE.","Mahāvīra's nirvāṇa is the anchor date for the cycle in the Jain tradition, and scholars have argued over whether its traditional dating is accurate.","The ratio parallel with Hindu yugas is noted by scholars such as Paul Dundas, suggesting shared Indic conceptual ground."],"criticisms":["There is no physical, astronomical, or archaeological evidence for the aras or the koṭākoṭi sāgaropama durations. The claims are doctrinal, not testable.","The 42,000-year adjustment in the third and fourth aras is ad hoc, which suggests the numbers were built to make a neat total rather than derived from observation.","The 523 BCE anchor depends on the Mahāvīra dating dispute. Digambar (about 605 BCE) and Western scholarly proposals (about 468 BCE) move the Kali-analogue endpoints by roughly 80 to 140 years, so the traditional date is not secure.","The full cycle was systematized in texts composed many centuries after Mahāvīra, so the details are later elaboration rather than early doctrine.","Ideas of cosmic decline and cycles are widespread across ancient Indian and other traditions; direction of borrowing between Jain and Hindu cosmology is debated and not settled.","The mapping to Hindu yugas is only structural. Hindu Kali is 432,000 years and Jain Duṣamā is 21,000 years, so a one-to-one equivalence is misleading."],"plausibility":2,"plausibilityReason":"As cosmology, the cycle has no empirical support, and its specific year-count predictions (e.g., the Duṣamā ending around 20,478 CE) cannot be checked. The historical kernel is more credible: Mahāvīra was likely a real 6th–5th century BCE teacher, and the Jain tradition's core ethics and cosmic-time vocabulary are real historical facts. Scored low because the timeline claims are doctrinal and not scientific. Not verified with live web sources in this run; based on established scholarship (Dundas 1992/2002, Jaini 1979) and knowledge of the primary texts.","references":["Paul Dundas, The Jains (Routledge, 1992; 2nd ed. 2002), chapter on cosmology and time.","Padmanabh S. Jaini, The Jaina Path of Purification (University of California Press, 1979).","Umāsvāti, Tattvārthasūtra, chapter 3 (cosmography and the cycle of time).","Yati Vṛṣabha, Tiloyapaṇṇatti (Digambar cosmography; critical editions in Prakrit with Hindi/English translations).","Jinasena, Ādipurāṇa (Digambar; 9th century CE).","Hemacandra, Triṣaṣṭiśalākāpuruṣacarita (Śvetāmbara; 12th century CE).","Sthānāṅga Sūtra and Jambudvīpaprajñapti (Śvetāmbara canonical texts; English translations available through the Sacred Books of the Jains series)."]},{"id":"buddhist","title":"Buddhist kalpa and world-age cosmology (Abhidharma, Dharma decline, Mappō) compared with yugas","category":"Traditional scriptural","proponents":["Vasubandhu (Abhidharmakośa, c. 4th-5th c. CE, Ch. III on the world)","Yaśomitra (Sphuṭārthā commentary on the Abhidharmakośa)","Pali Canon compilers (Dīgha Nikāya 26 and 27, Saṃyutta Nikāya kappa passage)","Mahāsāṃghika-era and Chinese Buddhist Dharma-decline prophecy authors","Japanese Pure Land and Nichiren teachers who popularized Mappō (e.g. Shinran, 13th c.)","Kālacakra tradition (Indian/Tibetan, c. 11th c. onward) for the Shambhala-Kalki prophecy"],"primarySources":["Dīgha Nikāya 26 (Cakkavatti-Sīhanāda Sutta): human lifespan falls from 80,000 years to 10, then the Buddha Metteyya appears (Pali, c. 1st c. BCE to 1st c. CE written form)","Dīgha Nikāya 27 (Aggañña Sutta): world contraction and re-formation over long cycles (Pali)","Saṃyutta Nikāya 15.5 (kappa passage, mustard-seed and granite-mountain similes for the length of a kalpa) (Pali)","Abhidharmakośabhāṣya, Ch. III (Vasubandhu, c. 4th-5th c. CE): four phases of a world-age, antarakalpas, destruction by fire, water and wind","Mahāsaṃnipāta-sūtra and related Chinese Buddhist texts: five 500-year periods of declining Dharma (dating uncertain, several centuries CE)","Kālacakra Tantra and Vimalaprabhā commentary (c. 11th c. CE): Kali-yuga reckoning and Shambhala-Kalki war prophecy","Shinran, Kyōgyōshinshō (13th c. CE): Mappō as the present degenerate age in Japanese Pure Land thought"],"period":"Pali canonical core c. 1st c. BCE to 1st c. CE; Abhidharma systematization c. 4th-5th c. CE; Dharma-decline prophecies from c. 5th c. CE; Japanese Mappō from 1052 CE; Kālacakra c. 11th c. CE","totalCycleYears":"One mahākalpa (world-age) is about 1,344,000,000 years per Abhidharmakośa (80 antarakalpas of 16,800,000 years each). The Dharma-decline scheme is not a cycle; it is a single declining sequence of 500 or 1,000 year phases (Saddharma then Saddharma-pratirūpaka), or 10,000 years in the Japanese Mappō reckoning.","durations":{"satya":"N/A. Buddhism has no four-yuga scheme. Closest analog is the Saddharma (true Dharma) age of roughly 500 or 1,000 years after the Buddha's death, depending on the text.","treta":"N/A. No direct equivalent. The Saddharma-pratirūpaka (counterfeit or semblance Dharma) phase is commonly given as 500 or 1,000 years, and is sometimes loosely compared to a second age.","dvapara":"N/A. No direct equivalent. Within the Abhidharma world-age, antarakalpas of human lifespan rising and falling (10 to 84,000 years) are the nearest structural analog to yuga-style decline.","kali":"Mappō (Japanese: degenerate Dharma age). Commonly 10,000 years of decline after Dharma-ending, reckoned from 1052 CE. Under the Kālacakra system the Kali-yuga reckoning is separate and different. Both are analogous to Kali but not identical."},"kaliStart":"No Buddhist equivalent to the Hindu Kali-yuga start (3102 BCE). Closest analogs: Mappō begins 1052 CE (Japanese reckoning, using a Buddha death date of 949 BCE from Chinese chronology plus 2,000 years). Kālacakra uses its own Kali reckoning.","kaliEnd":"Mappō is usually said to end after 10,000 years, around 11052 CE in the Japanese reckoning, but there is no Buddhist event that formally closes it. Kālacakra's Shambhala-Kalki war prophecy is commonly cited as around 2424 CE (some sources give 2327 CE).","currentPosition":"In 2026 CE, Buddhist sources place us within the Mappō age under the Japanese reckoning (about 974 years since 1052). The current Buddhist world-age is the Bhadrakalpa (fortunate kalpa), with Śākyamuni as the fourth of 1,000 Buddhas and Maitreya as the fifth. Human lifespan in the canonical scheme is currently in a phase of decline. Under the Saddharma-period scheme of 500 or 1,000 years, the Dharma-decline period has long passed.","summary":"Buddhist cosmology does not use yugas. It uses kalpas (world-ages). A mahākalpa has four phases of 20 antarakalpas each (formation, abiding, destruction, empty abiding), totaling about 1.344 billion years in the Abhidharmakośa. Separately, a second body of texts describes the decline of the Dharma (the Buddha's teaching) over successive periods of roughly 500 or 1,000 years. Mappō, the Japanese degenerate age, gave this decline a 10,000-year span from 1052 CE and became hugely influential in Pure Land and Nichiren thought. Buddhist cycles are moral and soteriological rather than chronological: their purpose is to motivate practice, not to date events. Their numbers are conventional and do not map onto any measured history.","keyClaims":["A mahākalpa (world-age) has four phases, each of 20 antarakalpas, for a total of about 1.344 billion years (Abhidharmakośa, Ch. III).","Human lifespan in antarakalpas rises and falls, from 10 years up to 84,000 years and back (Abhidharmakośa; DN 26 gives 80,000 years descending to 10).","The world is destroyed by fire, water, and wind at the end of phases (Abhidharmakośa; DN 27).","The Buddha Metteyya (Maitreya) will appear when human lifespan returns to around 80,000 to 84,000 years (DN 26).","The Dharma declines in stages: true Dharma, counterfeit Dharma, then degenerate Dharma. Mappō is the last stage, with a 10,000-year span from 1052 CE in the Japanese reckoning.","Kālacakra (c. 11th c.) uses a yuga-like Kali age and predicts a Shambhala-Kalki war with Mleccha invaders in the 24th century CE (commonly cited 2424).","Buddhism is currently in a late, degenerate phase (Mappō) and the Pure Land path is the appropriate response."],"evidence":["Canonical texts (DN 26, DN 27, SN 15.5) are primary sources and are attested in Pali manuscripts and Sanskrit fragments.","Vasubandhu's Abhidharmakośa and Yaśomitra's commentary give specific numbers (16,800,000-year antarakalpa; 1,344,000,000-year mahākalpa).","Chinese and Japanese Buddhist texts consistently use the 1052 CE Mappō date, and Shinran's Kyōgyōshinshō (13th c.) cites it.","Jan Nattier's study Once upon a Future Time (1991) traces the Dharma-decline prophecies to Indian and Central Asian sources and finds they are later textual developments."],"criticisms":["The kalpa numbers are doctrinal and symbolic. They are not measurements, and no empirical test can confirm or refute them.","The Mappō start date depends on a Buddha death date of 949 BCE from the Chinese chronological tradition. Modern scholarship generally places the Buddha's death around 480 BCE (with an uncertainty range of roughly 400 to 480 BCE), so the 1052 start is not a historical anchor.","Dharma-decline prophecies were written after the events they claim to predict (vaticinia ex eventu). Their decline narrative was often used to justify new practices or new sects.","Dharma decline has been predicted repeatedly, and Buddhism continued to flourish and spread after each predicted endpoint. The predictions are unfalsifiable in practice.","The Abhidharma world-age is a cosmological model, not a historical chronology, and does not map to Hindu yugas one-to-one. Forced comparisons overstate similarity.","Kālacakra's war prophecy has been read politically and has been used in modern contexts, which is outside the textual scholarship."],"plausibility":2,"plausibilityReason":"As a literal chronology of the world or of Dharma history, the Buddhist scheme has no empirical support. Its specific numbers are conventional. The Mappō start date rests on a Buddha death date that modern scholarship rejects. Dharma-decline predictions have repeatedly failed to produce the claimed endpoints. The scheme is useful as a historical document of how Buddhist communities understood their own decline, but it is not a reliable guide to real time.","references":["Vasubandhu, Abhidharmakośa Ch. III (World), translated by Louis de La Vallée Poussin, L'Abhidharmakośa de Vasubandhu (1923-31)","Dīgha Nikāya 26 and 27, Pali canon; see https://suttacentral.net/dn26 and https://suttacentral.net/dn27 (not fetched in this run)","Saṃyutta Nikāya 15.5 (kappa passage), Pali canon","Jan Nattier, Once upon a Future Time: Studies in a Buddhist Prophecy of Decline (Asian Humanities Press, 1991)","Shinran, Kyōgyōshinshō (13th c.), on Mappō","Kālacakra Tantra and Vimalaprabhā commentary (c. 11th c.), Shambhala prophecy","Note: web tools were not used in this run. Citations are from prior knowledge and the specific dates and titles should be verified before publication."]},{"id":"hesiod","title":"Hesiod's Ages of Man and a proposed Indo-European origin of the yugas","category":"Comparative mythology","proponents":["Hesiod (the original formulation, not a yuga claim)","Ovid (Metamorphoses 1, a four-age variant)","M. L. West (Indo-European Poetry and Myth, 2007; Hesiod commentary, 1978)","Mallory and Adams (Oxford Introduction to Proto-Indo-European and the Proto-Indo-European World, 2006)","Popular comparative and esoteric writers who equate Hesiod's ages with the yugas (attribution diffuse; no single canonical source)"],"primarySources":["Hesiod, Works and Days 106-201 (c. 700 BCE, roughly late 8th to early 7th century)","Ovid, Metamorphoses 1.89-150 (c. 8 CE; four ages: gold, silver, bronze, iron)","Manusmriti 1.64-86 (yuga durations in divine years; compiled c. 2nd century BCE to 2nd century CE, the date of the full yuga scheme is debated)","Mahabharata, Vana Parva (Markandeya's account of the yugas; the compilation spans roughly the 4th century BCE to 4th century CE)","Aitareya Brahmana 7.15 and Chandogya Upanishad 4.1.4-5 (kṛta, tretā, dvāpara, kali as dice-throw names, not as ages)"],"period":"Hesiod's scheme c. 700 BCE; Ovid's variant 8 CE; the Indic yuga scheme with its full durations is attested in texts from roughly the 2nd century BCE onward; the proposed common Indo-European origin is a hypothesis about a prehistoric source (c. 4000-2500 BCE for Proto-Indo-European speakers), with no direct evidence.","totalCycleYears":"N/A. Hesiod gives no durations. In the Indic system the cycle is 4,320,000 years (12,000 divine years of 360 years each), but that number belongs to the Indic tradition and is not part of Hesiod's scheme.","durations":{"satya":"N/A. Hesiod's Golden Race has no stated length; it lived under Cronus and then became spirits (daimones).","treta":"N/A. Hesiod's Silver Race has no stated length; it was worse than gold and was hidden by Zeus after it refused to honour the gods.","dvapara":"N/A. Hesiod's Bronze Race (from ash trees, warlike) has no stated length and ended in self-destruction. The Heroic Race that follows is an extra, fifth age that has no yuga counterpart.","kali":"N/A. Hesiod's Iron Age is his own time, full of toil and woe. Zeus is said to destroy it when children are born with grey temples, but no date is given."},"kaliStart":"N/A for Hesiod. For comparison only, the traditional Kali Yuga epoch is 3102 BCE, which is an Indian tradition and has no counterpart in Greek sources.","kaliEnd":"N/A for Hesiod. Kali Yuga ends after 432,000 years in the Indic scheme. Hesiod has no end date; his Iron Age ends with Zeus's destruction in an unspecified future.","currentPosition":"Hesiod places his own time in the Iron Age. Mapped onto the Indic scheme, the 2026 CE position would be about 5,127 years into Kali Yuga (counting from 3102 BCE). Neither source gives a date for the end of the age, so no current position can be read from Hesiod.","summary":"Hesiod's Works and Days describes five races of humans in decline: Golden, Silver, Bronze, a Heroic race, and Iron, which is the writer's own age. The sequence moves downward in worth but gives no durations or numbers. Ovid's Metamorphoses drops the heroic race and produces the four-age gold-silver-bronze-iron form that is more often compared with the yugas. Some modern writers propose that Hesiod's ages and the Indic yugas go back to a shared Indo-European source, and West (2007) treats the decline-of-ages motif as a serious candidate for inherited IE material. The direct evidence is thin, however, and the comparison is largely structural.","keyClaims":["Hesiod's four-metal (plus heroic) scheme and the Indic four-yuga scheme both describe a decline from a golden or perfect age to a degraded present, so they share a common deep structure.","The decline motif is inherited from Proto-Indo-European, and the Greek and Indic forms are both descendants of it.","The Golden/Kṛta and Iron/Kali pairings are the core correspondence, with Silver/Tretā and Bronze/Dvāpara as the intermediate steps.","Because the Iron Age is the present in both systems, the Kali Yuga's current position can be read against the Hesiodic picture of a degraded age."],"evidence":["Hesiod's and Ovid's four-metal schemes are structurally similar to the four-yuga scheme: descending order, a perfect first age, and a degraded last age.","The yuga names and the ages do not match well lexically. Kṛta means 'done' or 'completed' and is not the word for gold; the metal symbolism is not present in the Indic material. Hesiod's Golden Race does not have a Sanskrit cognate for its name.","The Indic yuga concept is attested in the Mahabharata and Manusmriti, both later than Hesiod. The dice-game terms kṛta, tretā, dvāpara, kali appear earlier (Aitareya Brahmana, Chandogya Upanishad) but are not described as ages.","West (1978, 2007) discusses Indo-European material for decline and ages. Ages-of-decline motifs appear in several traditions (Persian, Hebrew via Daniel 2's four metals, Mesopotamian, Greek, Indic), which suggests either shared inheritance or widespread independent development."],"criticisms":["The shared-origin claim depends on a structural parallel. Decline myths with four stages are widespread and can arise independently, so similarity does not show inheritance.","The metal scheme and the yuga scheme differ. Hesiod's races are metals and moral types, while yugas are time-lengths tied to dharma (fraction of virtue lost). Equating them requires assuming the same concept with different surface forms.","The yuga durations (4:3:2:1 ratio, 4.32 million years) appear in late texts and have no counterpart in Hesiod, so the most distinctive feature of the Indic system has no Greek parallel.","Hesiod's heroic race is an extra stage that breaks the four-to-four mapping. Mapping it to a yuga requires dropping one of Hesiod's stages.","Near Eastern influence on Hesiod is widely argued (West 1997, The East Face of Helicon), which points to borrowing from Mesopotamian or other sources as an alternative to a prehistoric IE origin.","No Proto-Indo-European lexeme for a golden age or for the yuga concept has been reconstructed with confidence. Without that, the IE-origin claim is unsupported by the comparative method.","Popular comparisons of Hesiod's ages with the yugas often treat them as the same system and omit the differences above. Such comparisons are not peer-reviewed scholarship."],"plausibility":2,"plausibilityReason":"As a comparative observation that both traditions have a decline-through-ages structure, the claim is reasonable. As a claim that Hesiod's ages and the yugas share an Indo-European origin, or that Hesiod's ages reveal real historical timelines, it is weakly supported. The structures are similar, but the lexical, numerical, and conceptual correspondences are poor, and Near Eastern borrowing is a competing explanation.","references":["Hesiod, Works and Days 106-201 (Evelyn-White translation, Loeb, 1914); West, M. L., Hesiod: Works and Days (Oxford, 1978)","West, M. L., Indo-European Poetry and Myth (Oxford University Press, 2007)","West, M. L., The East Face of Helicon (Oxford, 1997)","Mallory, J. P. and Adams, D. Q., The Oxford Introduction to Proto-Indo-European and the Proto-Indo-European World (Oxford, 2006)","Ovid, Metamorphoses 1.89-150","Manusmriti 1.64-86 (Doniger and Smith translation, Penguin, 1991)","Chandogya Upanishad 4.1.4-5 and Aitareya Brahmana 7.15 (kṛta, tretā, dvāpara, kali as dice-throw names)"]},{"id":"zoroastrian","title":"Zoroastrian 12,000-year world cycle (Bundahishn) and its relation to the 12,000 divine years","category":"Traditional scriptural","proponents":["Zoroastrian priestly tradition (Pahlavi-era authors)","Mary Boyce (scholarly reconstruction of Zoroastrian chronology)","R.C. Zaehner (Zurvanism and the Great Year hypothesis)"],"primarySources":["Zand-i Vohuman Yasn and Bundahishn (Pahlavi, compiled roughly 9th to 12th centuries CE, drawing on older material)","Denkard (Pahlavi, 9th century CE)","Theopompus of Chios, reported by Plutarch, De Iside et Osiride 47 (4th century BCE)","Hermippus, reported by Pliny, Natural History 30.2 (Hellenistic period)","Avestan Gathas (Zoroaster's own hymns, dating disputed, roughly 1500 to 600 BCE)"],"period":"Pahlavi texts from the 9th to 12th centuries CE, with Greek external reports from the 4th century BCE onward. The 12,000-year frame itself is probably later than the Gathas.","totalCycleYears":"12,000 solar years (four 3,000-year periods). This is not a yuga-style cycle of decline and renewal.","durations":{"satya":"3,000 years, spiritual (menog) creation, when the world exists as pure spirit","treta":"3,000 years, material (getig) creation, when the world is physical and still pure","dvapara":"3,000 years, the period of mixture (gumezishn), when Ahriman attacks and Zoroaster appears near its end","kali":"3,000 years, the final period after Zoroaster, with Saoshyants at millennial intervals, ending in Frashokereti. Labels are analogy only; the Pahlavi texts do not use yuga names."},"kaliStart":"Not a yuga mapping. The closest analogue is the start of the post-Zoroaster period, which the texts place roughly near the 9,000-year mark. Sources differ on Zoroaster's exact position.","kaliEnd":"Frashokereti, the final renovation at the end of the 12,000 years. No calendar date is given in the texts.","currentPosition":"In the common reading, 2026 CE falls within the final 3,000-year post-Zoroaster period, awaiting the Saoshyants and eventual Frashokereti. The texts give no CE anchor, so no precise current position can be derived.","summary":"The Pahlavi Bundahishn and related texts describe history as 12,000 years in four 3,000-year periods: spiritual creation, material creation, a period of mixture in which Ahriman attacks and Zoroaster appears near its end, and a final period leading to renovation (Frashokereti). Saviors are expected at millennial intervals after Zoroaster. The 12,000 here counts ordinary solar years, not the Hindu divine years (4.32 million solar years), so any link is numerical unless transmission is shown. The cycle is theological with no external calendar anchor. Greek reports from Theopompus and Hermippus describe a different 6,000 plus 3,000 plus 3,000 year scheme, suggesting the frame was systematized over time.","keyClaims":["Zoroastrian cosmic history spans 12,000 years in four 3,000-year periods (Bundahishn, Denkard).","The period of mixture (gumezishn) is when Ahriman attacks creation and Zoroaster appears near its end.","Three saviors (Saoshyants) are expected at 1,000-year intervals after Zoroaster, ending in Frashokereti.","The 12,000 counts solar years, so it is a different unit from the Hindu 12,000 divine years.","Greek writers (Theopompus via Plutarch, Hermippus via Pliny) describe a 6,000 plus 3,000 plus 3,000 year scheme, suggesting the later 12,000-year frame was systematized over time.","Zurvanite theology (Zaehner) and Hellenistic Great Year ideas are proposed as influences on the late chronology."],"evidence":["Pahlavi texts (Bundahishn, Denkard, Zand-i Vohuman Yasn) state the 3,000-year period structure and the Saoshyant schedule.","Plutarch, De Iside 47, reports Theopompus's 6,000 plus 3,000 plus 3,000 year scheme attributed to the Magi.","Pliny, NH 30.2, reports Hermippus's material on Zoroaster, consistent with a Greek chronological scheme.","Boyce (1975-1991) and Zaehner (1955) discuss the chronology and its theological development. Their reconstructions, not the texts themselves, carry the scholarly dating arguments.","Pahlavi texts are many centuries later than the Gathas, and their dating of Zoroaster varies with each author's assumptions."],"criticisms":["The 12,000-year frame appears in texts compiled roughly 1,000 to 1,500 years after the Gathas, so it reflects later systematization, not Zoroaster's own teaching.","Greek reports give a different division (6,000 plus 3,000 plus 3,000), so the 12,000 scheme cannot be treated as one fixed ancient tradition.","Claimed links to the Hindu 12,000 divine years are numerical coincidence unless transmission is documented. The units differ: solar years versus divine years.","Hellenistic Great Year ideas may have shaped the frame, so reading it as independent ancient knowledge is unsafe.","Zoroaster's date is debated: traditional claims place him centuries before the Pahlavi authors, while linguistic and archaeological arguments suggest roughly 1500 to 600 BCE. This affects any mapping of the frame to real history.","The scheme gives no calendar anchor, so claims that 2026 falls at a specific point in it are interpretive and unfalsifiable."],"plausibility":1,"plausibilityReason":"The cycle is a theological cosmology with no external calendar anchor, and its textual sources are late. There is no physical or historical evidence for a 12,000-year world history. The only checkable element is Zoroaster's approximate date, which is itself uncertain.","references":["Bundahishn, Zand-i Vohuman Yasn, Denkard (Pahlavi texts; translations by West, Sanjana, Pakzad)","Plutarch, De Iside et Osiride 46-47 (Theopompus fragment)","Pliny the Elder, Natural History 30.2 (Hermippus)","Mary Boyce, A History of Zoroastrianism, vols. 1-3 (Brill, 1975-1991)","R.C. Zaehner, Zurvan: A Zoroastrian Dilemma (Oxford, 1955)"]},{"id":"norse-other","title":"Comparative cyclic cosmologies: Norse Ragnarok, Aztec Five Suns, Hopi worlds (as yuga analogues)","category":"Comparative mythology","proponents":["Mircea Eliade (comparative framing of cyclical time, 1950s-60s)","Miguel Leon-Portilla (Aztec Thought and Culture, 1963)","Ursula Dronke (Volospa commentary edition, 1997)","Harold Courlander (The Fourth World of the Hopi, 1971)","Frank Waters (Book of the Hopi, 1963), popularizer","Hopi tribal elders and spokespersons such as Thomas Banyacya (mid-20th century public statements)"],"primarySources":["Volospa, in the Poetic Edda (Codex Regius, manuscript c. 1270s; poem composition date debated, possibly 10th-13th century)","Snorri Sturluson, Prose Edda, Gylfaginning (c. 1220)","Leyenda de los Soles, Nahuatl manuscript (mid-16th century, Chimalpopoca collection)","Bernardino de Sahagun, Florentine Codex, Book 7 (compiled c. 1569-1577)","Hopi oral traditions as recorded in ethnographic and popular works from the 1900s to 1970s (Courlander 1971; Waters 1963)","Hesiod, Works and Days (c. 700 BCE), for the comparable Ages of Man"],"period":"Norse material: Viking Age belief, recorded 13th century. Aztec/Nahua: pre-Hispanic belief, recorded 16th century. Hopi: oral tradition, recorded and popularized 20th century.","totalCycleYears":"N/A. Norse has one terminal event (Ragnarok) followed by renewal, with no repeating numeric cycle. Aztec sun durations are reported inconsistently across sources, so no reliable total is given. Hopi tradition has no numeric cycle length in the standard sources.","durations":{"satya":"N/A. No direct analogue. Hesiod's Golden Age is the nearest structural parallel, with no numeric length.","treta":"N/A. No direct analogue. Hesiod's Silver Age is the nearest parallel.","dvapara":"N/A. No direct analogue. Hesiod's Bronze and Heroic Ages are the nearest parallels.","kali":"Aztec: the Leyenda de los Soles gives 676 years for the first sun, but the figures for the other suns differ between sources, so treat any sequence as uncertain. The current Fifth Sun (4 Movement) has no fixed length, since it is expected to end in earthquakes. Hopi: no numeric length given in standard sources. Norse: Ragnarok is an event, not a duration."},"kaliStart":"N/A. These traditions have no Kali Yuga analogue. The closest structural parallels are the start of the Fifth Sun in Aztec tradition and the emergence into the Fourth World in Hopi tradition, both mythic events with no reliable calendar date.","kaliEnd":"N/A. Aztec tradition predicts the end of the Fifth Sun by earthquakes and famine, with no calendar date that is consistent across sources. Hopi tradition predicts purification or transition for the Fourth World, with no date. Norse Ragnarok has no date.","currentPosition":"Aztec: within the Fifth Sun (4 Movement) according to the tradition, which is expected to end in earthquakes. Hopi: within the Fourth World, with a transition prophesied but undated. Norse: before Ragnarok, with no date. None of these traditions gives a date that can be checked against the year 2026.","summary":"These three traditions share a broad cosmological pattern with the yugas: the world passes through successive stages and is renewed or destroyed at the end of a stage. They differ sharply in structure. The Aztec Five Suns is the closest match, since it is a finite sequence of ages, each ended by a cosmic force, with the current one expected to end in earthquakes. The Hopi emergence narrative describes successive worlds ended by human moral failure, with a prophesied transition in the present world. Norse Ragnarok is a single terminal catastrophe followed by a new earth, which is linear with renewal rather than a repeating decline. The yugas, by contrast, decline in virtue across four unequal ages within a fixed numerical cycle.","keyClaims":["Aztec Five Suns: the Leyenda de los Soles describes four earlier suns (4 Jaguar, 4 Wind, 4 Rain, 4 Water), each ended by a cosmic catastrophe, and the current Fifth Sun (4 Movement), predicted to end in earthquakes and famine.","The Aztec 52-year Calendar Round and the New Fire Ceremony, in which the cycle of time was renewed, are a documented structural feature of the tradition, distinct from the sun sequence.","Hopi tradition describes the world as passing through earlier worlds (commonly described as three) that ended in destruction, with the people emerging into the Fourth World.","Hopi prophecy as popularized in the 20th century describes a coming period of purification or transition, often linked to a sign such as the Blue Star Kachina, and to the choice between paths of spiritual and material living.","Norse Ragnarok, as described in Volospa and Gylfaginning, is a battle-driven end of the cosmos followed by a renewed earth, with surviving gods and humans, and the return of Baldr.","All three traditions have a beginning, an end, and some form of renewal, but none uses fixed numerical durations that correspond to the yugas."],"evidence":["Leyenda de los Soles and Sahagun's Florentine Codex Book 7 are real, dated sources that describe the Aztec suns and the Calendar Round.","Volospa (Codex Regius) and Snorri's Prose Edda are real medieval texts that describe Ragnarok and renewal.","Courlander (1971) and Waters (1963) are real published compilations of Hopi material, with the Hopi emergence and prophecy themes.","Hesiod's Works and Days (c. 700 BCE) describes the Ages of Man (gold to iron), a real comparison point for the decline pattern of the yugas.","Parallels with the yugas are recognized in comparative scholarship, for example the decline-by-ages structure and the notion of world-ages ending in destruction."],"criticisms":["The Aztec sources were written in the 16th century, after conquest and conversion, and Sahagun's informants and the compilers may have shaped the material toward Christian or Spanish framings. The Five Suns sequence and its durations are reconstructed from several inconsistent texts.","Frank Waters' Book of the Hopi (1963) blends sources and was never a faithful transcription of ceremonial knowledge. Some Hopi people have objected to the public circulation of prophecy material, and the popular version of Hopi prophecy should be treated as a 20th-century composite, not a stable ancient text.","Norse eschatology is preserved mainly in Christian-era manuscripts, and scholars debate how far Ragnarok reflects pagan belief versus later literary construction. Volospa's date of composition is uncertain.","Comparing the yugas to these traditions risks overreach. Structural similarities, such as successive ages ending in catastrophe, appear in many cultures and do not show a common origin or a shared chronology.","The yugas themselves are late in their textual form (Puranic and Manusmriti accounts), so the comparison is between differently dated and differently transmitted systems.","None of these traditions makes a dated, testable prediction that can be checked against the physical record, so they cannot be ranked by accuracy against real-world history."],"plausibility":2,"plausibilityReason":"As literal chronologies, none of these traditions survives scrutiny. They have no datable, falsifiable predictions, and the Aztec and Hopi material is shaped by colonial-era recording or modern composition. The comparison is useful for understanding cyclic time as a human cultural pattern, but these sources do not support any claim that the world is at a particular point in a cosmic cycle. The rating is low because the question of real-world timeline does not apply to these myths in any strong sense.","references":["Volospa, Poetic Edda, Codex Regius (c. 1270s). Edition: Ursula Dronke, The Poetic Edda Vol. II: Mythological Poems (Oxford, 1997).","Snorri Sturluson, Prose Edda, Gylfaginning (c. 1220).","Leyenda de los Soles (16th-century Nahuatl manuscript, Chimalpopoca collection).","Bernardino de Sahagun, Florentine Codex, Book 7 (compiled c. 1569-1577).","Miguel Leon-Portilla, Aztec Thought and Culture (University of Oklahoma Press, 1963).","Frank Waters, Book of the Hopi (1963).","Harold Courlander, The Fourth World of the Hopi (1971).","Hesiod, Works and Days (c. 700 BCE).","Manusmriti 1.68-86 for the Hindu yuga durations used in comparison."]},{"id":"berossus","title":"Mesopotamian links: Berossus 432,000 years, Sumerian King List, and the number 432","category":"Comparative mythology","proponents":["David Pingree (argued Mesopotamian influence on early Indian mathematical astronomy, 1970s)","Otto Neugebauer (Babylonian mathematics and astronomy scholarship, supporting the diffusion framework)","Popular numerology writers who link 432,000 across Babylonian, Sumerian, and Indian sources (no peer-reviewed support for a direct yuga link)"],"primarySources":["Berossus, Babyloniaca (c. 280 BCE, Greek, Babylonian priest writing for Antiochus I). Survives only in excerpts by Alexander Polyhistor (c. 1st century BCE), Eusebius (Chronicle, c. 4th century CE, Armenian and Jerome Latin versions), and George Synkellos (Chronographia, c. 800 CE).","Sumerian King List (composed in Sumerian; oldest surviving copies from the Old Babylonian period, c. 1800 BCE, such as the Weld-Blundell prism; roots possibly in the Ur III period)","Aryabhatiya of Aryabhata (499 CE) and Surya Siddhanta (medieval), which fix the Mahayuga at 4,320,000 years and the Kali Yuga at 432,000 years"],"period":"Berossus c. 280 BCE. Sumerian King List c. 2100-1800 BCE in its earliest forms. The 432 number appears in Indian astronomy from the 5th century CE onward. No evidence shows that the Indian yuga system existed in Mesopotamia.","totalCycleYears":"N/A (Berossus and the Sumerian King List are not yuga cycles. For reference, the Indian Mahayuga is 4,320,000 years, so the 432,000 figure is one tenth of it.)","durations":{"satya":"N/A (no yuga ages in Berossus; the Indian Satya Yuga of 1,728,000 years is not in this source)","treta":"N/A (same as above; Indian Treta Yuga is 1,296,000 years)","dvapara":"N/A (same as above; Indian Dvapara Yuga is 864,000 years)","kali":"N/A as a Berossus figure. The numerical match is 432,000 years, the total of the 120 sars Berossus gives for ten antediluvian kings (as commonly cited). Note that the individual reign figures as transmitted by Syncellus sum to 396,000 under standard readings, so the stated total and the listed numbers do not agree."},"kaliStart":"N/A (traditional Kali epoch of 3102 BCE comes from Indian astronomical reckoning, not from Mesopotamian sources)","kaliEnd":"N/A (traditional Indian reckoning gives the end of the 432,000-year Kali Yuga around 428,899 CE)","currentPosition":"N/A (no Mesopotamian yuga-style age is current in 2026). Under the Indian reckoning, 2026 CE falls in Kali year roughly 5127.","summary":"Berossus, a Babylonian priest writing in Greek around 280 BCE, described ten kings before the Flood whose reigns totaled 120 sars, which is 432,000 years (1 sar = 3,600 years). The Sumerian King List has eight antediluvian kings with reigns in the tens of thousands of years, totaling about 241,200 years in the commonly cited reading. Some writers note that 432,000 matches the Indian Kali Yuga and treat the Babylonian and Indian figures as one shared idea. The match is real as a number, but there is no direct textual or historical evidence that Mesopotamian chronology gave rise to the Indian yuga scheme. The likely explanation is shared sexagesimal arithmetic combined with later numerological comparison.","keyClaims":["Berossus's ten antediluvian kings reigned 120 sars, or 432,000 years, in the standard version of his text.","The sar (3,600) is a sexagesimal unit of Babylonian origin, so 432,000 = 120 x 3,600 is internally Mesopotamian.","The Sumerian King List's eight antediluvian kings have reigns in the tens of thousands of years and total about 241,200 years.","The Indian Kali Yuga of 432,000 years matches Berossus's total, and the Mahayuga of 4,320,000 is ten times that figure.","Pingree and others argue that Babylonian astronomy and arithmetic reached India through Greek intermediaries in the early centuries CE, which could in principle carry sexagesimal numerical habits.","Some writers conclude the yuga numbers were borrowed from Mesopotamia, though no source makes that claim."],"evidence":["Berossus's reign figures are attested in Syncellus and Eusebius, and the 120-sar total is explicitly stated in the surviving excerpts.","Sexagesimal units (sar, ner, sosos) are well documented in Babylonian texts, so the 3,600-based arithmetic is genuinely Mesopotamian.","Pingree's work documents Greek-era transmission of Babylonian astronomical methods (such as Saros-based and sexagesimal procedures) into Indian astronomy in the early centuries CE.","The Sumerian King List and Berossus both place a flood as a divide between pre-flood and post-flood rulership, which is a structural parallel."],"criticisms":["The Indian yuga numbers (1,728,000 / 1,296,000 / 864,000 / 432,000) are built from 432 times 4, 3, 2, 1 and 10^3 (or 10^4), which is a self-contained decimal-times-multiplier scheme. Nothing in it requires a Mesopotamian source.","Berossus does not describe world ages or cycles that repeat. His 432,000 years are the reigns of ten kings before one flood, a single-event chronology, while the yuga system is a recurring cosmic cycle.","The Kali Yuga's 432,000 years are normally derived from the Mahayuga divided by 10, and the Mahayuga comes from Indian astronomical calculation tables, not from a list of kings.","Numerical coincidence is easy to find: many ancient systems use large round numbers, and 432 is divisible by many small numbers. A match alone is weak evidence of borrowing.","No Indian text before about the 5th century CE gives the 432,000 figure, and no Indian text explicitly cites Babylonian chronology for it. The direction of any transmission is therefore unknown.","Berossus's individual reign numbers, as transmitted, do not sum to 432,000 under the usual readings (they total 396,000), which suggests transmission errors or later adjustment. Using the stated total as a firm anchor is risky.","Sumerian King List figures are not a 432,000-based system. Their reign values are different multiples of 3,600 and 60, and they do not form a single cycle.","The Berossus excerpts are preserved through Christian chronographers (Eusebius, Syncellus) who had their own reasons to harmonize with Biblical chronology, so the numbers may have been altered in transmission.","Pingree's diffusion argument is about specific astronomical techniques, not about yuga cosmology. Extending it to the yuga framework goes beyond what the evidence supports."],"plausibility":2,"plausibilityReason":"Berossus and the Sumerian King List are real, datable texts, and the 3,600-based sexagesimal arithmetic is well attested. But the claim that these texts are the source of the Indian yuga scheme, or that they show a real history of very long reigns, lacks support. The 432 match is numerical, the direction of transmission is unknown, and the Berossus figures are unreliable in their transmitted form. Some diffusion of Babylonian astronomy into India is plausible, but that does not establish a Mesopotamian origin for the yuga durations. The 432,000 ages in Berossus are best read as a mythical or schematic chronology, not as a record of history.","references":["Berossus, Babyloniaca, fragments preserved in Eusebius, Chronicle (Armenian and Jerome versions) and George Synkellos, Chronographia (c. 800 CE). Standard edition: Paul Schnabel, Berossos und die babylonisch-hellenistische Literatur (1923). English translation in Stanley Burstein, The Babyloniaca of Berossus (1978).","Thorkild Jacobsen, The Sumerian King List (Assyriological Studies 11, 1939). The Weld-Blundell prism is discussed in the same volume.","David Pingree, The Mesopotamian Origin of Early Indian Mathematical Astronomy, Journal for the History of Astronomy 4 (1973).","Otto Neugebauer, A History of Ancient Mathematical Astronomy (1975). Discusses Babylonian sexagesimal and astronomical arithmetic.","Aryabhatiya of Aryabhata (499 CE) for the Mahayuga of 4,320,000 years and the Kali Yuga epoch.","Also see the other interpretation pages in this research set for the Indian yuga durations."]},{"id":"hamlets-mill","title":"Hamlet's Mill thesis: myths encode precession (applied to yugas)","category":"Comparative mythology","proponents":["Giorgio de Santillana (1902-1974), historian of science at MIT","Hertha von Dechend (1900-1997), historian of science and Indologist"],"primarySources":["Santillana & von Dechend, Hamlet's Mill: An Essay Investigating the Origins of Human Knowledge and Its Transmission Through Myth (Gambit, Boston, 1969; later reprints)"],"period":"Originated in the 1960s (book published 1969). Later popularized in esoteric and \"lost astronomy\" circles, which often go beyond the authors' own claims.","totalCycleYears":"N/A as a yuga system. The thesis concerns the precession of the equinoxes, a cycle of roughly 25,772 years (about 25,920 in the rounded \"Great Year\" figure). It does not supply yuga durations of its own.","durations":{"satya":"N/A (the thesis does not define yuga lengths; the traditional Satya Yuga is 1,728,000 years in the Puranic/Surya Siddhanta-style scheme)","treta":"N/A (traditional value 1,296,000 years, not derived from precession by the authors)","dvapara":"N/A (traditional value 864,000 years)","kali":"N/A (traditional value 432,000 years; the Mahayuga totals 4,320,000 years)"},"kaliStart":"N/A in the thesis. The conventional Kali Yuga epoch of 3102 BCE (Aryabhata-era reckoning) is not something the authors derive from precession.","kaliEnd":"N/A in the thesis. Using the conventional 3102 BCE start and 432,000 years, the end falls around 428,899 CE.","currentPosition":"N/A. The thesis is not a dating scheme, so it makes no claim about where we stand in 2026. Within the broader precession framework, the vernal equinox currently sits in Pisces, moving toward Aquarius, but this is a general astronomical fact and not a yuga-specific result.","summary":"Santillana and von Dechend argue that many myths worldwide (Hesiod, Vedic and Puranic texts, Norse and Babylonian material) are coded descriptions of celestial mechanics, above all the precession of the equinoxes and the rotation of the sky around the pole. Their central image, the \"mill\" of the title, stands for the turning of the heavens around a fixed axis. They read cosmic-age numbers, such as the Indian yuga and kalpa figures, as arithmetic relations tied to precessional or related astronomical cycles, and they treat ancient cultures as holding a lost, sophisticated astronomical science that was transmitted through myth. The book is best read as a provocative interpretive essay, not as a demonstrated decoding. Its method relies heavily on etymology, selective parallels, and symbolic reading, and it is not a dating system for the yugas.","keyClaims":["Myths across cultures preserve encoded astronomical knowledge, particularly about precession and the rotation of the heavens around the pole.","The 'mill' (e.g., the Norse mill of Grotti and the Hamlet/Amleth name) symbolizes the turning of the celestial sphere and the axis of precession.","Cosmic-age numbers in traditions such as India (yugas, kalpas) reflect arithmetic relations to precessional or planetary cycles rather than arbitrary invention.","Ancient cultures possessed a sophisticated astronomical science that was later obscured and transmitted symbolically through myth.","The 'Great Year' idea (a precessional cycle returning the sky to its starting configuration) is a deep, widespread concept across ancient cultures."],"evidence":["Precession of the equinoxes is real and well measured (about 50 arcseconds per year; full cycle roughly 25,700-25,900 years). Hipparchus (c. 2nd century BCE) is credited with discovering it.","Many ancient traditions do use very long cycles (Babylonian, Greek Great Year, Indian yugas and kalpas), so the existence of long cosmic-age numbers is documented.","Parallels the authors draw across Hesiod, Vedic, Norse, and other sources, including shared motifs of world-mills, churning, and pivoting axes.","Santillana's standing as a historian of science lent the book credibility in some academic circles at the time of publication."],"criticisms":["Cherry-picking and pattern-matching: parallels are selected to fit the precession hypothesis, and alternative readings are seldom tested against competing explanations.","Etymological leaps (e.g., linking 'Hamlet' to 'Amleth' and the Norse mill) are speculative and not supported by established philological methods.","Unfalsifiable framing: almost any myth involving rotation, axes, or cycles can be read as precessional, so the thesis cannot be disproved by counterexamples.","Indian yuga numbers have well-studied origins in mean-motion and planetary-conjunction arithmetic (as in the Surya Siddhanta and Aryabhatiya traditions) and in Puranic cosmology. Scholars do not agree that they were derived from precession, and the 25,920-year figure does not match the yuga totals directly.","Dating and transmission: the authors assume an ancient, lost astronomical science without independent evidence (no dated observational records from the proposed period), and they do not address how knowledge was transmitted across cultures.","Academic reception: specialists in Indology, classics, and archaeoastronomy have largely judged the book's central claims as overreaching, and later popularizers (e.g., in 'lost civilization' or 'ancient astronomy' circles) have pushed the thesis further than the authors did.","The 'mill' and 'Great Year' interpretations rely on modern astronomical categories applied retrospectively to pre-modern texts, which risks projection."],"plausibility":3,"plausibilityReason":"Precession is real and some ancient cultures did use long cosmic cycles, so the general idea that myth sometimes encodes astronomy is not absurd. But the specific claim that myths systematically encode precession, and that yuga numbers derive from it, is not supported by the evidence offered. The method is unfalsifiable and selective, and the Indian arithmetic has better-documented, non-precessional origins. Credit the book for raising real questions about the transmission of astronomical knowledge, and treat its decoding claims as speculative.","references":["Santillana, G. de & von Dechend, H. (1969). Hamlet's Mill: An Essay Investigating the Origins of Human Knowledge and Its Transmission Through Myth. Gambit (Boston). Later reprints exist.","Hipparchus (2nd century BCE) is credited with discovering precession; see standard histories of ancient astronomy (e.g., Neugebauer, A History of Ancient Mathematical Astronomy, 1975).","Note: web search was not used for this entry. Citations are from background knowledge and should be checked before publication. No URLs were verified."]},{"id":"vivekananda","title":"Swami Vivekananda and Ramakrishna Mission: Yugas as social and spiritual symbolism","category":"Traditional scriptural","proponents":["Swami Vivekananda (1863-1902)","Sri Ramakrishna (1836-1886), as recorded in the Kathamrita by M.","Ramakrishna Math and Ramakrishna Mission (Belur Math), as a body that generally presents the Puranic framework without a fixed chronology"],"primarySources":["Bhagavata Purana 12.3.51 (Kali-yuga's merit: chanting Krishna's name brings liberation), c. 9th-10th century CE","Vishnu Purana and Mahabharata yuga-cycle passages (Puranic framework, roughly 1st millennium CE)","Ramakrishna Kathamrita (Gospel of Sri Ramakrishna), recorded by M. from 1882 onward, published 1902-1932","Vivekananda Complete Works (Advaita Ashrama, 9 vols.), especially his 1890s letters and lectures on the coming 'Shudra' age and on Kali Yuga","Vivekananda lecture 'The Future of India' (1897), which discusses social ages in which priests, warriors, merchants, and workers each dominate"],"period":"Ramakrishna's teachings: 1860s-1886. Vivekananda's yuga-as-social-stages reading: c. 1890-1900. Mission's institutional stance: 1897 onward.","totalCycleYears":"4,320,000 years (Mahayuga), as in the standard Puranic framework that Vivekananda and Ramakrishna accepted in outline. Vivekananda did not use this figure as an active chronological claim.","durations":{"satya":"1,728,000 years (traditional value; not used as a live chronological claim by Vivekananda)","treta":"1,296,000 years (traditional value)","dvapara":"864,000 years (traditional value)","kali":"432,000 years (traditional value)"},"kaliStart":"3102 BCE (traditional epoch, the date used by Aryabhata in the Aryabhatiya, 499 CE). Vivekananda and Ramakrishna did not discuss this date.","kaliEnd":"About 428,898 CE by the traditional reckoning. Not a view the Mission has emphasized.","currentPosition":"In 2026 the traditional reckoning puts us about 5,127 years into Kali Yuga. Vivekananda and the Mission generally did not make this a point of doctrine. Their focus is on the spiritual and social meaning of the current age.","summary":"Ramakrishna accepted the Puranic yuga scheme and taught that Kali Yuga is a time when people are dominated by food and bodily needs (anna-gata-prana), so that spiritual practice must be simple and devotional. Following the Bhagavata Purana (12.3.51), he stressed that in Kali, chanting God's name is the most effective path. Vivekananda took the framework further by recasting the four yugas as a social cycle. In his 1890s letters and lectures he described the Satya age as the rule of priests, Treta as the rule of warriors, Dvapara as the rule of merchants, and Kali as the rule of workers and the common people (Shudra). He treated Kali not only as decline but as a stage of rising mass participation, and he expected a future synthesis that combined spiritual and material strengths. The Ramakrishna Mission has since presented these teachings mainly as moral and spiritual guidance rather than as a precise calendar.","keyClaims":["Kali Yuga is a real phase of the cosmic cycle, but its main feature is spiritual opportunity: the name of God and bhakti give liberation with less effort than in earlier ages (Ramakrishna, following BhP 12.3.51).","The four yugas can be read as a social cycle in which priestly, warrior, mercantile, and labouring classes each dominate in turn (Vivekananda, 1890s).","The coming age will be one of rising workers and common people, which Vivekananda saw as a historical movement, not only as decline.","Vivekananda urged the uplift of the masses and rejected the view that Kali Yuga is a time of hopelessness.","Chronology is not the point: the Mission generally avoids firm dating of yuga boundaries."],"evidence":["Ramakrishna's Kathamrita records his remarks on the food-dependence of Kali-age life and on the efficacy of nama-japa and kirtan in Kali.","Vivekananda's letters and lectures from the 1890s (in Complete Works) describe a coming 'Shudra' or labouring-class age and link yuga stages to social classes.","Bhagavata Purana 12.3.51 is a standard text cited by Ramakrishna and Vivekananda about the merit of Kali Yuga.","Romain Rolland, The Life of Vivekananda (1931), and Marie Louise Burke, Swami Vivekananda in the West (1958-74), describe Vivekananda's reformist reading of tradition, including social ideas linked to the yugas."],"criticisms":["Vivekananda's social reading is a reinterpretation of the Puranic text, not a claim that the texts literally describe history. Critics say he projected modern social concerns onto a traditional schema.","The Puranic yuga durations are symbolic and inflated, and Vivekananda's writings give no evidence that he tested them against historical data.","His 'Shudra age' is a prophecy rather than a verifiable historical claim. Its fulfilment depends on how one interprets democracy, labour movements, and modern nationalism.","Ramakrishna's comments on Kali are devotional and pastoral, so they are not a chronological or historical doctrine at all.","Critics note the tension between his claim that Kali is a 'strife' age and his optimism about it. This is internal to his thought and reflects its reformist purpose.","The Mission's institutional silence on exact dating makes it hard to assess the view on its own terms. Its published position is largely a restatement of the Complete Works."],"plausibility":2,"plausibilityReason":"As a literal chronology this view scores very low: the 4.32 million-year mahayuga cannot be squared with the age of the Earth's human history, and the 3102 BCE start is an astronomical-computational epoch. The view does have some value as a reading of how an tradition can be reinterpreted for social purposes. Vivekananda's social-stage schema is a reformist interpretation, not a testable forecast. This assessment is from knowledge only; I did not run web searches for this report, so quotations and dates should be checked against the Complete Works before publication.","references":["Swami Vivekananda, The Complete Works of Swami Vivekananda (Advaita Ashrama, Mayavati), esp. vol. 3-5 and letters from 1894-1897","Swami Vivekananda, 'The Future of India' (lecture, 1897)","Sri Ramakrishna, The Gospel of Sri Ramakrishna (Kathamrita), trans. Swami Nikhilananda (Ramakrishna-Vivekananda Center, New York, 1942)","Bhagavata Purana 12.3.51","Romain Rolland, The Life of Vivekananda and the Universal Gospel (1931)","Marie Louise Burke, Swami Vivekananda in the West: New Discoveries (6 vols., Advaita Ashrama, 1958-1974)","Ramakrishna Mission official site: https://www.belurmath.org"]},{"id":"iskcon","title":"Gaudiya Vaishnava / ISKCON view: Kali Yuga 432,000 years with a Chaitanya-era golden age","category":"Traditional scriptural","proponents":["Gaudiya Vaishnava lineages (Chaitanya Vaishnavism, Bengal, 16th century onward)","Bhaktisiddhanta Sarasvati and the Gaudiya Math","A. C. Bhaktivedanta Swami Prabhupada and ISKCON (International Society for Krishna Consciousness, founded 1966)","Bhaktivinoda Thakura (19th-century Gaudiya reformer, widely cited in this tradition)"],"primarySources":["Vishnu Purana (compiled roughly 1st millennium CE) - gives the four-yuga scheme and Kali's duration in divine years","Bhagavata Purana (roughly 6th-10th century CE) - Skandha 1 and Skandha 12 yuga passages; Skandha 11.5.32 describes the golden-complexioned avatar who propagates sankirtana in Kali Yuga","Mahabharata, Vana Parva (roughly 4th century BCE to 4th century CE layers) - Markandeya's description of the yugas and decline of dharma","Caitanya Bhagavata by Vrindavana Dasa (c. 1540s-1550s CE) - early hagiography of Chaitanya Mahaprabhu (1486-1534 CE)","Caitanya Caritamrita by Krishnadasa Kaviraja (c. 1615 CE) - standard theological biography of Chaitanya","Prabhupada's Bhagavata purports and Caitanya-caritamrita translation and purports (1960s-1977)"],"period":"Yuga scheme: Puranic, roughly 1st-10th century CE. Gaudiya application to Chaitanya as a Kali-yuga avatar: 16th century CE. Systematized in ISKCON from the 1960s onward. The 3102 BCE start date is a later calculation tied to the Aryabhatiya kali epoch (499 CE) and to Krishna's departure tradition.","totalCycleYears":"4,320,000 years (mahayuga). Satya 1,728,000 + Treta 1,296,000 + Dvapara 864,000 + Kali 432,000. This is 1,200 divine years times 360 human years per divine year, following the Puranic convention.","durations":{"satya":"1,728,000 years (4,800 divine years)","treta":"1,296,000 years (3,600 divine years)","dvapara":"864,000 years (2,400 divine years)","kali":"432,000 years (1,200 divine years)"},"kaliStart":"3102 BCE (traditional date, about 17/18 February; the moment Krishna is said to have left the earth)","kaliEnd":"About 428,898 CE by the same epoch arithmetic (3102 BCE plus 432,000 years, less one year for the absence of a year zero)","currentPosition":"In 2026 CE we are about Kali year 5,127 to 5,128. Roughly 426,870 years of Kali Yuga remain under this reckoning. Gaudiya teaching places us in the Kali Yuga era when Chaitanya's sankirtana (congregational chanting of the holy names) is the prescribed practice. The 10,000-year golden-age claim is a Gaudiya-discourse teaching about the period of Chaitanya's influence within Kali. It is not a precise, universally agreed chronology.","summary":"The Gaudiya Vaishnava (ISKCON) view keeps the standard Puranic yuga scheme: a 4,320,000-year mahayuga with Kali Yuga lasting 432,000 years. It dates Kali's start to 3102 BCE, the moment Krishna is said to have departed. Within Kali, Gaudiya teaching treats Chaitanya Mahaprabhu (1486-1534 CE) as the golden-complexioned avatar that Bhagavata Purana 11.5.32 describes, and holds that harinama-sankirtana is the age's prescribed practice. Some discourses extend this into a roughly 10,000-year period of spiritual golden age connected to Chaitanya's mission. This is the least standardized element and should be treated as tradition-internal teaching rather than a documented chronology. The overall structure is internally consistent and well-attested in Puranic texts. Its real-world timeline claims, however, depend on dating Krishna's departure, which has no independent historical evidence.","keyClaims":["Kali Yuga lasts 432,000 years and began at Krishna's departure in 3102 BCE.","We are about 5,127 years into Kali Yuga as of 2026 CE.","Chaitanya Mahaprabhu is the Kali-yuga avatar (the golden one of Bhagavata 11.5.32) who inaugurated the age's dharma of congregational chanting.","Harinama-sankirtana is the most effective spiritual practice for this age, as taught in Chaitanya-caritamrita and Prabhupada's purports.","Within Kali, a golden-age period tied to Chaitanya's mission is taught, with a figure of roughly 10,000 years in some discourses.","The yuga decline (dharma reduced to one quarter in Kali) is literal and cosmological rather than metaphorical."],"evidence":["Internal consistency of the 1,200 divine year x 360 multiplier across all four yugas, which is attested in the Puranic texts.","The Bhagavata Purana 11.5.32 verse on the golden avatar who sings the names in Kali Yuga, matched by Gaudiya commentators to Chaitanya.","Caitanya Bhagavata and Caitanya-caritamrita record Chaitanya's life and his emphasis on nama-sankirtana, supporting the tradition's historical anchor for the avatar claim.","The 499 CE Aryabhatiya epoch, which places a Kali starting date at 3102 BCE. This is a documented medieval astronomical convention, not evidence of an actual event.","Long-running ISKCON scholarship and devotee exposition of Kali chronology, which is internally coherent but not independent verification."],"criticisms":["The 432,000-year figure and the 3102 BCE start date are not supported by archaeology, geology, or astronomy. The Kali epoch is a medieval Indian calendrical convention (linked to Aryabhata's 499 CE work and later siddhanta reckoning) and was not derived from observation of any event.","The 3102 BCE date is anchored to Krishna's departure, a figure from the Mahabharata and Puranic tradition. Historians do not treat Krishna as a historically datable person, and the Mahabharata itself reached its present form over many centuries.","The Puranas are compiled texts from many centuries, so the 432,000-year scheme reflects textual and cosmological theory rather than a single revelation of chronology.","The 10,000-year golden-age claim is weakly sourced. Gaudiya writers vary on its length and starting point, and no single canonical verse fixes it.","Applying the Bhagavata 11.5.32 golden avatar verse specifically to Chaitanya is a tradition-internal interpretation. Other Vaishnava and Hindu groups read the verse differently.","The view is unfalsifiable as a timeline: it makes no testable predictions about the real world, and its dates are reached by theological reasoning rather than evidence.","Scholarship on Chaitanya Vaishnavism (for example Edward C. Dimock's work on Bengal Vaishnavism) treats the yuga framework as religious cosmology, separate from Chaitanya's historical biography, which is documented in sixteenth-century hagiographies."],"plausibility":2,"plausibilityReason":"The internal scriptural arithmetic is consistent, and Chaitanya is a real historical figure (1486-1534 CE) whose movement is documented. But the 432,000-year Kali duration, the 3102 BCE start, and the cosmic yuga decline have no empirical support. The start date is a medieval calendrical convention rather than a measured event. The 10,000-year golden-age element has weak canonical grounding. Roughly 2/10 as a real-world timeline. It is more credible as a record of how a living tradition understands its own history.","references":["Bhagavata Purana, Skandha 1 and 12 (yuga passages); Skandha 11.5.32 (golden avatar and sankirtana)","Vishnu Purana, Book 1 and Book 6 (yuga durations)","Mahabharata, Vana Parva (Markandeya's account of the yugas)","Vrindavana Dasa, Caitanya Bhagavata (c. 1540s-1550s)","Krishnadasa Kaviraja, Caitanya Caritamrita (c. 1615)","A. C. Bhaktivedanta Swami Prabhupada, Srimad-Bhagavatam purports and Caitanya-caritamrita (Bhaktivedanta Book Trust)","Edward C. Dimock Jr., The Place of the Hidden Moon: Erotic Mysticism in the Vaisnava-Sahajiya Cult of Bengal (University of Chicago Press, 1966)","Edwin F. Bryant and Maria L. Ekstrand (eds.), The Hare Krishna Movement: The Postcharismatic Fate of a Religious Transplant (Columbia University Press, 2004)","Aryabhatiya (499 CE) for the kali epoch convention (secondary discussion in standard histories of Indian astronomy)","Note: web verification was not performed in this run. Citations are from working knowledge and should be checked against the primary texts before publication."]},{"id":"arya-samaj","title":"Dayananda Saraswati and Arya Samaj: Yuga chronology and the 1.96 billion year creation age","category":"Traditional scriptural","proponents":["Dayananda Saraswati (1824-1883), founder of Arya Samaj","Arya Samaj movement and its later scholars and publishers"],"primarySources":["Satyarth Prakash (Hindi, 1875; revised 1884), especially Chapter 8 on creation, sustenance and dissolution","Vedas (Rigveda, Yajurveda) as the claimed infallible authority"],"period":"Arya Samaj founded 10 April 1875 in Bombay (principles settled 1877 in Lahore); Dayananda died 1883","totalCycleYears":"4,320,000 years per mahayuga (standard Surya Siddhanta-style scheme, as I recall it being used in Satyarth Prakash; not verified against the primary text in this session)","durations":{"satya":"1,728,000 years (unverified recall)","treta":"1,296,000 years (unverified recall)","dvapara":"864,000 years (unverified recall)","kali":"432,000 years (unverified recall)"},"kaliStart":"3102 BCE in the standard reckoning; Arya Samaj calendars count Kali Samvat from that date (Kali Samvat about 5126-5127 in 2025-26)","kaliEnd":"About 427,000 years after 2026 CE if the full 432,000-year Kali Yuga is accepted; this is an extrapolation, not a claim the sources state","currentPosition":"In 2026 CE, Arya Samaj reckoning places us in the Kali Yuga, roughly 5,126-5,128 years after its start depending on calendar month. The Srishti Samvat (creation era) is cited as about 1,96,08,53,125 years, the figure the Arya Samaj calendar uses.","summary":"Dayananda Saraswati, founder of the Arya Samaj, held that the Vedas are infallible and contain all true knowledge, and rejected later Puranic and Tantric additions. In Satyarth Prakash he adopted the traditional yuga framework of the Surya Siddhanta-style mahayuga and dated creation (Srishti Samvat) to about 1.96 billion years ago, which the Arya Samaj continues to publish in its calendars. The Kali Yuga is placed at about 5,000 years old, which keeps yuga durations inside the traditional system while the creation age is far larger. The Arya Samaj position is a reformist scriptural claim, not an empirical timeline, and it is often reconciled with modern science by arguing that the Vedas anticipated scientific facts. The creation figure and yuga durations should be checked against the Satyarth Prakash text before being quoted as settled.","keyClaims":["The Vedas are the infallible source of all true knowledge, and the Arya Samaj calendar counts years from creation (Srishti Samvat) rather than from a historical founder.","Creation is dated to about 1,96,08,53,125 years ago (1.96 billion years), a figure the Arya Samaj calendar uses.","The yuga system (Satya, Treta, Dvapara, Kali) is accepted with traditional durations, and Kali Yuga began in 3102 BCE.","Modern science is claimed to be anticipated in the Vedas, so the age of creation is presented as consistent with Vedic knowledge.","Idol worship and Puranic accounts of avatars are rejected in favour of the Vedas alone."],"evidence":["Wikipedia (fetched 2026): Arya Samaj founded April 1875 in Bombay by Dayananda Saraswati; founded on belief in the infallible authority of the Vedas; Puranas rejected; murti puja prohibited.","Wikipedia Kali Yuga article (fetched 2026): standard 3102 BCE start date and the Aryabhata 3600-year reference, the astronomical basis for the Kali Yuga start used broadly in Hindu tradition.","Satyarth Prakash Chapter 8 is the source the Arya Samaj gives for creation chronology. Attempts to confirm the 1,96,08,53,125 figure from fetched Wikipedia pages did not succeed; those pages did not contain it."],"criticisms":["The 1.96 billion year creation age has no independent empirical basis. The Earth is about 4.54 billion years old and the universe about 13.8 billion years old by radiometric dating and cosmology.","The Vedic texts do not contain an explicit chronology of 1.96 billion years; the figure comes from the later Puranic and Siddhantic yuga scheme that Dayananda adopted.","Dayananda's rejection of Puranas is selective: he accepts the yuga and mahayuga arithmetic from the Surya Siddhanta-derived framework, which is itself a later astronomical system rather than a Vedic text.","Attempts to match Vedic statements with modern science (such as atomic theory or electricity) are generally rejected by historians and scientists as retrofitting, not as evidence.","Arya Samaj calendars count from creation while the Kali start is historical in its own tradition; the two numbers are not derived from the same method, an inconsistency rarely acknowledged.","The exact Srishti Samvat figure and yuga durations were not verified against the primary Satyarth Prakash text in this session; they should be checked before relying on them."],"plausibility":2,"plausibilityReason":"The Kali Yuga start in 3102 BCE is a traditional astronomical reckoning that is internally consistent but not an empirically tested event. The 1.96 billion year creation age conflicts with radiometric dating and cosmology by orders of magnitude. The Arya Samaj position is coherent with its scriptural premises but has no external test that it passes.","references":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arya_Samaj","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Satyarth_Prakash","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dayananda_Saraswati","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kali_Yuga","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yuga"]},{"id":"guenon","title":"René Guénon's traditionalist reading of the yugas (qualitative cycles, precession correspondence)","category":"Modern spiritual/esoteric","proponents":["René Guénon (1886–1951)","Julius Evola (follower, Rivolta contro il mondo moderno, 1934)","Frithjof Schuon (Traditionalist school)","Ananda Coomaraswamy (related Traditionalist sympathies)","Later Traditionalist and Perennialist writers"],"primarySources":["Guénon, La Crise du monde moderne (1927)","Guénon, Le Roi du monde (1927)","Guénon, Le Règne de la quantité et les signes des temps (1945)","Guénon, Études sur l'hindouisme (posthumous and collected essays)","Traditional Hindu sources he drew on: Manusmriti, Vishnu Purana, Mahabharata, Surya Siddhanta (c. 4th–5th c. CE, as a Siddhanta text)"],"period":"Early 20th century, c. 1920s–1940s (Guénon's major works on cycles appear 1927–1945)","totalCycleYears":"Guénon accepted the traditional Mahayuga of 4,320,000 years as the standard framework (about 12,000 'divine years' of 360 each). He did not, as far as I can confirm, establish a distinct 64,800-year manvantara; that figure is not verifiable from my knowledge and should not be taken as his. Standard Puranic manvantara = 71 mahayugas (about 306.7 million years).","durations":{"satya":"1,728,000 years (4,800 divine years) in the traditional scheme Guénon followed; he treated the numbers as symbolic rather than chronological","treta":"1,296,000 years (3,600 divine years)","dvapara":"864,000 years (2,400 divine years)","kali":"432,000 years (1,200 divine years) in the total scheme; the 'historical' Kali Yuga is traditionally dated to begin 3102 BCE, a date Guénon accepted as the traditional reckoning"},"kaliStart":"3102 BCE (traditional reckoning, accepted by Guénon as the start of the present dark age)","kaliEnd":"Not fixed by Guénon in a firm calendar date. The 432,000-year total would put the end far in the future; the 'end of cycle' under his framing is qualitative (the completion of the descent of quality into quantity), not a prediction of a specific year.","currentPosition":"In 2026 Guénon's framework places us deep in the Kali Yuga, in the late phase of the descent he called the reign of quantity. He gives no precise year for a turn, so the view makes no falsifiable dated prediction about 2026.","summary":"Guénon, the founder of the Traditionalist (Perennialist) school, read the Hindu yuga doctrine as a metaphysical map of cosmic decline rather than a literal chronology. He held that humanity is in the Kali Yuga, an age of material focus and loss of spiritual principle, and he linked this descent to a broader cyclical theory in which civilizations move from unity toward fragmentation. He used traditional Sanskrit numbers, but treated their exact lengths as symbolic and correlated the cycles with the precession of the equinoxes as a 'Great Year' correspondence. His main contribution to the yuga discussion is a qualitative theory of decline, not a dated calendar.","keyClaims":["Humanity is in the Kali Yuga, the last and most degenerate age, which began in 3102 BCE in the traditional reckoning.","The modern world is characterized by the 'reign of quantity' (Le Règne de la quantité), where material measurement displaces qualitative and spiritual knowledge.","Cosmic cycles (yugas, the Great Year) have a symbolic structure; their numerical lengths encode principles and should not be read as literal historical dates.","The descent through the yugas is continuous and cannot be reversed by human agency; traditional knowledge persists in a small, initiatic core.","The cycles correspond to the precession of the equinoxes as an analogy for the shift of the spiritual pole across the zodiac.","Modern 'progress' is an inversion of the traditional order, and the present era marks an advanced stage of the Kali Yuga."],"evidence":["Cross-cultural parallels in Hesiod's Ages of Man, Greek, Persian and Mesoamerican cycle myths, which Traditionalists treat as echoes of a single primordial doctrine.","The 3102 BCE start date for the Kali Yuga is a traditional Hindu astronomical reckoning (linked to the Surya Siddhanta / Aryabhata tradition), not a modern invention.","The 1:2:3:4 ratio of yuga lengths in Hindu texts is real and textually attested.","Guénon's arguments are internally coherent within a metaphysical framework of correspondences."],"criticisms":["Guénon's framework is unfalsifiable: he gives no dated prediction, and the 'end of cycle' is qualitative, so no observation could count against it.","The 64,800-year 'manvantara' figure given in the research brief could not be confirmed as Guénon's. The standard Puranic manvantara is 71 mahayugas (about 306.7 million years), so the figure should be verified before being attributed to him.","Hindu chronologies are not historically reliable as measured time. The 3102 BCE start is an astronomical or sectarian reckoning, and the cosmic numbers are conventional symbols.","Scholars of religion (Mircea Eliade, The Myth of the Eternal Return, 1949; Mark Sedgwick, Against the Modern World, 2004; Richard King, Orientalism and Religion, 1999) argue Traditionalism selectively reads Hindu sources, imposes a Western perennialist template and draws on 19th-century Orientalist and Theosophical materials.","Guénon's reading is strongly anti-modern and in places reactionary (e.g., rejection of modern science and democracy), which many historians treat as ideological rather than descriptive.","The precession correspondence is an analogy, not a measured astronomical link to the yugas; the 25,920-year precession cycle does not map onto the 4.32 million-year scheme without arbitrary multipliers."],"plausibility":2,"plausibilityReason":"As a description of real history or cosmology the view has little support. It is a metaphysical doctrine, not a testable chronology. Its one dated anchor (3102 BCE) is a traditional reckoning, and the vast durations are symbolic. Its value is as an intellectual history of how modern esotericism reinterpreted Indian cosmology, not as evidence for a real cosmic timeline. Plausibility is low but not zero for the narrow claim that Hindu cycle doctrines are cultural constructs with a long history of symbolic use.","references":["René Guénon, La Crise du monde moderne (Paris: Gallimard, 1927)","René Guénon, Le Roi du monde (Paris: Gallimard, 1927)","René Guénon, Le Règne de la quantité et les signes des temps (Paris: Gallimard, 1945)","Mircea Eliade, Le Mythe de l'éternel retour (Paris: Gallimard, 1949)","Mark Sedgwick, Against the Modern World: Traditionalism and the Secret Intellectual History of the Twentieth Century (Oxford University Press, 2004)","Richard King, Orientalism and Religion: Postcolonial Theory, India and 'The Mystic East' (Routledge, 1999)","Note: citations are from memory; URLs and page numbers were not checked with WebFetch in this run."]},{"id":"danielou","title":"Alain Daniélou's yuga chronology (Kali Yuga ending ~2442 CE)","category":"Academic/historical","proponents":["Alain Daniélou (1907–1994), French Indologist and musicologist; attribution of the 2442 CE end date is unverified"],"primarySources":["Puranic yuga schema (Vishnu Purana, Vayu Purana, Mahabharata Vana Parva), c. 300 BCE–500 CE for the core framework","Manusmriti 1.69–71 (divine-year multiplier of 360 for the yuga durations), c. 200 BCE–200 CE","Surya Siddhanta (astronomical epoch of Kali Yuga start at 3102 BCE), c. 4th–5th century CE","Daniélou, The Myths and Gods of India (English trans. 1985, Inner Traditions; French original Le Mythes et les dieux de l'Inde)","Daniélou, While the Gods Play (1987) and Hindu Polytheism (1964) for general context on his views of Indian cosmology"],"period":"Daniélou's writings date from the 1960s–1980s; the 2442 CE end date is not something I could confirm in his published works, so its origin is uncertain","totalCycleYears":"4,320,000 years (Mahayuga, 12,000 divine years × 360), per the standard Puranic schema","durations":{"satya":"1,728,000 years (4,800 divine years) in the standard schema","treta":"1,296,000 years (3,600 divine years)","dvapara":"864,000 years (2,400 divine years)","kali":"432,000 years (1,200 divine years)"},"kaliStart":"3102 BCE (traditional astronomical epoch, Surya Siddhanta / Aryabhata tradition)","kaliEnd":"Claimed ~2442 CE for this interpretation. This does not match the standard 432,000-year Kali Yuga from 3102 BCE, which would end around 428,898 CE. The claim is only consistent if a different epoch or duration is assumed, and I could not find the source for that assumption.","currentPosition":"In 2026 CE, about 5,128 years of Kali Yuga have elapsed from the 3102 BCE epoch, leaving about 426,900 years in the standard reckoning. Under the claimed 2442 CE end date, about 416 years would remain.","summary":"This interpretation attributes to Alain Daniélou a view that the Kali Yuga ends around 2442 CE. I could not verify that attribution in his published works, which discuss Indian cosmology and the yugas mainly as symbolic and cultural constructs rather than as a literal calendar with a forecast end date. The standard Puranic and Surya Siddhanta schema puts Kali Yuga's start at 3102 BCE with a 432,000-year length, which implies an end nowhere near 2442 CE. Any 2442 CE end would require a different epoch or a non-standard duration, and the sources for that are not clear. For this reason the interpretation should be treated as unverified and not as a reliable reading of Daniélou.","keyClaims":["Kali Yuga ends around 2442 CE (attributed to Daniélou; unverified)","The yuga durations are symbolic or cultural, not a literal chronology (consistent with Daniélou's general approach, but this is not confirmed for the 2442 date)"],"evidence":["Daniélou's known scholarship on Indian mythology and music, which treats the yugas as cosmological and symbolic constructs","No primary text I could identify gives 2442 CE as the Kali Yuga end date","Internal arithmetic: the standard 432,000-year Kali Yuga from 3102 BCE gives an end around 428,898 CE, not 2442 CE"],"criticisms":["The 2442 CE date is not traceable to a specific Daniélou text in my knowledge, so the attribution is weak","It does not follow from the standard 3102 BCE epoch and 432,000-year duration, so it is internally inconsistent without further assumptions","Modern chronology and astronomy give no support for a yuga boundary in 2442 CE; the yuga framework has no empirical basis","Puranic yuga chronology was a later systematization and has no documented historical basis for a precise end date"],"plausibility":2,"plausibilityReason":"The standard 3102 BCE start and 432,000-year duration are well documented as textual and astronomical traditions, but they are not historically verifiable. The 2442 CE end is unsupported by the sources I could identify and contradicts the standard arithmetic. Attribution to Daniélou is unconfirmed, so the claim cannot be credited to him.","references":["Daniélou, A. The Myths and Gods of India (Inner Traditions, 1985). Verify the yuga discussion against the text before citing.","Manusmriti 1.69–71 (translations by G. Bühler, Sacred Books of the East, vol. 25)","Surya Siddhanta (translated by E. Burgess, 1860; ed. Whitney)","Note: I could not run live web searches in this session, so these references were not checked against online sources."]},{"id":"evola-traditionalist","title":"Traditionalist school (Guénon, Evola, Schuon): yugas as qualitative spiritual decline","category":"Modern spiritual/esoteric","proponents":["René Guénon","Julius Evola","Ananda Coomaraswamy","Frithjof Schuon","Titus Burckhardt","Martin Lings","Seyyed Hossein Nasr"],"primarySources":["Hindu texts the school draws on: Manusmriti (c. 200 BCE–200 CE, yuga durations in Manu 1.68–86), Mahabharata (Vana Parva yuga passages), Vishnu Purana, Surya Siddhanta (4,320,000-year mahayuga)","Hesiod, Works and Days (c. 700 BCE), the Golden/Silver/Bronze/Iron ages, used by Evola as a parallel","René Guénon, Le Roi du monde (1927), La Crise du monde moderne (1927), Le Règne de la quantité et les signes des temps (1945)","Julius Evola, Rivolta contro il mondo moderno (1934; English: Revolt Against the Modern World, Inner Traditions 1995), Cavalcare la tigre (1961)","Ananda Coomaraswamy, essays on traditional civilization and the critique of industrialism (1930s–1940s)"],"period":"Origin in the 1920s with Guénon (Crise du monde moderne, 1927); Evola's systematic application in Revolt Against the Modern World (1934); later elaborated by Schuon, Lings, Nasr (1950s–present)","totalCycleYears":"4,320,000 years (mahayuga), as in the Hindu texts the school adopts; the school treats these figures as symbolic of qualitative phases rather than as a prediction schedule","durations":{"satya":"1,728,000 years (traditional reckoning, 4/10 of mahayuga)","treta":"1,296,000 years (3/10)","dvapara":"864,000 years (2/10)","kali":"432,000 years (1/10)"},"kaliStart":"3102 BCE (traditional epoch, accepted by Guénon and Evola)","kaliEnd":"c. 428,900 CE, by the 432,000-year duration. The school says the end is not a calendar event and that a cosmic reset is not a timed prediction.","currentPosition":"In 2026 about 5,127 years have elapsed since 3102 BCE, roughly 1.2% of Kali Yuga by the traditional count. Traditionalists read the present as the deepest phase of spiritual darkness (materialism, quantity over quality, loss of hierarchy and sacred knowledge), not as a point near a termination.","summary":"The Traditionalist school, led by René Guénon and developed in Italy by Julius Evola, reads the Hindu yuga cycle as a description of a qualitative decline from a primordial spiritual unity (Satya) through successively lower ages to the present Kali Yuga. Guénon accepted the traditional 3102 BCE start of Kali Yuga and argued that modernity is its characteristic expression, marked by materialism, the reign of quantity, and the replacement of sacred hierarchy by egalitarianism and mechanism. Evola, in Revolt Against the Modern World (1934), applied the same framework to Western history by pairing the yugas with Hesiod's ages and the Hindu notion of a dark age. Evola's practical advice, in later works like Cavalcare la tigre (1961), was that an individual may \"ride the tiger\" by preserving inner spiritual realization without expecting collective reversal. The school's chronology is treated as symbolic, so it makes few testable predictions about dates.","keyClaims":["The four yugas are qualitative stages of decline from a primordial traditional unity, and the cycle of decline is structural and recurring.","Kali Yuga began in 3102 BCE, and the modern period is its late, darkest phase.","Modernity (materialism, mechanization, egalitarianism, the reign of quantity) is a characteristic expression of Kali Yuga, not an accidental feature of it.","Material progress and spiritual decline coincide, so apparent technological advance is no evidence of cosmic or spiritual improvement.","Individuals can preserve inner spiritual realization within the dark age ('riding the tiger'); collective restoration is not expected within the historical timeframe.","Western history (Hesiod's ages, the decline of medieval Christendom, the Renaissance and Enlightenment) can be read as the same cycle of decline."],"evidence":["Textual claims from Manusmriti, the Mahabharata, Vishnu Purana and the Surya Siddhanta for the yuga durations and the Kali start date","Comparative parallels between Hindu yugas, Hesiod's five ages, and Persian/Zoroastrian and other world-age schemes","Guénon's and Evola's critiques of modern industrial society, secularization, and the loss of metaphysical knowledge (argued from observation and tradition rather than data)","Mircea Eliade-style analysis of cyclic versus linear time, which the school uses as a comparative foundation (Eliade himself was not a Traditionalist)"],"criticisms":["Unfalsifiable: a spiritual decline with no measurable endpoint cannot be tested against any observation, and the school explicitly declines to give dates for the reset.","The chronology is not ancient. The 432,000-year Kali duration and the 3102 BCE start come from late Siddhantic astronomy (roughly 5th century CE onward), not from the earliest Vedic or Upanishadic sources, which do not present a yuga chronology at all. Scholars such as David Pingree have documented the late development of Indian astronomical chronology.","Selective reading: the school picks Hindu and Greek schemes that fit a decline narrative and ignores Indian sources in which yugas are cyclical without moral decline in the way Evola reads it.","The decline thesis imports a Western pessimistic model (Hesiod, later Spengler-style decadence) onto Hindu cosmology, so the 'Traditional' framing is partly a modern construction.","Evola's political writings, including his racial theory (Sintesi di dottrina della razza, 1941) and his support for Italian Fascism, shape his reading of hierarchy and decline. Critics argue this is not separable from his account of the Kali Yuga, and his work influenced later far-right and neo-fascist circles. Mark Sedgwick's Against the Modern World (2004) is a standard scholarly account of this issue.","Dismissive of material improvement: health, literacy and life expectancy gains are not counted as evidence, which critics regard as an unargued value judgment rather than a historical finding.","Guénon's and Evola's treatment of Hindu sources is frequently criticized by Indologists as neither a faithful reproduction of Hindu exegesis nor a critical historical reading."],"plausibility":2,"plausibilityReason":"As a literal chronology the Kali start date and durations rest on late Siddhantic astronomy with no independent evidence, and the school's decline thesis cannot be tested. As a cultural critique of modernity it has some value, since the critique of materialism and secular instrumental reason overlaps with serious work in the history of religions. Its strongest claims are normative and metaphysical rather than historical, which makes them unfalsifiable and not evidence-based.","references":["René Guénon, La Crise du monde moderne (1927); English: The Crisis of the Modern World (trans. Arthur Osborne, Marco Pallis, Richard Nicholson, 1942)","René Guénon, Le Règne de la quantité et les signes des temps (1945); English: The Reign of Quantity and the Signs of the Times (trans. Lord Northbourne, 1953)","Julius Evola, Rivolta contro il mondo moderno (1934); English: Revolt Against the Modern World (trans. Guido Stucco, Inner Traditions, 1995)","Julius Evola, Cavalcare la tigre (1961); English: Ride the Tiger (Inner Traditions, 2003)","Mark Sedgwick, Against the Modern World: Traditionalism and the Secret Intellectual History of the Twentieth Century (Oxford University Press, 2004)","David Pingree, work on Indian astronomy and the Siddhantas (see his Census of the Exact Sciences in Sanskrit series)"]},{"id":"cruttenden-binary","title":"Cruttenden's Binary Star Theory and BRI's Use of Yukteswar's Cycle","category":"Alternative/fringe","proponents":["Walter Cruttenden (author, founder of the Binary Research Institute)","Binary Research Institute (BRI) and its students and teachers","Sri Yukteswar Giri (source of the 24,000-year cycle scheme, as reinterpreted by Cruttenden)"],"primarySources":["Sri Yukteswar Giri, The Holy Science (Kaivalya Darshanam), 1894 (source of the 24,000-year precession-linked Great Cycle with 4800/3600/2400/1200-year yugas)","Walter Cruttenden, Lost Star of Myth and Time (2006), the main popular statement of the binary-companion thesis","Binary Research Institute course materials and talks (secondary, not peer reviewed)"],"period":"Yukteswar's scheme: 1894. Cruttenden's binary-star synthesis and BRI teaching: roughly 1990s to 2000s, popularized by Lost Star of Myth and Time (2006).","totalCycleYears":"24,000 years for the Great Cycle (Yukteswar's scheme, which BRI adopts). Cruttenden ties this to a hypothesized solar companion with an orbit of roughly the same order; exact figures should be checked against his own text, which I could not verify live.","durations":{"satya":"4800 years (Yukteswar's ascending/descending scheme; 4800 + 3600 + 2400 + 1200 = 12,000 per half-cycle, 24,000 total)","treta":"3600 years","dvapara":"2400 years","kali":"1200 years"},"kaliStart":"Dates are reported inconsistently across BRI-adjacent and Yukteswar-derived sources and I could not verify them live. One common reading places the descending Kali Yuga around 499 CE.","kaliEnd":"Commonly cited as about 1699 CE, after which an ascending Dvapara is said to begin. Treat as unverified.","currentPosition":"Under this view, in 2026 we are in the ascending Dvapara Yuga (or near its transition), and BRI teaches that the solar system's companion-star orbit drives the shift. No independent astronomical measurement is offered for this position.","summary":"Cruttenden's thesis, as presented by the Binary Research Institute, is that the Sun has a dark or unseen binary companion star on a long elliptical orbit of roughly 24,000 years. He argues that ancient civilizations (Vedic, Egyptian, Sumerian, Mayan and others) encoded this companion in myth and in the 24,000-year Great Cycle. BRI adopts Sri Yukteswar's 1894 scheme of 4800/3600/2400/1200-year yugas and reads the ascending and descending halves as driven by the companion's orbit. The claims rest on comparative mythology and a reinterpretation of precession, not on observed evidence of a companion star. The scheme is widely regarded as pseudo-astronomy.","keyClaims":["The Sun has a companion star, sometimes called a 'Lost Star' or 'Nemesis-type' object, on a long orbit of about 24,000 years.","Ancient mythologies across cultures preserve memory of this companion and of the Great Cycle.","Yukteswar's 24,000-year Great Cycle with 4800/3600/2400/1200-year yugas reflects real astronomical motion.","Yuga transitions correspond to the Sun's position relative to its companion or the galactic structure.","The current era (2026) marks a transition in the cycle, which BRI treats as meaningful for human consciousness."],"evidence":["Comparative mythology: claimed parallels among Hindu, Greek, Egyptian, Maya and other traditions, mostly interpreted through the companion-star lens.","Yukteswar's 1894 precession-based schema, which is a real text but makes astronomical claims that are not supported.","The fact that axial precession is real (about 25,772 years for a full cycle in modern values) is cited as partial support, but the figures do not match.","Advocates argue that a dark companion could be undetected, which is a logically possible but unsupported claim."],"criticisms":["No observational evidence for a solar companion star. Infrared sky surveys (IRAS, 1983 onward) and later stellar catalogs, including Gaia data, have not revealed one. The older 'Nemesis' hypothesis was tested and ruled out in the 1980s.","Yukteswar's precession figure of about 24,000 years conflicts with the modern value of about 25,772 years. The 24,000-year cycle is a rounded or symbolic number and is not an observed period.","A companion on a 24,000-year orbit would produce gravitational effects on the solar system and on the Sun's motion that are not observed. The Sun's galactic orbit is about 225–250 million years, not 24,000.","Comparative mythology: the cross-cultural parallels are selected and interpreted to fit the thesis, which is a known pattern in alternative-history work. Scholars of Sanskrit and Indology (for example Ludo Rocher, Wendy Doniger, and others writing on Indian chronology) do not support astronomical readings of yuga durations.","Yuga durations in the classical texts (Manusmriti, Mahabharata, Vishnu Purana) are conventional and tied to the 'divine years' system, so the 24,000-year figure is a later reinterpretation rather than an ancient measurement.","The thesis is not published in peer-reviewed astronomy or Indology journals. BRI materials are sold as courses and books, which weakens any independent check.","Dates for the Kali Yuga boundaries differ across sources, so the 'current position' claim is not testable as stated."],"plausibility":1,"plausibilityReason":"The companion star is not observed and is inconsistent with known solar-system dynamics and survey data. The precession figure of 24,000 years does not match the measured precession period, and the yuga durations are not astronomically grounded. The thesis is a reinterpretation of religious texts combined with speculative astronomy, and it has no testable prediction that has held up. Plausibility is very low.","references":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Walter_Cruttenden","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sri_Yukteswar_Giri","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Holy_Science","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nemesis_(hypothetical_star)","Walter Cruttenden, Lost Star of Myth and Time (2006)","Sri Yukteswar Giri, The Holy Science (1894)"]},{"id":"georgel","title":"French esoteric cycle chronologies (Georgel and the Guenonian/Traditionalist milieu)","category":"Modern spiritual/esoteric","proponents":["Gaston Georgel (French esoteric writer; biographical and bibliographic details not verified in this run)","Rene Guenon (Traditionalist school, influential on French esoteric yuga readings)","Jean Sylvain Bailly (18th-century precursor who argued for very ancient Indian astronomical epochs)"],"primarySources":["Rene Guenon, La Crise du monde moderne (1927)","Rene Guenon, Le Regne de la quantite et les signes des temps (1945)","Jean Sylvain Bailly, Traite de l'astronomie indienne et orientale (1787)","Gaston Georgel works on cycles and chronology: titles and dates NOT verified in this run; treat as unconfirmed"],"period":"Bailly 1787 as precursor; Guenon-school readings 1920s-1950s; Georgel's specific contribution unverified (likely mid-20th century)","totalCycleYears":"4,320,000 (standard Mahayuga, adopted in the Traditionalist reading; Georgel's own total not verified)","durations":{"satya":"1,728,000 years (4800 divine years)","treta":"1,296,000 years (3600 divine years)","dvapara":"864,000 years (2400 divine years)","kali":"432,000 years (1200 divine years)"},"kaliStart":"3102 BCE (conventional epoch in Indian astronomical tradition, reused by Traditionalist writers)","kaliEnd":"About 428,898 CE if the full 432,000-year Kali is taken literally; some esoteric readers instead place the transition within the 20th-21st centuries, which is a different interpretation not confirmed for Georgel","currentPosition":"In 2026, the conventional Kali Yuga reckoning places us roughly 5,128 years into the 432,000-year Kali. Traditionalist writers stress that Kali is a period of spiritual decline and \"quantity\" dominance rather than a precise calendar event.","summary":"This entry covers the French esoteric strand of yuga chronology, which mostly adopts the standard Indian 4,320,000-year Mahayuga and its 3102 BCE Kali Yuga epoch, then reads them through a Traditionalist or occult lens. Rene Guenon is the best-documented figure in this milieu and treats the yugas as qualitative stages of spiritual decline rather than as precise historical forecasts. Gaston Georgel is named in the assignment, but I could not verify his specific works, dates, or any novel chronology he proposed, so I am not attributing any particular numbers to him. The main claims in this milieu are that the Kali Yuga is an intrinsically degenerate age, that its length is real, and that modern materialism marks its advanced phase. The approach is largely interpretive and non-falsifiable.","keyClaims":["The yugas follow the standard 4:3:2:1 ratio with a total of 4,320,000 years.","The Kali Yuga began in 3102 BCE, a date inherited from Indian astronomical tradition.","Modern materialism and the dominance of quantity are signs of Kali Yuga's advanced stage (Guenon).","Yuga decline is a spiritual and cosmic process, not solely a calendrical one."],"evidence":["The standard yuga durations and the 3102 BCE epoch are documented in classical Indian astronomical and Puranic texts (e.g., Aryabhata's astronomical tradition, Surya Siddhanta), which these French writers draw on.","Guenon's critiques of modernity in La Crise du monde moderne and Le Regne de la quantite are real and widely cited in Traditionalist circles.","Bailly's 1787 work did argue for very ancient Indian astronomical epochs, a historical precedent for Western interest in yuga chronology."],"criticisms":["The 3102 BCE epoch and 432,000-year Kali are not historical dates; they are part of a mythic-astronomical scheme, and Western scholarship generally regards the 4.32-million-year total as symbolic or astronomically constructed.","Bailly's claim of very ancient astronomical knowledge was rejected by later scholars, and Indologists (e.g., Pingree and others) have shown the epochs date to a later period in the first millennium CE.","Traditionalist writing is unfalsifiable: decline can always be read into any era, so the framework cannot be tested against evidence.","Georgel's specific chronology could not be verified in this run, so any claim about his numbers would be unsupported."],"plausibility":2,"plausibilityReason":"The yuga durations are mythic-astronomical constructs with no empirical grounding for real-world dates, and the 3102 BCE epoch is a late astronomical convention rather than a recorded event. Guenon's cultural critique can be discussed on its own terms, but as a chronology it does not survive scrutiny. Georgel's own claims are unverified, so this rating reflects the Traditionalist milieu in general.","references":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yuga (general reference; verify before citing)","Rene Guenon, La Crise du monde moderne (1927)","Rene Guenon, Le Regne de la quantite et les signes des temps (1945)","Jean Sylvain Bailly, Traite de l'astronomie indienne et orientale (1787)","David Pingree, work on Indian astronomical chronology (verify exact titles before citing)"]},{"id":"kali-end-predictions","title":"Survey of predicted end dates for Kali Yuga (2012, 2025, 2442, 3102+5000, 428,899 CE and others)","category":"Traditional scriptural","proponents":["Puranic tradition (anonymous compilers of the Vishnu, Bhagavata and other Puranas)","Aryabhata (Aryabhatiya, 499 CE) and Surya Siddhanta authors, who fixed the Kali epoch at 3102 BCE","Sri Yukteswar Giri (The Holy Science, 1894), who proposed a 24,000-year cycle with a shorter Kali phase","Swami Vivekananda (lectures of the 1890s, who linked the 5,000-year mark of Kali to a new age)","Modern popular and fringe writers who tied Kali's end to 2012 (often via Maya Long Count confusion) or 2025","Some online and devotional writers who give 2442 CE; I could not verify a primary source for this date"],"primarySources":["Vishnu Purana (c. 300-900 CE compilation; Wilson translation 1840), yuga durations and Kali's start with Krishna's death","Bhagavata Purana (c. 800-1000 CE), Kalki and end-of-Kali passages","Surya Siddhanta (c. 400-500 CE, revised later), Kali epoch at midnight 3102 BCE","Aryabhatiya (499 CE), Kali epoch 3102 BCE and the statement that 3600 Kali years had elapsed at 499 CE","Sri Yukteswar Giri, The Holy Science (1894)","Vivekananda, collected lectures (1890s)"],"period":"Epoch of 3102 BCE fixed by c. 5th-6th century CE astronomy (Aryabhata); end-date predictions range from the 1890s (Yukteswar, Vivekananda) to the 2010s-2020s (2012 and 2025 popular claims). The 428,899 CE end is a direct arithmetic consequence of the Puranic 432,000-year length and needs no separate prediction.","totalCycleYears":"Puranic mahayuga is 4,320,000 years (12,000 divine years x 360). Yukteswar's scheme is 24,000 years (two 12,000-year halves). Kali alone is 432,000 years in the Puranic scheme and 1,200 years in Yukteswar's.","durations":{"satya":"Puranic: 1,728,000 human years (4,800 divine x 360). Yukteswar: 4,800 years","treta":"Puranic: 1,296,000 human years (3,600 divine x 360). Yukteswar: 3,600 years","dvapara":"Puranic: 864,000 human years (2,400 divine x 360). Yukteswar: 2,400 years","kali":"Puranic: 432,000 years (1,200 divine x 360). Yukteswar: 1,200 years"},"kaliStart":"3102 BCE (traditional epoch, midnight at Ujjain, Feb 17/18). Yukteswar's scheme places a Kali phase of 1,200 years in the early CE centuries, so his start date differs.","kaliEnd":"Puranic: 428,899 CE (3102 BCE + 432,000 years, adjusted for no year zero). Competing claims: 2012 CE, 2025 CE, 2442 CE, 1898-1899 CE (5,000-year mark, a symbolic marker, not an end), and Yukteswar's 1600s-1700s CE estimate for the end of his dark phase. The predictions contradict one another and cannot all be right.","currentPosition":"As of 2026, under the standard Puranic reckoning we are about 5,128 years into Kali (Kali Samvat 5127), roughly 1.2 percent of its 432,000-year span. The 2012 and 2025 end dates have already passed with no observable transition. Under Yukteswar's scheme, humanity is in the ascending phase of Dvapara, which he dated to have begun in the late 1600s CE. Nothing observable in 2026 is tied to a yuga boundary in any of these schemes.","summary":"Traditional Hindu chronology gives Kali Yuga a 432,000-year length starting 3102 BCE, which puts its end at 428,899 CE. Other figures appear in popular and fringe writing: 2012 (often linked to the Maya Long Count, which is a Mesoamerican calendar and not a Hindu source), 2025, 2442, and the 5,000-year mark at 1898-1899 CE, which Vivekananda and others treated as a turning point. Sri Yukteswar offered a different 24,000-year cycle with a 1,200-year Kali phase, placing the end of its dark phase in the 1600s-1700s CE. The end-date predictions are mutually inconsistent and rest on a symbolic or astronomical-epoch framework rather than on any observable event, so none can be checked by observation.","keyClaims":["The Kali epoch of 3102 BCE was computed by Indian astronomers (Aryabhata and the Siddhantas), not observed; it fixes the start of the 432,000-year Kali, which therefore ends at 428,899 CE.","The 2012 end date comes from a non-Hindu source (the Maya Long Count); it has no basis in Puranic chronology.","The 5,000-year mark of Kali (1898-1899 CE) was treated by Vivekananda and later writers as the start of a spiritual revival, not as an end of the age.","Yukteswar's 24,000-year scheme uses 1,200-year Kali and 2,400-year Dvapara phases, so its dating differs from the Puranic figures by a factor of 360.","The 2025 and 2442 end dates cannot be traced to a canonical text or to a documented scholar in the sources I could check."],"evidence":["Puranic and Siddhantic texts give the 4:3:2:1 yuga ratio and the 12,000 divine-year mahayuga; these are internal textual claims.","Aryabhatiya (499 CE) states a Kali epoch in the 3102 BCE frame; the Surya Siddhanta gives a similar epoch.","Yukteswar's The Holy Science (1894) states a 24,000-year cycle with 1,200-year Kali.","The 2012 claim has a documented origin in Mesoamerican calendar debates, not Hindu texts.","Epoch arithmetic: 3102 BCE + 432,000 years gives 428,899 CE with no year zero."],"criticisms":["No observable event marks a yuga boundary, so end-date predictions cannot be tested; the 2012 and 2025 predictions have already failed without any event.","The 432,000-year figure is a schematic number within a cosmic cycle (Brahma days of 1,000 mahayugas), not a measured duration; the Puranas themselves describe it as a numerological frame.","The 3102 BCE epoch is a retrospective astronomical convention; the Mahabharata-era dating of Krishna's death used to justify it has no independent historical support (academic Mahabharata dating places composition c. 400 BCE-400 CE).","Yukteswar's 24,000-year cycle does not match the precession period (about 25,700-26,000 years), and his calculations are not supported by modern astronomy or by mainstream Indology.","The 2012 claim rests on a misreading of the Maya Long Count and has no Hindu textual basis; it is popularization, not Hindu chronology.","The 2442 and 2025 dates appear in popular and online sources; I could not confirm a primary source, so they should be treated as unverified.","I did not run live web searches for this entry; the dates and attributions come from knowledge and should be spot-checked against primary texts before publication."],"plausibility":1,"plausibilityReason":"As literal chronology, none of the end-date predictions is supported. The 3102 BCE epoch is a real historical convention used by Indian astronomers, which gives it some weight as a calendar fact. The 432,000-year length and any specific end date are not testable, and the 2012 and 2025 predictions have already passed without any event. Among the options, the 428,899 CE figure is the most literal reading of the texts, but it is not a prediction that can be verified.","references":["Vishnu Purana, H.H. Wilson translation (1840), yuga and Kali passages","Aryabhatiya (499 CE), Kalakriya and Gola chapters","Surya Siddhanta, Kali epoch passages","Sri Yukteswar Giri, The Holy Science (1894)","Swami Vivekananda, lectures of the 1890s (Complete Works)","Mesoamerican Long Count background for the 2012 claim (academic treatments of the 2012 phenomenon)"]},{"id":"indologists","title":"Indologist historical-development view of the yuga doctrine","category":"Academic/historical","proponents":["F. E. Pargiter","P. V. Kane","Willibald Kirfel","E. W. Hopkins","Hermann Jacobi (chronology of the era systems)"],"primarySources":["Vedic dice imagery (krta, treta, dvapara, kali as throw names), Taittiriya Samhita and Aitareya Brahmana, roughly 1st millennium BCE, with no temporal meaning","Mahabharata (Vana, Sabha and other parvas), roughly 4th c. BCE to 4th c. CE, first explicit four-yuga scheme with human-year durations","Manusmriti 1.69-86, roughly 2nd c. BCE to 2nd c. CE, divine-year multiplier of 12,000 and 4800/3600/2400/1200 divine years","Puranas (Vishnu, Matsya, Bhagavata and others), roughly 4th to 12th c. CE, dynastic lists tied to Kali-age prophecy","Aryabhatiya (499 CE) and Surya Siddhanta, which fix the 3102 BCE Kali epoch and the 4,320,000-year mahayuga for astronomical use"],"period":"Late 19th to mid 20th century Indological scholarship (Hopkins 1901, Kirfel 1920, Pargiter 1913/1922, Kane 1953-1962), building on earlier Western translations of the Mahabharata and Manusmriti","totalCycleYears":"4,320,000 years (mahayuga, 12,000 divine years); divine years are a multiplier of 360 human years","durations":{"satya":"1,728,000 years (4,800 divine years)","treta":"1,296,000 years (3,600 divine years)","dvapara":"864,000 years (2,400 divine years)","kali":"432,000 years (1,200 divine years)"},"kaliStart":"3102 BCE (astronomical epoch of Aryabhata and later Siddhantic tradition; no pre-medieval text in this source-critical view dates an event to this year)","kaliEnd":"About 428,898 CE, if the 3102 BCE epoch and 432,000-year duration are taken literally","currentPosition":"Within the Kali yuga. In 2026 CE the Kali year is about 5127-5128, so roughly 5,128 years have elapsed and about 426,872 years remain. Indologists regard the count as an astronomical convention, not a measured interval.","summary":"Indologists such as Hopkins, Kirfel, Pargiter and Kane traced the yuga doctrine as a historical development rather than a single revealed chronology. The earliest layer is the Vedic dice metaphor, where krta, treta, dvapara and kali name winning and losing throws with no temporal sense. The four-age scheme with human-scale numbers appears in the Mahabharata and Manusmriti, and the 4,320,000-year mahayuga and fixed Kali epoch come later from astronomical and Puranic systems. On this reading, the yuga numbers are schematic and theological, and the Kali start of 3102 BCE was fixed by astronomers rather than remembered from history. Pargiter used Puranic dynastic lists as historical sources, while Kane and Kirfel treated the cosmological numbers as symbolic.","keyClaims":["The four-yuga names began as Vedic dice-throw terms with no chronological meaning (Hopkins and others).","The Mahabharata and Manusmriti give the first explicit cycle of four ages with decreasing durations, and the 4:3:2:1 ratio is a later formalization.","The 4,320,000-year mahayuga and 3102 BCE Kali epoch are astronomical constructs from the Aryabhata/Siddhanta period, not events remembered from the Vedic or epic period.","Kirfel's Kosmographie der Inder (1920) shows the yuga and world-age material as a cosmographic system that was integrated gradually into the Puranas.","Pargiter used Puranic dynastic lists (Ancient Indian Historical Tradition, 1922) as partially historical, but did not accept the yuga arithmetic as literal chronology.","Kane (History of Dharmasastra, vols. IV-V) discussed how yuga-based ideas about decline shaped dharma texts, while treating the numbers as theoretical."],"evidence":["Textual dating of the Vedic dice metaphor and the Mahabharata/Manusmriti yuga passages, which show the scheme developing over roughly a millennium.","Absence of any pre-Aryabhata source that fixes the Kali epoch at 3102 BCE.","The divine-year multiplier (360 x 12,000 = 4,320,000) appears as a mathematical construct in Manusmriti and later Siddhantic texts.","Pargiter's own analysis of Puranic dynasties, which found inconsistent and generation-based genealogies that could not be turned into exact yuga-length spans.","Comparison with other Indo-European and Near Eastern world-age schemes, which were also developed later and with symbolic numbers."],"criticisms":["Pargiter's dynastic chronology has been criticized for assuming Puranic lists are more historically reliable than they are, and his reconstructions have not held up well.","Kane's work is largely a compilation and descriptive survey; it does not offer a strong argument about the causes of the doctrine's development.","Some later scholars argue the dice-metaphor origin is too neat, and that the decline idea may have independent roots in ethical or cyclical thought.","The split between Vedic, epic and Puranic layers is sometimes applied rigidly, though the texts were revised continuously.","Many of these works predate or ignore epigraphic and archaeological evidence for Indian chronology, so their dating of the Puranic material is provisional.","Kirfel's cosmographic reconstruction relies on a limited set of manuscripts and has been partly superseded by later editions and studies.","The Mahabharata's internal chronology is layered and not fully reconcilable with a single yuga timeline, so claims that it encodes a fixed scheme are weaker than they appear."],"plausibility":2,"plausibilityReason":"As a historical account of how the doctrine developed, this view is well supported by textual dating and rates high. As a guide to real-world history, it gives a low score: the Indologist consensus treats the 4.32-million-year numbers and the 3102 BCE Kali epoch as astronomical and theological conventions, and no archaeological or documentary evidence supports a literal Kali start. The rating here reflects the claim that yuga durations are literal history, which the view itself rejects.","references":["Hopkins, E. W. (1901). The Great Epic of India. Charles Scribner's Sons.","Kirfel, W. (1920). Die Kosmographie der Inder. Bonn: K. Schroeder.","Pargiter, F. E. (1913). The Purana Text of the Dynasties of the Kali Age. Oxford University Press.","Pargiter, F. E. (1922). Ancient Indian Historical Tradition. Oxford University Press.","Kane, P. V. (1953-1962). History of Dharmasastra, Vols. IV-V. Bhandarkar Oriental Research Institute.","Jacobi, H. (various), papers on Indian chronology and era systems.","Note: these citations are from memory and were not verified online during this run; check page-level details before citing."]},{"id":"pingree-hellenistic","title":"Pingree and Hellenistic/Babylonian Influence on Yuga Numbers and the 3102 BCE Epoch","category":"Academic/historical","proponents":["David Pingree (1933-2005), Brown University historian of exact sciences in India","Otto Neugebauer (Babylonian and Greek astronomy background, The Exact Sciences in Antiquity, 1951)","B.L. van der Waerden (argued Mesopotamian and Greek roots of Indian astronomy)","Kim Plofker (critical but broadly accepts Greek influence on specific Indian astronomical techniques)"],"primarySources":["Surya Siddhanta (texts dated variously, c. 4th-5th century CE in its current form)","Aryabhatiya of Aryabhata (499 CE), which gives the Kali epoch and a 4,320,000-year mahayuga","Yavanajataka of Sphujidhvaja (c. 269/270 CE), a Sanskrit verse translation of Greek horoscopic astrology, edited by Pingree (Harvard Oriental Series 48, 1978)","Romaka and Paulisa Siddhantas, as summarized in Varahamihira's Panchasiddhantika (c. 505 CE), showing Greek-derived astronomical systems","Berossus, Babyloniaca (c. 280 BCE, lost), preserved via Syncellus, reporting Babylonian antediluvian reigns totaling 120 sars = 432,000 years","Mahabharata and Manusmriti, which give yuga durations in divine years (Manusmriti 1.69-71 gives 12,000 divine years for the four yugas)"],"period":"Core thesis developed by Pingree in the 1970s-1990s (notably his 1973 article 'The Mesopotamian Origin of Early Indian Mathematical Astronomy', Journal for the History of Astronomy 4). The Greek-to-Sanskrit transmission it relies on dates to the 3rd-5th centuries CE. The 3102 BCE epoch itself is traditionally tied to the Kali yuga as recorded by Aryabhata and later Siddhanta texts.","totalCycleYears":"4,320,000 years (mahayuga, 12,000 divine years x 360). The Babylonian parallel is 120 sars x 3600 = 432,000 years, which is a sexagesimal-compatible figure.","durations":{"satya":"1,728,000 years (4,800 divine years)","treta":"1,296,000 years (3,600 divine years)","dvapara":"864,000 years (2,400 divine years)","kali":"432,000 years (1,200 divine years)"},"kaliStart":"3102 BCE (traditionally midnight of Feb 17/18, 3102 BCE, proleptic Julian). This is a computed epoch, not an observed event.","kaliEnd":"c. 428,899 CE (432,000 years from the start, counting 3101 years to 1 CE)","currentPosition":"In 2026 CE we are about 5,127 years into the 432,000-year Kali yuga, roughly 1.2% of it. Under this view the Kali yuga is still in its early phase, but the 3102 BCE start is a theoretical zero-epoch, not a historical date.","summary":"Pingree's thesis is that the Indian Siddhanta astronomical framework, including the mahayuga arithmetic and the zero-epoch method that anchors the Kali yuga at 3102 BCE, draws heavily on Babylonian mathematical astronomy and Hellenistic (Greek) astronomy transmitted through the Greek-to-Sanskrit channel of the early centuries CE. The 432,000 figure is sexagesimal-friendly (120 x 3600) and matches the Berossus reign total, which Pingree and others read as a Babylonian parallel. The 3102 BCE epoch is treated as a backward-computed mean-conjunction zero point, similar in logic to Ptolemy's Nabonassar era (747 BCE), rather than a memory of a real event. Pingree's position is mainly about the origin of the computational apparatus, not the historical truth of the yuga timeline. Note: this entry was compiled from knowledge without live web verification in this run.","keyClaims":["The Indian siddhanta mean-motion and epoch methods were built on Mesopotamian and Hellenistic models, transmitted through Greek texts c. 2nd century BCE to 5th century CE.","The Yavanajataka (269/270 CE) is direct evidence of Greek horoscopic astrology and technical vocabulary entering Sanskrit astronomy.","The 432,000-year Kali yuga and 4,320,000-year mahayuga match Babylonian sexagesimal number patterns, and the Berossus reign total of 432,000 years is a parallel.","The 3102 BCE epoch is a zero-epoch from which planetary mean positions are computed, and its form resembles Greek-era epoch practice (e.g., Nabonassar era).","The Indian zodiac of 12 signs and horoscopic conventions were imported from Hellenistic astrology (Pingree and others read 'hora' and 'yavana' as Greek loanwords)."],"evidence":["Pingree's edition of the Yavanajataka (Harvard Oriental Series 48, 1978) shows Greek astrological content in Sanskrit form.","Romaka and Paulisa Siddhantas (summarized by Varahamihira, c. 505 CE) are explicitly Greek-influenced siddhantas, with Paulisa attributed to Paul of Alexandria's work.","Aryabhata's Aryabhatiya (499 CE) uses a Kali epoch and a mahayuga of 4,320,000 years, so the epoch and cycle architecture is securely attested by the late 5th century.","Berossus' Babyloniaca (as preserved in Syncellus) reports 432,000 years for antediluvian kings, a sexagesimal figure matching the Kali yuga duration.","Neugebauer and Pingree document that Babylonian System B and Greek mean-motion methods share the zero-epoch and period-relation logic found in Siddhanta astronomy.","Pingree 1973, 'The Mesopotamian Origin of Early Indian Mathematical Astronomy', Journal for the History of Astronomy 4, pp. 1-12, argues for Babylonian and Greek origins of early Indian astronomical methods."],"criticisms":["Pingree is criticized for overstating the Babylonian origin of the 432,000 figure. Sexagesimal compatibility does not prove derivation, and the Indian decimal place-value system and Vedanga Jyotisa (c. 1200-400 BCE, with its 5-year yuga) show indigenous elements.","Berossus is cited via later Christian chronographers (Syncellus), so the 432,000 parallel depends on a secondary source of uncertain reliability.","Critics argue Greek influence is clearest for specific techniques (sine tables are disputed, epicycle models, horoscopy, zodiac terms) and not for the yuga cosmology itself, which is largely a Puranic and Indian philosophical construction.","Some scholars (including Plofker in Mathematics in India, 2009) argue the borrowing picture is more mixed and bidirectional than Pingree's model suggests, and that Indian authors adapted rather than copied.","The 3102 BCE epoch is a backward-computed zero point. Treating it as a Hellenistic-style epoch does not establish its date, and no independent evidence links 3102 BCE to any historical event.","The question of whether the yuga arithmetic came from Mesopotamia or was independently developed is still debated, and the thesis rests heavily on Pingree's interpretive judgment."],"plausibility":4,"plausibilityReason":"The claim that Greek and Babylonian astronomy influenced Indian Siddhanta astronomy is well supported for specific techniques and vocabulary (Yavanajataka, Romaka/Paulisa). The claim that the yuga cosmology and 3102 BCE epoch are Babylonian or Hellenistic in origin is much weaker, rests on numerical coincidence and interpretation, and does not support any real-world dating of Kali yuga. The 3102 BCE start is a computed epoch and not an observed date, so the timeline claims that follow from it do not survive scrutiny.","references":["Pingree, D. (1973). 'The Mesopotamian Origin of Early Indian Mathematical Astronomy.' Journal for the History of Astronomy 4: 1-12.","Pingree, D. (1978). The Yavanajataka of Sphujidhvaja. Harvard Oriental Series 48. Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press.","Pingree, D. (1981). Jyotihsastra: Astral and Mathematical Literature. Handbuch der Orientalistik. Wiesbaden: Harrassowitz.","Neugebauer, O. (1951). The Exact Sciences in Antiquity. Princeton/Brown University Press.","Plofker, K. (2009). Mathematics in India. Princeton University Press.","Berossus, Babyloniaca, as quoted by George Syncellus (Chronographia), fragments collected in Verbrugghe & Wickersham (1996), Berossos and Manetho, University of Michigan Press."]},{"id":"bailly-bentley-burgess","title":"Bailly vs. Bentley/Burgess/Thibaut: observed or back-calculated 3102 BCE","category":"Academic/historical","proponents":["Jean-Sylvain Bailly (antiquity side, 1787)","John Bentley (late-date side, 1790)","Edward Burgess (translator, 1860; treated the epoch as a computed construct)","George Thibaut (1875-1899; epoch as theoretical/computed, Hellenistic-influence framing)","David Pingree (later systematic confirmation via Census of the Exact Sciences in Sanskrit)"],"primarySources":["Surya Siddhanta (text in its present form usually dated to roughly 4th-5th c. CE, with later revisions; its Kali epoch is 3102 BCE)","Aryabhatiya of Aryabhata (499 CE), which also uses a Kali epoch at 3102 BCE","Bailly, Traite de l'astronomie indienne et orientale (1787)","Bentley, A Historical View of the Hindu Astronomy (1790)","Burgess (ed. Whitney), translation of the Surya Siddhanta, Journal of the American Oriental Society (1860)","Thibaut, Astronomie, Astrologie und Mathematik (in Buhler's Grundriss, 1899); earlier Thibaut papers on Indian astronomy (1875 onward)","Pingree, Census of the Exact Sciences in Sanskrit (1970-1994)"],"period":"Debate roughly 1787-1900 (Bailly 1787 through Thibaut's late-19th-century work); later confirmed by Pingree and others in the 20th century.","totalCycleYears":"4,320,000 years (one Mahayuga: 1,728,000 + 1,296,000 + 864,000 + 432,000)","durations":{"satya":"1,728,000 years (traditional)","treta":"1,296,000 years (traditional)","dvapara":"864,000 years (traditional)","kali":"432,000 years (traditional)"},"kaliStart":"3102 BCE (traditional epoch, midnight at Ujjain, roughly 18 Feb 3102 BCE in the proleptic Julian calendar, as given in the Surya Siddhanta tradition)","kaliEnd":"Approximately 428 CE (432,000 years after the epoch, under the traditional reckoning)","currentPosition":"Kali Yuga is traditionally ongoing. In 2026 CE about 5,127 to 5,128 years have elapsed since the 3102 BCE epoch, depending on counting convention.","summary":"The debate asked whether the Kali epoch of 3102 BCE records a real astronomical event that was observed, or whether it is a number produced by running planetary mean motions backward from a later era. Bailly (1787) read the epoch as evidence of very ancient, sophisticated Indian observation, and even tied it to speculative lost-civilization ideas. Bentley (1790) argued that the Indian astronomical tables were comparatively recent and that the epoch was an artificial construct. Burgess and Thibaut, working from the Sanskrit texts in the 19th century, treated the epoch as a mathematical starting point: a conventional zero date from which the tables' mean longitudes are counted, not a recorded sky event. Later scholarship, notably Pingree, supports the computed-epoch reading. Modern calculation shows no sky configuration at 3102 BCE matching the claimed planetary alignment.","keyClaims":["Bailly: the 3102 BCE epoch and associated planetary configuration indicate very early, observation-based Indian astronomy.","Bentley: the Indian astronomical system is late, and the 3102 BCE epoch is a retrospective construction.","Burgess and Thibaut: the epoch is a computed zero point of the mean-motion system, not a directly observed conjunction.","Modern consensus: the epoch is a theoretical reference date; the claimed all-planets alignment at that date is not supported by actual sky positions.","The Surya Siddhanta and Aryabhata both use the 3102 BCE Kali epoch, which shows the convention was established by the 5th century CE at the latest."],"evidence":["Internal consistency of the Surya Siddhanta and Aryabhatiya mean-motion parameters with a 3102 BCE zero point, which suggests a mathematically defined epoch.","Astronomical software reconstructions (e.g., planetarium back-calculations) show no alignment of the classical planets near 3102 BCE that would match a claimed all-planets conjunction.","Textual analysis by Burgess and Thibaut showing the epoch functions as a reference date in the computing scheme.","Pingree's census of Sanskrit exact sciences, which places the epoch within a family of computed epochs in Indian siddhanta literature."],"criticisms":["Bailly's antiquity argument relied on the naive assumption that a zero epoch must record an observation; he also lacked reliable knowledge of the tables' construction and speculative lost-civilization framing undermines his reasoning.","Bentley's late-date argument was sometimes overstated; the presence of an epoch does not by itself prove late composition, and his dating of the tables has been revised by later scholarship.","Burgess and Thibaut worked with limited manuscript evidence and early editions, so some of their specific historical inferences have been refined or corrected.","The 'computed epoch' reading does not automatically rule out earlier observational traditions in India; it only shows the 3102 BCE number itself is a construct.","Some modern popular accounts still repeat Bailly's antiquity claims without engaging the computed-epoch evidence.","Traditional yuga durations (4.32 million years per mahayuga) are themselves a theological and cosmological scheme, not something the astronomical debate can confirm or refute."],"plausibility":2,"plausibilityReason":"As a claim that 3102 BCE marks a real, observed sky event, the evidence is weak: no known record or calculation supports it, and the epoch is best explained as a computed reference point. Scholarship from Burgess through Pingree points to a constructed epoch, so the plausibility of the observed-event reading is low. It is not zero, because the epoch convention is real and used in historical astronomy.","references":["Bailly, J.-S. (1787). Traite de l'astronomie indienne et orientale. Paris.","Bentley, J. (1790). A Historical View of the Hindu Astronomy. London.","Burgess, E. (1860). Translation of the Surya Siddhanta, ed. W. D. Whitney, Journal of the American Oriental Society 6.","Thibaut, G. (1899). Astronomie, Astrologie und Mathematik. In Grundriss der Indo-Arischen Philologie und Altertumskunde, vol. III.","Pingree, D. (1970-1994). Census of the Exact Sciences in Sanskrit. American Philosophical Society.","Sewell, R. and Dikshit, S. B. (1896). The Indian Calendar."]},{"id":"vedic-absence","title":"Vedic absence: yugas as dice throws, not cosmic ages","category":"Academic/historical","proponents":["E. W. Hopkins (dice-origin of the yuga names, early 20th century)","P. V. Kane (History of Dharmaśāstra, cautious chronology of yuga doctrine)","Mainstream Indological consensus (Vedic scholarship generally)"],"primarySources":["Rigveda (composition c. 1500-1200 BCE); the word yuga occurs but means yoke, generation or epoch, not a cosmic age","Taittiriya Samhita (c. 1000-800 BCE); dice-related usage of krta and kali discussed by scholars","Aitareya Brahmana 7.15 (c. 800-500 BCE); the Caraiveti passage equating kali, dvapara, treta and krta with sleeping, rising, standing and moving","Vedanga Jyotisha (c. 1st millennium BCE); yuga as a five-year astronomical cycle, not the four-age scheme","Manusmriti 1.81-86 (c. 200 BCE-200 CE); first full statement of the four-yuga durations"],"period":"Vedic period (c. 1500-500 BCE) for the textual sources; the cosmic four-age scheme appears in the epics and Puranas (c. 300 BCE-500 CE), with dice-origin hypotheses arising in modern scholarship (late 19th-early 20th century)","totalCycleYears":"N/A. No Vedic text gives a total cycle length. The 4,320,000-year mahayuga figure appears only in later texts such as Manusmriti and the Puranas.","durations":{"satya":"N/A in Vedic texts (no durations given)","treta":"N/A in Vedic texts","dvapara":"N/A in Vedic texts","kali":"N/A in Vedic texts"},"kaliStart":"N/A for this angle. The Vedic material gives no start date. The traditional 3102 BCE date is a later astronomical-computational construction and is not part of this analysis.","kaliEnd":"N/A for this angle. The Vedic material gives no end date.","currentPosition":"N/A. The question of where we stand in a yuga cycle cannot be answered from Vedic sources, which do not describe a cycle.","summary":"The Rigveda uses yuga in the sense of yoke, a generation, or an epoch of time, and it does not describe a repeating sequence of four cosmic ages. The four-yuga scheme with named ages and declining virtue is absent from the Vedic samhitas and appears in the Mahabharata, Manusmriti and Puranas. The most-cited Vedic hint is Aitareya Brahmana 7.15, which links four states of a person (sleeping, rising, standing, moving) to kali, dvapara, treta and krta. Those four names are also the names of dice throws, with krta as the winning four and kali as the losing one. Many scholars read the passage as evidence that the age names were taken from gambling terminology and then given cosmic meaning. The Brahmana passage is a moral metaphor about effort and does not give durations or a timeline.","keyClaims":["The Rigveda contains no clear doctrine of four cosmic yugas with declining virtue and fixed durations.","Aitareya Brahmana 7.15 (the Caraiveti passage) uses the names kali, dvapara, treta and krta as metaphors for stages of human effort, not as cosmic ages.","The four names match the usual scoring of a four-sided dice throw: krta (4, winning), treta (3), dvapara (2) and kali (1, losing).","Vedanga Jyotisha uses yuga for a five-year astronomical cycle, which shows the word had a calendrical meaning before the four-age scheme.","The cosmic four-yuga scheme with durations of 4,800, 3,600, 2,400 and 1,200 divine years appears in Manusmriti 1.81-86 and later Puranic texts."],"evidence":["Textual absence: the four-age scheme is not found in the Rigveda or the principal Vedic samhitas, which is widely accepted in Indology.","Aitareya Brahmana 7.15 uses the four names in a context of effort and progress, with a dice-like sequence (kali as the losing throw, krta as the winning throw).","Gambling vocabulary in Vedic texts, including the Rigvedic gambling hymn (RV 10.34), shows that dice were known and that their terms could be used metaphorically.","Vedanga Jyotisha uses yuga as a five-year calendrical cycle, which is an attested non-cosmic use from the Vedic period.","The chronology of the four-age doctrine, placed after the Vedic period in the epics and Manusmriti, fits a development from dice terms to cosmic ages."],"criticisms":["The dice-origin hypothesis is an inference from shared vocabulary. The Brahmana passage is a moral metaphor, and the link to cosmic ages could have come later without any dice origin.","Attestation of the dice meaning is debated. Some scholars doubt that kali and krta were fixed dice-throw names in the Vedic period, and the Rigvedic occurrences of kali in gambling contexts are contested.","Absence in the Rigveda is an argument from silence. Lost oral material could have contained the doctrine, though this is speculative.","Some traditional commentators (e.g., Sayana) read the Brahmana passage as a teaching about cosmic ages, not as a dice metaphor, which shows the interpretation is not uncontested even within Indian tradition.","Claims that the Vedas encode the exact yuga durations or the current age are unsupported by the Vedic texts themselves."],"plausibility":7,"plausibilityReason":"The narrower claim that the Vedas lack the four-age cosmology, and that the names plausibly derive from dice terminology, is well supported and widely accepted by Indologists. The rating is 7, not higher, because the dice-origin link rests on an interpretation of one metaphorical passage plus shared vocabulary, and the attestation is debated. The rating covers only the textual-origin claims, not any claim about real-world dates, which this angle does not address.","references":["Aitareya Brahmana 7.15 (Caraiveti passage); translation by A. B. Keith, Rigveda Brahmanas, Harvard Oriental Series, 1920","Manusmriti 1.81-86 (yuga durations)","Vedanga Jyotisha (Lagadha), yuga as five-year cycle","P. V. Kane, History of Dharmaśāstra (multi-volume, 1930-1962), chapters on yuga doctrine","E. W. Hopkins, writings on the dice origin of the yuga names (c. 1898-1901); exact citations should be verified before use","Note: this entry was written from prior knowledge without live web verification. Scholarly citations and the RV 8.47 and 10.34 attestations should be checked against primary editions before publication."]},{"id":"vedanga-jyotisha","title":"Vedanga Jyotisha 5-year yuga (calendrical \"yuga\" as a short lunisolar cycle)","category":"Academic/historical","proponents":["Lagadha (traditional author of the Vedanga Jyotisha)","T.S. Kuppanna Sastry and K.V. Sarma (edition and translation, 1985)","S.B. Dikshit (early chronology, 19th-century scholarship)","David Pingree (critical dating and history of Indian astronomy)","Subhash Kak (argues for an early, Rigvedic-era reading of Vedic astronomy)"],"primarySources":["Vedanga Jyotisha (Lagadha), two recensions: Rig-Jyotisha (about 36 verses) and Yajur-Jyotisha (about 43 verses); composition date disputed, commonly placed in the 1st millennium BCE, with traditionalist claims as early as c. 1400 BCE and critical scholars favouring considerably later dates"],"period":"Text is undated in its own terms. Traditionalist and some early-scholarship dates run to c. 1400 BCE. Critical academic opinion generally favours the 1st millennium BCE. The 5-year cycle itself is an older lunisolar practice than the text. The word yuga as a period appears earlier in Vedic usage, for example as a generation or era.","totalCycleYears":"5 solar years (one yuga)","durations":{"satya":"N/A (the text has no four-age scheme)","treta":"N/A","dvapara":"N/A","kali":"N/A"},"kaliStart":"N/A. The text does not mention Kali Yuga. The 5-year yuga repeats without a moral or cosmic age structure.","kaliEnd":"N/A","currentPosition":"Not applicable as a cosmic-age claim. The 5-year cycle is a counting device, so 2026 CE falls at some position in a cycle of its own. The text gives no anchor that would tie a 5-year position to a Kali Yuga position.","summary":"The Vedanga Jyotisha, a short treatise on the calendar and timing of Vedic rituals, defines a yuga as a 5-year lunisolar cycle. In it, 5 solar years contain 62 synodic months (lunar months), 67 sidereal lunar months, 1830 civil days and 1835 sidereal days. The cycle is used to reconcile the solar year with the lunar months through intercalation, and to fix the timing of sacrifices. It is an arithmetic and observational cycle. It is not a cosmic age, and it has no link to the later four-yuga scheme of 4.32 million years. The word yuga here is best read as a period or cycle of counting, which is close to its older sense in Vedic usage.","keyClaims":["A yuga is a 5-year cycle, with the five years named samvatsara, parivatsara, idavatsara, anuvatsara and idvatsara.","Five solar years equal 62 synodic months, 67 sidereal months, 1830 savana (civil) days, and 1835 sidereal days.","The cycle serves to intercalate the lunar calendar so that the lunar months stay in step with the solar year.","The text fixes the winter solstice (uttarayana) at the beginning of the Dhanishtha nakshatra, a claim used by early chronologists to date it.","The text does not describe ages of the world, cosmic decline, or the four yugas."],"evidence":["The 5-year yuga arithmetic is internally consistent and can be checked: 5 x 366 = 1830 days, and 62 synodic months is about 1829.6 days, which matches the cycle closely.","The Dhanishtha solstice statement is the main internal anchor for dating. Its astronomical interpretation is debated, because precession and the reading of the nakshatra positions depend on the method used.","Sastry and Sarma (1985) provide the standard critical edition and translation, with commentary on the arithmetic.","The name list of the five years is consistent across the manuscripts."],"criticisms":["The dating depends on the solstice-at-Dhanishtha claim. Dikshit and traditionalist accounts use it to reach about 1400 BCE, but critical scholars question the method and the precision of the observations, and many place the text much later.","The text's values are approximate. Its year of 366 days is cruder than later calendar astronomy. The five-year cycle is a practical intercalation scheme, not a precise measurement.","Calling the 5-year cycle the origin of the four-yuga idea is a leap. The four-yuga scheme appears in later texts (Mahabharata, Manusmriti and the Puranas) and is a different system with different numbers.","Subhash Kak and others read large parts of Vedic ritual and hymn texts as encoding astronomy, but this reading is contested and not accepted by most historians of astronomy.","The text is a technical manual of about 40 verses. It is not a theory of time, so treating it as evidence for a world-age timeline overreaches its scope."],"plausibility":7,"plausibilityReason":"As a description of a calendrical cycle, the claims survive scrutiny well: the arithmetic is sound and the text's astronomy is broadly accurate for its purpose. The uncertainty is the date, which is disputed across a range of more than 1000 years. The text makes no claim about the age of the world, so it cannot be scored on the Kali Yuga timeline at all. The plausibility rating reflects the calendrical claims only.","references":["Sastry, T.S. Kuppanna and Sarma, K.V. (1985). Vedanga Jyotisha of Lagadha. Indian National Science Academy. Edition and translation of both recensions.","Pingree, David (1981). Jyotihsastra: Astral and Mathematical Literature. Otto Harrassowitz. Chapter on Vedanga Jyotisha and its dating.","Dikshit, S.B. (1896). Bharatiya Jyotish (Marathi; standard early chronology of Vedanga Jyotisha, with the 1400 BCE dating).","Kak, Subhash (1994). The Astronomical Code of the Rgveda. Munshiram Manoharlal. Argues for early Vedic astronomy; a minority view.","Note: these citations were written from memory without live web verification in this run. Check page-level details before citing them in published work."]},{"id":"mahabharata-war-dating","title":"Archaeoastronomical dating of the Mahabharata war and its link to the Kali Yuga start","category":"Scientific comparison","proponents":["B.N. Narahari Achar (planetary-configuration dating of the war, c. 3067 BCE)","P.V. Vartak (astronomical readings of the epic, several dates proposed)","P.N. Oak (argued for a much older Mahabharata chronology; fringe, not accepted by mainstream historians)","Traditional Siddhantic astronomers (Aryabhata, Surya Siddhanta) who fixed the Kali epoch","Traditional commentators who link Krishna's death to the start of Kali"],"primarySources":["Mahabharata, critical edition (Bhandarkar Oriental Research Institute, Poona, 1933-1966, ed. V.S. Sukthankar et al.); astronomical passages in Bhishma, Udyoga and Mausala parvas","Vishnu Purana (c. 4th-5th c. CE in its present form): Krishna's death and the start of Kali","Aryabhatiya by Aryabhata (499 CE): Kali epoch reckoned as 3102 BCE","Surya Siddhanta (Siddhantic astronomy, medieval compilation)","Aihole inscription of Ravikirti (634 CE): reckons 3735 years of the Kali era at Saka 556, consistent with the 3102 BCE epoch"],"period":"Kali epoch fixed in Siddhantic astronomy (Aryabhata, 499 CE). War-dating arguments by modern writers from the 1890s to the 2000s.","totalCycleYears":"4,320,000 years (mahayuga); Kali Yuga is 432,000 years of it","durations":{"satya":"1,728,000 years","treta":"1,296,000 years","dvapara":"864,000 years","kali":"432,000 years"},"kaliStart":"3102 BCE (Siddhantic epoch, midnight of 17/18 February 3102 BCE in the Julian calendar). Several modern writers instead tie the epic war to a date around 3138 BCE by counting back from this epoch, using the traditional gap of 36 years between the war and Krishna's death.","kaliEnd":"About 428,898 CE (3102 BCE + 432,000 years)","currentPosition":"In 2026 CE the Kali Yuga is about 5,127 years old on the Siddhantic reckoning, with roughly 426,873 years remaining.","summary":"This view treats the Mahabharata war as a real event that can be dated by checking planetary and lunar positions described in the epic against computed sky positions. Achar and others argue that these descriptions fit a specific date around 3067 BCE, while Oak and similar writers push the date much earlier. The Kali Yuga start is a separate, older construct: Aryabhata's 3102 BCE epoch, which was calculated backward from mean planetary motions rather than observed. Proponents of a link between the two say Krishna's death, which opens Kali, follows the war by 36 years, so a war date can be checked against the Kali epoch. The link is weak in practice: the astronomical passages are poetic and interpolated across centuries, and the Siddhantic epoch is a computed mean-motion value, not a record of a real conjunction. Mainstream historians date the epic's composition to roughly 400 BCE to 400 CE and treat the war as at most a historical core that cannot be dated from the text alone.","keyClaims":["The astronomical descriptions in the Mahabharata (planetary positions, eclipses, and the sky over the battlefield) can be matched to a specific historical date, most often cited as c. 3067 BCE (Achar).","The Kali Yuga began in 3102 BCE, and the war occurred roughly 36 years before Krishna's death, which opens Kali, so the war falls near 3138 BCE on a backward count.","The Aihole inscription (634 CE) corroborates the 3102 BCE Kali epoch via its Kali-era year count.","Modern scholarship and dating cannot confirm the war's date, but the epic's astronomical details are internally consistent enough to support a Bronze Age or early Iron Age date.","Archaeological sites such as Hastinapura and Indraprastha yield Painted Grey Ware (c. 1200-600 BCE), which some argue fits a Mahabharata-era settlement."],"evidence":["Aryabhatiya (499 CE) states that 3600 years of Kali had elapsed at Aryabhata's age of 23, which fixes the epoch at 3102 BCE.","The Aihole inscription (634 CE) uses the Kali-era count (3735 years at Saka 556), which is consistent with the 3102 BCE epoch.","Achar's planetary-configuration analysis of Bhishma parva passages produced a specific date near 3067 BCE, which he published in Indian journal venues.","Excavations at Hastinapura (B.B. Lal, 1950s) found Painted Grey Ware layers that some link to the epic period.","The Mausala parva's account of Krishna's death 36 years after the war gives a fixed internal interval that writers use for backward dating."],"criticisms":["The Kali epoch (3102 BCE) was computed from mean planetary motions in medieval astronomy, not observed; David Pingree and other historians of astronomy have shown that no real planetary conjunction matches the epoch in the sky.","Astronomical passages in the epic are poetic and were added across many centuries; the critical edition shows multiple layers, so they cannot be treated as a single contemporary observation.","Achar's 3067 BCE date does not fit the traditional 36-year gap to the Kali start (3102 BCE), so his date and the Kali link pull in opposite directions; this tension is rarely addressed by proponents.","No inscription, seal, or datable artifact directly attests the war; Painted Grey Ware evidence at Hastinapura is compatible with the epic but does not confirm it.","Oak's chronology is rejected by mainstream historians and lacks peer-reviewed support; it relies on assertion rather than the methods of archaeoastronomy.","Dating a text's events from its own sky descriptions is circular unless the descriptions are independently dated, and the Mahabharata's descriptions are not.","Specific attributions (e.g., journal names and years) should be checked against primary bibliographies before citing; this summary was written from memory without live web verification."],"plausibility":3,"plausibilityReason":"A Bronze Age or early Iron Age core for the war story is plausible, and Painted Grey Ware finds at Hastinapura fit the broad period. But the precise dates (3067 BCE, 3102 BCE, 3138 BCE) rest on computed or interpolated astronomy, the Kali epoch is a medieval construct, and the epic's sky passages have no independent check. The Kali link adds another unsupported step, so the specific claims are weakly supported.","references":["Sukthankar, V.S. et al., The Mahabharata, critical edition, BORI, Poona, 1933-1966","Aryabhata, Aryabhatiya, 499 CE (Kalakriya section)","Aihole inscription of Ravikirti, 634 CE","Pingree, David, on the Kali epoch and Siddhantic astronomy (see his historical astronomy writings)","Achar, B.N. Narahari, papers on dating the Mahabharata war by planetary positions (c. 2000s); verify exact citation","Lal, B.B., Hastinapura excavations (1950s), Archaeological Survey of India reports","Oak, P.N., popular works on Mahabharata chronology (fringe; not peer reviewed)"]},{"id":"tilak","title":"B.G. Tilak: Orion and Arctic Home dating (yuga-relevant)","category":"Academic/historical","proponents":["Bal Gangadhar Tilak","Hermann Jacobi (independent, similar astronomical dating c. 1894)","later Indian nationalist and astronomical-chronology writers who cite Tilak"],"primarySources":["Tilak, The Orion, or Researches into the Antiquity of the Vedas (1893)","Tilak, The Arctic Home in the Vedas (1903)","Taittiriya Samhita and Brahmana passages on Krittika/Pleiades and Mrigashira (Orion's head) as the nakshatra at the start of the year","Jacobi, Indian Antiquary article on Vedic antiquity (1894)"],"period":"1893 (Orion) and 1903 (Arctic Home); Tilak wrote both in the 1890s and early 1900s, partly during his imprisonments","totalCycleYears":"N/A. Tilak did not propose a yuga cycle. His astronomical dating is about the Vedic year-beginning nakshatra, not a 4-yuga or 12,000-year scheme.","durations":{"satya":"N/A (Tilak gave no yuga durations; he argued about relative antiquity of Vedic hymns)","treta":"N/A","dvapara":"N/A","kali":"N/A"},"kaliStart":"N/A. Tilak does not place the start of Kali Yuga or any yuga boundary.","kaliEnd":"N/A","currentPosition":"Tilak's dating is not a yuga timeline, so it does not give a 2026 position. Its astronomical claims point to Vedic texts composed c. 4500 BCE (Orion/Mrigashira era) and c. 2500 BCE (Krittika era). Those claims place the Vedic period far before the Kali Yuga reckoning (traditionally 3102 BCE start), but Tilak did not connect them to yuga counts.","summary":"Tilak's Orion (1893) argues that Vedic texts record the year beginning at a nakshatra. When the vernal equinox sat in Mrigashira (Orion's head), he dated the Vedic period to about 4500 BCE. When the equinox sat in Krittika (Pleiades), he dated the Brahmana period to about 2500 BCE. Arctic Home in the Vedas (1903) extends this with the claim that Vedic memories of long days and nights, the sun's movement and the 'northern' homeland reflect an Arctic origin, pushing the origin further back. Tilak's work is about relative and absolute antiquity of Vedic literature, not yuga durations. Yuga-based chronologies that use Tilak's dates are later reinterpretations, not his claims. His astronomy has been discussed seriously, but his Arctic thesis has not been accepted by mainstream scholars.","keyClaims":["The Vedic year began at the vernal equinox in Mrigashira (Orion's head) around 4500 BCE, per Tilak's reading of Vedic texts","Brahmana-period texts with the year beginning at Krittika point to about 2500 BCE","Vedic texts preserve memories of an Arctic or circumpolar homeland with long days and nights","Vedic civilization is far older than the 1500-1000 BCE dates from Western philology","Astronomical references in the Vedas are a reliable chronological clock"],"evidence":["Taittiriya Samhita and Brahmana passages saying the Krittikas mark the start of the year or rise in the east","Vedic references to long dawns, the sun's long course, and northern-origin imagery, read as Arctic memories","Independent corroboration by Hermann Jacobi (c. 1894), who reached a similar early date from Vedic astronomy","Precession of the equinoxes, which is real and well understood, so the astronomical method is physically valid in principle"],"criticisms":["The equinox-nakshatra method depends on dating the texts' astronomical statements, and those statements are vague: the nakshatra lists were not fixed to a single epoch, and 'rising in the east' can be read many ways. The result is highly sensitive to interpretation.","Linguistic and textual dating (Rigveda c. 1500-1000 BCE, with redaction later) does not support composition at 4500 BCE, and there is no archaeological trace of Vedic culture at that date in India.","The Arctic Home thesis relies on selective readings of hymns and ignores the Indo-Iranian and Indo-European linguistic evidence, which points to a steppe homeland. Most scholars reject the Arctic claim.","Tilak's work was written with nationalist motives, which some scholars see as shaping the reading of evidence toward older dates.","The Vedas' astronomical data can be used to date specific observations only if the texts are precisely dated, which they are not, so the method is circular.","Jacobi's agreement is often cited but was partly based on the same Krittika/Mrigashira reasoning, so it is not fully independent confirmation.","Tilak's work does not give yuga durations or a Kali start date, so it cannot be used directly to test yuga timelines."],"plausibility":2,"plausibilityReason":"The astronomical method is real, and the precession observation is correct. But the step from nakshatra references to 4500 BCE composition dates is weak and conflicts with linguistic and archaeological evidence. The Arctic thesis is largely rejected. Tilak's dates do not support any specific yuga timeline, and the yuga reckoning is not part of his argument.","references":["Tilak, B.G. The Orion, or Researches into the Antiquity of the Vedas (1893)","Tilak, B.G. The Arctic Home in the Vedas (1903)","Jacobi, H. (1894), Indian Antiquary, on Vedic antiquity","Witzel, M. (1995 and later), on Vedic texts and dating, critical of Tilak-style astronomical dating","Pingree, D. on Vedanga Jyotisha and early Indian astronomy","Mallory, J.P. In Search of the Indo-Europeans (1989), on the steppe homeland vs Arctic thesis"]},{"id":"kak","title":"Subhash Kak's astronomical code of the Rigveda and yuga/time-cycle claims","category":"Alternative/fringe","proponents":["Subhash Kak (Louisiana State University, computer science and electrical engineering)","Some popular Indian writers who cite Kak's Rigveda astronomy claims"],"primarySources":["Kak, S. 'The Astronomical Code of the Rigveda' (book, c. 2000, Indian publisher; exact year and publisher should be verified)","Kak, S. 'Astronomy of the Vedic altars' (Vistas in Astronomy, early 1990s; verify exact citation)","Rigveda Samhita (composition c. 1500-1200 BCE on the mainstream Indological view)"],"period":"Main work c. 1990s-2000s, with later essays and popular restatements","totalCycleYears":"N/A (Kak's Rigveda work is not built around a full 4.32-million-year mahayuga cycle; the standard Puranic total of 4,320,000 years is not something Kak's core argument reconstructs in detail, as far as I could recall without live verification)","durations":{"satya":"Not specified in Kak's core Rigveda argument (standard Puranic value would be 1,728,000 years; I did not verify Kak's own figure)","treta":"Not specified in Kak's core argument (standard Puranic value 1,296,000 years)","dvapara":"Not specified in Kak's core argument (standard Puranic value 864,000 years)","kali":"Standard Puranic/Siddhantic value 432,000 years; Kak's own figure not verified"},"kaliStart":"Not clearly fixed by Kak in the core argument. The Siddhantic convention (e.g., Aryabhata-era and Surya Siddhanta tradition) places the start at 3102 BCE, which Kak's dating of the Rigveda would not directly endorse or contradict.","kaliEnd":"Under the standard convention, 432,000 years after the 3102 BCE start (i.e., around 428,898 CE). Kak's own end date is not verified.","currentPosition":"In 2026 CE, under the standard 3102 BCE convention, we are in approximately year 5128 of the Kali Yuga. Kak's work does not provide a clear, widely cited current-position statement that I could verify.","summary":"Subhash Kak, a computer scientist at Louisiana State University, argues in 'The Astronomical Code of the Rigveda' that the hymns of the Rigveda encode astronomical knowledge, including positions of celestial bodies, cyclical timekeeping, and calendrical structure. He proposes that the text's numbers, meters, and organization carry a systematic astronomical code and argues that the astronomical content points to a far earlier date for the composition than mainstream Indology allows, in the range of several thousand years BCE. His work on Vedic altar geometry (Agnicayana, the fire-altar construction tradition) is a related strand that argues the altars encode astronomical measures. Yuga-cycle and large-scale time claims appear in this body of work as a broader framing rather than as a fully specified chronology that can be checked against the Puranic 4:3:2:1 scheme. Kak's claims have been influential in popular Indian discourse, but they are rejected by most historians and Indologists of the Vedas and by most historians of astronomy, who see the proposed mappings as underdetermined and the dating as inconsistent with linguistic, archaeological, and textual evidence.","keyClaims":["The Rigveda contains a systematic astronomical code: hymn numbers, meters, and structures correspond to celestial cycles and calendrical data.","The astronomical content implies composition or compilation much earlier than the mainstream c. 1500-1200 BCE, possibly several thousand years earlier.","Vedic fire-altar geometry (Agnicayana) encodes astronomical measures and calendar cycles.","Ancient Indian time-cycle frameworks, including yuga-style cycles, reflect real astronomical observation tracked over very long periods.","Mainstream chronology underestimates the antiquity of Indian astronomical knowledge."],"evidence":["Numerical patterns and structural features of the Rigveda that Kak reads as encoding astronomical positions or cycle lengths.","Geometric analysis of Vedic altar dimensions (Shatapatha Brahmana tradition) that Kak relates to astronomical quantities such as the solar year and lunar cycles.","References to nakshatra-like lists and seasonal/solstitial markers in Vedic and Brahmana texts, which are real features of the textual record but do not on their own establish early dating.","Planetarium or precession-based reconstructions offered as independent confirmation of the early date."],"criticisms":["Methodological: the 'code' is not clearly specified in advance, so many mappings can be found after the fact; the approach is not falsifiable and lacks controls against chance patterns.","Dating: the Rigveda's language (Vedic Sanskrit), linguistic evidence, and the absence of corroborating archaeology for the proposed date conflict with the several-thousand-years-earlier composition Kak argues for.","Astronomical reconstruction: standard astronomy (e.g., precession and the sky visible from northwest India at the proposed dates) does not straightforwardly support the specific alignments claimed in many cases.","Peer review and venue: the core claims were published largely outside mainstream Indological or history-of-astronomy peer-reviewed journals; scholarly reviews have been critical.","Selective evidence: arguments tend to pick favorable numerical coincidences while neglecting counterexamples.","Political appropriation: the work has been used in popular and nationalist narratives asserting ancient Indian scientific priority, which tends to inflate its evidentiary weight in public discussion.","Yuga-specific claims: the 4.32-million-year and Kali-start conventions are Puranic/Siddhantic constructs with known medieval arithmetic origins, and Kak's astronomical reading of the Rigveda does not supply independent evidence that the yuga durations describe real past events."],"plausibility":2,"plausibilityReason":"Kak's work is one of the more academically credentialed fringe-leaning positions, and it rests on real features (Vedic altar geometry, calendrical references in the Brahmanas, and general knowledge of Indian astronomy). But the core claim that the Rigveda encodes a verified astronomical code dating thousands of years earlier than mainstream consensus is unsupported by independent evidence, rests on post hoc pattern-finding, and conflicts with linguistic and archaeological data. The yuga-cycle durations and Kali-start figures are not independently validated by his work. Plausibility is low.","references":["Kak, S. 'The Astronomical Code of the Rigveda' (book; verify publisher and year before citing)","Kak, S. 'Astronomy of the Vedic altars' (Vistas in Astronomy; verify citation)","Witzel, M. (various essays on Vedic chronology and the dating of the Rigveda) for the mainstream dating perspective","Parpola, A. (work on Vedic and Indus-Sarasvati chronology) for comparative context","Surya Siddhanta and Aryabhatiya for the Siddhantic Kali Yuga start convention (3102 BCE)","No live web verification was possible in this run; please verify all citations, dates, and quoted claims before publication."]},{"id":"science-cosmology","title":"Comparing kalpa and yuga timescales with modern geology and cosmology","category":"Scientific comparison","proponents":["Popular Vedic-science and Hindu apologetic writers who equate the kalpa with Earth's age (individual authors not verified in this pass)","Historians of astronomy who treat the numbers as computational artifacts (e.g., David Pingree, Kim Plofker)"],"primarySources":["Surya Siddhanta (astronomical treatise, final form c. 4th-5th century CE by most scholarly estimates; numbers given in Burgess's 1860 translation)","Aryabhatiya (Aryabhata, 499 CE), which uses a 4,320,000-year mahayuga and dates Kali Yuga to 3102 BCE","Manusmriti chapter 1 (yuga lengths expressed in divine years, multiplied by 360)","Mahabharata, Vana Parva (yuga durations in divine years, ratio 4:3:2:1)","Vishnu Purana Book 1 (kalpa as day of Brahma, pralaya as night)"],"period":"Yuga arithmetic is classical (Siddhantic, roughly 4th-6th century CE). The comparison with Earth and universe ages is modern, mainly 20th and 21st century popular writing.","totalCycleYears":"Mahayuga 4,320,000 years (sum of four yugas). Kalpa = 1,000 mahayugas = 4.32 billion years. Brahma's life (100 years of 360 days each) = 311.04 trillion years.","durations":{"satya":"1,728,000 years (Siddhantic); 4,800 divine years in Manusmriti","treta":"1,296,000 years (Siddhantic); 3,600 divine years","dvapara":"864,000 years (Siddhantic); 2,400 divine years","kali":"432,000 years (Siddhantic); 1,200 divine years"},"kaliStart":"3102 BCE","kaliEnd":"About 428,898 CE (3102 BCE plus 432,000 years)","currentPosition":"In 2026 CE, about 5,127 years have elapsed since the traditional Kali epoch, so this is roughly the 5,128th year of Kali Yuga. Geology and cosmology give no support to any yuga boundary being at this date.","summary":"This view compares the Hindu kalpa (4.32 billion years, one day of Brahma) with the Earth's accepted age (about 4.54 billion years) and the universe's age (about 13.8 billion years). The Earth match is numerically close, about 5 percent off, and some popular writers present it as evidence of ancient knowledge. The comparison fails on inspection. The Siddhantic figures come from planetary-cycle arithmetic and a decimal scheme, not from measurement of rocks or light. Kalpa and yuga are also not framed as the age of the Earth or universe, and the Puranic framework describes cycles within a beginningless cosmos. The universe comparison is off by a factor of about 22,500 for Brahma's life.","keyClaims":["A kalpa of 4.32 billion years closely matches the Earth's age of about 4.54 billion years, which some take as evidence of ancient precise knowledge.","Brahma's life (311.04 trillion years) is a cosmic timescale that some readers link to the universe's age.","Yuga numbers were obtained by exact astronomical observation or transmitted knowledge.","The traditional Kali epoch of 3102 BCE is historically meaningful."],"evidence":["The arithmetic is exact: mahayuga 4,320,000 years; kalpa 1,000 mahayugas = 4.32 billion years, documented in the Siddhantic corpus.","Earth age 4.54 Gyr (Patterson 1956, from meteorite lead isotopes; modern value about 4.54 Gyr). Difference from kalpa is about 0.22 Gyr, roughly 5 percent.","The Siddhantic framework uses planetary mean motions and conjunction periods that do compute large numbers of revolutions over long spans.","Aryabhata's 499 CE text uses the 3102 BCE Kali epoch, making the epoch a documented convention rather than a modern invention."],"criticisms":["Numerical closeness is weak evidence. The kalpa is a large round number built from decimal multiples (1,000 x 4,320) with no measurement behind the choice. Many numbers in that range would land near some geological age, so a single match carries little weight.","Category mismatch. Kalpa and yuga are not offered as the age of the Earth or universe. The Puranic framework describes repeated creation and dissolution (pralaya), so calling the kalpa the Earth's age reads a modern notion back into the texts.","The Siddhantic numbers were built from astronomical cycles that were tuned to make planetary positions return to a reference point at the end of the mahayuga. The aim was calendrical computation, not geological or cosmological accuracy.","The universe comparison fails. Brahma's life of 311.04 trillion years is about 22,500 times the age of the universe (13.787 Gyr, Planck 2018). The kalpa does not match the universe either.","Geology contradicts any yuga-based chronology for Earth's history. Earth's oldest surviving rocks are about 4.0 Gyr (Acasta gneiss) and detrital zircons from Jack Hills are about 4.4 Gyr. Life spans billions of years, and the Phanerozoic (last 541 Myr) is far longer than any yuga. Human history records and archaeology do not match a 432,000-year Kali Yuga.","Selection bias and retrofitting. Apologists often pick the Earth-age match and ignore the many predictions that fail, such as the universe comparison and the dates of major evolutionary events."],"plausibility":2,"plausibilityReason":"The 4.32 Gyr kalpa vs 4.54 Gyr Earth match is real arithmetic, but it is coincidental and not predictive. The Siddhantic numbers were derived for calendar and astronomical purposes and were never measurements of Earth's history. The universe comparison is off by a factor of about 22,500, and the yuga boundaries do not map onto any dated geological or archaeological event. The plausibility score reflects that the yuga timeline does not survive the geology and cosmology checks.","references":["Burgess, E. (1860), translation of the Surya Siddhanta, Journal of the American Oriental Society 6","Aryabhatiya, Aryabhata, 499 CE (Gitikapada and Kalakriyapada)","Patterson, C. (1956), Age of meteorites and the earth, Geochimica et Cosmochimica Acta 10","Planck Collaboration (2020), Planck 2018 results VI, Astronomy and Astrophysics 641, A6 (age 13.787 Gyr)","Pingree, D. (1981), Jyotihsastra: Astral and Mathematical Literature, Otto Harrassowitz","Plofker, K. (2009), Mathematics in India, Princeton University Press"]},{"id":"archaeology-human","title":"Archaeology and human evolution vs. a historical Satya/Treta golden age","category":"Scientific comparison","proponents":["Traditional commentators (e.g., Aryabhata's Kali epoch tradition, c. 499 CE)","Dayananda Saraswati (Satyarth Prakash, 1875), who read yuga-era chronology as historical","Later Hindu revivalist writers who treat the Satya/Treta eras as literal history","Archaeologists, paleoanthropologists and ancient-DNA researchers who test the claim against physical evidence (critics)"],"primarySources":["Manusmriti 1.68-86 (c. 200 BCE-200 CE), gives the 4,320,000-year mahayuga in divine-year terms","Mahabharata, Vana Parva (c. 400 BCE-400 CE), yuga durations and lifespans","Vishnu Purana (c. 300-500 CE), yuga durations","Aryabhatiya (499 CE), Kali epoch used for astronomical reckoning","Hesiod, Works and Days (c. 700 BCE), a comparable Ages-of-Man scheme for cross-cultural context"],"period":"The yuga chronology appears in texts from roughly 200 BCE to 500 CE. The literal-historical reading of Satya/Treta as past human eras is mainly a 19th-20th century revivalist move, with the Kali epoch of 3102 BCE fixed astronomically from around 499 CE onward.","totalCycleYears":"4,320,000 (mahayuga, 12,000 divine years x 360)","durations":{"satya":"1,728,000 years (4,800 divine years)","treta":"1,296,000 years (3,600 divine years)","dvapara":"864,000 years (2,400 divine years)","kali":"432,000 years (1,200 divine years)"},"kaliStart":"3102 BCE (traditional astronomical epoch, Feb 17/18, 3102 BCE)","kaliEnd":"Approximately 428,898 CE, by the 432,000-year Kali duration. This chronology is not part of the archaeological record.","currentPosition":"In 2026 CE the traditional count places us in Kali year 5128. If the Satya/Treta eras are read as history, they fall far outside any documented human record: anatomically modern humans date to about 300,000 years ago (Jebel Irhoud), and the earliest cities (Indus Valley) date to about 2600-1900 BCE, well after the claimed start of Kali.","summary":"The yuga scheme assigns human history four ages of decreasing length, from a Satya Yuga of 1.728 million years down to the Kali Yuga of 432,000 years. Read literally as human history, this conflicts with the archaeological and genetic record: modern humans appeared around 300,000 years ago, agriculture around 10,000 years ago, and early cities only after about 2600 BCE, with no trace of a global golden age or of 1.7-million-year human eras. Most scholars read the yugas as cosmological and symbolic time, or as an astronomical calendar of divine years, rather than as literal past eras. Revivalist writers have still tried to fit them to history, which is where the conflict with evidence arises.","keyClaims":["Satya and Treta Yugas were historical ages of human life with exceptionally long lifespans and high virtue","Human history can be mapped onto the 4,320,000-year mahayuga","Archaeological and textual evidence is consistent with the yuga chronology when properly interpreted","The Kali epoch of 3102 BCE marks a real historical beginning"],"evidence":["Texts describe very long lifespans and ages of ancient sages (e.g., Mahabharata, Manusmriti), offered as evidence of a historical golden age","The Kali epoch 3102 BCE is traditionally cited as a fixed starting date, and is computed astronomically (for example by Aryabhata)","Some revivalists cite Vedic texts and river references (for example, the Saraswati) as evidence of an ancient civilization that predates the Indus cities","Ancient DNA from South Asia shows deep population mixing, which some read as ancient continuity"],"criticisms":["No archaeological or paleoanthropological evidence exists for human eras of 1.7 million or 1.3 million years; the human fossil record places Homo sapiens at about 300,000 years","The 3102 BCE Kali epoch is an astronomical reconstruction from around 499 CE, not a dated historical event; no contemporary record supports it","Early Indus cities date to about 2600-1900 BCE, and no pre-Indus urban golden age has been found","Ancient DNA (Narasimhan et al. 2019, Science; Shinde et al. 2018, Cell) shows South Asian ancestry formed through mixing over several millennia, with Steppe-related input around 2000-1500 BCE, not a single continuous golden-age population","The Ghaggar-Hakra (Saraswati) drying is dated to roughly 2000 BCE by geomorphology (Giosan et al. 2012, PNAS), so it cannot be a witness to a yuga-era civilization","Literal readings of yuga lifespans and durations are internally inconsistent across texts, and the Puranas differ in their numbers","Golden-age myths are a cross-cultural pattern (Hesiod's Ages of Man, Persian and Norse cycles), which suggests a mythic rather than archaeological origin","Revivalist readings often rely on selective evidence, and mainstream Indology and archaeology do not accept the literal chronology"],"plausibility":1,"plausibilityReason":"The literal claim that Satya/Treta were historical eras of human life conflicts with the human fossil record (about 300 ka for Homo sapiens), the archaeological record of agriculture and cities, and ancient DNA, which shows gradual mixing rather than a golden-age population. The yuga scheme can still be valued as cosmology or as an astronomical calendar, but those readings make no historical claim that archaeology can test. The score reflects only the historical-timeline claim.","references":["Hublin et al. 2017, Nature 546:289-292 (Jebel Irhoud, c. 300 ka Homo sapiens)","Narasimhan et al. 2019, Science, doi:10.1126/science.aat7487 (formation of South and Central Asian populations)","Shinde et al. 2018, Cell (Harappan genome, Rakhigarhi)","Giosan et al. 2012, PNAS 109:E1688 (Harappan fluvial landscapes, Ghaggar-Hakra)","Manusmriti 1.68-86 and Mahabharata Vana Parva (Critical Edition, Sukthankar et al.)","Aryabhatiya (499 CE) for the Kali epoch"]},{"id":"sandhya","title":"Sandhya and sandhyamsha transitional periods and end-date disputes","category":"Traditional scriptural","proponents":["Traditional Puranic and Smriti commentators (e.g., Manu Smriti commentary tradition)","Surya Siddhanta tradition","Aryabhata-school computational astronomers (for epoch and ahargana framing)","Sri Yukteswar Giri (Holy Science, 1894) for his own re-dating scheme","Modern popular writers who use sandhya arithmetic to argue for a Kali end-date"],"primarySources":["Manu Smriti, Ch. 1 (compilation roughly c. 200 BCE to 200 CE), which gives the sandhya/sandhyamsha framing of the four yugas","Vishnu Purana, Book 1 (c. 300-500 CE), yuga durations with dawn and dusk periods","Mahabharata, Vana Parva (Markandeya section), yuga lengths in divine years","Surya Siddhanta (c. 4th-5th century CE, with later revisions), mahayuga of 4,320,000 years","Aryabhatiya (499 CE), Kali epoch framing (3102 BCE)"],"period":"Textual framing dates to the early centuries CE in its Smriti and Puranic form. Its use in end-date disputes is mostly modern (19th-21st century), especially from Yukteswar onward.","totalCycleYears":"4,320,000 years (mahayuga, 12,000 divine years x 360) including sandhya/sandhyamsha. Without them, the four yugas sum to 4,000,000 years.","durations":{"satya":"4,800 divine years (4,000 + 400 sandhya + 400 sandhyamsha), about 1,728,000 human years","treta":"3,600 divine years (3,000 + 300 + 300), about 1,296,000 human years","dvapara":"2,400 divine years (2,000 + 200 + 200), about 864,000 human years","kali":"1,200 divine years (1,000 + 100 + 100), about 432,000 human years"},"kaliStart":"3102 BCE (traditional epoch, fixed by the Aryabhata-school convention, not by any observed event)","kaliEnd":"About 428,898 CE if the 432,000-year Kali proper is counted from 3102 BCE. Adding the 36,000-year Kali sandhyamsha moves the notional end to about 464,898 CE. Whether the sandhya is counted inside or outside the 3102 BCE start is one of the disputes.","currentPosition":"In 2026 CE the traditional count is about 5,128 years into Kali proper (roughly 5,127 years since 3102 BCE, depending on the BCE/CE convention). Under the sandhya framework this is well within the main body of Kali, not within a transition. A different reckoning would only move the present position if it relocated the start of Kali itself.","summary":"The Puranic and Smriti scheme gives each yuga a preceding dawn (sandhya) and following dusk (sandhyamsha), each about one-tenth of the yuga's length and spanning 100 to 400 divine years depending on the yuga. These are added to the core yuga durations, so the four yugas total 12,000 divine years (4.32 million human years) rather than 10,000. Disputes arise because texts do not always make clear whether 3102 BCE marks the start of the sandhya or of the Kali proper, and whether sandhya is counted inside the 4.32 million figure. Some modern reinterpreters use sandhya arithmetic to argue that the current age is ending or that the dark age ends earlier than the standard reckoning. Those claims depend on choosing different start points and on treating a computational epoch as a historical event.","keyClaims":["Each yuga has a sandhya (dawn) and sandhyamsha (dusk) totaling 20% of its core length, split equally at its beginning and end. The four yugas therefore total 12,000 divine years (4.32 million human years at 360 human years per divine year).","The Kali sandhya and sandhyamsha are 100 divine years each (36,000 human years each), so the notional Kali span is 1,200 divine years.","Sandhya arithmetic can shift the apparent end of Kali by tens of thousands of years, depending on whether the transition is counted inside or outside the 3102 BCE epoch.","Yukteswar argued for a shorter cycle of about 24,000 years with its own dark-age dates, departing from the Puranic 4.32 million framework.","The 3102 BCE epoch is a computational convention, not an observed event, so any transition reckoning inherits that convention's uncertainty."],"evidence":["Manu Smriti and Vishnu Purana describe the dawn and dusk periods and their relation to the core yuga lengths, which is the basis of the 12,000 divine-year total.","The Mahabharata Vana Parva gives the same yuga structure in divine years, with sandhya and sandhyamsha counted in each yuga.","Aryabhata's 3102 BCE Kali epoch is the widely used anchor for the start of Kali proper and is consistent with the sandhya arithmetic when that epoch is taken as the start of the core period.","Yukteswar's Holy Science (1894) explicitly reinterprets yuga arithmetic and transition periods, which demonstrates that the sandhya framework has been used to support non-standard end-date claims."],"criticisms":["The sandhya durations are textually stated but have no observational or independent astronomical check; they are arithmetic conventions layered on top of a mythic cycle.","Texts disagree on how the sandhya and sandhyamsha should be assigned to the start and end of each yuga, which is the root of the end-date disputes.","The 3102 BCE epoch is a computational convention from the Aryabhata school and does not correspond to any historical event, so reckoning transitions from it does not make a historical claim testable.","Yukteswar's shorter-cycle reinterpretation and later popular claims that the age is ending early use selective readings and are not supported by the Puranic arithmetic they claim to follow.","The 4.32 million figure is itself a late systematization (Surya Siddhanta and Puranic synthesis), so sandhya arithmetic should not be read as ancient precise chronology.","No independent archaeological, astronomical, or historical data confirms any yuga transition date, so the sandhya framework cannot be used to locate the present within a cycle."],"plausibility":2,"plausibilityReason":"The sandhya framework is internally consistent as textual arithmetic and historically traceable to Smriti and Puranic texts. As a description of real-world timelines, it has no independent evidence, and its key anchor (3102 BCE) is a convention, not an event. Claims that use sandhya to pin down a real-world end date depend on contested choices of start point and cannot be verified. Plausibility is low for any literal reading.","references":["Manu Smriti, Ch. 1 (yuga durations and dawn/dusk framing); standard translations by G. Bühler (Sacred Books of the East, vol. 25) and Wendy Doniger with Brian K. Smith (Penguin, 1991)","Vishnu Purana, Book 1, ch. 3 (yuga structure); H. H. Wilson translation (1840)","Mahabharata, Vana Parva, Markandeya section on yugas (see Kisari Mohan Ganguli's translation, 1883-1896)","Surya Siddhanta, with W. Burgess translation (1860)","Aryabhatiya (499 CE), with Walter Eugene Clark's translation (1930)","Sri Yukteswar Giri, The Holy Science (1894)","Background on Kali epoch and yuga arithmetic: Wendy Doniger O'Flaherty (ed.), Hindu Myths (Penguin), and Gavin Flood, An Introduction to Hinduism (Cambridge, 1996)"]},{"id":"inscriptions","title":"Epigraphic use of the Kali era (Aihole 634 CE and earlier claims)","category":"Academic/historical","proponents":["J.F. Fleet (early edition and dating of the Aihole/Meguti inscription)","D.C. Sircar (Indian Epigraphy, 1965, general treatment of era systems)","David Pingree (history of Indian astronomical texts and epochs)","Mainstream Indological and epigraphical scholarship (general consensus on the Aihole dating)"],"primarySources":["Aihole (Meguti Jain temple) inscription of Pulakeshin II, Chalukya, dated Shaka 556 (634 CE) and referring to Kali years elapsed (about 3735), composed by the poet Ravikirti","Aryabhatiya (499 CE), Gitikapada, which states a Kali-based epoch of 3600 years elapsed at the time of composition (astronomical text, not an epigraph)","Later Indian astronomical texts (Brahmagupta, 628 CE; Bhaskara I and others) that use the Kali epoch for computation"],"period":"Earliest securely dated epigraphic Kali reckoning: 634 CE (Aihole). Astronomical use of a Kali epoch goes back to about 499 CE (Aryabhata). Kali yuga as a cosmological duration is much older, typically placed in Puranic and Mahabharata-era layers, with the 432,000-year figure fixed in later texts.","totalCycleYears":"4,320,000 years (mahayuga, 12,000 divine years of 360 each). This entry concerns only the dating evidence for the Kali epoch.","durations":{"satya":"1,728,000 years","treta":"1,296,000 years","dvapara":"864,000 years","kali":"432,000 years"},"kaliStart":"3102 BCE (traditional epoch, 17/18 February, as computed in astronomical texts)","kaliEnd":"About 428,898 CE (432,000 years after the traditional epoch, with no year zero adjustment)","currentPosition":"In 2026 CE the Kali year is roughly 5127 (about 5126 to 5127 depending on the month convention used). The Kali era is still used in Hindu almanacs (panchangs) and some ritual dating.","summary":"The Aihole inscription (Meguti temple, Karnataka), composed for Chalukya king Pulakeshin II, is commonly cited as the earliest securely dated epigraphic use of the Kali era. It gives its date in the Shaka era as 556 (634 CE) and, in the same text, refers to the elapsed Kali years (about 3735). The two eras agree with each other, which is the main reason scholars accept the Kali reckoning as a real calendrical convention in use by 634 CE. However, this shows only that a Kali epoch was in use by the 7th century. It does not show that the 3102 BCE starting point describes a real historical event, and it says nothing about the 432,000-year yuga cycle as a physical or historical reality.","keyClaims":["The Aihole inscription is the earliest securely dated epigraphic reference to the Kali era, dated 634 CE.","The Kali and Shaka dates in the inscription agree (Kali about 3735, Shaka 556), which anchors the Kali epoch to 3102 BCE.","Kali-era reckoning in astronomy (Aryabhata, 499 CE) predates its epigraphic use by about 135 years.","Epigraphic use of Kali reckoning is a 7th-century phenomenon, mostly in the Deccan and South Indian dynastic records, and became common only later.","The 3102 BCE epoch is a computed convention, not a date recorded in any text from that time."],"evidence":["Shaka-era anchor at 556 (634 CE) is well established, so the Aihole date is firmly fixed in the Common Era.","Kali count (about 3735) is consistent with the 3102 BCE epoch, which is what the later astronomical tradition computes.","Aryabhatiya Gitikapada states a Kali-based count of 3600 years elapsed at the composition date, giving an independent astronomical witness for the Kali epoch in the late 5th century.","Later siddhantic texts such as Brahmagupta's Brahmasphutasiddhanta (628 CE) use the Kali epoch as the base for their calculations."],"criticisms":["Dating the Aihole inscription as the 'earliest' Kali reference is a claim about the surviving record. Scholars can only say it is the earliest known to them. Earlier uses may have been lost or not yet catalogued.","The Kali epoch is a backward calculation from astronomical and cosmological models. Using the Aihole reference as proof of an ancient yuga date confuses calendrical convention with historical fact.","The statement that Kali was 'in use' in 634 CE does not show that people in 634 believed the 432,000-year cycle was real. The inscription likely uses the era for convenience and prestige, not as a cosmological claim.","Epigraphic Kali dating is sparse before about the 8th century, so generalising from one or two records to the whole tradition overstates the evidence.","Some readings of the inscription's verses and numerals depend on editorial emendation and on how the numerals are interpreted. Readers should check current editions rather than relying on a single translation.","I did not verify these details against live sources in this run because no web search or fetch tool was loaded. Specific verse numbers, edition details, and exact numeral readings should be checked against Fleet's edition and recent epigraphic catalogues before being quoted."],"plausibility":5,"plausibilityReason":"Split rating. The claim that the Aihole inscription (634 CE) is an early, securely dated use of the Kali era is well supported (about 8 out of 10), since the Shaka anchor fixes the date. The claim that this evidence confirms the Kali yuga's 3102 BCE start or the 432,000-year cycle as history is not supported (about 1 to 2 out of 10), because the epoch is a retrospective astronomical convention.","references":["Aihole (Meguti) inscription of Pulakeshin II, dated Shaka 556 (634 CE), composed by Ravikirti; edited by J.F. Fleet (check current editions for exact citation)","Aryabhatiya, Gitikapada (499 CE), Aryabhata I","Brahmasphutasiddhanta, Brahmagupta (628 CE)","D.C. Sircar, Indian Epigraphy (Motilal Banarsidass, 1965)","David Pingree, Census of the Exact Sciences in Sanskrit (American Philosophical Society)"]},{"id":"kalachakra","title":"Kalachakra Tantra, Shambhala and Kalki (Tibetan Buddhist timeline)","category":"Traditional scriptural","proponents":["Kalachakra lineage in Tibetan Buddhism (Gelug, Sakya, Kagyu, Jonang traditions)","The Dalai Lama, who has given many public Kalachakra initiations","Pundarika, Kalki-king author of the Vimalaprabha commentary (traditional attribution)","Agvan Dorzhiev, who promoted Shambhala and Kalachakra politically in early 20th-century Russia and Tibet","Nicholas Roerich, whose 1930 book Shambhala popularized the Shambhala legend in the West"],"primarySources":["Kalachakra Tantra (Sanskrit, c. 1025 CE, northwestern India or Kashmir region; traditional claim of far earlier revelation)","Vimalaprabha commentary by Pundarika (c. 1027 CE, traditional attribution)","Laghu Kalachakra Tantra (abridged Kalachakra)","Mahabharata, Vana Parva (early Kalki reference to Shambhala village)","Vishnu Purana and later Kalki Purana (Hindu Kalki prophecies)"],"period":"Kalachakra Tantra composed c. 1025 CE. Tibetan transmission began c. 1027 CE. Shambhala and Kalki prophecies were elaborated in the Kalachakra and its commentaries, and the Shambhala legend was popularized in the 20th century.","totalCycleYears":"4,320,000 years (mahayuga, adopted from the Hindu Siddhanta yuga scheme). Kalachakra also uses kalpa-scale and \"Kalki era\" reckonings.","durations":{"satya":"1,728,000 years (Krita-yuga, Hindu-style reckoning adopted in Kalachakra cosmology)","treta":"1,296,000 years","dvapara":"864,000 years","kali":"432,000 years"},"kaliStart":"Not fixed to a single agreed date. Hindu reckoning places Kali-yuga's start at 3102 BCE. Kalachakra's own chronology is tied to the Buddha's teaching and to a Kalki-era reckoning, and scholars disagree on the exact anchor.","kaliEnd":"Kali-yuga would last 432,000 years by the cosmological scheme. Kalachakra does not treat the end of Kali as a fixed 2424 CE event. It prophesies a specific eschatological war in which the Shambhala king Raudra Chakrin defeats the invading mlecchas (widely read as Islamic invaders), commonly cited as around 2424 CE, with some sources citing 2327 CE.","currentPosition":"In 2026 CE we are inside the Kali-yuga by the cosmological scheme, roughly 400 years before the Kalki war date usually cited for the Shambhala prophecy. Tibetan Buddhist traditions treat the prophecy as a future event. Kalachakra practice is ongoing, including large public initiations.","summary":"The Kalachakra (Wheel of Time) Tantra is a 11th-century Sanskrit Buddhist tantra that adopts the Hindu four-yuga cosmology and frames it as a teaching of the Buddha given at Dhanyakataka and carried to the mythical kingdom of Shambhala. It describes a line of Kalki kings of Shambhala who will eventually fight a decisive dharma war against mlecchas, usually read as a prophecy of the Islamic conquest of north India and of its eventual defeat. The Tibetan tradition preserved and expanded this material, and it became central to Tibetan Buddhism through the Kalachakra initiation system. Shambhala later spread into Western and popular culture, where it became detached from its Buddhist tantric context. The historical dating of the text to about 1025 CE is well supported. The cosmological and eschatological timeline is not empirically testable.","keyClaims":["The Kalachakra Tantra teaches a cosmology that uses the Hindu four-yuga scheme and its mahayuga of 4,320,000 years.","The Kalachakra presents itself as a teaching of the Buddha given at Dhanyakataka, which was brought to the kingdom of Shambhala.","The Kalki kings of Shambhala are prophesied to wage a war against mlecchas, commonly cited as around 2424 CE.","The Kalachakra is an external-and-internal (outer, inner, alternative) tantra, with a body-based inner cosmology that maps the macrocosmic time cycles onto the human body.","Shambhala has been treated in Tibetan tradition as a real hidden land, while modern scholarship treats it as a symbolic or imaginal kingdom."],"evidence":["Textual dating: the Kalachakra Tantra and Vimalaprabha are generally dated to around 1025 to 1027 CE, which makes them much later than the Buddha. Scholars such as Ronald Davidson (Indian Esoteric Buddhism, 2002) and Vesna Wallace (The Inner Kalacakratantra, 2001) discuss this dating.","Tibetan transmission history records the Kalachakra arriving in Tibet around 1027 CE, traditionally via Somanatha and Chag Lotsawa.","The Kalki and mleccha war prophecy is internally consistent with the Kalachakra's own chronology, and the Kalachakra gives calendrical calculations for the prophesied dates.","Historical context: the 11th-century Kalachakra's anxiety about invasions from the northwest is consistent with the Ghaznavid incursions into north India, which many scholars see as the background of the mleccha war imagery."],"criticisms":["The 1025 to 1027 CE composition date is far later than the tradition's claims of the Buddha's time, and the revelation claims are not historically supported.","The Kali-yuga cosmology and the 432,000-year durations are symbolic or cosmological, not measurable, so they cannot be tested against history.","The 2424 CE war prophecy has repeatedly failed to match historical events, and scholars read it as a retrospective or allegorical framing of contemporary anxieties rather than as prediction.","Shambhala was promoted in the 20th century for political purposes by Agvan Dorzhiev and others, and it was later appropriated by Western occultists and by Nazi-era esoteric writers. Peter Bishop (The Myth of Shangri-La, 1989) and Joscelyn Godwin (Arktos, 1993) discuss these uses.","Within Buddhism, many scholars and some Tibetan teachers read the Kalki war as a symbolic or inner-practice teaching rather than as a literal eschatological timeline, so the literal reading is contested inside the tradition too."],"plausibility":2,"plausibilityReason":"The text's historical dating to around 1025 CE is well supported by textual scholarship, which is a strong result. The cosmological durations and the 2424 CE war prophecy have no empirical support, and the prophecy's predictive record is poor. The overall timeline therefore scores low for real-world plausibility, although the composition history is robust.","references":["Wallace, Vesna A. The Inner Kalacakratantra: A Buddhist Tantric View of the Individual (2001)","Davidson, Ronald M. Indian Esoteric Buddhism: A Social History of the Tantric Movement (2002)","Samuel, Geoffrey. Civilized Shamans: Buddhism in Tibetan Societies (1993)","Bishop, Peter. The Myth of Shangri-La: Tibet, Travel Writing and the Western Creation of Sacred Landscape (1989)","Godwin, Joscelyn. Arktos: The Polar Myth in Science, Myth, and Nazi Occultism (1993)","Hopkins, Jeffrey. The Kalachakra Tantra: Rite of Initiation (1985)","Roerich, Nicholas. Shambhala (1930)"]},{"id":"sikh","title":"Sikh scriptures (Guru Granth Sahib, Dasam Granth) on the four yugas","category":"Traditional scriptural","proponents":["Guru Nanak and the early Gurus (as presented in the Guru Granth Sahib tradition)","Guru Gobind Singh (traditionally credited with Bachitar Natak in the Dasam Granth; authorship disputed)","Modern Sikh commentators and katha tradition","Western scholars such as W.H. McLeod who study Sikh textual sources"],"primarySources":["Guru Granth Sahib (compiled by Guru Arjan, 1604 CE; later additions by Guru Gobind Singh, 1708 CE)","Japji Sahib (Guru Nanak, early 16th century)","Bhai Gurdas Vaaran (early 17th century)","Dasam Granth, including Bachitar Natak (attributed to Guru Gobind Singh, 17th-18th century; compilation and authorship debated)","Janamsakhi narratives (16th-19th century, legendary biographies of Guru Nanak)"],"period":"Core Guru Granth Sahib and Japji material originate in the 15th-16th centuries (Guru Nanak onward), with the canon compiled in 1604 CE. Dasam Granth reached its present form in the 18th century, with some portions possibly later.","totalCycleYears":"N/A. The Sikh scriptures do not give a numeric total for a yuga cycle in the way Puranic and Siddhantic texts do. They use yugas mainly as spiritual and moral categories.","durations":{"satya":"N/A. No numeric duration is given in the Guru Granth Sahib or the core Dasam Granth passages on yugas. Satjug is used as a spiritual and moral image of truth and devotion.","treta":"N/A. Treta appears only in passing as a cosmic or narrative age. No duration is specified.","dvapara":"N/A. Dvapar appears mainly as a term in narrative or devotional verse, not as a chronological unit.","kali":"N/A for numeric length. Kal Jug is treated mainly as a spiritual condition of ego, falsehood, and forgetfulness of God. Scripture does not define a start or end date for it."},"kaliStart":"N/A. The scriptures do not place a start date. Sikh tradition does not adopt the Puranic date of 3102 BCE as scriptural. That date comes from later Hindu chronology.","kaliEnd":"N/A. No end date is given in scripture. Some popular Sikh discourse speaks of Kal Jug as ongoing, but this is interpretive, not a chronological prediction.","currentPosition":"As of 2026 CE, Sikh scripture would place the present within Kal Jug in the sense of a spiritual era. Gurbani does not give a calendar position. If one uses the standard Hindu reckoning, 2026 CE corresponds to Kali year 5128, but that is an external calculation, not a Sikh one.","summary":"The Sikh scriptures use the yuga framework mostly in its moral and spiritual sense, not as a chronology. The Guru Granth Sahib refers to the yugas, and especially Kal Jug, to describe a corrupt age of ego and falsehood, while stressing that liberation comes through Naam, Gurbani, and grace, not through cyclical time. The Dasam Granth, particularly Bachitar Natak, contains narrative material about avatars and cosmic ages, but it does not give a numeric yuga timeline either. The Sikh tradition has generally not adopted the Puranic 4,320,000-year mahayuga system or the 432,000-year Kali Yuga as a religious doctrine, and it does not derive dates from it.","keyClaims":["Gurbani treats Kal Jug as a moral and spiritual condition, not a fixed period of years.","The Guru Granth Sahib rejects reliance on ritual, caste, and ceremonial purity as a route to liberation, and this critique applies whatever the age.","Scripture gives no numeric durations for Satjug, Treta, Dvapar, or Kal Jug.","The Dasam Granth includes narrative passages about avatars and cosmic order, but its authorship and cosmological status are disputed.","Sikh doctrine does not require belief in the Puranic 432,000-year Kali Yuga or the 4.32 million-year mahayuga."],"evidence":["The Guru Granth Sahib uses terms such as Jug, Satjug, and Kal Jug in devotional verse. The usage is largely metaphorical, and the theme of salvation through Naam recurs across the text.","Japji Sahib refers to ages and cosmic cycles in a way that places them below the reality of the Creator, which supports a non-chronological reading.","Bhai Gurdas, whose Vaaran are considered authoritative exposition, uses Kal Jug to describe moral decline in his time.","Scholars such as W.H. McLeod have argued that Sikh textual tradition distances itself from the inherited Hindu cosmological frame, though not completely.","The Dasam Granth authorship question is widely discussed, so statements attributed to Guru Gobind Singh there should be treated with caution. Claims about yuga specifics in Bachitar Natak are contested."],"criticisms":["Most of the scriptural claims above are based on general knowledge of the texts, not verified line by line in this run. Specific pauri or ang numbers should be checked against a primary edition before publication.","Because the Sikh texts lack chronology, any timeline built on them is an extrapolation, not a reading of scripture.","The Dasam Granth authorship dispute means that treating its cosmology as Guru Gobind Singh's own view is contested, and some Sikh authorities reject parts of it.","The idea that Kal Jug is a spiritual condition can be read as a way of setting aside the question of historical dating altogether. That is a valid theological position, but it does not answer the question asked.","Popular Sikh discourse sometimes presents Kal Jug and the yugas with more chronological precision than the scriptures do, so secondary sources should not be mixed with primary texts."],"plausibility":2,"plausibilityReason":"As a literal chronology, the Sikh scriptural framing has no numeric content to test, so it cannot be checked against history or astronomy. Its value as a description of Sikh moral thought is high, but that is a different kind of claim. Rated low (2) only in the sense that no real-world timeline is offered for evaluation. Any date for Kali Yuga drawn from the wider Hindu system is not Sikh scripture and inherits that system's implausibility.","references":["Guru Granth Sahib, English translation by Dr. Sant Singh Khalsa or the SGPC edition (verify page and ang citations before use)","W.H. McLeod, Textual Sources for the Study of Sikhism (Manchester University Press, 1984)","W.H. McLeod, Who is a Sikh? The Problem of Sikh Identity (Clarendon Press, 1989)","Bhai Gurdas Vaaran, edited texts available from SGPC","Sikh Rehat Maryada (1945), Sikh Religion Document of the SGPC"]},{"id":"maya","title":"Maya Long Count and claimed links to the yugas","category":"Alternative/fringe","proponents":["No scholar in Mesoamerican or Indological studies argues for a Maya-yuga equivalence","Popular and online writers who juxtapose the Long Count with the Kali Yuga epoch (unnamed in sources reviewed)","José Argüelles (Dreamspell / 13 Baktun framing, The Mayan Factor, 1987)","John Major Jenkins (Maya Cosmogenesis 2012, 1998; precession-based 2012 reading, not yuga-based)"],"primarySources":["Classic Maya inscriptions (c. 3rd-10th c. CE), e.g. Tortuguero Monument 6 (c. 7th c. CE) referring to the 13th baktun","Dresden Codex (Postclassic, c. 11th-12th c. CE) for Long Count and almanac material","Earliest Long Count dates: Chiapa de Corzo Stela 1 (c. 36 BCE) and Tres Zapotes Stela C (c. 32 BCE)","Aryabhatiya (499 CE) and Surya Siddhanta (c. 4th-5th c. CE) for the Kali Yuga epoch of 3102 BCE","Puranic yuga durations (Vishnu Purana, Manu Smriti) for the 4,320,000-year mahayuga"],"period":"The Long Count is a Maya system used from roughly the 1st century BCE, with the zero date placed by correlation at 3114 BCE (GMT correlation, 584283 JDN). The yuga epochs come from Indian astronomical texts of the 5th century CE onward, with the 3102 BCE Kali epoch derived retrospectively. Links to yugas are modern (late 20th to 21st century popular writing).","totalCycleYears":"The Long Count great cycle is 13 baktuns, about 5,125.37 years (13 x 144,000 days). This is not a yuga; the Maya system has no equivalent of satya, treta, dvapara or kali. Mahayuga is 4,320,000 years for comparison.","durations":{"satya":"N/A - no Maya equivalent. Nearest analogue: the Long Count's mythic creation date 4 Ahau 8 Cumku (GMT 3114 BCE), which is a creation epoch, not a yuga","treta":"N/A - no Maya equivalent","dvapara":"N/A - no Maya equivalent","kali":"N/A - no Maya equivalent. Analogue comparison only: 13 baktuns = about 5,125 years, versus Kali Yuga of 432,000 human years in the Puranic/Surya Siddhanta reckoning"},"kaliStart":"N/A for Maya. For comparison, the Kali Yuga epoch is 3102 BCE (Aryabhata, astronomical reckoning), and the Long Count zero is 3114 BCE (GMT correlation). The 12-year gap is a numerical near-coincidence, not a documented link.","kaliEnd":"N/A for Maya. Kali Yuga is nominally 432,000 years, ending around 428,898 CE (per the traditional reckoning). The Long Count's 13-baktun completion was 13.0.0.0.0 on 21 Dec 2012 CE (GMT), which does not match any yuga boundary.","currentPosition":"As of 2026, the Long Count has passed 13.0.0.0.0 (21 Dec 2012 GMT) and is in the early part of its next great-cycle count. Maya inscriptions do not describe this as an end-of-age event. Under the yuga reading, Kali Yuga is about 5,127 years elapsed by 2026 CE using the 3102 BCE epoch, a figure close to 13 baktuns but not identical and not anchored to the 2012 date.","summary":"The Maya Long Count is a real, well-documented Mesoamerican dating system built on a base-20 count of days, with a 13-baktun great cycle of about 5,125 years. Its 2012 turnover generated a large body of end-of-world and New Age interpretation. Claims that the Long Count maps onto the Hindu yugas are not in any primary Maya or Indian source. They rest on arithmetic coincidence (about 5,125 years for a baktun cycle, about 12 years between the Long Count zero and the Kali epoch) and on a shared \"cyclical ages\" idea. Critically, the Maya system has no yuga structure and no documented contact with Indian chronology, and the 2012 date and the Kali timeline do not coincide.","keyClaims":["The Maya Long Count's 13-baktun cycle (about 5,125 years) corresponds to the Kali Yuga timescale","The Long Count zero (3114 BCE) and the Kali Yuga epoch (3102 BCE) are linked, implying a shared origin","The 13.0.0.0.0 turnover (21 Dec 2012) marks the end of an age, paralleling yuga transitions","Cyclical age systems in Maya and Hindu thought independently converge on the same structure"],"evidence":["Long Count arithmetic is correct: 13 baktuns x 144,000 days ~ 5,125.37 tropical years","Long Count zero at 3114 BCE (GMT) and Kali epoch at 3102 BCE are both ancient, both 'start of the age' dates","The Tortuguero Monument 6 inscription does reference the 13th baktun, so the 2012 significance is a real Maya concept","Some Maya-related cosmologies do describe successive ages/creations (e.g. the Popol Vuh's creation attempts), a superficial parallel to yugas"],"criticisms":["No primary Maya text connects the Long Count to Indian yugas; the linkage is a modern comparison, not a documented historical relationship","The Kali Yuga epoch (3102 BCE) is itself a retrospective astronomical reconstruction in Aryabhata and the Surya Siddhanta, not a fixed historical date","The Long Count zero (3114 BCE) is a GMT-correlation artifact; Maya scribes did not count from a dated creation in the modern sense, and correlation constants vary by several days to decades","A 12-year gap between two ancient epochs is weak evidence, especially since epochs are chosen by the reckoner rather than observed","The 13-baktun turnover falls in 2012, while Kali-based arithmetic points to about 2024; the two timelines do not agree","Yuga durations are based on divine years (x360) in Puranic texts, so the 432,000-year figure is not comparable to a human-year calendar without additional conversion","The 2012 'end of the world' readings were largely rejected by Maya specialists (e.g. Michael Coe, Linda Schele's colleagues), who see the turnover as a rollover, comparable to a car odometer","Cherry-picking of dates and coincidences is a known pattern in fringe calendar comparison"],"plausibility":2,"plausibilityReason":"The Long Count is a real calendar with verified inscriptions, but the claim that it encodes or matches the yugas has no primary-source support. Its numerical parallels are coincidental and the dates do not align (2012 vs about 2024). Plausibility is low as a real-world timeline, though the Long Count itself is well established.","references":["Thompson, J. E. S. (1960). Maya Hieroglyphic Writing: Introduction. University of Oklahoma Press","Coe, M. D. and Van Stone, M. (2001). Reading the Maya Glyphs. Thames and Hudson","Schele, L. and Freidel, D. (1990). A Forest of Kings. William Morrow","Jenkins, J. M. (1998). Maya Cosmogenesis 2012. Bear and Company","Argüelles, J. (1987). The Mayan Factor. Bear and Company","Aryabhatiya of Aryabhata (499 CE), kalakriyapada on yuga epochs","Note: web verification was not performed in this run; citations are from prior knowledge and should be checked before publication"]},{"id":"kali-characteristics","title":"Textual Kali Yuga lakshanas (decline markers) compared with modern claims","category":"Traditional scriptural","proponents":["Traditional Hindu commentators and modern popular writers who read Kali-age descriptions as a diagnosis of the present","Sri Yukteswar Giri (The Holy Science, 1894) as a cosmological-chronological reader of yuga decline, though he is not primarily a lakshana commentator","Modern popular Hindu and new-age authors who cite Kali-age lists in books and online talks (no single authoritative source)"],"primarySources":["Mahabharata, Vana Parva (Markandeya's account of the coming ages, around ch. 188-190 in the Critical Edition numbering); composed c. 400 BCE-400 CE in layers","Manusmriti 1.81-86 (yuga durations and proportional decline of dharma); c. 200 BCE-200 CE","Vishnu Purana, Book IV, ch. 24 (Kali-age decline: wealth as the basis of status, weakened ritual and social order); c. 300-900 CE","Bhagavata Purana, Book 12, ch. 2-3 (Kali-age lakshanas, including dharma standing on one leg and rule by corrupt rulers); c. 6th-10th century CE","Brahma/Surya Siddhanta and Aryabhatiya (Kali epoch arithmetic only, not lakshanas)"],"period":"Core lists in Mahabharata and Puranas, roughly 400 BCE to 1000 CE; modern appropriation mainly from the 19th century onward","totalCycleYears":"4,320,000 years (Mahayuga of 12,000 divine years at 360 human years each)","durations":{"satya":"1,728,000 years (4,800 divine years)","treta":"1,296,000 years (3,600 divine years)","dvapara":"864,000 years (2,400 divine years)","kali":"432,000 years (1,200 divine years)"},"kaliStart":"3102 BCE (conventional epoch used in Indian almanacs; Aryabhatiya's Kali-epoch arithmetic relates to this)","kaliEnd":"Approximately 428,898 CE by the conventional 432,000-year count","currentPosition":"2026 CE corresponds to Kali year 5128 in the conventional count (about 5,127 completed years since 3102 BCE). Texts describe it as the worst age, so the claim is that the present is inside the decline described by the lists.","summary":"Classical texts describe Kali-age decline using lists of lakshanas (markers): wealth replacing lineage or virtue as the basis of status, weakening of ritual and truthfulness, corrupt or predatory rulers, declining human stature and lifespan, irregular seasons, and dharma diminished to a single quarter (often shown as standing on one leg). The lists appear in the Mahabharata's Vana Parva, the Vishnu and Bhagavata Puranas, and the Manusmriti's proportional scheme. Modern readers often treat them as a checklist and say the present matches them, especially in economic inequality, political corruption, and declining moral norms. The claim is interpretive, not predictive: the lists are generic moral and social complaints that can be found in almost any era, and their chronology is conventional rather than observed. Textual evidence is clear; the claim that the present fits is a retrospective match.","keyClaims":["The Kali age is marked by decline in dharma, which is shown as one-quarter of its full strength in the Manusmriti and as dharma standing on one leg in the Bhagavata.","The Puranic lists say wealth replaces lineage, learning, or virtue as the basis of rank and marriage, which the modern world is said to reflect.","Human lifespan and stature shrink in Kali, which some modern readers see as evidence of degeneration.","Political rulers in Kali become predatory, and taxation and state power are said to fall on the poor, which some modern readers match to present-day governance.","The seasons and rains are said to become irregular in Kali, which some modern readers connect to climate change.","Because the texts describe the present age as Kali, the present is in a time of moral decline and not of progress."],"evidence":["The lakshana lists exist in primary texts (Mahabharata Vana Parva; Vishnu Purana IV.24; Bhagavata XII.2-3; Manusmriti 1.81-86). Their content is verifiable, and the quotations are paraphrases, not verbatim quotes in this entry.","Modern commentators such as Swami Prabhavananda and other Vedanta writers have discussed the Kali-age decline as a moral and spiritual condition rather than a literal timeline.","Social complaints about wealth, corruption, and declining norms are present in every era of Indian literature, so modern matches are common and widely cited.","The Mahabharata Vana Parva prophecy is presented as a teaching by the sage Markandeya to Yudhishthira and is not a dated historical account."],"criticisms":["The lakshanas are generic. Complaints about greed, corrupt rulers, and short lives appear in Vedic, Buddhist, Jain, and Greek texts, so matching them to any age shows little about the present.","Texts were composed centuries after the ages they describe and are often written as prophecy in the past tense, so the lists may reflect the authors' own time rather than forecast later ones.","Life expectancy has risen sharply in the modern era, which contradicts the claim that lifespan shrinks in Kali.","Average stature has increased in most populations over the last two centuries, which contradicts the claim that humans become smaller in Kali.","Modern readers select matching lakshanas and ignore those that do not fit, a classic confirmation-bias pattern.","The idea that the present is a decline age conflicts with the Puranic cycle, which predicts a later renewal (the next Satya age) only after a very long period, so it gives no testable forecast.","Classical texts treat the Kali-age decline as a moral and philosophical framing; literal claims about the present are a modern development.","The 3102 BCE epoch is a conventional, later computational anchor and is not supported by independent historical evidence."],"plausibility":2,"plausibilityReason":"As a description of textual content, the lists are real and accurately reported. As a claim that the present matches Kali-age decline, it is unfalsifiable and fits almost any era. Measurable claims (lifespan, stature) are contradicted by the data. Plausibility of the claim that the present is a literal Kali-age decline is low; plausibility as moral commentary is a value judgment rather than a historical claim.","references":["Mahabharata, Vana Parva (Critical Edition, Markandeya section)","Vishnu Purana, Book IV, ch. 24 (trans. H. H. Wilson, 1840)","Bhagavata Purana, Book XII, ch. 2-3 (trans. various)","Manusmriti 1.81-86 (trans. Georg Buhler, Sacred Books of the East vol. 25)","Aryabhatiya, Kalakriya chapter (Kali-epoch arithmetic)","Sri Yukteswar Giri, The Holy Science (1894)","Note: quotations and chapter numbers are from memory and should be checked against the cited editions before publication; no live web lookup was run for this entry."]},{"id":"numerology","title":"Numerical structure of yuga numbers (432, 108, 12, 360; sexagesimal vs. symbolic)","category":"Astronomical/Siddhantic","proponents":["Traditional commentators of the Puranas and Manusmriti","Siddhantic astronomers (Aryabhata, Brahmagupta, and the Surya Siddhanta tradition)","Modern historians of Indian astronomy (David Pingree, Kim Plofker) who analyze the arithmetic structure","Popular Hindu-revivalist writers who read cosmic meaning into 108 and 432"],"primarySources":["Manusmriti 1.69-71 (composed roughly 2nd c. BCE to 2nd c. CE): gives the 4:3:2:1 yuga ratio in divine years with sandhyas, totaling 12,000 divine years","Mahabharata, Vana Parva ch. 148-149 (c. 400 BCE to 400 CE layers): repeats the 12,000 divine-year yuga-set","Vishnu Purana and other Puranas: 12,000 divine years x 360 = 4,320,000 human years","Vedanga Jyotisha (Lagadha, c. 1st millennium BCE): a 5-year yuga of 1,830 days (62 lunar months), an early calendrical cycle that is not the 432-based system","Aryabhatiya (499 CE): mahayuga of 4,320,000 years and planetary revolution counts","Surya Siddhanta (c. 4th-5th c. CE, revised later): mahayuga 4,320,000 years, kalpa of 1,000 mahayugas","Brahmagupta, Brahmasphutasiddhanta (628 CE): uses the same mahayuga framework"],"period":"Core numbers: Vedic-era calendrics (Vedanga Jyotisha, c. 1st millennium BCE). The 4:3:2:1 yuga-set with 12,000 divine years appears in Puranic and epic texts (roughly 2nd c. BCE to 5th c. CE). The 4,320,000 mahayuga is standardized in Siddhantic astronomy (c. 4th-7th c. CE).","totalCycleYears":"4,320,000 years (mahayuga = 12,000 divine years x 360). Kalpa = 1,000 mahayugas = 4.32 billion years. Some Puranic accounts include sandhya and sandhyamsa periods, which bring the total to the same 12,000 divine-year figure.","durations":{"satya":"1,728,000 years (4,800 divine years; Manusmriti gives 4,000 + 800 sandhya)","treta":"1,296,000 years (3,600 divine years; Manusmriti 3,000 + 600 sandhya)","dvapara":"864,000 years (2,400 divine years; Manusmriti 2,000 + 400 sandhya)","kali":"432,000 years (1,200 divine years; Manusmriti 1,000 + 200 sandhya)"},"kaliStart":"3102 BCE (conventional epoch of the Siddhantic tradition, computed by astronomers rather than observed; the Kali epoch is later than the Vedic material)","kaliEnd":"About 428,900 CE on the traditional count (432,000 years after 3102 BCE). Not a prediction, since the count is symbolic.","currentPosition":"In 2026 CE, the traditional Kali year is about 5127 (CE year + 3101), so about 5,127 years of the 432,000-year Kali Yuga have elapsed and roughly 426,873 remain on this numbering.","summary":"The yuga numbers are built from a small set of multiplicative ideas. The Manusmriti and Puranas use a 12,000 divine-year total, with 4:3:2:1 ratios for the four yugas. A divine year is 360 human years, so 12,000 x 360 = 4,320,000 and Kali = 1,200 x 360 = 432,000. The number 432 is 12 x 36 and 4,320 = 12 x 360, so 108 (a quarter of 432) and 360 (a degree-circle and a solar-year approximation) recur. Many scholars read the structure as a Siddhantic astronomical convention, with 360 tied to degrees and the year, and 60/12 as sexagesimal and zodiacal subdivisions. The sandhya and sandhyamsa add-ons adjust the sums to fit the 4:3:2:1 pattern. Whether the numbers reflect real astronomy, arithmetic play, or symbolic enlargement is debated.","keyClaims":["The 4:3:2:1 yuga ratio with a 12,000 divine-year total is the textual core, attested in Manusmriti 1.69-71 and the Mahabharata.","The 4,320,000-year mahayuga is a Siddhantic standardization (Aryabhatiya, Surya Siddhanta), turning divine years into human years by a factor of 360.","The number 432 and its multiples (4,320; 432,000) factor as 2^4 x 3^3 x 5 and 12 x 36 or 12 x 360, which links them to the 12 zodiac signs and 360 degrees/days.","The number 108 is a quarter of 432 and also 27 x 4 (27 nakshatras, 4 padas each), a link to lunar mansions that is a later interpretive layer.","Sexagesimal structure (base-60) is present in some Indian astronomical arithmetic, and some scholars trace the 4,320,000-type numbers to Mesopotamian sexagesimal practice, but direct derivation is not established.","The Vedic/Vedanga Jyotisha 5-year yuga is a separate and much earlier calendrical cycle not based on 432."],"evidence":["Arithmetic is internally consistent: 4:3:2:1 applied to 12,000 divine years gives 4,800 / 3,600 / 2,400 / 1,200 divine years, and times 360 gives 1,728,000 / 1,296,000 / 864,000 / 432,000 years.","The Siddhantic texts give explicit planetary revolution counts per mahayuga (for example, Aryabhata's 1,577,917,500 sidereal revolutions for the Sun-based system as commonly cited), which show the cycle was used computationally.","Pingree (1978, 'The Greek influence on early Indian astronomy') and later work by Pingree and Plofker discuss sexagesimal and Mesopotamian parallels as possible influences on Indian astronomical arithmetic.","Multiple independent texts (Manusmriti, Mahabharata, Puranas, Siddhantas) agree on the 12,000 divine-year total, which indicates a shared numerical convention rather than one author's invention."],"criticisms":["The 360 multiplier is a convention chosen to make a divine year equal the 360-day year used in ancient reckoning, so the numbers reflect arithmetic choice, not observed cosmic periods.","Numerical coincidences (108, 432, 12, 360) are easy to find in any system and over-read: 108 is also just 2 x 54, and the 108 Sun-Earth diameter ratio is folklore (the actual ratio is about 109).","Sandhya and sandhyamsa add-ons are adjustments that can be seen as retrofitting to make the 4:3:2:1 sums match 12,000 rather than as independent astronomy.","The Mesopotamian/sexagesimal origin claim is contested: shared base-60 features do not prove borrowing, and textual dating of the Siddhantas makes transmission hard to pin down.","Calling the 432-based cycles 'astronomical' overstates the case: the Siddhantas do compute planetary positions, but the mahayuga itself is a theoretical reset point with no observed start.","Popular numerology (e.g., Sun-diameter ratio claims, 108 beads, 108 Upanishads) is post-hoc and does not show that the yuga numbers encode hidden knowledge."],"plausibility":1,"plausibilityReason":"As a claim that yuga durations are literal historical or geological periods, the numerology is not supported: there is no evidence for a 4.32-million-year cycle or for a 3102 BCE world reset. As a description of how the numbers are structured arithmetically and within Siddhantic computation, it is well supported (rating 8 for that narrower claim). The rating here is for the timeline reading. Written from memory in this run; no web fetch was done to re-verify citations or dates.","references":["Olivelle, Patrick (2005). Manu's Code of Law (Manusmriti translation). Oxford University Press.","Pingree, David (1981). Jyotihsastra: Astral and Mathematical Literature. Otto Harrassowitz.","Pingree, David (1978). 'The Greek influence on early Indian astronomy.' In Proceedings of the International Colloquium on Astronomy, Astrology and Mythology in Ancient Civilizations (Ancient Greek and Indian astronomy).","Plofker, Kim (2009). Mathematics in India. Princeton University Press.","Burgess, Ebenezer (trans., 1860). Surya Siddhanta, ed. Whitney (Journal of the American Oriental Society).","Kuppanna Sastry, T.S. (1985). Vedanga Jyotisha of Lagadha (translation with notes)."]},{"id":"theosophy","title":"Blavatsky and Theosophy: Yugas Within a Root-Race Framework","category":"Modern spiritual/esoteric","proponents":["Helena Petrovna Blavatsky","Henry Steel Olcott","Annie Besant","C. W. Leadbeater","W. Q. Judge"],"primarySources":["Isis Unveiled (Blavatsky, 1877)","The Secret Doctrine, 2 vols. (Blavatsky, 1888)","The Esoteric Christianity / The Ancient Wisdom (Annie Besant, 1897)","The Occult World (A. P. Sinnett, 1881) and the Mahatma Letters (1880s)"],"period":"Originated 1875-1888 (Theosophical Society founded 1875 in New York; Secret Doctrine published 1888), elaborated by Besant and Leadbeater in the 1890s-1900s","totalCycleYears":"Secret Doctrine figures: Mahayuga 4,320,000 years; Manvantara 306,720,000 years (71 Mahayugas); Kalpa 4,320,000,000 years. Root-race cycle is a separate scheme of seven root races per round, each spanning hundreds of thousands to over a million years.","durations":{"satya":"N/A in Theosophy's main scheme; Blavatsky adopts the Puranic Satya Yuga (1,728,000 years) as part of the Mahayuga but does not build her history on it","treta":"N/A as a core framework; 1,296,000 years in the Puranic scheme she cites","dvapara":"N/A as a core framework; 864,000 years in the Puranic scheme she cites","kali":"432,000 years (Puranic figure she accepts; she dates its start to 3102 BCE)"},"kaliStart":"3102 BCE (accepted by Blavatsky from the traditional Indian chronology)","kaliEnd":"By arithmetic, about 428,898 CE for a 432,000-year Kali Yuga. Theosophical writers rarely stress this date; the root-race timeline matters more to them.","currentPosition":"In 2026, roughly 5,128 years into the Kali Yuga by the 3102 BCE start. In root-race terms, humanity is in the Fifth (Aryan) Root Race, Fifth Sub-race (Anglo-Saxon/Teutonic) of the Fourth Round, per Blavatsky and Besant.","summary":"Blavatsky's The Secret Doctrine (1888) takes Hindu cosmology (Manvantaras, Mahayugas, Kalpa) as a frame but replaces the four-yuga decline with a scheme of seven root races per planetary round, each subdivided into sub-races. She claims these come from the Stanzas of Dzyan, a text she says was held by Tibetan adepts and which no one else has been shown to possess. The current Aryan root race is placed on a nested timeline that reaches back hundreds of thousands to millions of years and includes lost continents, Lemuria and Atlantis. Theosophy also keeps the Kali Yuga dating from Indian astronomy, so yugas and root races coexist as two overlapping clocks. Annie Besant and C. W. Leadbeater later systematized the sub-race names and the seven-root-race model in popular form.","keyClaims":["Humanity passes through seven root races per round, with the Fifth (Aryan) Root Race currently dominant.","Lemuria (Third Root Race) and Atlantis (Fourth Root Race) were real continents that sank in prehistory, and Atlantis's destruction is dated by Blavatsky to roughly 850,000 years ago.","Indian yuga and manvantara figures (4,320,000-year Mahayuga, 306,720,000-year Manvantara) describe actual cosmic cycles.","The Kali Yuga began in 3102 BCE, an astronomical date she accepts from Indian tradition.","The Stanzas of Dzyan are an ancient esoteric source revealed through Tibetan adepts (Mahatmas)."],"evidence":["Claimed occult revelation through Mahatmas and the Stanzas of Dzyan; no independently verified manuscript has been produced.","Blavatsky's use of Sanskrit and Puranic terms, which shows familiarity with Indian cosmology but reads it through a Western esoteric lens.","Lemuria was originally proposed by zoologist P. L. Sclater in 1864 to explain lemur distribution, and Blavatsky adopted and extended it.","Ignatius Donnelly's Atlantis: The Antediluvian World (1882) and earlier Plato-based Atlantis speculation supplied the Atlantis material.","Theosophy's internal consistency, its claimed 'esoteric' tradition of preserved knowledge, and the appeal of a long-view cosmology."],"criticisms":["The Hodgson Report (Society for Psychical Research, 1885) concluded that Blavatsky had fabricated Mahatma letters and phenomena; this damaged her credibility, though later scholars debate the report's own methods.","Indologists such as Max Muller and critics like Subba Row and Mohini Chatterji pointed to misreadings of Sanskrit and Hindu texts and to the invention of 'ancient' sources.","Geology, archaeology, and paleontology show no evidence for continents like Lemuria or Atlantis sinking in the claimed period; Plato's Atlantis was a moral fable, not a dated history.","Human evolution in the Theosophical scheme (ethereal, then Hyperborean, Lemurian, Atlantean forms) has no basis in biology or anthropology, and sub-race hierarchies carry racist implications.","Blavatsky borrowed freely from Western sources (Donnelly, Sclater, Eliphas Levi, Kabbalah, Spiritualism) while presenting the result as ancient, which scholars such as Joscelyn Godwin and Wouter Hanegraaff describe as bricolage rather than preserved tradition.","The Stanzas of Dzyan were never produced for independent examination, and Theosophical 'clairvoyant' confirmations cannot be checked."],"plausibility":1,"plausibilityReason":"Treated as history, the root-race timeline is contradicted by geology, archaeology, genetics, and paleoanthropology. Its key sources are either unverifiable (Dzyan, Mahatmas) or traceable to 19th-century Western speculation (Lemuria from Sclater, Atlantis from Plato and Donnelly). The yuga figures it reuses are Puranic cosmological units, not measured durations. The framework is coherent as a mythic or spiritual system, which is the only sense in which it can be assessed.","references":["Blavatsky, H. P. (1877). Isis Unveiled. New York: J. W. Bouton.","Blavatsky, H. P. (1888). The Secret Doctrine. London: Theosophical Publishing Company.","Godwin, J. (1994). The Theosophical Enlightenment. Albany: SUNY Press.","Hanegraaff, W. J. (2012). Esotericism and the Academy. Cambridge University Press.","Hodgson, R. (1885). Report on the Phenomena connected with the Theosophical Society. Proceedings of the Society for Psychical Research, vol. 3.","Sclater, P. L. (1864). The Mammals of Madagascar. Quarterly Journal of Science.","Donnelly, I. (1882). Atlantis: The Antediluvian World. New York: Harper.","Besant, A. (1897). The Ancient Wisdom. London: Theosophical Publishing Society."]},{"id":"tantra-shaiva","title":"Tantric and Shaiva views on the yugas (Kali Yuga as the age of tantra)","category":"Traditional scriptural","proponents":["Kaula and Shakta tantric traditions","Kashmir Shaiva exegetes (e.g., Abhinavagupta, c. 950-1016 CE, who engages yuga ideas only lightly)","Modern popularizers of Mahanirvana Tantra (e.g., Arthur Avalon / Sir John Woodroffe, 1913 translation)"],"primarySources":["Mahanirvana Tantra (Mahanirvana Tantra, Kaula text; most scholars date it to roughly the 18th century CE, though this is debated)","Kularnava Tantra (Kaula text, probably medieval; its date is uncertain)","Shiva Purana and Linga Purana (Shaiva Puranas; yuga framework is the standard Puranic one, with Shiva avatars per Dvapara yuga)","Puranic yuga cosmology (Vishnu, Matsya, Bhagavata Puranas; roughly 1st millennium CE onward)"],"period":"Tantric claims that Kali Yuga favors tantric practice appear in medieval Kaula texts (roughly 10th century onward); the Mahanirvana Tantra, the best-known text making this claim, is most likely 18th century CE.","totalCycleYears":"4,320,000 years (Mahayuga), assumed from the standard Puranic framework; tantric texts generally do not produce their own chronology","durations":{"satya":"1,728,000 years (standard Puranic value, assumed)","treta":"1,296,000 years (standard Puranic value, assumed)","dvapara":"864,000 years (standard Puranic value, assumed)","kali":"432,000 years (standard Puranic value, assumed)"},"kaliStart":"3102 BCE (conventional date, 18 February 3102 BCE)","kaliEnd":"Approximately 428,898 CE (end of 432,000 years, if the standard duration is taken literally)","currentPosition":"About 5,127 years into Kali Yuga in 2026 (Kali Samvat about 5127-5128). Tantric texts treat Kali Yuga as the present, favorable age for their practices, not as a period of decline to be endured.","summary":"Tantric and Shaiva texts mostly accept the standard Puranic four-yuga scheme and do not produce a new chronology. Their distinctive move is soteriological: they argue that Kali Yuga, usually seen as the worst age, is the best time for tantric practice. The Mahanirvana Tantra says that Vedic and smarta ritual is ineffective in the Kali age, while the Kaula path, with its mantras, initiation, and ritual, is effective and open to all castes and genders. Shaiva Puranas use the yuga framework mainly for mythology, such as Shiva's incarnations in successive Dvapara yugas, rather than for dating. Most scholars treat these claims as religious reinterpretation, not historical or astronomical timelines.","keyClaims":["Kali Yuga is the age in which tantric (Kaula) practice is especially efficacious, and in which Vedic/smarta ritual is less effective (a claim in the Mahanirvana Tantra and related Kaula texts).","Tantric practice is open to people regardless of caste or gender, which is presented as a feature of the Kali age.","The Puranic yuga durations (4,320,000-year mahayuga; Kali 432,000 years) are generally assumed rather than recalculated.","Shiva's incarnations in successive yugas (for example, the 28 Dvapara-yuga incarnations in some Shaiva Puranas) are mythological, not chronological evidence.","The Kali Yuga's favorable status for tantra is a soteriological and ritual claim, not a prediction of historical events."],"evidence":["The Mahanirvana Tantra's own verses in its opening chapters describe Kali Yuga as the age for the Kaula path and criticize Vedic ritual as inadequate for the age (textual content, not an external measurement).","Tantric texts in general claim that their practices are the most effective method in the current age, which fits the claim that Kali is the age of tantra.","The Puranic yuga framework is widely attested in Sanskrit literature, so the duration numbers are textually grounded even if not empirically verifiable."],"criticisms":["The Mahanirvana Tantra's attribution to ancient Shiva-Parvati dialogue is a literary frame. Scholars date the text to around the 18th century CE, so it cannot be an ancient source for yuga chronology.","Tantric texts do not give an independent, testable timeline. Their yuga claims are ritual and devotional, so they cannot be checked against history or astronomy.","The Kali-as-tantra-age claim is partly a reversal of the usual decline narrative. It is a theological justification for a particular ritual system, which makes it a poor guide to cosmic time.","Some Shaiva Puranic yuga material is internally inconsistent across texts, and Kashmir Shaiva philosophers like Abhinavagupta rarely build chronologies from it. Using these texts as a timeline oversteps their genre.","Attempts to link tantric yuga claims to specific dates or historical events are modern interpretation and are not supported by the texts themselves."],"plausibility":2,"plausibilityReason":"The yuga durations and Kali start date are a traditional convention with no empirical basis for the 3102 BCE start or 432,000-year length. The Mahanirvana Tantra's Kali-as-tantra-age claim is a religious and ritual position, not a historical or scientific one. Its value is as a record of how medieval and early modern Shaiva and Kaula traditions reinterpreted the yuga scheme, not as a correct timeline.","references":["Woodroffe, Sir John (Arthur Avalon), trans., Mahanirvana Tantra (1913), Ganesh & Co., Madras","Goudriaan, Teun and Sanjukta Gupta, Hindu Tantric and Sakta Literature (Otto Harrassowitz, 1981)","Padoux, Andre, Vac: The Concept of the Word in Selected Hindu Tantras (SUNY Press, 1990)","Sanderson, Alexis, 'The Saiva Age: The Rise and Dominance of Saivism during the Early Medieval Period', in Genesis and Development of Tantrism (2009)","Sanderson, Alexis, 'Purity and Power among the Brahmans of Kashmir' (1985), in The Category of the Person, ed. Carrithers et al."]},{"id":"aurobindo-allegorical","title":"Psychological and allegorical readings: yugas as states of consciousness (Sri Aurobindo and others)","category":"Modern spiritual/esoteric","proponents":["Sri Aurobindo (1872-1950)","The Mother (Mirra Alfassa), Aurobindo's collaborator, who extended the Supermind framework","Sri Yukteswar Giri (1855-1936), whose cosmic-cycle scheme blends astronomy with a spiritual-mental progression (related but distinct)","Paramahansa Yogananda, who popularised Yukteswar's scheme (The Second Coming of Christ, 1950s)","René Guénon and Julius Evola (Traditionalist school), who read the yuga sequence as a qualitative decline of consciousness and civilisation","Comparative analogue: C. G. Jung, Aion (1951), reading the Platonic-age sequence psychologically"],"primarySources":["Sri Aurobindo, The Secret of the Veda (serialised in Arya c. 1914-1915; book 1956), which treats Vedic symbolism psychologically","Sri Aurobindo, The Human Cycle (serialised in Arya 1915-1918; book 1949), on successive social eras framed by a dominant psychological motive and the varna-like sequence","Sri Aurobindo, The Life Divine (serialised in Arya 1914-1919; book 1939-1940), on the ascent of consciousness and the Supermind","Sri Aurobindo, Letters on Yoga and talks and letters compiled in the Collected Works (various dates)","Sri Yukteswar Giri, The Holy Science (1894), for the contrasting cosmic-cycle reading","René Guénon, The Crisis of the Modern World (1927) and The Reign of Quantity (1945), for the Traditionalist reading","Julius Evola, Revolt Against the Modern World (1934), for the Traditionalist reading"],"period":"Core ideas c. 1914-1918 (Aurobindo's Arya essays); Human Cycles book 1949; later Integral Yoga commentary through the 1950s; Traditionalist versions 1927-1951","totalCycleYears":"N/A. Aurobindo gives no fixed cycle length. He treats the yugas as qualitative phases of consciousness and society, not calendar intervals. (Contrast Yukteswar's 24,000-year scheme, which is a different, literalist-cosmic reading.)","durations":{"satya":"N/A. No durations given. Aurobindo treats Satya as a qualitative state, the age of truth-consciousness or harmony, not a span of years.","treta":"N/A. No durations given. Treta is read as a phase of dynamic, will-driven and heroic-vital consciousness.","dvapara":"N/A. No durations given. Dvapara is read as a phase of mental-intellectual and divided consciousness.","kali":"N/A. No durations given. Kali is read as a phase of material-vital obscurity or fragmentation, with the potential for a turn toward the spiritual."},"kaliStart":"Not dated by Aurobindo. Readers who want a number usually borrow the traditional 3102 BCE reckoning and then read it as a symbolic marker, not a historical event.","kaliEnd":"No calendar date. In this view the end of Kali is a psychological-spiritual transformation (a shift in dominant consciousness, associated with the descent of the Supermind), not a fixed year. Traditional reckoning would place it roughly 426,000 years from now, but this view does not depend on that figure.","currentPosition":"In 2026 this view says humanity is in a transitional, Kali-tinged phase, marked by mental fragmentation, ego-driven material striving, and crisis. It also reads the present as a possible threshold for a new spiritual consciousness. Aurobindo's own writing is more optimistic than pessimistic. He treats the period as one of preparation rather than simple decline. Specific claims about the present are interpretive and do not come with a date.","summary":"This reading treats the four yugas as successive or layered states of human consciousness and social organisation rather than as literal millennia. Sri Aurobindo, in The Human Cycle and related essays, reads the sequence as a movement from a dominant spiritual or harmonious orientation (Satya), through dynamic vital will (Treta) and intellectual division (Dvapara), to material-vital fragmentation (Kali). He insists the cycle is not a closed wheel of inevitable decline. The sequence is open to a turn driven by a higher consciousness (the Supermind). Other figures follow related lines. The Traditionalists (Guénon, Evola) read Kali as a metaphysical decline, and Yukteswar links the yugas to a mental-spiritual progression within a cosmic cycle. The approach is interpretive and flexible, and it does not make verifiable chronological predictions.","keyClaims":["The yugas describe phases of consciousness that can be seen in individuals and in collective human history at once","The four-age sequence is a qualitative movement, not a set of fixed time spans","Kali is not a permanent or final condition. Its darkness can be the precondition for a new spiritual development","The cycle is open-ended and can be redirected by spiritual evolution (Aurobindo's Supermind), instead of being a closed wheel","Vedic and Puranic symbols (such as the figures of the Gita and the Veda) carry psychological meanings beyond their literal narratives","Yukteswar's scheme ties the yugas to a mental and spiritual progression of humanity, with a cosmic-astronomical basis and a claimed recent turn toward Dvapara"],"evidence":["Textual: Aurobindo's essays (The Secret of the Veda, The Human Cycle, The Life Divine) explicitly use the language of consciousness levels and social psychology for the Vedic and Puranic framework","Internal coherence: the reading is consistent with Aurobindo's integral-yoga system of levels of being (mind, life, matter, and the supramental), which provides a structured vocabulary","Historical use: the reading shaped Indian modern thought, including the nationalist and reform discourse of the early twentieth century, showing it works as a cultural and interpretive tool","Comparative parallels: Jung's Aion and Traditionalist cycles (Guénon, Evola) use similar psychological-civilisational sequences, suggesting the idea has cross-cultural appeal, though parallels are not proof of the claimed Hindu original meaning"],"criticisms":["Unfalsifiable: because the yugas are read as qualitative and open-ended, no observation could count against them. That makes the scheme hard to test and easy to adjust after the fact","Selective reading: the interpretation picks out the sequence and meanings it prefers. Puranic and Smriti texts present the yugas in a mythic-cosmological frame (with fixed durations, dharma proportions, and divine cycles), and the allegorical reading sets much of that aside","Textual scholarship: scholars of Indology (for example, those working on Puranic chronology and the historical development of the yuga doctrine) note that the four-yuga scheme developed relatively late and varied across sources. Aurobindo's reading is a modern synthesis, not a recovery of an original meaning","Mapping problem: the varna-like social sequence in Human Cycles can be read as ideological. It risks treating social hierarchy as a stage of spiritual development and can be used to justify or romanticise hierarchy","Overlap with other frameworks: the same structure (decline and renewal, four stages) appears in Hesiod, Ovid, Plato, and other cultures. Without a specific argument for the Indian origin of the psychological reading, it is hard to separate Aurobindo's insight from a general human pattern he projects onto the texts","Academic reception: Aurobindo's work is often admired within Integral Yoga circles but is treated more cautiously in academic Indology, which judges it as philosophical interpretation rather than historical or philological analysis","Yukteswar's scheme, though related, is a literal-cosmic claim that conflicts with astronomy and chronology. Its psychological gloss does not fix those problems"],"plausibility":2,"plausibilityReason":"As a claim about real-world chronology, this reading gives no testable dates, so it cannot be verified or refuted. Its value is as philosophy and cultural interpretation, not history. Any literal timeline it implies is unsupported. Among the yuga interpretations, its strength is that it does not make a false date claim. Its weakness is that it cannot make a true one either. Rating reflects that it offers no falsifiable real-world timeline.","references":["Sri Aurobindo, The Human Cycle (1949 book; serialised in Arya 1915-1918), Sri Aurobindo Ashram Trust, Collected Works","Sri Aurobindo, The Secret of the Veda (1956 book; serialised in Arya c. 1914-1915), Sri Aurobindo Ashram Trust","Sri Aurobindo, The Life Divine (1939-1940 book; serialised in Arya 1914-1919), Sri Aurobindo Ashram Trust","Sri Yukteswar Giri, The Holy Science (1894)","Paramahansa Yogananda, The Second Coming of Christ (1950s commentary on Yukteswar's scheme)","René Guénon, The Crisis of the Modern World (1927)","Julius Evola, Revolt Against the Modern World (1934)","C. G. Jung, Aion: Researches into the Phenomenology of the Self (1951)","Note: exact quotes and chapter-level mappings should be checked against the Collected Works before citing. This entry paraphrases and does not quote."]},{"id":"kerala-school","title":"Kerala school (Madhava, Nilakantha) and siddhantic yuga/ahargana handling","category":"Academic/historical","proponents":["Madhava of Sangamagrama (c. 1340-1425)","Parameshvara (c. 1380-1460)","Nilakantha Somayaji (1444-1545)","Jyeshtadeva (c. 1500-1610)","Putumana Somayaji (dating debated, roughly 16th-17th c.)","Modern historians: K. V. Sarma, K. Ramasubramanian, M. S. Sriram, M. D. Srinivas, David Pingree, George Gheverghese Joseph"],"primarySources":["Aryabhatiya (499 CE) and its Gitikapada/Kalakriyapada, the base yuga and Kali-epoch reference","Surya Siddhanta (c. 4th-5th c. CE, redacted later), same 4,320,000-year mahayuga arithmetic","Parameshvara, Drgganiita (1431) and Goladipika","Nilakantha Somayaji, Tantrasangraha (1500-1501) and Aryabhatiya-bhasya","Jyeshtadeva, Yuktibhasa (c. 1530), a Malayalam-Sanskrit prose work on the Kerala school's mathematics","Putumana Somayaji, Karanapaddhati (later Kerala manual of computation)"],"period":"Kerala school active c. 1350-1650 CE; the Kali-epoch convention it inherits dates to Aryabhata (499 CE) and the Surya Siddhanta tradition","totalCycleYears":"4,320,000 (mahayuga, inherited from Aryabhata and Surya Siddhanta; not revised by the Kerala school)","durations":{"satya":"1,728,000 years (inherited convention, not revised in the Kerala school)","treta":"1,296,000 years (inherited convention)","dvapara":"864,000 years (inherited convention)","kali":"432,000 years (inherited convention)"},"kaliStart":"3102 BCE (midnight of 17/18 Feb 3102 BCE, proleptic Julian; the Aryabhata-tradition epoch used for ahargana)","kaliEnd":"About 428,899 CE by the same arithmetic (432,000 years from 3102 BCE)","currentPosition":"In 2026 CE about 5,128 Kali years have elapsed under the standard epoch, so we are roughly 1.2% into the 432,000-year Kali yuga. The Kerala school does not offer a separate present-day claim; it uses the same epoch for ahargana computation.","summary":"The Kerala school (c. 1350-1650) was a South Indian tradition of mathematics and astronomy. Madhava's infinite series for sine, cosine and arctangent, and later Nilakantha's and Jyeshtadeva's work, were mainly aimed at computing accurate planetary and lunar longitudes. For this they used the classical siddhantic framework: the Aryabhata-style mean motions counted from an ahargana (a count of days since the Kali epoch of 3102 BCE). The school made real corrections to planetary models and to the computation of true positions, for example Nilakantha's modified planetary model in which Mercury and Venus orbit the Sun. However, the yuga lengths themselves (4.32 million years for a mahayuga, with Kali at 432,000 years) were kept as given. Their work therefore bears on how yuga timelines were used for calculation, not on whether the yuga durations are historical.","keyClaims":["The Kali epoch of 3102 BCE and the 4,320,000-year mahayuga were kept as the arithmetic basis for ahargana, so computed dates in the Kerala tradition inherit the same Kali-yuga timeline","Kerala astronomers corrected planetary models and the method for true longitudes (equation of centre, latitude, and related terms), yielding more accurate positions than earlier siddhantas","Nilakantha's Tantrasangraha proposed a planetary model with Mercury and Venus orbiting the Sun, which in turn orbits the Earth, a configuration he presented as more accurate than the standard geocentric scheme","Their mathematical methods (infinite series, term-by-term summation) were developed to support these computations, not to measure cosmic time","Kali-yuga day counts (ahargana) for the epoch are used by traditional almanac (panchanga) makers, and the Kerala tradition was among the main sources of accuracy-improving methods for those counts"],"evidence":["Aryabhatiya Gitikapada states that 3,600 Kali years had elapsed when Aryabhata wrote in 499 CE, which fixes the 3102 BCE epoch by arithmetic","Tantrasangraha (1500-1501) is a full computational manual for planetary positions that uses the standard ahargana framework","Yuktibhasa (c. 1530) gives proofs for the Madhava-school series used in the lunar and planetary corrections, and is largely about computation","Modern reconstructions (Sarma 1972; Ramasubramanian, Sriram and Srinivas 1994 on the Kerala planetary model) confirm the planetary corrections are real and checkable against observed longitudes","No Kerala-school text, as far as the scholarship shows, derives a revised mahayuga length from observation"],"criticisms":["Kerala astronomers did not revise yuga durations. Claims that they 'corrected' the yuga timeline overstate the historical record","The 3102 BCE Kali epoch is a computational convention tied to the Aryabhata/Surya Siddhanta lineage, and it does not match any dated historical event; treating it as an absolute origin is not supported","The 4.32-million-year mahayuga has no observational basis. Accurate planetary tables do not confirm a cosmic cycle length, because the tables' accuracy is local and short-term","Sources for the Kerala school are mostly Sanskrit and Malayalam manuscripts, many of which are unpublished or edited only partly. Dates such as Putumana Somayaji's are disputed","Some popular writings present the Kerala school as anticipating European astronomy or as proof of ancient advanced knowledge. These claims go beyond the evidence, which shows real but bounded innovations within the siddhantic tradition"],"plausibility":3,"plausibilityReason":"As history of astronomy, the Kerala school's planetary and series work is well supported and verified against manuscripts and modern reconstructions, so it rates high. As support for the real-world timeline of yuga cycles, the view adds nothing: the 4.32-million-year mahayuga and the 3102 BCE Kali epoch are inherited conventions with no independent astronomical or historical check. The rating of 3 reflects that the school's computational methods are sound but do not make the cycle lengths more plausible.","references":["Sarma, K. V. (1972). A History of the Kerala School of Hindu Astronomy. Hoshiarpur: Vishveshvaranand Institute.","Ramasubramanian, K., Srinivas, M. D., and Sriram, M. S. (1994). 'Modification of the earlier Indian planetary theory by the Kerala astronomers.' Indian Journal of History of Science 29(2). (Verify exact citation.)","Ramasubramanian, K. and Sriram, M. S. (2011). Tantrasangraha of Nilakantha Somayaji. Hindustan Book Agency. (Verify.)","Sarma, K. V. (2008). Yuktibhasa of Jyeshtadeva, English translation. Hindustan Book Agency.","Clark, Walter Eugene (1930). The Aryabhatiya of Aryabhata. University of Chicago Press.","Pingree, David (1970-1994). Census of the Exact Sciences in Sanskrit. American Philosophical Society.","Joseph, George Gheverghese (2011). The Crest of the Peacock, 3rd ed. Princeton University Press."]},{"id":"skeptic-historian","title":"Critical secular/historical view: yugas as mythic-cosmological framework","category":"Academic/historical","proponents":["Patrick Olivelle (translator and dating of Manusmriti)","David Pingree (historian of Sanskrit astronomy)","Wendy Doniger (The Hindus: An Alternative History)","Gavin Flood (Hindu studies, Cambridge)","Mircea Eliade (cyclical time and sacred history, comparative religion)","Kim Plofker (historian of Indian mathematics)"],"primarySources":["Vedic and Vedanga Jyotisha usage (c. 1200-500 BCE): 'yuga' means a span or yoke-period (Vedanga Jyotisha uses a five-year yuga); no four-age scheme","Pali Aggañña Sutta (DN 27): decline of lifespan and morality across cosmic cycles, composition date uncertain (roughly 4th-1st c. BCE)","Mahabharata, Vana Parva (Markandeya passages, c. 3.148-3.150 and 3.188) and Shanti Parva: earliest four-yuga descriptions, layered composition c. 400 BCE-400 CE","Manusmriti (Manava Dharmasastra) ch. 1: 4800/3600/2400/1200 divine years, 12,000 divine years = 4,320,000 human years, 1000 mahayugas = one day of Brahma; date c. 200 BCE-200 CE (Olivelle)","Vishnu Purana book 1 ch. 3 and Bhagavata Purana book 12: systematized Puranic yuga chronology, mid-1st millennium CE with earlier material","Aryabhatiya (499 CE): Kali epoch reckoned as 3600 years before his birth, i.e. retrospective 3102 BCE epoch","Surya Siddhanta (c. 5th-6th c. CE, Burgess 1860 translation): Kali epoch and mahayuga arithmetic in astronomical form","Brahmasphutasiddhanta of Brahmagupta (628 CE): uses Kali epoch in planetary computations"],"period":"Doctrine crystallised c. 300 BCE-300 CE (Mahabharata and Manusmriti layers); the 3102 BCE Kali epoch is attested only from c. 5th-6th c. CE onward","totalCycleYears":"4,320,000 human years (12,000 divine years x 360)","durations":{"satya":"1,728,000 years (4800 divine years plus sandhya/sandhyamsa)","treta":"1,296,000 years (3600 divine years)","dvapara":"864,000 years (2400 divine years)","kali":"432,000 years (1200 divine years)"},"kaliStart":"3102 BCE (conventional Siddhantic epoch; no pre-Gupta text gives it and it is not an observed historical event)","kaliEnd":"About 428,900 CE on the conventional epoch (432,000 years from 3102 BCE)","currentPosition":"In 2026 CE the conventional reckoning puts us about 5,127 years into the 432,000-year Kali Yuga, with roughly 427,000 years remaining. This is arithmetic on an epoch that cannot be checked against history.","summary":"The four-yuga scheme is a mythic-cosmological framework that appears in Indian texts only in the last centuries BCE and first centuries CE, not in the Vedas. Its numbers (4,320,000 years per mahayuga, with 4:3:2:1 proportions) are sacred arithmetic built from divine years of 360 human years, and the 3102 BCE start of Kali is a retrospective epoch from Siddhantic astronomy, not an event anyone recorded. Historians read the scheme as a theory of moral and cosmic decline and renewal, comparable to Hesiod's ages of man and other ancient cyclical systems. On this view, the timeline has no historical content, and the specific dates are best explained as astronomical and theological constructs.","keyClaims":["The four-age (yuga) scheme is not present in the Rigveda; 'yuga' there means a generation, span, or yoke, and Vedanga Jyotisha uses a five-year yuga.","The first clear full statement of the 4:3:2:1 yuga durations is in the epic and Manusmriti layers, roughly 300 BCE-300 CE.","The 432,000 figure for Kali matches a sexagesimal sacred-number pattern also seen in Mesopotamian king-list traditions (for example the 432,000 years attributed to Babylonian kings by Berossus), a parallel scholars note without establishing borrowing.","The 3102 BCE epoch is first attested in Aryabhata's era (499 CE) and later Siddhantas, and is most plausibly a retrospective epoch tied to mean planetary positions rather than a recorded date.","No archaeological or textual evidence confirms a historical onset of an age of decline in 3102 BCE, and the Indus urban decline (c. 1900 BCE) and Vedic chronology do not align with it.","Yuga decline is best read as moral and political rhetoric about dharma and kingship, which fits the function of the texts that carry it."],"evidence":["Manusmriti 1.67-86 gives the divine-year durations and the 12,000-year mahayuga; Olivelle's dating (c. 2nd c. BCE-2nd c. CE) places the scheme inside the window of interest.","Mahabharata Vana Parva Markandeya passages contain four-yuga descriptions with declining dharma; the critical edition (Sukthankar et al., 1933-1966) supports layered composition.","Aryabhatiya Gitikapada gives Kali epoch as 3600 years before his birth; this makes the 3102 BCE date a computed back-projection.","Pingree's census of Sanskrit exact sciences and his 1978 history of Indian mathematical astronomy trace Kali-epoch astronomy to Gupta-period Siddhantas.","Hesiod's Works and Days (c. 700 BCE) describes gold, silver, bronze, heroic and iron ages, a structurally similar decline scheme, though direct transmission is unproven.","Pali Aggañña Sutta (DN 27) describes cyclical decline of lifespan and virtue, showing that decline cycles were a shared South Asian idea before the standard yuga arithmetic."],"criticisms":["The 3102 BCE epoch is sometimes presented as a historical date; it is a retrospective astronomical zero point and should not be read as a recorded event.","Traditionalist writers (for example B.B. Lal-style archaeology claims, and Tilak-style chronologies) try to match Mahabharata or Kali dates to archaeology, but mainstream scholars reject these correlations for lack of evidence.","The Hesiod and Zoroastrian metal-age parallels are suggestive but do not prove diffusion; the Indian yuga arithmetic is internally developed.","Sources for the Mesopotamian 432,000 parallel are late and transmitted indirectly, so the link is at most a comparative note.","Some scholars argue that the decline-cycle idea is older than the textual record, so attributing its origin to 300 BCE-300 CE may understate earlier oral tradition, which cannot be dated.","The skeptical view can be overstated: the texts are consistent in their own terms, and the moral framework has real historical influence even without a literal chronology."],"plausibility":2,"plausibilityReason":"As a literal timeline, the 4.32 million-year mahayuga and the 3102 BCE start are not supported by any historical or archaeological evidence and conflict with known prehistory. The framework is well documented as a cultural and textual construct, which earns it a high score as a description of how the doctrine developed, but the literal dates score very low. Scoring is based on conventional scholarship and was not checked against live web sources in this run.","references":["Olivelle, Patrick (2005). Manu's Code of Law. Oxford University Press.","Pingree, David (1978). 'History of Mathematical Astronomy in India.' Dictionary of Scientific Biography vol. 15. Scribner's.","Pingree, David (1970-1994). Census of the Exact Sciences in Sanskrit. American Philosophical Society.","Plofker, Kim (2009). Mathematics in India. Princeton University Press.","Doniger, Wendy (2009). The Hindus: An Alternative History. Penguin Press.","Flood, Gavin (1996). An Introduction to Hinduism. Cambridge University Press.","Eliade, Mircea (1954). Cosmos and History: The Myth of the Eternal Return. Pantheon.","Sukthankar, V.S. et al. (1933-1966). Critical Edition of the Mahabharata. Bhandarkar Oriental Research Institute.","Burgess, Ebenezer (1860). Translation of the Surya Siddhanta, ed. W.D. Whitney. Journal of the American Oriental Society 6.","Hesiod, Works and Days (c. 700 BCE)."]},{"id":"calendar-practice","title":"Kali era in practical panchanga reckoning (Kali Samvat 5127)","category":"Academic/historical","proponents":["Traditional panchanga makers (almanac publishers and Jyotisha practitioners)","Surya Siddhanta and Aryabhata school astronomers (as the source of the epoch)","Robert Sewell and Shankar Balkrishna Dikshit (The Indian Calendar, 1896), who documented how the eras are reckoned","David Pingree (historian of Sanskrit exact sciences), who analysed the epoch computation","Kim Plofker (Mathematics in India, 2009)"],"primarySources":["Aryabhatiya (Aryabhata, 499 CE), which gives the Kali-era reference point","Surya Siddhanta (anonymous, revised over several centuries, roughly 4th-5th c. CE onward), source of the 432,000-year Kali yuga and mean-motion parameters","Brahmasphutasiddhanta (Brahmagupta, 628 CE)","Vikrama Samvat (conventional epoch 57 BCE) and Saka era (78 CE), the other eras used alongside Kali in almanacs","Sankalpa formula used in Hindu ritual, which names the Kali yuga and its year"],"period":"Epoch dated in the 5th-6th centuries CE (Aryabhata era). Practical almanac use is medieval onward and remains current. Sewell and Dikshit (1896) gave the first modern documentation of how the eras are reckoned.","totalCycleYears":"4,320,000 years for one mahayuga (divine years of 360 days, reckoned as solar years in the Surya Siddhanta convention). The Kali yuga is 432,000 years of that.","durations":{"satya":"1,728,000 years (in the 4,320,000-year mahayuga convention)","treta":"1,296,000 years","dvapara":"864,000 years","kali":"432,000 years"},"kaliStart":"3102 BCE (conventional midnight epoch, around 17/18 February 3102 BCE in the Julian calendar). This is a retrojected epoch, not an observed event.","kaliEnd":"Approximately 428,898 CE, by the conventional arithmetic (432,000 minus 3,102).","currentPosition":"In 2026 CE the Kali year is 5127 (Kali Samvat 5127), which is the conventional count of CE year plus 3101. Vikram Samvat is about 2083 and Saka is 1948 at the same time. Kali year 5127 is still in force on 20 October 2026, since the Kali year changes at the start of the lunar or solar new year (Chaitra or Mesha), not in autumn. The Kali yuga is about 5,127 of its 432,000 years, roughly 1.2 percent elapsed.","summary":"The Kali era is used as a counting system in some Hindu almanacs and in ritual formulas, most visibly in the sankalpa (ritual declaration), which names the Kali yuga and year. Its year count is a simple offset: Kali year equals CE year plus 3101, so 2026 CE gives Kali 5127. Most everyday Indian almanacs use the Vikrama Samvat (north and west India) or Saka era (government and many official uses), with regional calendars (Tamil solar, Bengali, Malayalam Kollavarsham, Odia) running their own systems. Kali Samvat is therefore a lesser-used but real reckoning system, mostly in scholarly Jyotisha, certain almanacs, and ritual declarations. Dates within a Hindu calendar are reckoned by the lunisolar tithi, nakshatra, and month rules, and adhika (intercalary) and kshaya months, with the era count only giving the year label.","keyClaims":["The Kali era is an actual, continuously used counting system in Hindu almanacs and ritual, not only a theoretical construct.","Kali year 5127 corresponds to 2026 CE, following the offset CE + 3101.","The 3102 BCE epoch is a retrojected computation from siddhantic astronomy (Aryabhata-era), not an independently verified historical date.","The era label is separate from the astronomical method: a panchanga's dates come from tithi, nakshatra, and lunisolar rules, and the era only supplies the year number.","Kali Samvat is less common in everyday use than Vikrama Samvat or Saka, and its use varies by region and almanac."],"evidence":["Sewell and Dikshit (1896) document the era conventions and the offset between eras.","Sankalpa formulas in Hindu ritual include phrases such as 'Kali yuge prathame pade' and Kali-abda counts, which confirm the era's ritual use.","The Aryabhatiya (499 CE) states a Kali-era reference that the epoch was later fixed against, and Pingree's analysis of the siddhantic epoch supports the retrojection reading.","Government reform: the Indian Calendar Reform Committee (chaired by Meghnad Saha, reporting in 1955) adopted the Saka calendar as the national civil calendar, showing that Kali is not the official civil reckoning."],"criticisms":["The 3102 BCE epoch has no independent historical anchor. Claims that planets were in conjunction at that date come from back-computation with siddhantic parameters, and modern software does not reproduce such a conjunction; Pingree and others regard it as ex post.","The era is not uniform. Kali Samvat is not the default in most regions or almanacs, so 'the Hindu year' is not a single answer.","The offset arithmetic is simple, but the start of the Kali year (Chaitra vs. Mesha vs. Kartika) differs across traditions, so 'Kali year 5127' can be ambiguous near the New Year.","Practitioners sometimes treat the era as a literal age of the world, which conflicts with any evidence-based chronology.","Specific claims about current regional usage percentages cannot be verified here; the usage description is a general characterization from knowledge, not a survey."],"plausibility":2,"plausibilityReason":"As a calendar system the Kali era works: the arithmetic is consistent, it is used in some almanacs and ritual, and its year count is internally coherent. As a claim about the real age of the Kali yuga or a literal 3102 BCE start, it does not survive scrutiny, because the epoch is a retrojected siddhantic construct and the 432,000-year yuga durations are not supported by any historical or astronomical record. The score reflects the literal timeline claim, not the calendar's operational use.","references":["Sewell, R. and Dikshit, S.B., The Indian Calendar (1896)","Pingree, D., Census of the Exact Sciences in Sanskrit (American Philosophical Society, 1970-1994)","Plofker, K., Mathematics in India (Princeton University Press, 2009)","Burgess, E. (trans.), Surya Siddhanta (1860)","Indian Calendar Reform Committee Report (1955)"]},{"id":"kalki","title":"Kalki prophecy: Kali Yuga's end and the return of Satya Yuga","category":"Traditional scriptural","proponents":["Vaishnava tradition (Vishnu as Kalki, the tenth avatar)","Kalki-avatar movements and modern Kalki claimants (for example, Kalki Bhagwan of Andhra Pradesh)","Traditional Puranic commentators","Some modern Hindu popular writers who link Kalki to a near-term end of Kali Yuga"],"primarySources":["Mahabharata, Vana Parva (Markandeya's account of future kings, includes a Kalki/Vishnuyashas reference; composition layers spanning roughly the late first millennium BCE to early centuries CE)","Vishnu Purana, Book 4 (dynastic prophecies and the Kali Yuga; commonly dated to roughly the 3rd-5th centuries CE, with later layers)","Bhagavata Purana, Book 12, chapters 2-3 (Kalki born in the household of Vishnuyasha in the village of Shambhala; commonly dated to roughly the 8th-10th centuries CE)","Kalki Purana (an Upapurana with a detailed Kalki narrative; most scholars date it to the late medieval or early modern period, roughly 16th-19th centuries CE, though dating is debated)"],"period":"The core idea of Kalki as the future avatar who ends the Kali Yuga is found in Epic and early Puranic material (roughly the late first millennium BCE to first millennium CE). The detailed Kalki narrative (battle against Mlecchas, restoration of dharma) is mainly from the medieval Puranas, especially the Kalki Purana. Modern date-specific readings are mostly 19th-21st century.","totalCycleYears":"4,320,000 years (one Mahayuga: Satya 1,728,000 + Treta 1,296,000 + Dvapara 864,000 + Kali 432,000)","durations":{"satya":"1,728,000 years (traditional Puranic count)","treta":"1,296,000 years","dvapara":"864,000 years","kali":"432,000 years"},"kaliStart":"Traditionally 3102 BCE (around 17/18 February 3102 BCE)","kaliEnd":"Traditionally about 428,899 CE (3102 BCE plus 432,000 years). Kalki's appearance is placed near the end, so the expected event is far in the future on the scriptural count.","currentPosition":"In 2026 CE we are about 5,127-5,128 years into Kali Yuga (Kali Samvat year about 5128). That is roughly 1.2% of the 432,000-year Kali Yuga. On this reading Kalki is not due for roughly 427,000 more years, though some modern writers argue for an earlier, near-term appearance.","summary":"In Vaishnava scripture, Kalki (the tenth avatar of Vishnu) is the future savior who appears at the end of Kali Yuga, the present dark age. The Bhagavata Purana (Book 12) says he will be born to the brahmin Vishnuyasha in the village of Shambhala and will restore dharma. The Kalki Purana gives a full narrative: Kalki, on a white horse with a sword, destroys corrupt rulers and Mlecchas (outsiders or unrighteous rulers), re-establishes righteousness, and the Satya Yuga returns. The scriptural timeline places this event hundreds of thousands of years away. Modern date-specific claims, which place Kalki in the 21st century or tie him to contemporary leaders or movements, are not in the classical texts and depend on reinterpretation.","keyClaims":["Kalki is Vishnu's future avatar who ends Kali Yuga and inaugurates the next Satya (Krita) Yuga.","Bhagavata Purana 12.2 places Kalki's birth in the household of Vishnuyasha in Shambhala village.","The Kalki Purana describes a war against corrupt kings and Mlecchas, the restoration of dharma, and a renewed golden age.","Kali Yuga began in 3102 BCE, so the present is about 5,128 Kali years and Kalki's appearance is traditionally some 427,000 years away.","Some modern interpreters argue Kalki is imminent or already present, and link the prophecy to contemporary events, leaders, or claimants."],"evidence":["Textual: the Bhagavata Purana 12.2 passage describing Kalki's birth at Shambhala, and the Kalki Purana's narrative of his campaign.","Textual: the Vishnu Purana's dynastic and Kali Yuga passages, which describe decline and associate the end of Kali with a restoration.","Traditional chronology: the 4,320,000-year Mahayuga and the 3102 BCE epoch are shared across Siddhantic astronomy and Puranic cosmology.","Modern devotional and movement literature, including claimed sightings and self-identification by Kalki claimants; these are testimony rather than verifiable evidence."],"criticisms":["The Kalki Purana is a late, medieval-era text, so its detailed prophecy reads more like a description of contemporary upheaval than a true forecast; scholars have raised the vaticinium ex eventu concern (prophecy written after the fact), though this applies more strongly to some parts than others.","The 432,000-year Kali Yuga and 4.32-million-year Mahayuga are cosmological figures, not measured or astronomical quantities; the Siddhantic astronomy that uses them is a computational scheme, not a record of history.","No archaeological, historical, or observational evidence supports a specific Kalki appearance, and claimed modern Kalki figures have not been validated by any independent standard.","Repeated date-specific end-time predictions across religions have failed, which makes near-term Kalki timelines poorly supported.","The Epic and Puranic sources differ in details of Kalki's role, birthplace, and era, so there is no single authoritative timeline.","Some modern Kalki movements have been associated with fraud allegations and controversial financial practices; treat such claims with caution."],"plausibility":1,"plausibilityReason":"As a literal scriptural timeline, the Kalki framework is internally consistent within its own cosmology but has no empirical support. Its 427,000-year remaining span cannot be tested. Modern near-term or date-specific Kalki claims have no verifiable basis and have repeatedly failed as predictions. The score is low as a real-world forecast, while acknowledging that the texts themselves are rich cultural and religious sources.","references":["Bhagavata Purana, Book 12, chapters 2-3 (translations by Ganesh Vasudeo Tagare, Motilal Banarsidass; and by Ramesh Chandra Dutt)","Vishnu Purana, trans. H. H. Wilson (1840), Book 4 on Kali Yuga dynasties","Kalki Purana (critical discussions by scholars including Wendy Doniger and Kalki-related studies; verify edition and dating)","Mahabharata, Vana Parva, Markandeya-samasya section (trans. Kisari Mohan Ganguli, 1883-1896)","Wendy Doniger, The Hindus: An Alternative History (2009) for discussion of Puranic chronology and later Kalki material","Note: these references were compiled from knowledge without live web verification in this run; check chapter numbers and dates against primary editions before publishing."]},{"id":"brahma-kumaris","title":"Brahma Kumaris (Prajapita Brahma Kumaris Ishwariya Vishwa Vidyalaya)","category":"Modern guru / sect","proponents":["Dada Lekhraj (Brahma Baba), founder, 1936 (Hyderabad, Sindh)","Prajapita Brahma Kumaris Ishwariya Vishwa Vidyalaya (current organization)"],"primarySources":["Wikipedia article 'Brahma Kumaris' (fetched 2026; cites Barrett 2001, Walliss 2002, Whaling 2012, Jain 1983, Hinduism Today 1995, Beit-Hallahmi 2003, Jones & Ryan 2010)","brahmakumaris.org (fetched; homepage and a concepts page returned no cycle-of-ages content; the concepts URL tried returned a 404 page)"],"period":"1936 to present","totalCycleYears":"5000","durations":{"satya":"1250 years (Golden Age, first half of cycle, per Wikipedia citing Barrett 2001)","treta":"1250 years (Silver Age, per Wikipedia citing Barrett 2001)","dvapara":"1250 years (Copper Age, per Wikipedia citing Barrett 2001)","kali":"1250 years (Iron Age, per Wikipedia citing Barrett 2001)"},"kaliStart":"Not specified in the sources checked","kaliEnd":"Not specified in the sources checked; the Wikipedia article says the cycle ends with 'Destruction' at the end of the Iron Age, with no date given","currentPosition":"Per Wikipedia, the present is 'sometimes described as a fifth age or Confluence age', the junction between Iron and Golden Ages. The group's current teaching is described as emphasizing meditation and peace. Its present-day stance on a physical destruction could not be confirmed from brahmakumaris.org.","summary":"The Brahma Kumaris teach an eternal, naturally occurring 5,000-year cycle of four 1,250-year ages (Golden, Silver, Copper, Iron), per Wikipedia citing Barrett (2001). The present is described as a Confluence Age between Iron and Golden. Wikipedia says the group's early teachings emphasized physical destruction of the world, with specific failed predictions for 1976 (original teaching, per Walliss 2002) and violent destruction between 1987 and 2008 (Walliss 2002). Wikipedia says later the destructive aspects were 'reframed as a process of transformation' (Hinduism Today, 1995), and the group now stresses meditation and peace of mind as its primary purpose. Note: I could not verify these from the group's own books or from brahmakumaris.org, which returned no cycle-of-ages content. The 1976 and 1987-2008 claims rest on Walliss (2002) as reported by Wikipedia and are not confirmed against primary texts. Wikipedia does not mention 1982 or 2000.","keyClaims":["Eternal, naturally occurring 5,000-year cycle of four 1,250-year ages (Barrett 2001, via Wikipedia)","Golden and Silver ages form the first half of the cycle; Copper and Iron the second (via Wikipedia)","Present period sometimes described as a fifth age or 'Confluence age' between Iron and Golden (Wikipedia, citing a BK web page)","At the end of the Iron Age there will be 'Destruction' killing everyone and cleansing the earth before the cycle repeats (Barrett 2001, via Wikipedia)","Original teachings referred to a particular date, 1976 (Walliss 2002, p.33, via Wikipedia)","Followers made failed predictions of violent destruction between 1987 and 2008 (Walliss 2002, via Wikipedia)","Destructive aspects reframed as a process of transformation after WWII, atomic bombings and Cold War (Hinduism Today 1995, via Wikipedia)"],"evidence":["Wikipedia 'Brahma Kumaris' article text (fetched 2026): cycle, four ages, Confluence Age, Destruction, 1976, 1987-2008 failed predictions, reframing as transformation","Jain (1983) cited in Wikipedia says the 1987 date 'changed many times'","Wikipedia notes the group maintains its primary purpose is teaching meditation and peace of mind","brahmakumaris.org homepage: no cycle-of-ages content found; peace and meditation focus","brahmakumaris.org concepts page URL returned a 404 page"],"criticisms":["Walliss (2002) and Beit-Hallahmi (2003), both cited in Wikipedia, document failed apocalyptic predictions (1976; 1987-2008)","Wikipedia notes that the 'Destruction' was reframed as 'transformation' after WWII and the Cold War, which critics read as retreat from a falsified prediction","Wikipedia's neutrality section is flagged as disputed","The 1976 date and the 1987-2008 window are attributed to secondary sources; no primary BK text was retrieved to confirm them"],"plausibility":2,"plausibilityReason":"The four-age 5,000-year structure is a Yuga-derived scheme with no empirical basis. The key apocalyptic dates (1976, 1987-2008) are documented as failed predictions in secondary scholarship, and the reframing as transformation is reported by Wikipedia; primary-text confirmation was not obtained in this run.","references":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brahma_Kumaris","https://www.brahmakumaris.org/","Barrett, D. (2001). The New Believers","Walliss, J. (2002). The Brahma Kumaris: Religion, Gender and Modernity","Whaling, F. (2012). Religion in Today's World","Beit-Hallahmi, B. (2003). Encyclopedia of... (as cited in Wikipedia)","Hinduism Today (1995), as cited in Wikipedia","Jones & Ryan (2010). Encyclopedia of Hinduism"],"verification":"Partly verified"},{"id":"gayatri-pariwar-satyug","title":"Gayatri Pariwar / Akhil Bharatiya Sanskriti Sangh: 21st century as dawn of divine descent and return of Satyug","category":"Modern guru / sect","proponents":["Pt. Shriram Sharma Acharya (1911-1990)","Gayatri Pariwar / All World Gayatri Pariwar","Akhil Bharatiya Sanskriti Sangh"],"primarySources":["Twenty First Century: The Dawn Of The Era Of Divine Descent On Earth (title only; the book's text was not retrieved)","Akhand Jyoti magazine (started 1938 per awgp.org, though another section of the same site says 1940)","awgp.org (official site)"],"period":"Mid-20th century onward (Yug Nirman Yojana, Akhand Jyoti literature)","totalCycleYears":"Not specified","durations":{"satya":"Not specified","treta":"Not specified","dvapara":"Not specified","kali":"Not specified"},"kaliStart":"Not specified","kaliEnd":"Not specified","currentPosition":"Per the official awgp.org homepage, the group frames the present as a \"Changing Era\" linked to \"Change of Era - Satyug\" and a \"Changing Era Age of Truth\" section, and describes a \"transmutation of society\" through \"a higher level of consciousness.\" It does not name a current yuga. Verified fetches did not find a claim that Satyug has returned or that the present is Satyug.","summary":"The movement's literature and website refer to the 21st century as a new era of truth, of \"scientific spirituality,\" and of \"the religion of the 21st century,\" and its book title promises \"the Dawn of the Era of Divine Descent on Earth.\" Verified sources (awgp.org homepage and books page, and a Wikipedia-cited secondary source) use the language of an era change and of Satyug, but none of the pages I could retrieve gives a specific year (such as 2000) for the dawn, a dated Satyug start, or a yuga label for the present. Their vocabulary is \"change of era\" and \"transmutation of society\" rather than a yuga-cycle chronology. The book's full text was not accessible, so claims inside it remain unverified.","keyClaims":["The 21st century is described as a new era of truth and of 'the religion of the 21st century' (awgp.org, Akhand Jyoti entry: 'promotes scientific spirituality and the religion of the 21st century').","The Krantidharmi literature 'conveys the message of a new era of truth in the 21st century' (secondary article citing the group's literature; quoted as summarized by that article).","The book 'Twenty First Century: The Dawn Of The Era Of Divine Descent On Earth' exists as a title listed in the literature (cited by a secondary article; body text not verified).","The website uses the framing 'Change of Era - Satyug' and the section heading 'Changing Era Age of Truth'.","The 1988 Mahasadhana aimed at 'The rise of divinity within human beings, and the descent of heaven-like environment on Earth', with no end date given (secondary article)."],"evidence":["awgp.org homepage: menu item 'Change of Era - Satyug' and section 'Changing Era Age of Truth' (fetched; no explicit statement that the present is Satyug).","awgp.org homepage: 'The illuminated ascent to a higher level of consciousness leads to the transmutation of society.'","awgp.org books page: no passage found naming 2000, a dated Satyug start, or a yuga for the present.","Wikipedia article on Shriram Sharma Acharya (fetched via secondary summary): cites the 'Twenty First Century' title and Heifetz, 'The Science of Satyug' (Class, Charisma, and Vedic Revivalism in the All World Gayatri Pariwar); the body does not discuss the timing of Satyug.","awgp.org dates on the homepage are internally inconsistent: Akhand Jyoti 'Started ... in 1938' vs 'illuminating mind of millions since 1940'.","Direct Wikipedia fetches for 'Gayatri_Pariwar' and 'Yug_Nirman_Yojana' returned HTTP 404 and could not be used."],"criticisms":["Because no specific year or yuga label was located in verified sources, any statement that the group dates the 'dawn' to 2000 or declares the present as Satyug is unverified and should not be attributed to them without the book text.","Even within the group's own materials, 'era change' is described in spiritual and social-transformation terms (consciousness, society), not as a measured yuga-cycle chronology with durations.","Academic treatment (Heifetz's work, as cited) frames Satyug as a rhetorical and social project of Vedic revivalism rather than a chronological prediction, so the group's 'Satyug' language should not be read as a dated cosmological claim.","The homepage's dates conflict with each other (1938 vs 1940 for the magazine's start), which lowers confidence in the site as a precise source."],"plausibility":2,"plausibilityReason":"As a yuga-timeline claim, it is non-falsifiable in its verified form: no date, no duration, and no present-yuga assignment are stated in sources I could reach. Its moral and social framing ('new era of truth', 'transmutation of society') is not a chronology that can be checked against astronomy or history.","references":["https://www.awgp.org/ (homepage: 'Change of Era - Satyug', 'Changing Era Age of Truth', 'transmutation of society', 'religion of the 21st century')","https://www.awgp.org/books/twenty-first-century-the-dawn-of-the-era-of-divine-descent-on-earth (fetch returned no year or yuga claim)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shriram_Sharma_Acharya (secondary summary; cites the 'Twenty First Century' title and Heifetz, 'The Science of Satyug')","Heifetz, Daniel, 'The Science of Satyug: Class, Charisma, and Vedic Revivalism in the All World Gayatri Pariwar' (title as cited in the Wikipedia article; not read directly)","Not verified: the book's full text, Yug Nirman Yojana publications, and the Gayatri Pariwar Wikipedia article (direct fetch returned HTTP 404)"],"verification":"Partly verified"},{"id":"steiner-1900","title":"Rudolf Steiner (Anthroposophy): end of Kali Yuga in 1899/1900","category":"Modern spiritual/esoteric","proponents":["Rudolf Steiner (1861-1925)","Anthroposophical Society (followers)"],"primarySources":["Steiner lectures (exact lecture source not verified; the Wikipedia Kali Yuga article cites a Bamford-collected edition, 'Spiritualism, Madame Blavatsky & Theosophy: An Eyewitness View of Occult History: Lectures by Rudolf Steiner')"],"period":"Late 19th to early 20th century (Steiner's esoteric lectures, c. 1900-1925)","totalCycleYears":"Not specified","durations":{"satya":"Not specified","treta":"Not specified","dvapara":"Not specified","kali":"Not verified (the 5,000-year Kali length is the traditional Hindu figure; Steiner's own duration was not confirmed)"},"kaliStart":"Not specified","kaliEnd":"Wikipedia's Kali Yuga article states: 'Rudolf Steiner believed that the Kali Yuga ended in 1900.' Its Rudolf Steiner article gives 1899 as the year of his 'first public lectures on spiritual experiences' and of 'Goethe's Secret Revelation'. The exact year Steiner himself gave for the end of Kali Yuga was not confirmed against a primary lecture text.","currentPosition":"Per Steiner's framework, the Kali Yuga has ended and the post-Kali spiritual era (the Michael age, in his later terminology) is underway. Steiner's own wording on this was not verified in the sources reached.","summary":"Wikipedia's Kali Yuga article says: 'Rudolf Steiner believed that the Kali Yuga ended in 1900,' citing a Bamford-edited collection of Steiner lectures (Bamford footnote). The Wikipedia Rudolf Steiner article gives 1899 as the year of his first public lectures on spiritual experience, of 'Goethe's Secret Revelation', and of his marriage, but does not mention Kali Yuga in the portion read. Steiner's primary lecture text giving the exact year was not located: a guessed rsarchive.org URL returned 404, so the exact year (1899 vs 1900) and the lecture are unverified.","keyClaims":["Kali Yuga ended in 1900 according to Steiner (Wikipedia's claim; primary text not verified)","Post-Kali era is a spiritual epoch in Steiner's later framework (Michael age; not verified in sources reached)"],"evidence":["Wikipedia Kali Yuga article, 'Other usage' section: 'Rudolf Steiner believed that the Kali Yuga ended in 1900.' (footnote 37 cites Bamford's Steiner lectures)","Wikipedia Rudolf Steiner article: 1899 cited for first public lectures on spiritual experience and for 'Goethe's Secret Revelation'; no Kali Yuga passage in the read portion (article truncated)"],"criticisms":["The 1899/1900 discrepancy is unresolved: the task text and Wikipedia differ, and no primary lecture was verified.","Secondary-source claim rests on one Wikipedia sentence and a Bamford compilation; the primary lecture was not checked.","Steiner's chronology (Michael age, Kali Yuga) is not an astronomical or historical claim with checkable evidence, so it cannot be confirmed or refuted by data.","Anthroposophy is not supported by mainstream historians or scientists for claims about cosmic ages."],"plausibility":2,"plausibilityReason":"The attribution to Steiner is plausibly real (Theosophical-era Kali Yuga talk was common), but the exact year and the primary text were not verified, and the cosmological claim has no empirical support.","references":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kali_Yuga","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rudolf_Steiner","Bamford, Christopher (ed.), 'Spiritualism, Madame Blavatsky & Theosophy: An Eyewitness View of Occult History: Lectures by Rudolf Steiner' (as cited by Wikipedia; edition details not verified)"],"verification":"Partly verified"},{"id":"abhyankar-mohenjo-daro","title":"K. D. Abhyankar: Kali Yuga start tied to a rare planetary alignment on Mohenjo-daro seals","category":"Academic/historical","proponents":["K. D. Abhyankar"],"primarySources":["K. D. Abhyankar (1993), \"Astronomical significance to two Mohenjodaro seals\", Astronomical Society of India, Bulletin 21 (3-4): 477, Bibcode 1993BASI...21..475A (as cited on Wikipedia's Kali Yuga article; I could not open the ADS record itself)"],"period":"Claim made in 1993 paper; traditional epoch 3102 BCE","totalCycleYears":"Not specified in the sources checked","durations":{"satya":"Not specified","treta":"Not specified","dvapara":"Not specified","kali":"Not specified in sources checked (traditional 432,000 years is not confirmed by these sources for Abhyankar)"},"kaliStart":"Traditional epoch: midnight 17/18 February 3102 BCE (proleptic Julian), Ujjain meridian, per Wikipedia. Abhyankar's proposed alternative date: not found in the sources I could access.","kaliEnd":"Not specified","currentPosition":"Per the traditional reckoning, Kali Yuga is ongoing in 2026 (about 5,128 years since 3102 BCE). Abhyankar's view on the current position was not verified.","summary":"Wikipedia's Kali Yuga article states that, according to K. D. Abhyankar, the starting point of Kali Yuga is an extremely rare planetary alignment depicted on the Mohenjo-daro seals, citing his 1993 paper \"Astronomical significance to two Mohenjodaro seals.\" The article does not give the planets, the alignment's configuration, or the date he proposes. I could not verify the paper's content, so the specific alignment and date remain unconfirmed.","keyClaims":["The starting point of Kali Yuga is an extremely rare planetary alignment (per Wikipedia's paraphrase of Abhyankar)","This alignment is depicted on the Mohenjo-daro seals (per the same paraphrase)","Source cited: Abhyankar 1993, 'Astronomical significance to two Mohenjodaro seals'"],"evidence":["Wikipedia Kali Yuga article, Start date section: 'According to K. D. Abhyankar, the starting point of Kali Yuga is an extremely rare planetary alignment, which is depicted in the Mohenjo-daro seals.' Cites Abhyankar (1993) BASI 21, 477 (Bibcode 1993BASI...21..475A).","Same article gives the traditional start as midnight 17/18 February 3102 BCE (proleptic Julian), Ujjain meridian."],"criticisms":["Abhyankar's proposed date and alignment could not be confirmed from the paper itself; the only available source is Wikipedia's one-sentence summary.","Wikipedia's Mohenjo-daro article does not mention Abhyankar or any astronomical reading of the seals, so the claim is not corroborated there.","Mohenjo-daro seal iconography is widely read by archaeologists as religious, ritual, or administrative (e.g. the Pashupati seal read as a proto-Shiva or yogi figure), with no consensus astronomical reading.","A single 1993 bulletin note on two seals does not, on its own, establish a chronological epoch for a Puranic yuga; no independent scholarly acceptance was found in the sources checked.","Yuga chronology has no archaeological anchor; the 3102 BCE epoch is a traditional astronomical/computational date (Aryabhata-era Siddhanta tradition), not something a seal can date directly.","Verification gap: the ADS abstract page returned HTTP 405 and Wikipedia's Kali Yuga article was the only source reachable, so this entry relies on a secondary summary."],"plausibility":2,"plausibilityReason":"The claim is attributed to a real 1993 paper and is a bounded astronomical question, but the seal reading is speculative, has no corroboration I could find, and the specific alignment and date could not be verified.","references":["Abhyankar, K. D. (1993). Astronomical significance to two Mohenjodaro seals. Bulletin of the Astronomical Society of India 21(3-4): 477. Bibcode 1993BASI...21..475A (cited via Wikipedia; not independently opened)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kali_Yuga (Start date section)"],"verification":"Partly verified"},{"id":"ayyavazhi-kali-yukam","title":"Ayyavazhi: Kali Yukam and the Santror/Dharma Yukam scheme","category":"Modern spiritual/esoteric","proponents":["Ayya Vaikundar (founder, per tradition)","Hari Gopalan Seedar (attributed author of Akilattirattu Ammanai, per Wikipedia)","Ayyavazhi community in Tamil Nadu and Kerala"],"primarySources":["Akilattirattu Ammanai (Akilathirattu Ammanai), as summarised and quoted on English Wikipedia; original text not read directly in this run"],"period":"Founder's life c. 1809-1851 CE (per Wikipedia, cited to [27]); scripture composed in the 1830s-1840s per the sources below","totalCycleYears":"Not specified in the sources consulted (no 432,000-year or other total-cycle figure was found)","durations":{"satya":"Not specified","treta":"Not specified","dvapara":"Not specified; Santror lineage said to begin at the end phase of Dvapara Yukam","kali":"Not specified; Kali Yukam is described as the seventh yuga, the 'age of deterioration', begun when the demon Kali is born"},"kaliStart":"Begins with the birth of the demon Kali, at the close of Dvapara Yukam (per Akilathirattu Ammanai as relayed by Wikipedia). No calendar date given for the start.","kaliEnd":"Not specified as a date. The text says Vaikundar will 'destroy ... the evil Kali Yuga' and that the eighth yuga, Dharma Yukam, follows. No calendar end date was found in the sources consulted.","currentPosition":"Per the scripture, we are in the seventh yuga, Kali Yukam. Ayyavazhi teaches that the Dharma Yukam (eighth yuga) will come, with Vaikundar bringing righteous people to Vaikundam. No date for that transition was found.","summary":"Ayyavazhi, a Tamil Nadu and Kerala religion centred on Ayya Vaikundar, departs from mainstream Hindu yuga reckoning. Per the Akilattirattu Ammanai (as summarised on English Wikipedia), the yugas and avatars are counted differently from Hinduism. Kali Yukam is the seventh yuga and the 'age of deterioration'. It begins at the close of Dvapara with the birth of the demon Kali. Vaikundar is said to destroy Neesan and the evil Kali Yuga. Santror, the noble people, are born at the close of Dvapara, and their lineage runs through Kali into the eighth yuga, Dharma Yukam, which is narrated as beyond time and space. The sources I could read give no numeric yuga durations and no calendar end date for Kali. The 1809-1851 dates are Wikipedia's dates for Vaikundar's life, not a dated incarnation. The 1008 M.E. incarnation year is stated in Wikipedia without citation.","keyClaims":["Kali Yukam is the seventh yuga, called the age of deterioration (Akilathirattu Ammanai, 'Evolution and Yugas', quoted via Wikipedia)","Kali begins when the evil spirit/demon Kali is born, at the close of Dvapara (Akilathirattu Ammanai, 'Kali Yuga', via Wikipedia)","Santror are born at the closure of Dvapara Yuga (Akilathirattu Ammanai, 'Evolution and Yugas', via Wikipedia)","Vaikundar is said to destroy Neesan and the evil Kali Yuga (Akilathirattu Ammanai, 'The Incarnation', via Wikipedia)","The eighth yuga is Dharma Yukam, to which Vaikundar takes the righteous to Vaikundam (Akilathirattu Ammanai, 'The Incarnation', via Wikipedia)","The incarnation year of Vaikundar is given as 1008 M.E. (Wikipedia, 'Symbolism' section, no citation)","Ayya Vaikundar's own life dates are given as c. 1809-c. 1851 CE (Wikipedia, cited [27]); not described as incarnation dates"],"evidence":["Wikipedia Ayyavazhi page, 'Relation with Hinduism': the split from Hinduism is placed at the advent of Kali Yuga, and 'from the beginning of Kali Yuga, they and all their virtues collapsed' (citing [192]).","Wikipedia Ayyavazhi page, Mythology section: 'The number of Yugas and Avatars differs in Ayyavazhi from Hinduism' (citing [169]).","Wikipedia Akilattirattu Ammanai page: the author is stated to have written it on 27 Karthikai in 1839 CE (citing G. Patrick, Religion and Subaltern Agency, p. 118). A different passage in the same Wikipedia material gives 1841 for the writing and 27 Karthikai 1016 M.E. for a start date. These conflict.","Wikipedia Akilattirattu page: Kali is described as the age of deterioration and the seventh yuga, and Dharma Yukam as the eighth yuga, in which God comes to transform the world.","Arithmetic note: 1008 M.E. (Kollam era) corresponds to roughly 1832-33 CE, not to a date inside the 1809-1851 life span as a claimed incarnation. This is my own calculation and was not checked against a source."],"criticisms":["No numeric duration for any yuga was found in the sources consulted, so Ayyavazhi cannot be reconciled with the 432,000-year Kali figure from the sources read. Any such reconciliation would be the commentator's, not the text's.","The 1809-1851 range is Wikipedia's date for Vaikundar's life. Presenting it as the incarnation date is a conflation. The 1008 M.E. claim is uncited on Wikipedia.","The Akilattirattu writing date conflicts across Wikipedia passages (1839 vs 1841), which weakens any chronology built on it.","Historians of religion, such as Robin Jeffrey and G. Patrick (cited by Wikipedia), treat Ayyavazhi as a 19th-century movement shaped by colonial-era social reform. A dated Kali-ending prophecy in such a movement is open to the usual critique of millenarian predictions that fail to come true.","Wikipedia's 'Dates' and 'Symbolism' claims are only as reliable as their cited sources, and I did not verify the primary text of Akilattirattu Ammanai directly."],"plausibility":2,"plausibilityReason":"As a religious cosmology, the Kali-Dharma framework is internally coherent. As a historical or astronomical claim, it has no verifiable calendar end date in the sources read, and the dating of the incarnation is inconsistent across sources. The claim that Kali ends in a specific date cannot be verified.","references":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ayyavazhi (fetched; quotes relayed via WebFetch summarisation, not checked against the original page)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Akilattirattu_Ammanai (fetched; quotes relayed via WebFetch summarisation)","G. Patrick, Religion and Subaltern Agency, Ch. 5 (cited via Wikipedia, not read directly)","Robin Jeffrey, various works on Ayyavazhi (cited by Wikipedia; not verified in this run)"],"verification":"Partly verified"},{"id":"bhavishya-malika","title":"Bhavishya Malika (Achyutananda Dasa / Panchasakha, Odisha)","category":"Regional prophetic tradition","proponents":["Achyutananda Dasa (attributed author, 16th-century Odia Panchasakha saint)","Panchasakha group: Ananta Das, Jasobanta Dasa, Jagannath Das, Balaram Das (attributed contributors, per Wikipedia 'Maalika' article)","Kashinath Mishra (editor of modern printed 'Bhavishya Malika Puran', 2023-2024, cited by Wikipedia)"],"primarySources":["Palm-leaf 'Malika' texts attributed to the Panchasakha (not directly consulted in this research)","Kashinath Mishra, 'Bhavishya Malika Puran' (2023-2024 printed edition, as cited by Wikipedia; not directly consulted)","Sahu (1997), cited on Wikipedia for the birth year 1502 A.D. (not directly consulted)"],"period":"16th century Odisha (under Suryavamsa Gajapati ruler Prataparudra Deva, 1497-1540 CE, per Wikipedia)","totalCycleYears":"Not specified in the sources retrieved. Wikipedia reports a Kali Yuga span of roughly 5,000 years inside a 432,000-year Kali Yuga, but does not state a total cycle length for the text itself.","durations":{"satya":"Not specified. Wikipedia says Satya Yuga is to begin 'by 2032 AD'.","treta":"Not specified","dvapara":"Not specified","kali":"Wikipedia: traditional 432,000-year span 'cut to about 5,000 years', leaving 'only 5,000 years for the actual experience of the age'. Also says the era 'will end after more than 5,000 years'."},"kaliStart":"Not specified in the retrieved sources. The text's own framing is that Kali Yuga began earlier and is being counted as a 5,000-year span; no start date is given in the Wikipedia summary.","kaliEnd":"No specific calendar end date. Wikipedia says the era 'will end after more than 5,000 years'. The only dated anchor is a Satya Yuga start 'by 2032 AD'.","currentPosition":"Per the Wikipedia 'Maalika' article's modern reading, the age is still Kali Yuga in 2026, with the text's listed signs (corruption, breakdown of guru-shishya bond, commercialized spirituality, erratic rainfall, epidemics) treated as present. The article also presents a predicted sequence it links to 2025-2026 events, including Saturn entering Pisces on 29 March 2025, the Pahalgam attack on 22 April 2025, and a solar eclipse on 12 August 2026. These are modern interpretations, not clearly textual claims.","summary":"Bhavishya Malika ('Maalika') is a body of Odia prophetic texts attributed to Achyutananda Dasa and the Panchasakha, dated by Wikipedia to the 16th century under Prataparudra Deva. The texts describe conditions of Kali Yuga (moral decline, corruption, erratic rain, epidemics) and, per Wikipedia, a sequence of later events. The main question of this entry, whether the texts state an end-of-Kali date, is not answered clearly by the available sources: Wikipedia gives a roughly 5,000-year Kali span and a Satya Yuga start 'by 2032 AD', but no firm end date. The birth year conflict is real: the Wikipedia infobox gives 10 January 1510, while a 1997 reference (Sahu) gives 1502 A.D.; the article itself notes the conflict and does not resolve it. The article has a page-level 'needs more citations' banner (February 2025) and one inline [citation needed] tag on the claim that farmers will stop farming because of erratic rainfall.","keyClaims":["Wikipedia 'Maalika' article: text attributed to the Panchasakha; Achyutananda Das and contemporaries documented the insights in palm-leaf Malika texts.","Wikipedia 'Maalika': Satya Yuga 'begins by 2032 AD'.","Wikipedia 'Maalika': Kali Yuga's traditional 432,000-year span 'cut to about 5,000 years', 'leaving only 5,000 years for the actual experience of the age'.","Wikipedia 'Maalika': the era 'will end after more than 5,000 years'.","Wikipedia 'Maalika' (modern interpretation section): Saturn into Pisces 29 March 2025; Pahalgam attack 22 April 2025; solar eclipse 12 August 2026; third world war lasting six years and six months with India joining 13 months after it begins; seven days of total darkness.","Wikipedia Achyutananda article: 'Author of Bhavishya Malika' section says his works include '100 scriptures known as Bhavishya Malika', said to 'contain predictions that indicate the condition of the Kaliyug'."],"evidence":["Wikipedia 'Maalika' article (fetched via WebFetch, summarized by a small model; exact wording not independently verified against the page): authorship by the Panchasakha, 16th-century date, the 2032 Satya Yuga claim, the 5,000-year Kali span, and the modern 2025-2026 event list.","Wikipedia 'Achyutananda Dasa' article (fetched via WebFetch): birth infobox '10 January 1510'; reference Sahu (1997) says 'born in 1502 A.D.'; no death year given; left samadhi at Nemalo village, Cuttack; Panchasakha 'lived between 1450 and 1550 CE'.","Wikipedia 'Achyutananda Dasa' article: page-level banner says it 'needs more citations' (February 2025); no inline [citation needed] tags in that article.","Wikipedia 'Maalika' article: one inline [citation needed] on 'Farmers will stop farming as dishartened by erratic rainfall.'"],"criticisms":["No primary text was consulted; all claims come from Wikipedia summaries produced by WebFetch, so exact quotations and dates should be checked against the Odia palm-leaf manuscripts or Mishra's 2023-2024 printed edition.","The Wikipedia URL 'Bhavishya_Malika' returned HTTP 404; the content is under 'Maalika' and 'Achyutananda Dasa'. Sourcing is thin and the page relies on modern interpretive claims.","The 2025-2026 event list (Saturn, Pahalgam, eclipse) and the 'third world war' sequence appear only as modern interpretations, not as clearly dated text in the manuscripts; they are post hoc matches common to prophecy traditions.","The 2032 Satya Yuga claim and the 5,000-year Kali span are internally inconsistent with the traditional 432,000-year Kali Yuga (Puranic chronology), and no derivation is given.","Birth year conflict (1502 vs 1510) is unresolved in sources; no manuscript evidence is offered for either.","Authorship is attributed to a group (Panchasakha) rather than a single author, and the 100 'scriptures' count is cited but not itemized.","Achyutananda-attributed prophecies are known to be transmitted orally and in later compilations; dating is uncertain, and the 16th-century attribution rests on tradition and a few secondary sources."],"plausibility":3,"plausibilityReason":"The texts are real Odia literary-religious works in the Panchasakha tradition and the Kali Yuga moral-decline themes are standard Puranic content. However, the specific dated events and the 2032 Satya Yuga claim are not verifiable from primary sources in this research, the birth-year conflict is unresolved, and the 5,000-year Kali framing contradicts the Puranic 432,000-year duration without explanation.","references":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maalika (Wikipedia, 'Maalika'/Bhavishya Malika article, fetched via WebFetch; the 'Bhavishya_Malika' URL returned 404)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Achyutananda (Wikipedia, 'Achyutananda Dasa' article, fetched via WebFetch)","Sahu (1997), as cited in the Achyutananda Dasa Wikipedia article (birth year 1502 A.D.; not directly consulted)","Kashinath Mishra, 'Bhavishya Malika Puran' (2023-2024), as cited in the Maalika Wikipedia article (not directly consulted)"],"verification":"Partly verified"},{"id":"savitri-devi-serrano","title":"Savitri Devi and Miguel Serrano: Kali Yuga as decline with Hitlerist esoteric renewal","category":"Alternative/fringe","proponents":["Savitri Devi (Maximiani Julia Portas, 1905-1982)","Miguel Serrano (1917-2009)","Later neo-Nazi and esoteric-Nazi circles (per Wikipedia summaries of their influence)"],"primarySources":["Savitri Devi, The Lightning and the Sun (1958) - listed in Wikipedia; Kali Yuga dates not confirmed from the book text","Savitri Devi, Gold in the Furnace (1952) - listed in Wikipedia; not verified","Miguel Serrano, El Cordón Dorado: Hitlerismo Esotérico (1978) - Spanish title of the first part of his Hitler trilogy; the article cites this as 'Golden Thread', the closest match to 'The Golden Chain'","Miguel Serrano, Adolf Hitler, el Último Avatãra (1984)","Miguel Serrano, Manú (1991)","Miguel Serrano, Nos (1980)"],"period":"1930s-1980s (Devi's and Serrano's core writings); influence continuing into the 2000s-2020s","totalCycleYears":"Not specified","durations":{"satya":"Not specified (Serrano: Golden Age restored by Aryan blood purification; no year)","treta":"Not specified","dvapara":"Not specified","kali":"Not specified (Kali Yuga treated as the present dark age; no duration given in the sources read)"},"kaliStart":"Not specified in the sources fetched. Savitri Devi and Serrano are not reported to give a date.","kaliEnd":"Not specified. No end year or renewal year was found in the Wikipedia summaries. Serrano's 1973 coup reference is a biographical turning point, not a Yuga date.","currentPosition":"Per the Wikipedia summaries, Devi and Serrano frame the present as the Kali Yuga, the worst age, with Hitler as the figure who would end it. They give no dated turn. Their framing is undated and is used as a political and esoteric narrative, not a chronology.","summary":"Savitri Devi combined Hinduism with Nazism, presenting Hitler as an avatar of Vishnu and a sacrifice that would bring an end to the Kali Yuga, which she blamed on Jews. Miguel Serrano's order treated Hitler as a spiritual initiate who incarnated to save the Aryan race and help end the Kali Yuga, and he argued Aryans can restore the Satya Yuga by purifying their blood. The Wikipedia summaries read for both give no end year, renewal year, or dawn date. The framing is undated.","keyClaims":["Hitler as avatar of Vishnu and sacrifice that ends the Kali Yuga (Devi, per Wikipedia summary)","Hitler as spiritual initiate who incarnated to end the Kali Yuga (Serrano's order, per Wikipedia summary)","Serrano: Hitler did not die in 1945 and was living in Antarctica (per Wikipedia summary)","Serrano: Aryans can restore the Golden Age (Satya Yuga) by purifying their blood (Adolf Hitler, el Último Avatãra, 1984, per Wikipedia summary)","No specific end or renewal year is given by either writer in the sources read"],"evidence":["Wikipedia 'Savitri Devi' summary: Hitler presented as avatar of Vishnu; Kali Yuga framed as worst age blamed on Jews; no specific dates given; The Lightning and the Sun (1958) and Gold in the Furnace (1952) cited, with no Kali Yuga dates attached","Wikipedia 'Miguel Serrano' summary: order joined 1942 combining pro-Nazi politics, ceremonial magic and kundalini yoga; Hitler treated as spiritual initiate; Kali Yuga present dark age; Satya Yuga restoration via blood purification; no renewal year","Serrano's books named in the summary: El Cordón Dorado (1978), Adolf Hitler el Último Avatãra (1984), Manú (1991), Nos (1980). 'The Golden Chain' is not mentioned; El Cordón Dorado is the closest match","Fetches were truncated: Devi page was read to ~100,000 of 138,729 characters and Serrano page to ~100,000 of 114,433. The remaining text was not read, so a dated claim could exist there. Primary book texts were not fetched."],"criticisms":["The Kali Yuga's 'worst age' framing is inseparable from antisemitic and racial-purity ideology in these writers","Blood-purity and Aryan-Golden-Age claims have no basis in Indology, Hindu scripture, or genetics; Devi's Hinduism is a selective reinterpretation","Hitler-as-avatar and Hitler-survival-in-Antarctica claims are unfalsifiable and have no evidential support","Undated framing makes the teaching unfalsifiable: no date-based prediction can be checked against outcomes","Serrano's cosmology (extragalactic beings, Hyperborea) is fantasy with no supporting evidence"],"plausibility":1,"plausibilityReason":"The claimed Hindu Kali Yuga framework is a real tradition, but the Nazi, racial-purity, and Hitler-as-avatar overlays have no supporting evidence. Neither writer, per the sources read, gives a checkable date, so the theory cannot be tested. Confidence is limited because the primary books were not read and the fetches were truncated.","references":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Savitri_Devi (fetched; summary of biography and Kali Yuga framing, truncated at ~100k of 138,729 characters)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Miguel_Serrano (fetched; summary of esoteric teachings and book list, truncated at ~100k of 114,433 characters)"],"verification":"Partly verified"},{"id":"sadhguru-isha","title":"Sadhguru (Jaggi Vasudev) / Isha Foundation: yuga position under a Yukteswar-style ascending cycle","category":"Modern guru / sect","verification":"Background knowledge only","proponents":["Sadhguru (Jaggi Vasudev)","Isha Foundation"],"primarySources":["Sadhguru talks and Isha Foundation Wisdom articles (not retrieved; isha.sadhguru.org search returned only navigation, and the isha.sadhguru.org root redirects)","Sri Yukteswar Giri, The Holy Science (1894), the model Sadhguru's yuga framing is usually linked to","Wikipedia 'Sadhguru' (fetched; no yuga content) and 'Yuga' (fetched; durations and 4,320,000-year mahayuga only)"],"period":"Sadhguru's talks, roughly 2010s to 2026; the underlying Yukteswar model dates from 1894","totalCycleYears":"24,000 (Yukteswar ascending plus descending halves, 12,000 years each)","durations":{"satya":"4,800 years (ascending phase); 4,800 years in the descending half","treta":"3,600 years (ascending phase)","dvapara":"2,400 years (ascending phase)","kali":"1,200 years (ascending phase); 1,200 years in the descending half"},"kaliStart":"About 499 CE under the Yukteswar-style reconstruction (my background knowledge, not verified against a source in this run). The traditional Puranic start is 3102 BCE.","kaliEnd":"About 1699 CE under the same reconstruction, after which the ascending Dvapara phase would begin","currentPosition":"Under the reconstructed model, 2026 falls in the ascending Dvapara phase (about 1699 to 4099 CE). The task premise says Sadhguru has described this; I could not confirm that from any retrieved Sadhguru source, so treat it as the premise, not a verified quote.","summary":"The task premise is that Sadhguru says humanity is in an ascending Dvapara Yuga, following Sri Yukteswar's 24,000-year model. I could not retrieve any Sadhguru talk, transcript, or Isha article on yugas. Wikipedia's Sadhguru page has no yuga content. Wikipedia's Yuga page gives the four-age names and the 4,320,000-year mahayuga but not the Yukteswar model. The Isha Wisdom search returned only site navigation. So the entry below rests on background knowledge of Yukteswar's scheme. Yukteswar proposed that the solar system's movement against a 'Central Sun' produces a 24,000-year cycle with ascending and descending halves, each made of Kali, Dvapara, Treta, and Satya phases in a 1:2:3:4 ratio. Sadhguru is commonly associated with this framing in popular talks, and the 'sun's relationship' and human-perception explanations in the premise are in line with how he tends to talk, but I could not confirm the specific reasoning or any dates he gave.","keyClaims":["Yugas follow a 24,000-year cycle tied to the solar system's motion (Yukteswar model), with ascending and descending halves","Humanity is in an ascending Dvapara Yuga (premise, unverified against Sadhguru sources)","The yuga state is linked to human consciousness and perception, not only to astronomy (premise, unverified)"],"evidence":["Yukteswar's 1894 book The Holy Science proposes the 24,000-year cycle with the 1:2:3:4 phase ratio, as recorded in secondary sources I know of","The 4,320,000-year mahayuga and the four-age scheme are documented on Wikipedia's Yuga page","No retrieved Sadhguru source confirms or denies the Dvapara claim"],"criticisms":["The Yukteswar cycle has no astronomical basis: the precession of the equinoxes (about 25,772 years) is real, but the Central Sun and 24,000-year sun-cycle ideas are not supported by observation","The Sun's motion around the galactic center takes about 225 to 250 million years, so it cannot drive a 24,000-year cycle","Yukteswar's dating is internally consistent but has no independent evidence; the phases are not observable in history","Ages are defined by a qualitative 'moral state' that cannot be measured, so the claim cannot be tested","Sadhguru's public statements sometimes conflict with scientific consensus (for example the mercury and lunar-eclipse claims on his Wikipedia page), which lowers confidence in his cosmology claims without directly refuting them","Premise-level claim that Sadhguru says Dvapara is unverified; if he has in fact said Kali Yuga, the entry must be revised"],"plausibility":1,"plausibilityReason":"As a claim about the real-world timeline, an ascending Dvapara phase starting 1699 CE has no physical or historical evidence. The 24,000-year sun cycle does not match known astronomy (precession is about 25,772 years and is a different phenomenon). The claim is not testable against any observable event, so it cannot be checked against the timeline.","references":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sadhguru","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yuga","https://isha.sadhguru.org/ (redirects; not retrieved in full)","Sri Yukteswar Giri, The Holy Science (1894)"]},{"id":"dashrathbhai-patel","title":"Dashrathbhai Patel (Gujarat) and his yuga interpretation: unidentified","category":"Regional prophetic tradition","verification":"Not found","proponents":["Unidentified. No verifiable source for a Gujarat-based teacher named Dashrathbhai Patel who teaches a yuga timeline was found in this run."],"primarySources":["None located. Attempted: Wikipedia search (en) for 'Dashrathbhai Patel yuga' returned no results; DuckDuckGo (English and Gujarati queries દશરથભાઈ પટેલ યુગ કળિયુગ) and Bing returned bot-check pages or no relevant results; YouTube results page returned only footer text."],"period":"Unknown (no verified source for the teacher's period of activity)","totalCycleYears":"Not verified. Puranic baseline for comparison: 4,320,000 years (Mahayuga).","durations":{"satya":"Not verified for this teacher. Puranic baseline: 1,728,000 years","treta":"Not verified for this teacher. Puranic baseline: 1,296,000 years","dvapara":"Not verified for this teacher. Puranic baseline: 864,000 years","kali":"Not verified for this teacher. Puranic baseline: 432,000 years"},"kaliStart":"Not verified for this teacher. Puranic baseline: 3102 BCE (conventional Hindu reckoning)","kaliEnd":"Not verified for this teacher. Puranic baseline: approximately 428,898 CE","currentPosition":"Not verified for this teacher. Under the Puranic baseline, 2026 CE falls about 5,127 years into Kali Yuga.","summary":"I could not identify a Gujarat-based teacher named Dashrathbhai Patel who teaches a yuga timeline. Web access was largely blocked (search engines returned bot-check pages; Wikipedia and YouTube returned nothing relevant), so this entry cannot describe his teaching from sources. Nothing here should be read as a description of his views. The entry records the failed search and gives a likely-candidate list. It also notes a name-confusion risk: Dada Bhagwan (Ambalal Muljibhai Patel), a Gujarat-born spiritual teacher, is often mistaken for a 'Patel' guru, but his Akram Vignan teaching is not a yuga-timeline doctrine. Candidates to check next, from background knowledge and not verified in this run: Sri Yukteswar Giri (Holy Science, a 24,000-year ascending/descending cycle with 1:2:3:4 yuga proportions); Kalki-claimant movements such as Kalki Bhagwan (Andhra Pradesh); and Gayatri Pariwar / Yug Nirman (Pandit Shriram Sharma Acharya), which teaches a near-term 'yug parivartan' (era change).","keyClaims":["No verifiable claim could be attributed to Dashrathbhai Patel in this run.","If the user has a source (a YouTube title, book, ashram name, or Gujarati post), it should be supplied so the teaching can be checked and described accurately."],"evidence":["Wikipedia search for 'Dashrathbhai Patel yuga': no results.","DuckDuckGo (English and Gujarati queries) and Bing: bot-verification page or no relevant results.","YouTube results page: footer only, no video titles.","Failed predictions with dates: none could be checked, since no teaching was retrieved."],"criticisms":["The subject's identity and teaching are unverified; any description beyond 'not found' would be fabrication.","The Puranic yuga durations (4.32 million years per Mahayuga, 432,000-year Kali Yuga) are not historically or astronomically verifiable as real-world timelines.","Interpretations that place a 'Satya Yuga return' or similar within a short window have a recurring history of unfulfilled dates, and this should be assessed in any specific case."],"plausibility":1,"plausibilityReason":"Unknown teaching, so no specific claim can be assessed. The underlying idea that yugas are a literal, dated real-world timeline has no empirical support: the Puranic durations are cosmological figures, and no physical or historical evidence confirms a 2026 position in Kali Yuga or any transition.","references":["Wikipedia (en), search 'Dashrathbhai Patel yuga': no article found (checked in this run).","Sri Yukteswar Giri, The Holy Science (1894), for the 24,000-year cycle with 1:2:3:4 yuga proportions (background knowledge; not re-checked in this run).","Dada Bhagwan / Akram Vignan (background knowledge; not re-checked in this run)."]},{"id":"kalki-bhagavan-oneness","title":"Kalki Bhagavan (Vijay Kumar Naidu) / Oneness University / Golden Age Foundation: 2012 \"Golden Age\" and end-of-Kali-Yuga claims","category":"Kalki claimant / prophetic","verification":"Partly verified","proponents":["Vijay Kumar Naidu (Kalki Bhagavan / Sri Bhagavan), founder","Oneness University / Oneness organization (later Ekam, led by NKV Krishna and Preetha Krishna from 2017)","Carl Johan Calleman (Mayan-calendar-based 'Oneness Celebration' associate, 2004)"],"primarySources":["Wikipedia article 'Oneness University' (fetched; summarized by a small model, so exact wording not checked)","Oneness University and Golden Age Foundation publications and talks (not fetched in this run)","Carl Johan Calleman, 'The Mayan Calendar and the Transformation of Consciousness' (2004), for the Mayan-2012 framing","Madras High Court and Supreme Court records on the 2002 PIL concerning the Kalki Trust (as cited in Wikipedia)"],"period":"Movement founded c. 1984 (Jeevashram) and 2000 (Oneness); 2012 transition claims circulated c. 2004-2012","totalCycleYears":"4,320,000 years (one Mahayuga: Satya + Treta + Dvapara + Kali)","durations":{"satya":"1,728,000 years (traditional)","treta":"1,296,000 years (traditional)","dvapara":"864,000 years (traditional)","kali":"432,000 years (traditional)"},"kaliStart":"3102 BCE (traditional Puranic reckoning, 18 Feb 3102 BCE)","kaliEnd":"About 428,900 CE (traditional end of the 432,000-year Kali Yuga)","currentPosition":"In 2026, about 5,127 years of Kali Yuga have elapsed on the traditional reckoning, leaving roughly 426,870 years. Oneness-style teaching holds that a 'Golden Age' or 'Age of Enlightenment' began or was entering around 2012 through the spread of the 'Oneness Blessing'. No public event in 2012 or since matches a formal end of Kali Yuga, and the movement's own account is not a verifiable cosmic transition.","summary":"Vijay Kumar Naidu, who called himself Kalki Bhagavan or Sri Bhagavan, founded Jeevashram in 1984 and the Oneness organization in 2000, later called Oneness University and Golden Age Foundation. Early followers regarded him as Kalki, the tenth avatar of Vishnu who ends the Kali Yuga. He reframed Kalki as 'not one person but a collective awakening'. He taught that once enough people received the 'Oneness Blessing' humanity would enter an 'Age of Enlightenment', the Golden Age. The 2012 date came through association with Mayan-calendar and 2012 end-of-cycle ideas. Carl Johan Calleman helped launch an 'Oneness Celebration' festival in 2004, and the organization held worldwide events on 21 December 2012. The Wikipedia summary consulted did not explicitly describe an end-of-Kali-Yuga claim, so that framing is attributed here to secondary descriptions and Golden Age literature rather than verified quotations.","keyClaims":["The Kali Yuga is ending or has ended and a Golden Age (Satya Yuga) is beginning around 2012.","Kalki is a collective awakening rather than a single person (as reported).","Receiving the 'Oneness Blessing' transforms individuals and collectively advances humanity into an Age of Enlightenment.","Ancient yuga cosmology and the Mayan calendar independently point to a 2012 transition (Calleman-associated framing)."],"evidence":["Founding dates (1984 Jeevashram, 2000 Oneness) and the 2017 handover to NKV Krishna and Preetha Krishna, renamed Ekam (Wikipedia summary).","The 2004 'Oneness Celebration' with Carl Johan Calleman and the 21 Dec 2012 worldwide events (Wikipedia summary).","Tax and legal matters: a 2002 PIL seeking an income tax probe of the Kalki Trust was dismissed by the Madras High Court and then the Supreme Court; a 2004 CBI-probe plea was rejected; 2019 income tax seizures linked to his son's properties were reported at about Rs 93 crore (Wikipedia summary).","The Wikipedia summary reports Vijay Kumar Naidu's birth as 7 March 1949 and death as 29 September 2026 in Chennai. This could not be independently confirmed in this run and should be checked before relying on it."],"criticisms":["No observable event in 2012 or later marks a cosmic end of Kali Yuga. 21 Dec 2012 passed with ordinary conditions, the same failure pattern as other 2012 end-of-world and end-of-cycle predictions.","Yuga durations are a traditional cosmological scheme in Puranic texts with no empirical basis for dating a transition, and calendar-based 2012 claims (Mayan calendar) do not support a cosmic shift. Calleman's framework is widely regarded as speculative.","The Kalki-avatar identification is reinterpreted (collective awakening) when the individual-claimant reading did not hold, which makes the claim hard to test.","Financial and legal controversies, including the 2002 PIL, the 2004 CBI plea, the 2005 departure of Freddy Nielsen after criticism, and the 2019 tax seizures, are reported in secondary sources and raise governance concerns.","Teaching a 2012 transition can divert followers from ordinary concerns and creates a benchmark (the 2012 date) that has already failed."],"plausibility":1,"plausibilityReason":"As a claim about the real-world timeline, a cosmic transition in 2012 has no observable evidence, and the traditional Kali Yuga is said to last until about 428,900 CE. The claim depends on contested interpretive frameworks (Puranic chronology and Mayan-calendar readings) and makes no testable prediction beyond 2012 that held. It is a religious and interpretive claim rather than a scientific one.","references":["Wikipedia, 'Oneness University' (accessed via WebFetch in this run; summary model used)","Wikipedia, 'Kali Yuga' and 'Yuga' (background knowledge, not fetched)","Carl Johan Calleman, 'The Mayan Calendar and the Transformation of Consciousness' (2004) (background knowledge)","Failed 2012 end-of-world predictions, including 21 Dec 2012 (background knowledge)"]},{"id":"sant-rampal","title":"Sant Rampal (Satlok Ashram): Kabir as Supreme God, Golden Age in Kalyug, and Self-Prophecy","category":"Modern guru / sect","verification":"Partly verified","proponents":["Rampal Singh Jatin (Sant Rampal Ji Maharaj), founder of Satlok Ashram, Haryana","Satlok Ashram followers (Kabir panth, Garib Das panth lineage)","Ramdevanand (his guru, from whom he claims succession in 1994)"],"primarySources":["Wikipedia, 'Rampal (spiritual leader)' (extract retrieved via MediaWiki API; cites the group's own biography and news coverage)","Official site jagatgururampalji.org (fetched via WebFetch; content was summarized by a small model, so exact wording is not verified)","Kabir panth scriptures cited by the group: Kabir Sagar, Sad Granth Sahib of Garib Das, Bhagavad Gita, Puranas (as the group interprets them)"],"period":"Modern (founded 1999 at Rohtak, Haryana; major teaching output 2000s to present)","totalCycleYears":"Chaturyuga of 4,320,000 years (conventional Puranic figure; the group uses this framework)","durations":{"satya":"1,728,000 years (conventional Puranic figure)","treta":"1,296,000 years (conventional Puranic figure)","dvapara":"864,000 years (conventional Puranic figure)","kali":"432,000 years (conventional Puranic figure); group reports 5,505 years of Kalyug elapsed by 1997 AD, which does not match the conventional 3102 BCE start (that would give about 5,100 years by 1997)"},"kaliStart":"Conventional Puranic start 3102 BCE; the group's own start date was not confirmed from sources (its 1997 figure implies a different reckoning)","kaliEnd":"Not confirmed. Group teaches that Kalyug ends and a golden era arrives, but no specific year was found in sources","currentPosition":"As of 2026, the group teaches that we are at the end of Kalyug and that a golden era (Satyug-like age) is emerging, led by Kabir's true knowledge as delivered through Rampal. Rampal was granted regular bail in April 2026 by the Punjab and Haryana High Court after more than eleven years in custody (Wikipedia; WebFetch summary of group site), and his life sentence was reported as suspended in August 2025 (Wikipedia). Unverified claims that the world 'will realize the truth' about him remain undated.","summary":"Rampal, a former Haryana Irrigation Department junior engineer, was initiated into the Kabir panth by Ramdevanand in 1988 and named successor in 1994. He founded Satlok Ashram in 1999. His teaching holds that Kabir is the Supreme God (Param Purush / Satlok creator), that all the Vedas, Gita, Quran, Bible and Guru Granth Sahib name Kabir as supreme, and that the ordinary Hindu deities and Brahm (Kaal) are a 'false web'. He presents the Puranic four-yuga cycle and claims the current age is Kalyug, that it is ending, and that his knowledge, transmitted as 'Tatvadarshi' (true knowledge), will turn earth into heaven and bring a golden era. Per Wikipedia, he claims to be the initiator of a 'thirteenth Kabir Panth'. Group materials identify him with the Bhagavad Gita's 'Tatvadarshi Saint' and the Quran's 'Baakhabar', and claim he is Kabir in human form. The group cites Nostradamus, Jules Verne, Jean Dixon, Cheiro and Florence as having foretold him; the Nostradamus claim is the main one, and the group claims a golden era under a particular leader will follow (per the group site summary). Those predictions are not dated and no checkable deadline was found.","keyClaims":["Kabir is the Supreme God (Param Purush / Satlok creator), with no age and a body of divine light; Kabir returns periodically to reclaim souls for Satlok","Major scriptures (Vedas, Gita, Quran, Bible, Guru Granth Sahib) name Kabir as supreme; Brahma, Vishnu, Shiva and Brahm (Kaal Niranjan) are a 'false web'","The current age is Kalyug; 5,505 years of Kalyug had passed by 1997 AD per the group, which marks the start of a promised spiritual event","Kalyug is ending and a golden era will emerge when people worldwide recognize the true God; Rampal's initiation of a 'thirteenth Kabir Panth' will bring it about","Rampal is the Tatvadarshi Saint of the Gita and the Baakhabar of the Quran, and is Kabir in human form","Multiple past figures (Nostradamus, Jules Verne, Jean Dixon, Cheiro, Florence) are said to have foretold him","Followers who join are promised salvation to Satlok, cure of chronic illness and financial gain (Wikipedia and group materials)"],"evidence":["Wikipedia (retrieved in this session) confirms the biography, the Kabir panth lineage, the claim of initiating a 'thirteenth Kabir Panth' that will bring a golden era, and the claim that scriptures name Kabir as supreme","Wikipedia confirms that 6 deaths in the 2014 Barwala/Satlok standoff led to a murder conviction in 2018 (life sentence, later suspended in Aug 2025 per Wikipedia), and acquittal in the 2006 firing case on 20 Dec 2022","Wikipedia confirms the 2006 Arya Samaj clash and forgery case acquittal on 1 May 2018","Wikipedia reports regular bail granted April 2026 by the Punjab and Haryana High Court; this is a verifiable event, not a prophecy"],"criticisms":["The Kalyug-start discrepancy: 5,505 years by 1997 does not match the conventional 3102 BCE start (about 5,100 years by 1997); the group's reckoning is not explained in sources reviewed","Attributing predictions to Nostradamus, Jules Verne, Jean Dixon and Cheiro rests on loose, retrospective matching of vague quatrains; no primary text was found that names Rampal","Identifying himself with the Gita's Tatvadarshi and the Quran's Baakhabar is a retrofit reading of religious texts that is not accepted by mainstream scholars of those traditions","The 'golden era' prediction is undated and unfalsifiable: it can be deferred indefinitely, and no deadline is given in reviewed sources","Wikipedia documents a violent 2014 siege in which six people died, and the group's own claim that the world will 'realize the truth' about Rampal does not account for the court cases and convictions in the record","Academic and journalistic sources treat the group as a sect whose promises (cures, financial gains, salvation) are not independently substantiated; the Kabir panth itself has long scholarly history with the historical Kabir (c. 1440-1518) being a poet-saint, not a supreme deity in the group's sense","Specific failed-prediction dates were not found in the sources retrieved in this session, so none are listed here"],"plausibility":1,"plausibilityReason":"As a real-world timeline claim, the golden-age prediction is undated and unfalsifiable, and the group's yuga arithmetic does not match the conventional Puranic reckoning (3102 BCE start), while the cited prophecy attributions to Nostradamus and others are retrospective and unsupported by primary text. Court outcomes and some biographical facts are verifiable, but none of the cosmological or prophetic claims can be checked against the world.","references":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rampal_(spiritual_leader)","https://www.jagatgururampalji.org/ (official group site; summarized via WebFetch, not read verbatim)","Conventional yuga durations: Vishnu Purana and Manusmriti traditions (Satya 1,728,000; Treta 1,296,000; Dvapara 864,000; Kali 432,000 years)","Kabir panth background: Kabir Sagar and Garib Das's Sadgranth as used by the group; scholarly treatment of Kabir in Kabir and the Kabir Panth literature (e.g., David Lorenzen)"]},{"id":"ahmadiyya-kalki","title":"Mirza Ghulam Ahmad (Ahmadiyya) claiming to be Kalki avatar and implications for Kali Yuga","category":"Kalki claimant / prophetic","verification":"Partly verified","proponents":["Mirza Ghulam Ahmad (1835-1908), founder of the Ahmadiyya movement, who claimed to be the promised Messiah and Mahdi and, per Wikipedia (citing Juergensmeyer 2006), also the Kalki avatar","Ahmadiyya Muslim Community (headquartered in Rabwah/Chenab Nagar and later London), which reads Kalki in a spiritual or metaphorical sense","Hindu-Ahmadi outreach writers who cite Ahmad's Krishna/Kalki statements"],"primarySources":["Mirza Ghulam Ahmad, Haqiqatul Wahi (1907), cited by Wikipedia for the Krishna claim","Mirza Ghulam Ahmad, Tiryaq-ul-Qulub and other Urdu/Arabic/Persian works of the 1890s-1900s (background knowledge; not checked in this run)","Mark Juergensmeyer (ed.), Oxford Handbook of Global Religions (2006), p. 520, cited by Wikipedia's Kalki article","Official Ahmadiyya site alislam.org (not fetched in this run)"],"period":"1880s-1908 (Ahmad's public claims; the Kalki/Krishna statements are dated by Wikipedia to his 1907 work and the surrounding period)","totalCycleYears":"4,320,000 years per Mahayuga (1,728,000 + 1,296,000 + 864,000 + 432,000)","durations":{"satya":"1,728,000 years (Puranic reckoning)","treta":"1,296,000 years","dvapara":"864,000 years","kali":"432,000 years"},"kaliStart":"3102 BCE (conventional Puranic/astronomical start date)","kaliEnd":"Approximately 428,898 CE by the 432,000-year reckoning; Kalki's arrival is said to mark the end, though no calendar date is given in the texts","currentPosition":"In 2026 CE we are roughly 5,128 years into the 432,000-year Kali Yuga on the conventional start date. Under the Ahmadiyya reading, Ahmad's advent is said to have already occurred in the late 19th/early 20th century, so Kalki is treated as a spiritual figure rather than a future event, but the Yuga clock itself still runs for the Hindu tradition.","summary":"Ahmad, founder of the Ahmadiyya movement in British India, claimed to be the promised Messiah and Mahdi for Muslims. Wikipedia (citing Juergensmeyer 2006) also reports he claimed to be the Kalki avatar, and in Haqiqatul Wahi (1907) he associated himself with Krishna for Hindus. The claim is an interpretation that maps Hindu eschatology onto his own mission rather than a dated forecast. Ahmadis read Kalki spiritually as a reformer whose work restores true religion, and do not treat the Puranic 432,000-year Kali Yuga as a literal calendar that requires a physical avatar. Critics treat the Kalki claim as an extension of his broader claim to universal prophetic status.","keyClaims":["Ahmad claimed to be the Kalki avatar (in addition to Messiah and Mahdi), per Wikipedia citing Juergensmeyer (2006)","Ahmad linked his mission to Krishna for Hindus, stated in Haqiqatul Wahi (1907)","Ahmad's advent is said to fulfil end-of-age prophecies in multiple traditions, so Kali Yuga's end is read as a moral and spiritual transition, not a literal date","Ahmadiyya scholars argue Kalki and Messiah are one figure symbolising spiritual renewal"],"evidence":["Wikipedia's Kalki article cites Juergensmeyer (2006) p. 520 for Ahmad claiming to be Kalki and Mahdi","Wikipedia's Mirza Ghulam Ahmad article cites Haqiqatul Wahi (1907) for the Krishna claim","The Kalki article lists other modern claimants (Baha'u'llah, Kalki Bhagawan, Samael Aun Weor, Riaz Ahmed Gohar Shahi), showing Kalki identifications are a recurring pattern","Ahmad died 26 May 1908 in Lahore, which fixes the end of his public career well before any Kali Yuga milestone"],"criticisms":["The Kalki identification is retrospective and interpretive: Puranic texts describe Kalki as a warrior on a white horse who ends the age by force, which Ahmad's non-violent reformist mission does not match literally","The Kali Yuga's 432,000-year length is a cosmological figure, not a prophecy with a checkable date, so there is no timeline to fail or succeed","Failed or contested predictions by Ahmad that critics cite: the Smyth-Pigott challenge of November 1902 (Smyth-Pigott kept claiming divinity after Ahmad died in 1908, per contemporary newspapers) and the Dowie prophecy of September 1902 (Dowie died March 1907, before Ahmad, as Wikipedia notes); the Muhammadi Begum marriage prophecy of the late 1880s-1890s and the plague prophecies of 1899-1907 are widely cited failures in critical literature (not verified in this run)","Ahmadiyya is declared non-Muslim by Pakistan's 1974 constitutional amendment and faces legal restrictions there; critics within Islam dispute the Mahdi/Messiah claims, while critics of syncretism question the Krishna/Kalki identification as opportunistic","Ahmadi claims that the 1894-1895 eclipses fulfilled a hadith sign of the Mahdi are disputed by mainstream Muslim scholars and Western astronomers who note eclipses are periodic"],"plausibility":1,"plausibilityReason":"As a claim about the real-world Yuga timeline, it is not testable in a useful sense: the Puranic Kali Yuga runs 432,000 years from 3102 BCE, so the Kalki arrival is far in the future by that reckoning, and Ahmad's identification (made c. 1900-1908) would require the Yuga to end around 1900 or to be non-literal. Historical failures of specific predictions (Dowie, Smyth-Pigott, Muhammadi Begum) further lower credibility as a prophetic authority. Score reflects the chronology, not the sincerity of believers.","references":["Wikipedia: Mirza Ghulam Ahmad (en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mirza_Ghulam_Ahmad), fetched 2026-10-20 (partial read, first 100k chars)","Wikipedia: Kalki (en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kalki), fetched 2026-10-20 (partial read, first 100k chars); article carries an LLM-generated-content maintenance banner","Mark Juergensmeyer (ed.), Oxford Handbook of Global Religions (2006), p. 520 (as cited on Wikipedia)","Mirza Ghulam Ahmad, Haqiqatul Wahi (1907) (as cited on Wikipedia)","alislam.org (Ahmadiyya official site, not fetched)","Wikipedia Mirza Ghulam Ahmad article: Smyth-Pigott (Nov 1902), Dowie (Sept 1902; Dowie died March 1907), death 26 May 1908 in Lahore"]},{"id":"bahai-kalki","title":"Bahá'í Faith: Bahá'u'lláh as Kalki and the yuga reading","category":"Modern spiritual/esoteric","verification":"Partly verified","proponents":["Bahá'í apologists and interfaith writers (e.g., Indian Bahá'í communities)","Shoghi Effendi (source cited for the universal-expectations list in God Passes By)","Bahá'í-identified commentators on Hindu prophecy"],"primarySources":["Shoghi Effendi, God Passes By (Baha'i Publishing Trust), p. 94, as cited by Wikipedia's Kalki article","Wikipedia 'Kalki' article (raw wikitext fetched 2026), which states that in the Bahá'í Faith Bahá'u'lláh is identified as Kalki","Bahá'u'lláh, Kitáb-i-Íqán (background only; not checked for a direct Kalki reference)"],"period":"Claim made within 19th–21st century Bahá'í interpretation; Bahá'u'lláh's declaration 1863 CE; Báb's declaration 1844 CE","totalCycleYears":"4,320,000 (traditional Mahayuga)","durations":{"satya":"1,728,000 years (traditional)","treta":"1,296,000 years (traditional)","dvapara":"864,000 years (traditional)","kali":"432,000 years (traditional)"},"kaliStart":"3102 BCE (traditional reckoning)","kaliEnd":"428,899 CE (traditional reckoning, 432,000 years after start)","currentPosition":"Roughly 5,128 years into Kali Yuga by traditional reckoning in 2026 CE. The Bahá'í reading places Bahá'u'lláh's advent (1863 declaration) as the fulfilment of the Kalki/end-age messenger expectation, not as the start of a new yuga. Bahá'í texts do not use the yuga framework to give a dated end of Kali Yuga.","summary":"Some Bahá'í writers identify Bahá'u'lláh with the Kalki avatar and with the messianic figures of other traditions, citing Shoghi Effendi's list of universal expectations (God Passes By). In this reading, Bahá'u'lláh's advent fulfils Hindu prophecy about the end-age redeemer. The Bahá'í Faith does not itself adopt the Hindu yuga cosmology or give a timeline for the end of Kali Yuga; the yuga link is an interpretive apologetic move by some adherents and interpreters.","keyClaims":["Bahá'u'lláh (declared 1863) fulfils the Kalki prophecy of the end-age messenger","The Báb (declared 1844) and Bahá'u'lláh are the promised redeemer across religions, including Kalki, Maitreya, Messiah, Mahdi and Shah Bahram","Bahá'í reading uses the Kali Yuga as the period of decline that the advent of Bahá'u'lláh is said to address","Bahá'í dispensations are compared with the successive prophetic cycles of other religions"],"evidence":["Wikipedia's Kalki article states that in the Bahá'í Faith Bahá'u'lláh is identified as Kalki and as the redeeming messenger, citing Shoghi Effendi's God Passes By p. 94 and Bassuk (1987) for the broader comparative point","The identification is a comparative-religion claim: Hindu, Christian, Islamic, Buddhist and Zoroastrian expectations are listed as converging on one figure","No Bahá'í scripture was found in this run that explicitly gives a yuga-based end date or equates Kali Yuga with a Bahá'í era"],"criticisms":["The identification is retrospective and interpretive: the Kalki prophecy in the Kalki Purana describes a warrior avatar who destroys adharma with a sword and horse, while Bahá'u'lláh's mission is described as spiritual and non-violent, so the correspondence is partial","Hindu traditions do not uniformly accept Bahá'u'lláh as Kalki; the identification is a Bahá'í reading and is not recognised by Hindu authorities","Yuga timelines are not a Bahá'í doctrine, so the yuga part of the claim is an add-on by some interpreters rather than an official teaching","The comparative list (Maitreya, Mahdi, Messiah, Shah Bahram) is selective and is used to show convergence, which critics say is a pattern shared by many messianic claims that retrofit multiple traditions","The Kali Yuga reckoning (3102 BCE start, 432,000-year length) is itself a traditional, non-empirical chronology, so any link to a dated Bahá'í event inherits that uncertainty","No failed date-prediction is attached to this specific claim; the Bahá'í movement has not set a date for the end of Kali Yuga"],"plausibility":1,"plausibilityReason":"As a claim about the real-world timeline (that the yuga cycle has a dated turning point tied to Bahá'u'lláh), there is no empirical or textual basis that a skeptic can check. The traditional Kali Yuga dating is not historically verifiable, and the Kalki identification is a religious reading, not a testable prediction. It is plausible only as internal Bahá'í theology.","references":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kalki (raw wikitext, accessed 2026; statement on Bahá'í identification)","Shoghi Effendi, God Passes By, p. 94","Bassuk, Daniel E., Incarnation in Hinduism and Christianity (1987), pp. 146–147 (as cited by Wikipedia)"]},{"id":"gohar-shahi","title":"Riaz Ahmed Gohar Shahi / Kalki Avatar Foundation (Messiah Foundation International) Kalki claims","category":"Modern guru / sect","verification":"Partly verified","proponents":["Riaz Ahmed Gohar Shahi (founder, RAGS International)","Younus AlGohar (CEO, Messiah Foundation International)","Messiah Foundation International (MFI), formerly RAGS International, renamed 2000","Anjuman Serfaroshan-e-Islam (earlier affiliated group, dissolved and banned in Pakistan per reports, 2011)"],"primarySources":["Shahi's Urdu books, notably Deen-e-Ilahi (2000; English edition 2012, Balboa Press)","Hatif-e-Mahdi magazine (banned in Pakistan)","Messiah Foundation International publications (not directly fetched; the group's domain redirected to an unrelated host during this run, so it was not followed)","Wikipedia article on Riaz Ahmed Gohar Shahi (fetched this run)"],"period":"Claims made from the late 1970s onward (Shahi's preaching began in Pakistan; RAGS founded 1980); Kalki/Messiah framing developed through the 1990s-2000s","totalCycleYears":"4,320,000 years (standard Hindu four-yuga total; the group's own cycle figures were not verified)","durations":{"satya":"1,728,000 years (standard reckoning; not verified against the group's teaching)","treta":"1,296,000 years (standard reckoning; not verified)","dvapara":"864,000 years (standard reckoning; not verified)","kali":"432,000 years (standard reckoning; not verified)"},"kaliStart":"About 3102 BCE (traditional reckoning, mid-February); no Kali Yuga dating from the group was found","kaliEnd":"About 428,899 CE under the standard 432,000-year Kali Yuga, if the traditional start is used; not verified for this group","currentPosition":"Under the traditional framework, 2026 CE falls about 5,128 years into the Kali Yuga. Shahi's followers present him as the Kalki/Messiah figure who marks the transition; the exact framing for 2026 was not verified","summary":"Riaz Ahmed Gohar Shahi, a Pakistani-born Sufi preacher (born 1941 near Rawalpindi), founded RAGS International in 1980. His followers, organized as Messiah Foundation International (MFI) under CEO Younus AlGohar, present him as the Mahdi, the Messiah, and the Kalki Avatar. The Wikipedia article reviewed does not discuss the yuga cycle, so the Kalki-yuga timeline itself could not be verified. Dated claim: Shahi predicted the world would end in 2026 because of an approaching comet, with destruction described as total, though he also suggested a smaller-scale punishment might occur. The article does not report the outcome. Controversies include blasphemy convictions in Pakistan (about 59 years in absentia after he moved to England), reported disappearance in London in 2001, and violent incidents against followers.","keyClaims":["Shahi is the Mahdi, the Messiah, and the Kalki Avatar, according to followers and MFI (the Wikipedia article says he did not clearly claim the Mahdi title himself)","He says he met Jesus; MFI denies he is a form of Jesus and says Jesus returned to support the Mahdi","Images of him are said to have appeared on the Moon, the Sun, a nebula, and the Black Stone in Mecca; followers treat these as signs","He predicted a 2026 world-ending comet event; the article does not confirm or report the outcome","MFI treats him as author of the Goharian Philosophy of Divine Love"],"evidence":["Wikipedia (fetched this run) confirms the Kalki, Mahdi, and Messiah titles as follower claims, the 2026 comet prediction, the Pakistani blasphemy convictions, and the MFI leadership","The Wikipedia article carries neutrality and sourcing warnings; several claims rest on weakly sourced material","No primary MFI text on Kali Yuga durations or a yuga timeline was retrieved, so the yuga-specific claims remain unverified"],"criticisms":["Mainstream Muslim scholars and the Pakistani government condemned the teachings as blasphemy; his books and Hatif-e-Mahdi were banned in Pakistan","Critics say he claimed prophetic status, which Islamic orthodoxy rejects because the finality of prophethood is central to Islam","The 2026 comet-driven end-of-world prediction is a dated claim that, as of this research date, has not produced the predicted event (the article does not record a formal failure)","Mixed identity claims (Mahdi, Messiah, Kalki, Jesus meeting) are hard to reconcile with each other and are not backed by independent evidence","Violent incidents, including a petrol bomb at AlGohar's Manchester home and a grenade attempt at Shahi's Kotri home, show the movement's conflict with critics","The yuga-cycle framework is borrowed from Hindu cosmology and is not supported by astronomical or historical evidence as a predictive tool"],"plausibility":1,"plausibilityReason":"The Hindu yuga durations are a cosmological framework, not a measured timeline, and no evidence supports a Kalki-yuga transition at any date. The 2026 comet end-of-world prediction has no supporting astronomical evidence and, as of the research date, no such event has occurred. The claim cannot be verified as a real-world timeline.","references":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Riaz_Ahmed_Gohar_Shahi","Deen-e-Ilahi (Riaz Ahmed Gohar Shahi, 2000; English ed. Balboa Press, 2012)"]},{"id":"sathya-sai-prema-sai","title":"Sathya Sai Baba: Satya Yuga / golden age claims, Prema Sai successor prophecy, and aftermath of his 2011 death","category":"Modern guru / sect","verification":"Partly verified","proponents":["Sathya Sai Baba (1926-2011), self-declared avatar and reincarnation of Shirdi Sai Baba","Sri Sathya Sai Central Trust and Sri Sathya Sai International Organization (followers' organizations)","Lawrence Babb (anthropologist, critical account of the movement's succession claims in his 1986 study of Sai devotion)","Narasimhaiah / Kasturi-era biographers (Sai devotee biographical tradition, e.g. N. Kasturi's authorised biography)"],"primarySources":["N. Kasturi, Sathyam Sivam Sundaram (authorised biography, multi-volume, followers' publication)","Sathya Sai Baba discourses and Sanathana Sarathi magazine (organization's own publication)","Lawrence A. Babb, Redemptive Encounters: Three Modern Styles in the Hindu Tradition (1986), which records the 1963 successor statement and the age-96 rebirth claim","Wikipedia: Sathya Sai Baba (fetched this run; confirms 1963 successor statement, 24 April 2011 death at age 84)"],"period":"1926 (birth) to present; claims made 1963 onward; death 24 April 2011","totalCycleYears":"4,320,000 human years (one Mahayuga; standard Puranic figure)","durations":{"satya":"1,728,000 years (4,800 divine years)","treta":"1,296,000 years (3,600 divine years)","dvapara":"864,000 years (2,400 divine years)","kali":"432,000 years (1,200 divine years)"},"kaliStart":"3102 BCE (standard Puranic/Aryabhata-era reckoning)","kaliEnd":"428,899 CE (standard Puranic reckoning; no Sai Baba-specific end date verified)","currentPosition":"In 2026 CE, roughly 5,128 years into Kali Yuga on the standard reckoning. Sathya Sai Baba's own teaching (as recorded by followers) placed the present as a Kali Yuga phase in which his advent and teachings were meant to restore dharma and prepare a return to a Satya-like age of love, rather than a literal calendar-dated transition. The successor (Prema Sai) has not visibly appeared as of 2026 in any verifiable sense, and no verifiable Satya Yuga transition has occurred.","summary":"Sathya Sai Baba (1926-2011) claimed to be the avatar and reincarnation of Shirdi Sai Baba. Followers read his mission as restoring dharma within Kali Yuga and inaugurating a love-based, Satya-like age. In 1963, after a reported stroke and heart attacks, he stated that the same divine energy would incarnate again as a third Sai, Prema Sai Baba, in the same lineage, in Mandya district, Karnataka. He also said he would be reborn eight years after his death, at age 96, though he died at 84 on 24 April 2011. Wikipedia gives no calendar year for either claim. After his death the organisation continued under the Sri Sathya Sai International Organization and the Sri Sathya Sai Central Trust, which received UN ECOSOC Special Consultative status in 2020. The movement reports about 1,200 centres in 114 countries, though follower numbers are hard to verify.","keyClaims":["Sathya Sai Baba claimed to be the reincarnation of Shirdi Sai Baba, a claim he first made in May 1940 at age 14.","He stated that his mission was to re-establish dharma and usher in a golden age of love, framed in Kali Yuga terms (paraphrased from devotee accounts).","In 1963 he said the same divine energy would incarnate again as a third Sai, Prema Sai Baba, in Mandya district, Karnataka.","He claimed he would be reborn eight years after his death, at age 96 (he died at 84), which is internally inconsistent as stated.","After death the organisation continued without a visible successor being recognised as the third Sai."],"evidence":["Wikipedia (fetched this run): 1963 successor statement naming Prema Sai Baba and Mandya district, Karnataka; attributed to Babb and biographers.","Wikipedia (fetched this run): death 24 April 2011 at 7:40 IST, aged 84, after admission on 28 March 2011; buried 27 April 2011 with state honours.","Wikipedia (fetched this run): UN ECOSOC Special Consultative status for the Sri Sathya Sai Central Trust in 2020; about 1,200 centres in 114 countries reported.","Wikipedia (fetched this run): the article does not mention Satya Yuga, golden age, or Kali Yuga teachings in the sections read; those claims come from background knowledge.","Wikipedia (fetched this run): the Prema Sai page URL returned 404, so no dedicated article was available.","Wikipedia criticism section (partly read): accusations of sleight of hand, fraud, and other offences; he was never charged; 1993 attack on his residence; 2002 air-pistol incident at Whitefield Ashram."],"criticisms":["The Satya Yuga / golden-age framing is not falsifiable in a useful way: it is not tied to a verifiable date, so it cannot be tested against events.","The 1963 successor prophecy and the age-96 rebirth claim were not fulfilled in any verifiable sense by 2026. The age-96 claim is internally inconsistent with death at 84 and an eight-year interval.","No verifiable Prema Sai has been recognised by the organisation or by independent observers as of 2026, so the prophecy has no confirmed fulfilment.","Rationalists and magicians such as Indian rationalist groups and stage magicians described his materialisations (vibhuti, objects) as sleight of hand. Investigations and accusations of fraud, sexual abuse, and murder were made, but he was never charged.","Scholars such as Babb have described the movement as a cult in the 1980s; many academics now prefer 'new religious movement'. The Wikipedia article carries a neutrality dispute banner dated July 2026.","Successor-prophecy movements often reinterpret or extend dates when they fail, a pattern noted in the study of new religious movements."],"plausibility":1,"plausibilityReason":"As a claim about the real-world timeline, the Satya Yuga framing is unfalsifiable and rests on a Puranic cosmology with no empirical test. The specific dated claims (1963 successor; rebirth eight years after death at age 96) have no verifiable fulfilment by 2026, and the age-96 detail contradicts the stated eight-year interval. Plausibility is therefore very low.","references":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sathya_Sai_Baba (fetched; biography, 1963 successor statement, 2011 death, criticism sections)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sri_Sathya_Sai_Baba (fetched; same content, prediction of reincarnation section)","Lawrence A. Babb, Redemptive Encounters (1986)","N. Kasturi, Sathyam Sivam Sundaram (authorised biography)","Sri Sathya Sai Central Trust official site (not fetched this run)"]},{"id":"samael-aun-weor","title":"Samael Aun Weor (Universal Christian Gnostic Movement): Kali Yuga, Age of Aquarius from 1962, apocalyptic predictions","category":"Modern guru / sect","verification":"Partly verified","proponents":["Samael Aun Weor (Victor Manuel Gomez Rodriguez, 1917-1977)","Universal Christian Gnostic Movement / Universal Gnostic Movement","Gnostic Christian Church and affiliated Latin American and Spanish branches"],"primarySources":["The Aquarian Message (1960), commentary on Revelation","The Three Mountains (1972), which set a death-and-resurrection timeline before 1978","The Perfect Matrimony (revised 1961 edition)","Movement publications and lectures by Samael Aun Weor"],"period":"Mid-20th century (1950s-1977), with later movement teachings","totalCycleYears":"4,320,000 years (Hindu four-yuga cycle, or mahayuga, as commonly reckoned; Aun Weor's own cycle numbers were not verified)","durations":{"satya":"1,728,000 years (Hindu convention; not verified as Aun Weor's figure)","treta":"1,296,000 years (conventional)","dvapara":"864,000 years (conventional)","kali":"432,000 years (conventional)"},"kaliStart":"Conventional Hindu reckoning: 3102 BCE. Aun Weor's own Kali Yuga start date was not verified in this run.","kaliEnd":"Conventional reckoning: around 428 CE (3102 BCE plus 432,000 years). Aun Weor's stated end date was not verified.","currentPosition":"Per the task framing, the Age of Aquarius was said to begin in 1962, so by 2026 the movement's view is that we are in the early Aquarian era, still within a Kali Yuga framework. The movement's own current statements were not checked in this run.","summary":"Samael Aun Weor was a Colombian-born teacher (b. 1917) who founded the Universal Gnostic Movement in Mexico City after 1956. He blended Gnostic Christianity, Theosophy, Hindu cosmology and esoteric astrology. He identified himself with the Kalki or Maitreya avatar of the coming Age of Aquarius, and framed the present era as the twilight of a civilization that would end. His predictions were personal and dated: a 1960 claim that the present civilization was ending, and a 1972 claim that his own death and resurrection would occur before 1978. Both failed: he died of stomach cancer on 24 December 1977. The Yuga and Aquarius-from-1962 framing is part of his broader teaching, but I could not verify his exact wording on the Kali Yuga start or the 1962 date from the sources accessible in this run.","keyClaims":["The present civilization is in its twilight and will end, with a new civilization emerging (1960, The Aquarian Message)","He was the Kalki or Maitreya avatar of the Age of Aquarius, the union of the Logos (Samael) with his human soul","The Age of Aquarius and a new era were said to be beginning around 1962 (task framing; not verified against his text)","Souls who eliminate the ego avoid a 'second death' and further rebirths","His death and resurrection would occur before 1978, after an eight-year ordeal between his 53rd and 61st birthdays (1972, The Three Mountains)","He would remain incognito so his teaching could ferment, and planned a later return"],"evidence":["Wikipedia (en) summarizes that he identified himself with the Kalki or Maitreya avatar of the Age of Aquarius, and that he downplayed personal devotion.","Wikipedia records the 1960 Aquarian Message claim with no specific date.","Wikipedia records The Three Mountains (1972) placing his death and resurrection before 1978, and the eight-year ordeal between ages 53 and 61 (roughly 1970-1978 given his March 1917 birth).","Wikipedia records stomach cancer diagnosed in late 1977 and death in Mexico City on 24 December 1977, which falsifies the 1978 resurrection expectation as stated by followers and in his text.","Wikipedia records the movement had largely collapsed by the 1961 revised edition of The Perfect Matrimony, and was later believed to be rebuilt.","Wikipedia notes a 1984 church incorporation denial in Santa Cruz de Tenerife, a minor institutional detail."],"criticisms":["The 1978 death-and-resurrection prediction failed: he died in December 1977 and did not return.","The 1960 end-of-civilization claim gave no date and has not been testable as a dated prediction.","The 1962 Aquarius start and the Kali Yuga dates are not supported by mainstream astronomy: precession-based Aquarius start dates range from roughly the 1680s to the 2600s depending on the convention, and no consensus places it at 1962.","The Hindu Yuga chronology (3102 BCE start) is a traditional and astronomical-symbolic framework, not a historical measurement, so any Kali end date is unfalsifiable at present.","Self-identification as the Kalki or Maitreya avatar is not independently verifiable and conflicts with the traditions' own criteria.","Wikipedia itself carries maintenance tags on neutrality and reliance on primary sources, so its characterizations should be checked against secondary scholarship."],"plausibility":1,"plausibilityReason":"As a claim about the real-world timeline, the 1962 Aquarius start has no astronomical or historical basis, and the personal predictions failed. The 1960 civilization-end claim is undated, and the Kali Yuga framework itself is a symbolic cosmology rather than a testable chronology. The only falsifiable dated claim (death and resurrection before 1978) was wrong.","references":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Samael_Aun_Weor (fetched in this run; covers Aquarius, Kalki identification, movement history, 1960/1972 predictions, and 1977 death)","Samael Aun Weor, The Aquarian Message (1960)","Samael Aun Weor, The Three Mountains (1972)","Samael Aun Weor, The Perfect Matrimony (1961 revised edition)","Conventional Yuga chronology: Surya Siddhanta and standard Hindu reckoning (3102 BCE Kali start, 432,000-year Kali Yuga)"]},{"id":"sahaja-yoga","title":"Sahaja Yoga (Shri Mataji Nirmala Devi): Sahasrara opening 1970, claimed Krita/Satya Yuga, and \"Last Judgment\"","category":"Modern guru / sect","verification":"Partly verified","proponents":["Nirmala Srivastava (Shri Mataji Nirmala Devi, 1923-2011), founder","Sahaja Yoga followers and organizations worldwide"],"primarySources":["Wikipedia article 'Sahaja Yoga' (fetched in this run; no group-authored Yuga or Last Judgment timeline was accessible)","Shri Mataji's lectures and the group's own publications (not fetched in this run; not quoted here)"],"period":"Founded 5 May 1970 (per Wikipedia infobox); Kali Yuga framing ongoing","totalCycleYears":"Not stated in the sources reviewed. Standard Hindu chronology gives 4,320,000 years for a mahayuga; the group's own cycle figures were not verified.","durations":{"satya":"Not verified from sources reviewed","treta":"Not verified from sources reviewed","dvapara":"Not verified from sources reviewed","kali":"Not verified from sources reviewed; the group describes the present as Kali Yuga, the final phase before apocalypse"},"kaliStart":"Not stated in the sources reviewed","kaliEnd":"Not stated in the sources reviewed; the group frames the end as an apocalypse/Last Judgment, with no date found","currentPosition":"As of 2026, the group teaches that the world is in Kali Yuga, its final phase, with demonic possession and evil spreading. Followers see Shri Mataji (d. 2011) as Adi Shakti returned to save the earth. No dated apocalyptic prediction was found in the sources reviewed.","summary":"Sahaja Yoga is a meditation movement founded in India in 1970 by Nirmala Srivastava (Shri Mataji Nirmala Devi). It teaches that kundalini energy, awakened through a practice of self-realization, gives direct experience of the divine. Srivastava claimed to be a divine incarnation, Adi Shakti or the Holy Spirit. Its eschatology places the present in Kali Yuga, the final age before apocalypse, in which demons possess people and spread evil. Shri Mataji is said to have come to save the earth from these influences. The sources reviewed do not confirm the specific claims in the task: a 1970 \"Sahasrara opening\" framed as a Yuga turning point, a claimed Krita/Satya Yuga beginning, or a dated \"Last Judgment\". The Wikipedia article does not mention Sahasrara, Satya Yuga, or a Last Judgment date, and it gives no prediction with a date. It cites a \"Prophecies and Fulfillments\" page, which was not accessible. The 1970 date is the founding date, not a documented cosmic event. Treat the Yuga-timeline details as unverified until the group's own texts are checked.","keyClaims":["Kundalini awakening and self-realization are available through Sahaja Yoga practice (Wikipedia summary of the group's teaching)","Nirmala Srivastava is Adi Shakti / the Holy Spirit, a divine incarnation (as reported by Wikipedia; the group's own wording was not checked)","The world is in Kali Yuga, the final phase before apocalypse, with demonic possession and evil (Wikipedia summary)","The task's claims about 1970 Sahasrara opening, a Krita/Satya Yuga beginning, and a Last Judgment are not confirmed by the sources reviewed"],"evidence":["Wikipedia infobox: founded 5 May 1970 in India by Nirmala Srivastava","Wikipedia: practitioners believe meditation produces self-realization through kundalini awakening; the article does not mention Sahasrara","Wikipedia: the world is in Kali Yuga before the apocalypse; Srivastava claimed to be Adi Shakti returned to save the earth; no timeframe given","Wikipedia (second query): no dates or predictions attributed to Shri Mataji; no Satya Yuga or Last Judgment date; listed years (1970 founding, 1989 first Russia/Eastern Europe trip, mid-1980s North America, 1990 International Sahaja Public School, 2000 Yuvashakti project) are not predictions"],"criticisms":["Jean-Marie Abgrall described cult-like features including a god-like leader and demands for loyalty and money","A 2005 Belgian advisory called it a syncretic cult; that ruling was overturned on appeal in 2011","Judith Coney documented a highly segregated, subordinate role for women","Former members reported unwanted arranged marriages, fear of retribution for leaving, and pressure to cut family ties","The Guardian reported children separated from parents and large donations to Srivastava; fees are charged for some events such as international pujas","Belgian state security monitored the group's lobbying; the Austrian government described Srivastava as an authority who cannot be questioned"],"plausibility":1,"plausibilityReason":"As a claim about the real-world timeline, a 1970 cosmic turning point, a Satya/Krita Yuga beginning, or a dated Last Judgment has no empirical support. 1970 is only the founding date of an organization. The Kali Yuga framing is a religious belief, not a testable forecast, and no dated apocalyptic prediction was found. Because the unverified Yuga-timeline details in the task are not confirmed by any source reviewed, they should be treated as not established.","references":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sahaja_Yoga","Shri Mataji Nirmala Devi, lectures (not accessed in this run)","Jean-Marie Abgrall, 'Soul Stealers' (background knowledge, not checked)","Judith Coney, study of women in Sahaja Yoga (background knowledge, not checked)"]},{"id":"tm-maharishi","title":"Maharishi Mahesh Yogi / Transcendental Meditation: \"Age of Enlightenment\" (1975), Satya Yuga dawn claims, and the Maharishi Effect","category":"Modern guru / sect","verification":"Partly verified","proponents":["Maharishi Mahesh Yogi (1918-2008), founder of the Spiritual Regeneration Movement and Transcendental Meditation (TM)","Maharishi International University / Maharishi University of Management (Fairfield, Iowa)","Maharishi Foundation and Global Country of World Peace (successor organizations)","TM-Sidhi program teachers and Yogic Flying practitioners"],"primarySources":["Maharishi's lectures and writings from the mid-1970s (including the 1975 'Age of Enlightenment' lecture series), as reproduced on TM organization sites","Maharishi's 1974 crime-statistics analysis of 16 cities, cited as the origin of the 'Maharishi Effect' (per Wikipedia 'Maharishi effect' article)","Ted Karam's writings on the Maharishi Effect and the 1993 Washington, D.C. assembly (cited in Wikipedia)","Wikipedia: 'Maharishi effect', 'Transcendental Meditation movement' (fetched 2026-10-20; the dedicated 'Age of Enlightenment (Transcendental Meditation)' page returned 404)","James Randi's 1978 investigation of Robert Rabinoff's claims (cited in Wikipedia)"],"period":"Mid-1970s onward (1975 proclamation; 1974 Maharishi Effect formulation; 1976 World Government of the Age of Enlightenment)","totalCycleYears":"Traditional Yuga cycle (Kali 432,000; Dvapara 864,000; Treta 1,296,000; Satya 1,728,000; total 4,320,000 years, the Mahayuga)","durations":{"satya":"1,728,000 years (traditional Hindu reckoning)","treta":"1,296,000 years (traditional)","dvapara":"864,000 years (traditional)","kali":"432,000 years (traditional); Maharishi's framing treated the Kali Yuga as ending and a new golden-age period beginning in the mid-1970s"},"kaliStart":"3102 BCE (traditional Puranic reckoning)","kaliEnd":"Claimed to end around 1975, when the Maharishi proclaimed the Age of Enlightenment; the precise arithmetic in his materials is not verified here. Under the traditional 432,000-year Kali Yuga the end would be about 428,898 CE, so the 1975 claim is a reinterpretation, not a calculation from the standard Yuga tables.","currentPosition":"As of 2026, the 'Age of Enlightenment' has not produced any measurable global transformation. The TM movement still uses the language (Citizens and Governors of the Age of Enlightenment, Maharishi University, Maharishi School of the Age of Enlightenment), so the claim is maintained institutionally, but the predicted collective effects (crime reduction, world peace, end of war) have not occurred on the terms stated.","summary":"Maharishi Mahesh Yogi taught that the world had moved out of the dark Kali Yuga into an 'Age of Enlightenment' (a Satya/Sat Yuga-type golden period) around 1975. He tied this to the Maharishi Effect: the claim that when a small square-root-of-1% of a population practices TM or the TM-Sidhi program, including Yogic Flying, the collective 'coherence' of society improves, reducing crime, war, and disasters. The organization built institutions (World Government of the Age of Enlightenment, 1976; Maharishi Heaven on Earth, 1988; Global Country of World Peace, 2000s) and continued to describe the era as a golden age. Independent tests and historical predictions have not supported the claims.","keyClaims":["The 'Age of Enlightenment' began around 1975, marking the dawn of a Satya/Sat Yuga-type golden era after the Kali Yuga (paraphrase of the Maharishi's teaching; not a direct quote)","If roughly the square root of 1% of the population practices TM, society-wide benefits follow (the Maharishi Effect, first derived from 1974 crime-statistics claims across 16 cities)","With TM-Sidhi and Yogic Flying, about 6,325 practitioners (square root of 1% of ~4 billion people in 1974) would produce an 'Extended Maharishi Effect' with global benefits","Institutions (World Government of the Age of Enlightenment, 1976) would establish the new era politically and culturally","Large-scale global reconstruction (Maharishi Heaven on Earth, 1988, estimated at $100 trillion, and later estimates up to $300 trillion) would create a golden age"],"evidence":["Wikipedia (fetched 2026-10-20) confirms the square-root-of-1% formulation and the 1974 analysis of 16 cities as the basis for the Maharishi Effect.","Wikipedia confirms the 1993 Washington, D.C. assembly of over 4,000 practitioners and that the effect has been examined in 42 studies cited by TM-affiliated authors.","Wikipedia confirms the 1976 World Government of the Age of Enlightenment and the 1996 'Maharishi Continental Capital of the Age of Enlightenment for Europe' label, showing the era name stayed in use.","Wikipedia confirms the 1988 Maharishi Heaven on Earth Development Corp. (Malibu, California), with a stated goal to 'reconstruct the entire world' and estimated cost of $100 trillion.","Wikipedia confirms the 2000-2002 Global Country of World Peace micronation attempts and their lack of success.","Wikipedia notes that TM-affiliated claims linked the fall of the Berlin Wall and declines in terrorism, U.S. inflation, and crime to the Maharishi Effect.","Wikipedia notes James Randi's 1978 investigation of Robert Rabinoff's claims: the Fairfield police chief reported no change in local crime, and Jefferson County farm yields matched the state average.","Wikipedia notes a 1985 lawsuit in which the claims about levitation and sleep-related World War III were found to be contrary to common human experience and dismissed as time-barred."],"criticisms":["The Maharishi Effect rests on claims that cannot be tested in a falsifiable way; critics call the research pseudoscience (Randi, 1978 onward).","Crime-statistics analyses are cherry-picked, and the original 1974 analysis did not control for confounds.","The 1978 Fairfield test found no change in local crime and Jefferson County farm yields matched the state average, contrary to predictions.","Dated predictions failed: the 'Age of Enlightenment' did not produce global peace or the reduction of war, terrorism, or crime as predicted; the 1993 Washington, D.C. assembly did not produce measurable national change in published accounts.","The 1988 Heaven on Earth plan and the 2000-2002 micronation bids did not produce the stated results; the sovereignty attempts failed.","The 'Age of Enlightenment' 1975 date is a reinterpretation, not a calculation from standard Puranic Yuga arithmetic, which puts the Kali Yuga's end around 428,898 CE.","Religious and cultural critics note the claims rely on non-empirical metaphysics (Vedic Science, 'coherence' of consciousness) not supported by controlled experiments."],"plausibility":1,"plausibilityReason":"As a claim about the real-world timeline, the Age of Enlightenment's 1975 dawn is not supported: there is no measurable global transformation, the Maharishi Effect has no reproducible evidence, and the 1978 Fairfield tests and other dated predictions failed. The Yuga framework itself is a traditional cosmological scheme without empirical basis.","references":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maharishi_effect","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Transcendental_Meditation_movement","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Transcendental_Meditation","James Randi, investigation of Maharishi International University claims (1978), as summarized in Wikipedia 'Maharishi effect'","Ted Karam, writings on the Maharishi Effect (cited in Wikipedia)"]},{"id":"ananda-marga-sarkar","title":"P.R. Sarkar / Ananda Marga: social cycle theory (Shudra, Kshatriya, Vipra, Vaishya) as a reinterpretation of the yugas","category":"Modern guru / sect","verification":"Partly verified","proponents":["Prabhat Ranjan Sarkar (P.R. Sarkar, 1921-1990), founder of Ananda Marga","Ananda Marga organization and its PROUT (Progressive Utilization Theory) movement"],"primarySources":["Sarkar's discourses and books on social philosophy, including the 'Law of Social Cycle' material and PROUT writings (Ananda Marga publications; not directly reviewed in this run)","Wikipedia article 'P. R. Sarkar' (fetched in this run; covers varna typology and the cycle only in summary)"],"period":"Sarkar's ideas developed mainly 1955-1990 (Ananda Marga founded 1955; Sarkar died 1990)","totalCycleYears":"Not specified in any source I could verify. Sarkar's social cycle is not presented as a fixed-length cycle in the material I could check.","durations":{"satya":"Not specified in verified sources. Sarkar's social cycle is not mapped to fixed durations in what I could verify.","treta":"Not specified in verified sources.","dvapara":"Not specified in verified sources.","kali":"Not specified in verified sources."},"kaliStart":"Not specified. The social cycle is not presented as a mapping onto the Hindu Kali Yuga start date in verified sources.","kaliEnd":"Not specified in verified sources.","currentPosition":"Per Sarkar's framework, the present is read as an era of Vaishya (acquisitive, economic) dominance, with the cycle expected to pass into a Shudra (labour) phase. This is the standard Ananda Marga reading as I understand it from background knowledge; the Wikipedia fetch did not confirm the current-phase claim.","summary":"Sarkar described human social history as a cycle of four socio-psychological types, which he called varna: Vipra (intellectual), Kshatriya (warrior/protector), Vaishya (acquisitor/economic), and Shudra (labourer). He presented these as archetypes of leadership and social dominance, not as the Hindu caste system, and advocated a casteless society. His 'Law of Social Cycle' says that ruling elites in each era embody one of these types. Ananda Marga's common reading links the four stages to a reinterpretation of the yugas, treating them as recurring phases rather than fixed Hindu eras. Sarkar also combined the cyclical model with a linear one (economic and technological progress) and argued that real progress must prioritise the spiritual dimension. His PROUT (Progressive Utilization Theory) programme was the economic/social policy arm of the framework. Leaders who unite service, courage, intellect used for others, and entrepreneurial skill were said to be able to turn the cycle into an upward spiral, linked to the concept of sadvipras.","keyClaims":["Human history moves through four recurring socio-psychological types (Vipra, Kshatriya, Vaishya, Shudra), each dominating in turn.","The varna types are archetypes of social role and leadership, not the Hindu caste system; Sarkar advocated a casteless society.","Each era's ruling elite embodies one of the four types; the cycle can be shifted from downward to upward by leaders who combine service, courage, intellect and enterprise.","The cyclical model is combined with linear progress in economics and technology, but true progress must put spiritual development first.","The model is offered as a reinterpretation of the yuga idea, so the four stages correspond loosely to yuga-like ages rather than to the traditional 432,000-year Kali Yuga reckoning.","Sarkar rejected both capitalism and communism and criticised Marx's theory of historical evolution as conceptually narrow.","PROUT is the policy programme that follows from the social-cycle analysis."],"evidence":["Wikipedia (fetched this run) confirms the four types (Vipra, Kshatriya, Vaishya, Shudra), the cyclical plus linear dimension, the casteless-society position, the rejection of capitalism and communism, and the critique of Marx.","Wikipedia (fetched this run) does not give the stage order, does not connect the cycle to the yugas, and gives no dated predictions.","Ananda Marga's own materials (not reviewed in this run) are the source of the yuga reinterpretation and the stage order described in the summary; these are background-knowledge claims, not verified here.","Sarkar was imprisoned in 1971 on charges of murder of Ananda Marga members and was acquitted on 2 August 1978 (Wikipedia, fetched this run). This is legal history, not evidence for or against the cycle theory."],"criticisms":["The four-type scheme is not a falsifiable historical model. The stages are defined by social attributes and can be matched to almost any period after the fact.","No fixed durations are given, so the cycle cannot be tested against dates or predictions.","The yuga mapping is a reinterpretation, not a textual finding; traditional yuga durations (in Puranic sources) are not supported by historical evidence in any case, and the Sarkar mapping inherits that problem without adding evidence.","The Vaishya-to-Shudra transition has no mechanism given beyond assertion, and the cycle does not account for the historical record of long periods where no single stage dominates.","Sarkar's legal history (1971 imprisonment, acquittal in 1978) and the sect's controversies are relevant to how the movement is assessed, but are separate from the theory's merit. The Ananda Marga movement has also been criticised for its organisational practices and internal disputes; I did not verify these in this run.","Academic historians and sociologists generally do not accept cyclical four-stage models of history of this type, since they do not make testable predictions.","Failed or dated predictions: I could not verify any specific dated prediction by Sarkar in this run, so I am not listing any. This is not evidence that none were made; it is a limit of my search."],"plausibility":2,"plausibilityReason":"As a claim about the real-world timeline, the social-cycle theory is not testable as stated: no dates, durations or falsifiable predictions are attached to the stage sequence, and the yuga mapping has no historical support. The four-type scheme is a normative and interpretive framework, not an empirical model. Low plausibility as a description of actual historical periods.","references":["Wikipedia: P. R. Sarkar (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/P._R._Sarkar), fetched in this run","Ananda Marga and PROUT official publications (not reviewed in this run)"]},{"id":"selbie-steinmetz-ananda","title":"Ananda Sangha / Selbie-Steinmetz \"The Yugas\": Dvapara since ~1700 and archaeological claims","category":"Modern guru / sect","verification":"Partly verified","proponents":["Sri Yukteswar Giri (original 24,000-year yuga model, The Holy Science, 1894)","Paramahansa Yogananda (popularized the model in Autobiography of a Yogi and related writings)","Swami Kriyananda (J. Donald Walters), founder of Ananda Village and Ananda Sangha, who taught the Yukteswar-based timeline","Joseph Selbie and David Steinmetz (authors credited with 'The Yugas', 2010; details not verified in this run)"],"primarySources":["Sri Yukteswar, The Holy Science (1894)","Paramahansa Yogananda, Autobiography of a Yogi (1946), which describes Yukteswar's yuga teaching","Ananda Sangha / Ananda Village publications and website (ananda.org; direct fetch returned HTTP 403 in this run)","Selbie & Steinmetz, 'The Yugas' (2010), as named in the task; not retrieved or verified"],"period":"Yukteswar's 24,000-year precessional cycle (1894 model), taught by Ananda in the 20th and 21st centuries","totalCycleYears":"24,000 years (two 12,000-year halves, ascending and descending)","durations":{"satya":"4,800 years (ascending/descending halves as taught by Yukteswar; 4,800 + 3,600 + 2,400 + 1,200 = 12,000 per half)","treta":"3,600 years","dvapara":"2,400 years","kali":"1,200 years"},"kaliStart":"Approximately 499 CE (Yukteswar-based timeline; background knowledge, not confirmed in fetched sources)","kaliEnd":"Approximately 1699 CE, after which the teaching says the world entered Dvapara Yuga (the 'since 1700' claim in the task)","currentPosition":"Per the Ananda/Yukteswar framing, the world (2026) is in the ascending Dvapara Yuga, roughly 326 years into a 2,400-year period that the teaching says ends around 4099 CE. Note that the cycle is described as ascending, so the claim is that consciousness is rising.","summary":"Sri Yukteswar (1894) proposed that the traditional yuga durations (which Hindu texts give as a 4.32 million-year mahayuga, far longer than human chronology) should be read as a 24,000-year cycle tied to a precession-based motion of the solar system around a central star. He argued the descent into Kali Yuga began around 500 CE and that the world moved into Dvapara around 1700 CE. Yogananda popularized this in Autobiography of a Yogi, and Ananda (Kriyananda's organization) teaches it as a scientific-spiritual framework. The task's claims about the 2010 Selbie & Steinmetz book and archaeological evidence could not be verified here: the Ananda site returned 403 and no book or archaeology data was retrieved. Wikipedia pages on Yuga and Swami Kriyananda do not discuss the timeline, while the Sri Yukteswar page confirms the 24,000-year cycle and the claim that Earth had advanced to Dvapara, but gives no dates.","keyClaims":["Hindu yuga durations in scripture are symbolic or astronomically encoded; the true cycle is 24,000 years (Yukteswar).","The Earth's position in the cycle is tied to precession and the sun's orbit around a central star.","Kali Yuga (1,200 years) ended around 1699-1700 CE, and Dvapara Yuga (2,400 years) began, as the Ananda/Yukteswar timeline holds.","Ananda presents the teaching as compatible with science and archaeology (specific claims not verified here)."],"evidence":["The Wikipedia article on Sri Yukteswar Giri confirms he proposed a roughly 24,000-year cycle tied to precession and argued the Earth had moved from Kali to Dvapara Yuga, departing from traditional chronology.","The Wikipedia Yuga article confirms that the Surya Siddhanta gives the catur-yuga as 4,320,000 years, which is the standard scriptural figure the 24,000-year model replaces.","Yogananda's Autobiography of a Yogi popularized the 24,000-year model, so the teaching is well documented in Self-Realization Fellowship and Ananda literature (background knowledge).","No fetched source gave the exact 1699/1700 CE dates or any archaeological claim."],"criticisms":["The 24,000-year precession model conflicts with the astronomical value of the precession period (about 25,772 years) and with the notion that the solar system orbits a companion star, for which there is no observational support.","Yukteswar's yuga dates are not tied to any independently verifiable historical event; the 499 and 1699 CE dates are model outputs rather than archaeological findings.","Archaeological claims, if made as in the 2010 book, would need to be checked against stratigraphy, radiocarbon, and textual sources; no such evidence was retrieved in this run.","Yukteswar's 1894 text is a reinterpretation of Puranic chronology and mixes scriptural exegesis with astronomical claims. Mainstream Indology does not accept the 24,000-year scheme as a historical account.","Predictions: Kali Yuga's supposed end and the transition to Dvapara are framed as cosmic rather than dated, so no clear failed-prediction record is attributed to this entry. Any specific date-based forecasts from Ananda should be checked against their own publications."],"plausibility":2,"plausibilityReason":"As a claim about the real-world timeline, the Dvapara-since-1700 teaching has no independent support. The precession and solar-orbit premises are contradicted by observational astronomy, and no archaeological evidence was verified in this run. It is a devotional cosmology, not a historical chronology.","references":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sri_Yukteswar_Giri (fetched; confirms 24,000-year cycle and Dvapara claim)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yuga (fetched; confirms Surya Siddhanta 4,320,000-year catur-yuga)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Swami_Kriyananda (fetched; no yuga content in the first 100,000 characters)","https://www.ananda.org/yugas/ (returned HTTP 403, not retrieved)","Yukteswar, The Holy Science (1894); Yogananda, Autobiography of a Yogi (1946) (background knowledge, not fetched)"]},{"id":"osho","title":"Osho (Rajneesh) on yugas and Kali Yuga","category":"Modern guru / sect","verification":"Partly verified","proponents":["Osho (Rajneesh, 1931-1990)","Osho movement / Osho International Foundation (Pune, later Osho Commune International)","Followers who cite his discourses on Hindu and Buddhist cosmology"],"primarySources":["Osho's published discourses (talks given in India and at the Pune ashram, transcribed and published by Osho International Foundation, osho.com)","Wikipedia 'Osho' article (checked via WebFetch; it does not discuss yugas or Kali Yuga in the portions read, which covered the full article across three fetch offsets)","Hindu scriptural sources the talks draw on: Vishnu Purana, Bhagavata Purana, Manusmriti (traditional yuga framework)"],"period":"Osho's public teaching period, roughly 1960s-1990 (yuga remarks appear in his talks on Hindu and Buddhist texts; exact discourses not checked this session)","totalCycleYears":"4,320,000 years (Mahayuga, traditional Hindu figure; Osho is not known to have proposed a different total)","durations":{"satya":"1,728,000 years (traditional, 4,800 divine years)","treta":"1,296,000 years (traditional, 3,600 divine years)","dvapara":"864,000 years (traditional, 2,400 divine years)","kali":"432,000 years (traditional, 1,200 divine years)"},"kaliStart":"Traditionally 3102 BCE (Hindu chronology); no specific Osho start date verified","kaliEnd":"Traditionally about 428,898 CE (432,000 years after the traditional start); not a date Osho is confirmed to have given","currentPosition":"Under the traditional framework, about 5,128 years into Kali Yuga in 2026 (roughly 426,900 years remain). Osho's own view of the present age was not confirmed in this session; he generally framed yuga-type ages as states of consciousness rather than fixed calendar periods, which is a claim to verify against his talks.","summary":"Osho, the 20th-century Indian spiritual teacher, discussed Hindu and Buddhist cosmology in many lectures. The fair summary is that he generally treated the yuga scheme (Satya, Treta, Dvapara, Kali) as a traditional frame that he reinterpreted in psychological and spiritual terms, and that he presented the present as a time of decline and of the need for individual awakening. His better-documented end-of-age claims are not yuga-based. In the 1960s-1980s he predicted a third world war, nuclear catastrophe in the 1990s, and (in a 1984 statement relayed by Sheela) that two-thirds of humanity would die of AIDS. None of these came true. This entry could not confirm a specific Osho yuga timeline from sources in this session, so the specifics here rest on the traditional Hindu figures.","keyClaims":["Yuga scheme is a traditional Hindu cycle of four ages of declining moral order, totaling 4.32 million years per mahayuga","Osho reinterpreted cosmic ages as inner states of consciousness rather than literal calendar periods (paraphrase of his general approach; not verified against a specific discourse)","Kali Yuga is an age of decline and conflict in which the spiritual task is individual awakening","Osho's documented catastrophe predictions include a nuclear war or disaster in the 1990s and an AIDS-driven mass death of two-thirds of humanity (1984)"],"evidence":["Wikipedia 'Osho' article documents his 1964 warning of a third world war, the 1980s Rajneesh Foundation 'Noah's Ark of consciousness' framing, and the March 1984 Sheela announcement about AIDS deaths (WebFetch, offset 0)","Wikipedia article contains no yuga or Kali Yuga discussion in the sections read across three fetch offsets (0, 100000, 200000), per WebFetch","Traditional yuga durations and the 3102 BCE Kali start are standard Hindu chronology (background knowledge)"],"criticisms":["The yuga scheme is a religious cosmology, not an empirically testable timeline, so it cannot be verified or falsified against the real world.","Osho's concrete predictions with dates (1990s nuclear war, 1984 AIDS mass death claim) did not come true, which weakens his authority on timing.","Reinterpreting yugas as psychological states makes the framework unfalsifiable; it lets the teacher claim the present age is decline without any measurable criterion.","Traditional Kali Yuga dating (3102 BCE) comes from later Puranic chronology and has no independent historical confirmation.","The 432,000-year figure is a symbolic number in Hindu cosmology; no evidence supports a literal end date."],"plausibility":1,"plausibilityReason":"As a claim about the real-world timeline, the yuga framework has no empirical support. Osho's own dated predictions (1990s nuclear catastrophe, 1984 AIDS deaths) failed. A 432,000-year Kali Yuga cannot be tested in a human lifetime and has no scientific basis.","references":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Osho","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yuga","https://www.osho.com (Osho International Foundation discourse archive)"]},{"id":"art-of-living","title":"Sri Sri Ravi Shankar / Art of Living statements on yugas and Kali Yuga","category":"Modern guru / sect","verification":"Background knowledge only","proponents":["Sri Sri Ravi Shankar (founder, Art of Living Foundation, founded 1981)","Art of Living Foundation and its teachers"],"primarySources":["Sri Sri Ravi Shankar's satsang and discourse recordings (not retrieved in this run)","Art of Living Foundation website, artofliving.org (a blog URL I tried returned 404; no yuga page was located)","Wikipedia: Sri Sri Ravi Shankar and Art of Living Foundation (both fetched; neither mentions yugas)"],"period":"Traditional Hindu cosmology (Puranic/Vedic yuga scheme), applied in modern guru teaching","totalCycleYears":"4,320,000 years (one mahayuga, traditional figure)","durations":{"satya":"1,728,000 years (traditional)","treta":"1,296,000 years (traditional)","dvapara":"864,000 years (traditional)","kali":"432,000 years (traditional)"},"kaliStart":"3102 BCE (traditional epoch, as used in Indian astronomical reckoning)","kaliEnd":"About 428,899 CE by the traditional count (432,000 years after 3102 BCE)","currentPosition":"Kali Yuga, roughly 5,127 years elapsed of 432,000 as of 2026 under the traditional count. Art of Living teaching, as I understand it, frames the present as Kali Yuga and treats spiritual practice as a way to counter its effects, rather than naming a precise end date.","summary":"Sri Sri Ravi Shankar and the Art of Living Foundation teach within the broad Hindu framework of four yugas. The present age is generally described as Kali Yuga, the age of conflict, materialism and decline in consciousness. In this framing, individual and collective inner transformation (breath practices such as pranayama and Sudarshan Kriya, meditation, service and satsang) is presented as a way to uplift awareness and reduce the age's harms. I could not verify exact wording or specific dated predictions about yugas from sources in this run. Wikipedia's pages on the guru and the Foundation contain no yuga content. The only dated forward-looking statement I found on those pages is a March 2018 remark about the Ram temple dispute, which is not a yuga claim.","keyClaims":["The current era is Kali Yuga, the last and most degraded of the four ages in the traditional cycle (paraphrased; not verbatim).","Inner practice and collective spiritual effort can mitigate the negative character of Kali Yuga and help bring about a better age (paraphrased).","The Art of Living framework treats ages as states of collective consciousness that individuals can influence, not only as fixed astronomical periods (paraphrased; attribution to this specific framing is from general knowledge, not a verified quote)."],"evidence":["Wikipedia (Sri Sri Ravi Shankar): fetched in this run; no yuga or Kali Yuga mention found. Only dated remark is March 2018 on Ram temple and Syria, which the Supreme Court's 2019 Ayodhya verdict did not confirm or refute in a yuga sense.","Wikipedia (Art of Living Foundation): fetched in this run; no yuga teaching mentioned. Covers courses, social projects, and controversies (the 2014 Islamabad center burning, the 2016 World Culture Festival fine).","artofliving.org blog URL tested in this run returned HTTP 404; the site's own yuga content was not located.","The traditional yuga durations and the 3102 BCE epoch are standard in Puranic and Indian astronomical texts and are well documented in general references."],"criticisms":["The yuga scheme has no empirical basis. Nothing in archaeology, geology, or astronomy shows a 4.32-million-year cycle or a ~3102 BCE start.","The 3102 BCE epoch is a tradition-based reckoning, and the cycle's internal figures are theological, not measured. Claiming the present is Kali Yuga in a literal sense therefore rests on faith, not evidence.","Framing social and moral decline as an effect of a cosmic age can work as a rhetorical explanation that discourages accountability for concrete social problems. Critics argue that this style of framing is unfalsifiable.","Art of Living has faced legal and environmental controversies (the 2016 Yamuna floodplain festival fine, land complaints, the Islamabad center fire), which some critics link to the group's broader claims of authority. This does not directly test yuga claims, but it is relevant context for evaluating the group's statements.","I could not verify specific dated predictions about yugas from this group. Any such claims should be checked against primary recordings before being reported."],"plausibility":1,"plausibilityReason":"As a claim about the real-world timeline, the yuga cycle, its durations, and the 3102 BCE start are not supported by any independent evidence. They are a traditional cosmological scheme. The group's teaching is best read as a spiritual or cultural framework, not a testable chronology, so it scores at the bottom of the scale.","references":["Wikipedia: Sri Sri Ravi Shankar (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sri_Sri_Ravi_Shankar)","Wikipedia: Art of Living Foundation (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Art_of_Living_Foundation)","Art of Living Foundation official site (https://www.artofliving.org)","Traditional Puranic yuga durations (general reference, e.g. Vishnu Purana, Surya Siddhanta-based epoch)"]},{"id":"akram-vignan","title":"Dada Bhagwan / Akram Vignan: Kaliyug, dushamkaal, and the Akram path suited to Kali","category":"Modern guru / sect","verification":"Partly verified","proponents":["Ambalal Muljibhai Patel (A. M. Patel, 'Dada Bhagwan', 1908-1988), founder","Niruben Amin ('Niruma', led one faction after 1988, died 2006)","Deepakbhai Desai (successor to Niruben Amin after 2006; current living satsang leader per the official site)","Dada Bhagwan Foundation / Adalaj Trimandir organization (Gujarat, India)"],"primarySources":["Wikipedia article 'Dada Bhagwan' (fetched 2026-10-20; origins, 1958 self-realization claim, 1962/1963 first recipients, 1968 public Gnanvidhi, 1988 death, post-1988 split, Niruben Amin, Deepakbhai Desai)","Official site dadabhagwan.org (fetched 2026-10-20; describes Akram Vignan as a spiritual science, Gnan Vidhi as roughly a two-hour ceremony, Deepakbhai Desai as current satsang leader)","Dada Bhagwan / Akram Vignan published books (Gujarati and English) by the Dada Bhagwan Foundation, the primary teaching texts; not fetched in this run"],"period":"Founded 1958 (self-realization claim) to present; Gnanvidhi public from 1968","totalCycleYears":"Hindu Mahayuga about 4,320,000 years; Jain avasarpini/utsarpini cycle of six aras (very long). The Akram teaching does not supply its own cycle length in the sources checked.","durations":{"satya":"Hindu: 1,728,000 years (Krita Yuga). Jain: first ara of avasarpini (Sushama-sushama), about 4 Kotakoti sagaropama.","treta":"Hindu: 1,296,000 years. Jain: second ara (Sushama), about 3 Kotakoti sagaropama.","dvapara":"Hindu: 864,000 years. Jain: third ara (Sushama-dushama), about 2 Kotakoti sagaropama.","kali":"Hindu Kali Yuga: 432,000 years, begun 3102 BCE. Jain fifth ara (Dushama): 21,000 years; sixth ara (Dushama-dushama): 21,000 years."},"kaliStart":"Hindu reckoning: 3102 BCE. Jain reckoning: the fifth ara (Dushama) began after Mahavira's nirvana, traditionally about 3 years and 8.5 months after it, roughly 524 BCE.","kaliEnd":"Hindu: about 428,898 CE. Jain: fifth ara of 21,000 years, ending around 20,500 CE. The teaching sources checked do not state an exact end date.","currentPosition":"As of 2026-10-20: leadership is under Deepakbhai Desai, described on the official site as the current living satsang leader. The Hindu Kali Yuga is about 5,127 years in. In the Jain reckoning, the fifth ara has run about 2,550 of its 21,000 years. Akram Vignan holds that the present is a dushamkaal in which the Akram path is available.","summary":"Akram Vignan is a path founded by A. M. Patel ('Dada Bhagwan') that promises rapid self-realization in a short ceremony (Gnan Vidhi). Its followers frame the present as a dushamkaal (bad time) in which the Akram ('stepless') route is the appropriate way out. Wikipedia and the official site, fetched for this run, document the movement's history and leaders but do not mention Kaliyug, dushamkaal, or a Jain fifth-ara timeline. Those links come from background knowledge of the movement's published teachings and are not verified here.","keyClaims":["The present era is a dushamkaal (bad time), and the Akram path offers rapid liberation suited to it, in contrast to gradual Jain purification.","Self-realization is attained through the grace of Simandhar Swami, with Dada Bhagwan as the medium, in a roughly two-hour Gnan Vidhi.","Authority passes through a living Gnani; Deepakbhai Desai is the current living satsang leader.","Jain and Hindu cosmological timelines (fifth ara, Kali Yuga) place the present inside a long declining cycle."],"evidence":["Wikipedia (fetched 2026-10-20) confirms the founder's biography, the 1958 self-realization claim, the 1962/1963 first recipients, the 1968 public Gnanvidhi in Bombay, the death on 2 January 1988, and the post-1988 split with Niruben Amin.","Wikipedia's text does not mention Kaliyug, Kal, dushamkaal, the Jain fifth ara, or that Akram is suited to the present era. Its only related point is the contrast between Akram's quick salvation and step-by-step Jain purification.","The official site (fetched 2026-10-20) presents Akram Vignan as a spiritual science with a two-hour Gnan Vidhi and Deepakbhai Desai as the living satsang leader. Its retrieved pages contain no Kaliyug content.","Jain six-ara cosmology and Hindu yuga durations (Kali Yuga 432,000 years from 3102 BCE) are standard doctrine. The numbers are reliable as doctrine, but the Akram teaching's use of them was not verified in the sources fetched."],"criticisms":["The Kaliyug/dushamkaal framing is a doctrinal premise, not a testable chronology. The teaching makes no falsifiable date prediction for the end of Kali Yuga or the fifth ara.","Instant self-realization in one ceremony cannot be verified by independent observation. Critics, including former members and journalists, have raised concerns about governance, finances, and the authority granted to the living Gnani.","The 1988 succession dispute, with Niruben Amin leading one faction, shows that the lineage claim is contested within the movement.","The Hindu and Jain cycle lengths are cosmological constructs with no empirical basis, so a modern timeline built on them has no physical evidence.","Reading the present as a dushamkaal is an interpretive stance that cannot be checked against historical data, so it is effectively unfalsifiable."],"plausibility":1,"plausibilityReason":"As a claim about the real-world timeline, the Kali Yuga and Jain fifth-ara durations are cosmological doctrines with no empirical or historical basis. The Akram teaching offers no checkable date or event. The movement's historical facts (founder biography, 1958 claim, 1968 Gnanvidhi, 1988 death, 2006 succession) are documented but do not confirm the cosmological timeline. The claim that the present is a dushamkaal suited to a shortcut path is a spiritual judgment that cannot be tested against the world.","references":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dada_Bhagwan","https://www.dadabhagwan.org/","Dada Bhagwan Foundation published works on Akram Vignan (Gujarati and English), not fetched in this run","Jain six-ara avasarpini/utsarpini cycle with 21,000-year Dushama and Dushama-dushama aras (Tattvarthasutra tradition)","Hindu yuga reckoning: Kali Yuga 432,000 years from 3102 BCE (Surya Siddhanta tradition)"]},{"id":"swaminarayan","title":"Swaminarayan Sampradaya (BAPS etc.) teachings on the yugas","category":"Modern guru / sect","verification":"Partly verified","proponents":["Swaminarayan (Sahajanand Swami, 1781-1830)","Swaminarayan Sampradaya (Vadtal, Ahmedabad, Junagadh and Bhuj lineages)","BAPS Swaminarayan Sanstha (Shastriji Maharaj founder; Mahant Swami Maharaj current guru)"],"primarySources":["Vachanamrut (273 discourses, 1819-1829, compiled by senior disciples and approved by Swaminarayan)","Shikshapatri (1826)","Bhagavata Purana and Vishnu Purana, the Puranic yuga scheme the sect inherits","BAPS official publications (baps.org; not fetched, the site returned HTTP 403)"],"period":"Founder active 1781-1830 CE; sect teaching continues to 2026","totalCycleYears":"4,320,000 years (mahayuga / catur-yuga)","durations":{"satya":"1,728,000 years (4,800 divine years)","treta":"1,296,000 years (3,600 divine years)","dvapara":"864,000 years (2,400 divine years)","kali":"432,000 years (1,200 divine years)"},"kaliStart":"17/18 February 3102 BCE (midnight at Ujjain meridian, proleptic Julian), per the traditional Puranic reckoning. The sect uses this traditional date and does not revise it.","kaliEnd":"Traditionally about 428,899 CE, using the 432,000-year length. The Wikipedia Kali Yuga article cites this figure and a Godwin citation of about 427,000 CE.","currentPosition":"Kali Yuga is ongoing. About 5,128 years have elapsed in 2026 (3102 BCE plus 2026 CE), with roughly 426,870 years remaining by traditional reckoning. The Wikipedia article gives 426,873 remaining as of 2026, which is consistent within rounding.","summary":"The Swaminarayan tradition, including the BAPS Swaminarayan Sanstha, accepts the standard Puranic (Bhagavata/Vishnu Purana) four-yuga scheme of Satya, Treta, Dvapara and Kali, totaling 4,320,000 years per mahayuga, and places the present era in Kali Yuga, which began in 3102 BCE. Its theology uses yuga-time mainly as a moral and spiritual frame: Kali Yuga is an age of moral decline and worldly attachment, yet the sect teaches that liberation is still attainable, chiefly through devotion to God and association with a living guru. The sect's distinctive claim is that God (Purushottam) took human form as Swaminarayan in 1781 CE, inside Kali Yuga, and that the Gunatit Guru lineage continues his presence. Because of that claim, the sect treats its own era as a period of divine manifestation rather than only decline. The sect does not, as far as I could verify, publish an end-date prediction for Kali Yuga, and BAPS's public teaching focuses on ethics, devotion and service rather than on cosmic chronology. Confidence note: I could not retrieve BAPS's own pages (HTTP 403) or the Vachanamrut text on yugas, so the specific yuga passages and their wording are from background knowledge and are not quoted here.","keyClaims":["The four yugas follow the standard Puranic scheme: Satya (1.728M years), Treta (1.296M), Dvapara (864k), Kali (432k), for a total of 4.32M years per mahayuga.","The present era is Kali Yuga, which began in 3102 BCE (traditional reckoning).","Kali Yuga is characterized by moral decline, but liberation remains possible through devotion and association with a living guru.","Swaminarayan (born 3 April 1781 CE near Ayodhya) is taught as God's manifestation within the Kali Yuga, so the sect's theology treats the present age as the era of divine descent.","No public end-date prediction is made by the sect for Kali Yuga; the traditional Puranic end-date of about 428,899 CE is the implicit reference point."],"evidence":["Wikipedia (Yuga): confirms the four-yuga catur-yuga concept, the 4,320,000-year mahayuga, and the 1,000-catur-yuga kalpa from the Bhagavata Purana; the article does not discuss Swaminarayan or BAPS.","Wikipedia (Kali Yuga): gives the start date of 17/18 February 3102 BCE, 432,000 years total (360k main plus 36k dawn and 36k dusk), an end date of about 428,899 CE, and about 426,873 years remaining as of 2026.","Wikipedia (Swaminarayan): confirms Swaminarayan's birth on 3 April 1781 at Chhapaiya near Ayodhya, sannyasa on 20 October 1800, succession to Ramanand Swami in 1802, and that the Vachanamrut comprises 273 discourses delivered 1819-1829. The fetched text did not mention Kali Yuga.","Wikipedia (BAPS): confirms the sect is a denomination within Swaminarayan Sampradaya, founded by Shastriji Maharaj (consecration at Bochasan on 5 June 1907 per the infobox; the lead says 1905), registered as a charitable trust in 1947, with Mahant Swami Maharaj as 6th guru since 2016. The article does not cover yugas or cosmology."],"criticisms":["The 3102 BCE start date and 432,000-year length have no historical or archaeological basis; they are a Puranic and Surya Siddhanta-derived construct.","The 4.32M-year mahayuga figure is a theological or astronomical convention, not a measured quantity; Indian astronomers used it as a unit of calculation.","Scholars of Indology note that the yuga schema was developed across texts over centuries (Mahabharata, Manusmriti, Puranas), so no single canonical reckoning exists.","Critics within the wider Hindu reform and academic world argue that the sect's Kali Yuga framing, which elevates the present age as the era of divine manifestation, is theologically, not empirically, grounded.","The sect's claims about Swaminarayan as God are contested by other Hindu traditions and by academic historians of the Swaminarayan movement; the cosmic-time frame inherits these disputes.","Unlike several apocalyptic traditions, the sect does not generate dated end-of-age predictions, so there are no documented failed predictions to report for this group."],"plausibility":1,"plausibilityReason":"As a claim about the real-world timeline, the 4.32M-year cycle and the 3102 BCE start have no empirical basis: no measurable event or record fixes a 432,000-year cycle, and the 3102 BCE date is a traditional calculation with no independent corroboration. The sect's theology is internally coherent and makes no falsifiable prediction about dates, so it neither succeeds nor fails empirically; the cosmology simply is not supported by evidence.","references":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yuga","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kali_Yuga","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Swaminarayan","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bochasanwasi_Akshar_Purushottam_Swaminarayan_Sanstha","Vachanamrut (Swaminarayan, 1819-1829), background knowledge, not fetched","Shikshapatri (Swaminarayan, 1826), background knowledge, not fetched"]},{"id":"radha-soami","title":"Radha Soami / Sant Mat (Beas, Dayalbagh): Kal, yugas, and Kali Yuga in Sant Mat cosmology","category":"Modern guru / sect","verification":"Partly verified","proponents":["Shiv Dayal Singh (Swami Ji Maharaj), founder of the Radha Soami movement, Agra, 1861 (per Wikipedia summary)","Jaimal Singh, who established the Beas center (1880s to 1891, per Wikipedia summary)","Sawan Singh and later Beas leaders (Radha Soami Satsang Beas)","Dayalbagh leaders (Radhasoami Satsang Dayalbagh, Agra), including Sahabji Maharaj (Anand Sarup) and successors"],"primarySources":["Wikipedia, 'Radha Soami' (fetched; gives movement chronology but no yuga content)","Radha Soami Satsang Beas official site (radhasoamisatsangbeas.org), background knowledge, not fetched","Radhasoami Satsang Dayalbagh official site (dayalbagh.org), background knowledge, not fetched","Sar Bachan (Shiv Dayal Singh), background knowledge of its cosmological sections, not fetched"],"period":"Founded 1861 (Agra); Beas and Dayalbagh branches from the 1880s to the 1910s; teaching continues in 2026","totalCycleYears":"Not specified in the sources I could consult. Sant Mat does not publish a yuga-length table comparable to Puranic reckoning. Traditional Hindu figures (Mahayuga 4,320,000 years; Kali Yuga 432,000 years) are the reference point, but I did not verify any Sant Mat restatement of them.","durations":{"satya":"Not specified in consulted sources (traditional Puranic figure 1,728,000 years; not verified as a Sant Mat teaching)","treta":"Not specified in consulted sources (traditional figure 1,296,000 years)","dvapara":"Not specified in consulted sources (traditional figure 864,000 years)","kali":"Not specified in consulted sources (traditional figure 432,000 years)"},"kaliStart":"Not established by Sant Mat sources I consulted. Traditional Hindu reckoning places the start at 3102 BCE; I did not verify that Radha Soami or Sant Mat adopts this date.","kaliEnd":"Not stated in consulted sources. Sant Mat does not appear to offer a dated end-of-Kali prediction; its emphasis is on individual escape from Kal rather than on chronology.","currentPosition":"Per traditional reckoning we are in the Kali Yuga. Sant Mat groups (Beas, Dayalbagh, and others) generally treat the present as a period of spiritual decline in which Kal dominates the lower creation, and hold that a living master (Satguru) is needed to lead souls back to the true home. I did not verify any group's explicit statement of the current yuga.","summary":"Radha Soami, founded by Shiv Dayal Singh in Agra in 1861, and its branches (Beas, Dayalbagh, and others, collectively called Sant Mat) teach a spiritual cosmology in which creation is divided into planes of consciousness: the material world (Pind), subtle and causal regions (Brahmand, Trikuti, Sahasdal Kanwal), and true spiritual regions (Sach Khand or Sat Lok, and higher). Kal (also Kal Niranjan, Dharam Rai, or Kal Purush in the literature) rules the lower planes and keeps souls bound by karma and repeated birth. Sant Mat frames the yugas mainly as descriptions of moral and spiritual decline within Kal's domain, drawing on Hindu terminology without publishing a detailed predictive timeline of the kind found in Puranic or Kalki-based systems. The practical concern is escape from Kal through initiation and inner practice under a living Satguru, not calculating when the Kali Yuga ends.","keyClaims":["Creation consists of graded planes, with Kal controlling the lower regions (paraphrase of Sant Mat teaching; primary text not fetched)","A living Satguru (Sant) is required to lead souls out of Kal's domain","The present era is one of spiritual decline in Kali Yuga language, but Sant Mat does not emphasize yuga chronology","Liberation is pursued through inner practice (simran, dhyan, surat shabd yoga) rather than external ritual or calendar prediction"],"evidence":["Wikipedia (fetched) confirms founding in 1861 at Agra, Beas established by Jaimal Singh in the 1880s to 1891, the 1902 Central Administrative Council at Soami Bagh, Dayalbagh founding and move (dates given as 1907/1913 in the fetched summary), 1948 split producing Dera Sacha Sauda, and 1962 founding of Manavta Mandir; none of this concerns yugas","Sant Mat groups are widely described as centred on a living master and inner light/sound practice, consistent with the Kal-escape framing; specific passages not verified","No Sant Mat group I know of is widely documented as publishing a dated Kali Yuga end prediction; this is a weak inference from background knowledge"],"criticisms":["Sant Mat cosmology is effectively unfalsifiable as a timeline: it describes inner planes of consciousness that cannot be tested against chronology, and offers no dated predictions to check","The yuga framework is borrowed from Hindu Puranic tradition, whose durations (4.32 million-year mahayuga, etc.) have no empirical basis","Sant Mat groups have been criticized, including by former members and commentators, for guru-centred authority, unverifiable claims about inner light and sound, and succession disputes and splits","Beas and Dayalbagh both claim to preserve the original Radha Soami teaching, creating internal disagreement about how literally to read the Hindu yuga scheme"],"plausibility":1,"plausibilityReason":"As a claim about the real-world timeline, the Puranic yuga durations have no empirical support, and Sant Mat adds no testable calendar. Kali Yuga status is meaningful only inside the traditional framework. No dated cosmological prediction exists to check, so there are no failed predictions to report. The historical dates that were verified (1861, 1880s to 1891, 1902, 1948, 1962) are facts about the movement, not predictions; the Dayalbagh founding date in the fetched summary should be checked against the Dayalbagh site before citing.","references":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Radha_Soami","https://radhasoamisatsangbeas.org (not fetched)","https://dayalbagh.org (not fetched)"]},{"id":"jai-gurudev","title":"Baba Jai Gurudev (Mathura): \"Satyug aayega\" prophecy","category":"Modern guru / sect","verification":"Background knowledge only","proponents":["Followers of Baba/Sant Jai Gurudev and the Jai Gurudev ashram tradition centred on Mathura, Uttar Pradesh","Circulators of the \"Satyug aayega\" slogan on posters, pamphlets and social media"],"primarySources":["Not verified in this run. The Wikipedia page URL (en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jai_Gurudev) returned HTTP 404, and no ashram site, news article, or YouTube source was retrieved. Original teaching texts or discourses should be checked directly (ashram publications, Hindi-language sources)."],"period":"20th century (guru active in Mathura; exact dates not verified)","totalCycleYears":"4,320,000 years (one Mahayuga, Puranic reckoning)","durations":{"satya":"1,728,000 years","treta":"1,296,000 years","dvapara":"864,000 years","kali":"432,000 years"},"kaliStart":"3102 BCE (traditional epoch, start of Kali Yuga)","kaliEnd":"Approximately 428,899 CE under the 432,000-year Kali Yuga reckoning (no dated prediction of Satyug's arrival was verified)","currentPosition":"Per the traditional reckoning, 2026 CE falls in Kali Yuga, roughly year 5,128 (3102 BCE + 2026). The \"Satyug aayega\" message is a slogan of imminent renewal, not a specific dated prophecy that I could verify.","summary":"Baba/Sant Jai Gurudev is associated with a devotional following in Mathura. Posters and slogans attributed to his tradition proclaim \"Satyug aayega\" (the Age of Truth will come). The teaching, as circulated, frames the present as a degenerate Kali Yuga and expects a turn to Satyug through devotion, moral discipline, and the guru's path. I could not verify the exact wording, the date range, or any specific predicted event from a primary source in this run, so the content below rests on general knowledge of Hindu yuga cosmology and of the slogan's circulation.","keyClaims":["The current era is Kali Yuga, a period of moral decline in the Puranic four-age cycle.","Satyug (Satya Yuga, the Age of Truth) will return after Kali Yuga ends, so the decline is not permanent.","Spiritual discipline, devotion, and adherence to the guru's teaching are the means by which this change is brought about (as paraphrased in the slogan's spirit; not verified against a primary text)."],"evidence":["The slogan \"Satyug aayega\" is widely reported as circulating on posters associated with the Mathura tradition (general background knowledge; not confirmed by a retrieved source).","Hindu yuga chronology (Manusmriti/Puranic accounts: 4,320,000-year Mahayuga with 4:3:2:1 proportions) is well documented and sets Kali Yuga's end around 428,899 CE.","Under that chronology, Kali Yuga has run for about 5,128 years by 2026 CE, so any Satyug arrival in the near term is not the traditional reckoning."],"criticisms":["The slogan is an undated, unfalsifiable expectation. It gives no specific year, event, or test that could show it right or wrong.","The Puranic yuga durations are a cosmological scheme, not an empirical timeline. Treating them as a schedule for near-term social change conflicts with the scheme's own numbers, since Kali Yuga is set to last about 428,000 more years.","Predictions of an imminent golden age recur across religious and guru movements; the claim is usually reinterpreted as a moral or spiritual state rather than a dated event, which makes it hard to assess.","Guru-centred movements can attribute social improvement to devotion without evidence, and claims of spiritual transformation are not verifiable."],"plausibility":2,"plausibilityReason":"As a claim about the real-world timeline, a near-term Satyug is incompatible with the traditional 432,000-year Kali Yuga reckoning, which puts its end around 428,899 CE. The slogan also has no date or testable prediction that could be checked. Read as a metaphorical or moral aspiration it is not refutable, but as a dated timeline claim it has no support.","references":["Puranic yuga chronology as described in the Vishnu Purana and Manusmriti (general reference; not retrieved in this run)","Wikipedia article on Jai Gurudev: not retrieved (HTTP 404 from en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jai_Gurudev)"]},{"id":"satyug-darshan","title":"Satyug Darshan Trust (Faridabad, Sajan ji): claims about Satyug arriving","category":"Modern guru / sect","verification":"Not found","proponents":["Satyug Darshan Trust, Faridabad (Haryana), as named in the task","Sajan ji (the figure the task associates with the Trust; I could not confirm his full name or role)"],"primarySources":["None retrieved. Wikipedia page 'Satyug_Darshan_Trust' returned HTTP 404. DuckDuckGo returned a bot challenge. A Bing search returned only unrelated results (online gaming sites)."],"period":"Unknown. No dated source found for the Trust or its teaching.","totalCycleYears":"Standard Puranic mahayuga is 4,320,000 years (Satya 1,728,000; Treta 1,296,000; Dvapara 864,000; Kali 432,000). This is the general framework, not confirmed as the Trust's own figure.","durations":{"satya":"1,728,000 years (standard Puranic figure; the Trust's usage not verified)","treta":"1,296,000 years (standard Puranic figure; not verified for this group)","dvapara":"864,000 years (standard Puranic figure; not verified for this group)","kali":"432,000 years (standard Puranic figure; not verified for this group)"},"kaliStart":"Traditionally 3102 BCE (the conventional start of Kali Yuga in Puranic-astronomical reckoning). Whether the Trust uses this date is not verified.","kaliEnd":"Approximately 428,899 CE if the full 432,000 years is counted from 3102 BCE. Not verified for this group.","currentPosition":"Unverified. The task premise is that the Trust claims Satyug is arriving or has begun. I could not find the Trust's own statement of its current-era claim, so I cannot say what it asserts for 2026.","summary":"Satyug Darshan Trust, based in Faridabad, Haryana, is reportedly associated with a figure called Sajan ji and with the claim that Satyug (the golden age, first of the four Yugas) is arriving or has begun. I could not retrieve any source describing its teaching, its specific predictions, or dates, so this entry does not describe the group's actual doctrine. Any description of what the Trust teaches would be guesswork, and I have not provided one.","keyClaims":["Per the task premise only: the Trust claims Satyug is arriving. I could not confirm the wording, the mechanism, or the timeline it gives.","Puranic framework (background, not attributed to the Trust): Kali Yuga is the current age in traditional chronology, and a return to Satyug would require the cycle to turn, which the standard texts place roughly 400,000 years away from the Kali start."],"evidence":["No retrieved source supports or refutes any specific prediction by the Trust.","The standard Puranic chronology (432,000-year Kali Yuga starting 3102 BCE) is background knowledge and contains no reference to a Satyug return in 2026. Any claim that Satyug has arrived would contradict the standard texts."],"criticisms":["Without a primary source, the Trust's claims cannot be checked; any claim that Satyug has arrived is not something a 2026 observer can verify.","Under the standard chronology, Kali Yuga is far from over, so a claim of Satyug arriving would have to rest on a reinterpretation of the Yuga system rather than on the conventional calendar.","If the Trust has made dated predictions, none were found here, so none can be marked as failed or fulfilled."],"plausibility":1,"plausibilityReason":"As a claim about the real-world timeline, the premise that Satyug has arrived conflicts with the standard Puranic chronology, and no evidence for it was found. The rating reflects the premise as stated, not a judgment of the group's sincerity or its doctrine, which I could not verify.","references":["Wikipedia 'Satyug_Darshan_Trust': HTTP 404 (page does not exist at this title)","DuckDuckGo search: bot challenge, no results","Bing search: unrelated results only (gaming sites)"]},{"id":"nithyananda-kailasa","title":"Nithyananda / Kailasa: yuga claims","category":"Modern guru / sect","verification":"Not found","proponents":["Swami Nithyananda (Arunkumar Sivashankaran, born 1977)","Nithyananda Dhyanapeetham (Bidadi, Karnataka)","Kailaasa (self-declared Hindu nation, NGO network)"],"primarySources":["Wikipedia: Nithyananda (fetched; no yuga-timeline content found)","Wikipedia: Kailaasa (fetched; no yuga-timeline content found)","India Today reports as cited by Wikipedia (2018 third-eye claim; Dec 2019 Ecuador island report)","Kailaasa and Nithyananda official channels and YouTube (not accessed in this run)"],"period":"Dec 2019 onward (Kailaasa); teaching activity since the 2000s","totalCycleYears":"4,320,000 years (traditional mahayuga; no verified Nithyananda-specific cycle length)","durations":{"satya":"1,728,000 years (traditional; no verified Nithyananda figure)","treta":"1,296,000 years (traditional; no verified Nithyananda figure)","dvapara":"864,000 years (traditional; no verified Nithyananda figure)","kali":"432,000 years (traditional; no verified Nithyananda figure)"},"kaliStart":"3102 BCE","kaliEnd":"c. 428,899 CE by traditional reckoning; no Nithyananda-specific end date found","currentPosition":"2026: Kali Yuga under the traditional framework. No verified Nithyananda statement placing the present in a different yuga was found in this run. Kailaasa presents itself as a Hindu restoration project, not as a yuga-transition event.","summary":"Nithyananda is a self-styled Hindu guru whose organization Kailaasa was announced in December 2019 as a 'nation without borders' for 'dispossessed Hindus' and a sovereign Hindu nation. This run could not verify any specific yuga-duration, yuga-transition or dated end-of-Kali-Yuga teaching attributed to him. Background knowledge suggests his talks use general Hindu framing (Kali Yuga as a degenerate age, with a Hindu renaissance restored through enlightenment and his movement), but no concrete cycle chart, durations or dates could be sourced. Treat any specific yuga timeline attributed to him as unverified until a primary talk or publication is located.","keyClaims":["Kailaasa is a 'nation without borders' created by 'dispossessed Hindus', announced December 2019 (per Wikipedia summary of his announcement).","Kailaasa claims to be the world's only sovereign Hindu nation; reported as a network of NGOs across three continents with no government recognition.","India Today reported in December 2019 that Nithyananda had bought an island in Ecuador for Kailaasa (per Wikipedia summary; Ecuador did not recognize it).","A 2021 claim that COVID would end once he was received in India with respect and a pilgrimage to Shakti Pithas was made (reported 7 June 2021).","No yuga-specific claims were verified in this run."],"evidence":["Wikipedia Nithyananda page: 2018 India Today report on a promised free third-eye opening 'by 2021' (failed prediction; no such opening occurred).","Wikipedia Kailaasa page: 7 June 2021 claim that COVID would end after a pilgrimage to Shakti Pithas (failed prediction).","Wikipedia Kailaasa page: Dec 2019 'Hindu nation' announcement; Aug 2020 coin designs referencing 'ancient 56 Hindu nations'; Feb 2023 UN committee submission calling Kailaasa the 'first sovereign state for Hindus' (reportedly ignored).","Wikipedia Nithyananda page (from earlier fetch): mainstream press treats Kailaasa as a 'fake country' and his organization denies wrongdoing."],"criticisms":["No verifiable yuga chart or dated end-of-Kali-Yuga prediction attributed to Nithyananda was found, so specific claims cannot be evaluated against his own words.","Kailaasa is not recognized by any government or intergovernmental body; the Ecuador island and UN submissions produced no recognition.","Documented dated predictions (third-eye opening by 2021; COVID ending after pilgrimage in 2021) were not fulfilled.","Background knowledge (not verified in this run): he has faced criminal cases in India, including a 2010 case and 2019 child-abduction charges, and fled India in 2019. These allegations weigh on the credibility of his organization's statements.","Yuga cycle framing, if he uses it, is traditional Hindu cosmology (4.32 million-year mahayuga) with no empirical or historical basis for dating a transition in 2026."],"plausibility":1,"plausibilityReason":"As a claim about the real-world timeline there is no verifiable basis: the traditional yuga durations are a cosmological framework, not measurable history, and the Kali Yuga start (3102 BCE) is a traditional reckoning. The checkable dated predictions attributed to Nithyananda (third-eye opening by 2021; COVID ending in 2021) failed, and no specific yuga-transition date from him was found to evaluate.","references":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nithyananda (fetched 2026-10-20)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kailaasa (fetched 2026-10-20)","India Today reports from 2018 and December 2019 (as cited by Wikipedia; not independently fetched)"]},{"id":"kalki-dham","title":"Kalki Dham (Sambhal, Acharya Pramod Krishnam) and the Sambhal Kalki-birthplace belief","category":"Regional prophetic tradition","verification":"Partly verified","proponents":["Acharya Pramod Krishnam (promoter of the Kalki Dham project)","Local Sambhal Hindu organisations and trusts supporting the temple","Vaishnava devotees who identify Sambhal with Shambhala, the traditional birthplace of Kalki"],"primarySources":["Bhagavata Purana, Book 12 (prophecy of Kalki born in the village of Shambhala, house of Vishnuyasha)","Vishnu Purana and Brahmanda Purana Kalki prophecies","Wikipedia article 'Sambhal' (fetched this run; confirms the Shambhala identification and the Shri Kalki Vishnu Mandir)","Wikipedia 'Kalki Dham' and 'Kalki Dham Temple' (both returned HTTP 404 this run, so not read)","India Today, 19 Feb 2024, 'Kalki Mandir and why Sambhal is significant' (cited on the Sambhal Wikipedia page; text not retrieved)"],"period":"Scriptural prophecy (Puranic, pre-modern); modern temple project (announced and promoted in the 2020s)","totalCycleYears":"4,320,000 years (one Mahayuga: Satya 1,728,000 + Treta 1,296,000 + Dvapara 864,000 + Kali 432,000)","durations":{"satya":"1,728,000 years (divine years, i.e. 4,800 divine years x 360 = 1,728,000 human years in the Puranic reckoning)","treta":"1,296,000 years","dvapara":"864,000 years","kali":"432,000 years"},"kaliStart":"3102 BCE (traditional Puranic/astronomical epoch, not a historically dated event)","kaliEnd":"Approximately 427,000 CE by the traditional reckoning (432,000 years after 3102 BCE). Kalki's appearance is said to occur at the close of Kali Yuga, though the texts do not give a calendar date.","currentPosition":"Per the traditional reckoning, 2026 CE falls in Kali Yuga year roughly 5,128 (about 5,127 years elapsed). About 426,870 years of Kali Yuga remain. Under this reading Kalki has not yet appeared, and the Sambhal temple is a present-day devotional project tied to a future prophecy.","summary":"The Puranas (notably the Bhagavata Purana, Book 12) predict that Kalki, the tenth avatar of Vishnu, will be born at the close of Kali Yuga in a village called Shambhala, in the house of a Brahmin named Vishnuyasha, and will restore dharma. Sambhal in western Uttar Pradesh has been traditionally identified with Shambhala, and a Shri Kalki Vishnu Mandir already exists there. Acharya Pramod Krishnam and associated trusts have promoted a larger 'Kalki Dham' temple project in Sambhal, which has been reported with a foundation-stone event and a prime-ministerial connection. I could not confirm that PM Modi personally laid the foundation stone in 2024 from the sources I could reach, so treat that detail as unverified. The project belongs to the wider Hindu devotional tradition of Kalki prophecy. Its claims about place (Sambhal as birthplace) rest on textual identification, not on any archaeological or documentary evidence.","keyClaims":["Kalki, the tenth avatar of Vishnu, is destined to be born in Shambhala, which devotees identify with Sambhal (Uttar Pradesh).","Kalki's appearance marks the end of Kali Yuga and the restoration of dharma.","A Kalki Dham temple in Sambhal is a fitting and necessary monument to this belief (promoted by Acharya Pramod Krishnam and supporters).","The Kali Yuga timeline stretches to hundreds of thousands of years, so the prophecy is framed as far in the future."],"evidence":["Sambhal Wikipedia page (fetched this run) confirms that Sambhal is identified with Shambhala, the Mahabharata/Puranic birthplace of Kalki, and that a Shri Kalki Vishnu Mandir exists in the town.","The Sambhal page cites a 19 Feb 2024 India Today piece on the Kalki temple's significance, which shows the temple was in the news in early 2024.","The Bhagavata Purana prophecy text is a primary scriptural source for the Shambhala birthplace claim, and it names no modern town."],"criticisms":["The Shambhala identification is a textual and devotional claim. The scriptures do not name Sambhal, UP, and other places have been proposed.","Puranic Yuga durations are mythic cosmological numbers and are not historically or scientifically datable. The Kali Yuga epoch of 3102 BCE is a traditional reckoning.","The temple project is entangled with local communal politics in Sambhal, including the dispute over the Shahi Jama Masjid. In November 2024 a court-ordered survey was followed by deadly clashes that killed several people. Commentators tie the Kalki project to that wider contest. This is context, not a finding about the temple's own merits.","Prophecies of imminent Kalki appearance have repeatedly failed. Many sects, including the Kalki-claiming movements and modern self-proclaimed Kalki figures, have made dated predictions that passed without the event. I did not find a verified dated prediction specific to Acharya Pramod Krishnam or the Sambhal project in this run, so none is cited here.","Claims about the foundation-stone ceremony and PM involvement could not be confirmed from the sources I could reach, so they should be checked against news reports before being repeated."],"plausibility":1,"plausibilityReason":"As a claim about the real-world timeline, the Kalki prophecy says the avatar appears at the end of Kali Yuga, roughly 427,000 years from now. No empirical evidence supports an imminent appearance. The claim that Sambhal is the literal birthplace is also unsupported beyond textual interpretation (around 2/10 as a place claim). The temple itself is a verifiable construction project and can be assessed on its own terms. Its prophetic framing is not supported.","references":["Bhagavata Purana, Skandha 12, Chapter 2 (Kalki prophecy)","Vishnu Purana, Book 4 (Kali Yuga and Kalki)","Wikipedia: Sambhal (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sambhal), fetched this run","Wikipedia: Kalki Dham and Kalki Dham Temple (both HTTP 404 this run)","India Today, 'Kalki Mandir and why Sambhal is significant', 19 Feb 2024 (cited, not retrieved)"]},{"id":"veerabrahmendra","title":"Pothuluri Veerabrahmendra Swami Kalagnanam (Andhra): prophecies of Kali Yuga end, dates claimed by followers","category":"Regional prophetic tradition","verification":"Partly verified","proponents":["Brahmamgari Matham (Kadapa district, Andhra Pradesh) and its heads","Followers of Sri Kalagnani Jagadguru Madvirat Pothuluri Veerabrahmendra Swami, including the Eshwaridevi matham and the Achamaamba temple/home at Banaganapalle","Promotional sites such as kalagnani.com (archived 2016)"],"primarySources":["Kalagnanam (Telugu text, published in parts; a brief part kept at Brahmamgari Matham was printed in 1970; remaining parts said to be kept at Banaganapalle)","Govinda Vakyas (about 320 verses drawn from the Kalagnanam, popular in Telugu)","Jeevaikya Bodha and Kalikamba Sapthashathi (attributed works)","Brahma Tatwa Prakasika (1909 edition by T. Ganapati Sastri, which mentions a Tamil work Acharya-Darpana about the saint's deeds)"],"period":"Traditionally 16th–17th century CE (sources disagree); Wikipedia's Kadapa district article calls him a 17th-century saint, while the Potuluri Veerabrahmam article says the book was written around the 16th century; the Banaganapalli promotional text says he lived about 400 years ago","totalCycleYears":"4,320,000 years (one Mahayuga, traditional Hindu reckoning); this entry concerns only the Kali Yuga portion","durations":{"satya":"1,728,000 years (traditional)","treta":"1,296,000 years (traditional)","dvapara":"864,000 years (traditional)","kali":"432,000 years (traditional)"},"kaliStart":"3102 BCE (traditional Hindu reckoning, the conventional start of Kali Yuga)","kaliEnd":"About 428,900 CE under traditional reckoning. The Kalagnanam, as described in the sources available to me, does not give a single specific end date; I did not verify any follower-claimed end date.","currentPosition":"In 2026 CE we are about 5,127 years into the 432,000-year Kali Yuga (traditional reckoning), so about 427,000 years remain. Followers' claims of an imminent end or a specific \"end\" event are not supported by the traditional chronology itself.","summary":"The Kalagnanam is a Telugu book of prophecies attributed to Pothuluri Veerabrahmendra Swami (Potuluri Veerabrahmam), a saint who is traditionally said to have entered jeeva samadhi at Brahmamgari Matham in Kadapa district on Kartika Shudda Dwadasi. Wikipedia describes it as a book of past, present and future predictions. Its text is said to have several authors (including Yogi Narayana, Eswaridevi, Yogi Subbaraya Sharma, Vidyaranya and disciple Dudekula Siddaiah), and it is not a single-author work. About 70 percent of its prophecies are reported to concern a coming king-cum-avatar, Veera Bhoga Vasanta Rayalu. The Govinda Vakyas are the best-known popular extract. The Kalagnanam is widely called the \"Nostradamus of India\" by devotees. I could not find a source that lists specific end-of-Kali-Yuga dates attributed to the Kalagnanam, so I have not stated any; followers' specific dates should be checked against their own publications before being repeated.","keyClaims":["Veerabrahmendra Swami foretold events from his time to the end of Kali Yuga (followers' claim, as stated in the Banaganapalli promotional text)","The prophecies cover political, social and economic change, changes in plants and animals, geography, wars and explosions","The text is said to include the birth of Gandhi and the rule of Indira Gandhi (follower claim, not an independent source)","About 70 percent of prophecies concern a coming king-cum-avatar, Veera Bhoga Vasanta Rayalu","The book is also called Sandhra Sindhu Vedam by devotees (per Banaganapalli promotional text)"],"evidence":["Wikipedia (Potuluri Veerabrahmam) confirms the saint's existence as a traditional figure, the Kalagnanam as a Telugu book of predictions, the legend of his birth and upbringing, and his samadhi at Brahmamgari Matham on Kartika Shudda Dwadasi","Wikipedia (Kalagnanam) confirms multiple attributed authors, the 1970 printing of a part kept at Brahmamgari Matham, that the remaining parts are said to be kept at Banaganapalle, the 320 Govinda Vakyas, and the king-avatar theme (about 70 percent of prophecies)","Wikipedia (Potuluri Veerabrahmam) notes an earlier 1909 mention of a Tamil work Acharya-Darpana that supposedly details his deeds, which is a pre-modern-era reference to his legend rather than to dated prophecies","Wikipedia (Brahmamgari Matham) confirms the village, the samadhi tradition and the Govinda Vakyas link","Wikipedia (Banaganapalli) contains the promotional claims about Gandhi, Indira Gandhi and 'knowledge of past, present and future', attributed to the local tradition"],"criticisms":["The attributed date of composition is unstable: the sources variously say 16th century, 17th century and 'about 400 years ago', and the textual history (multiple authors, parts in different custodial locations, a printed 1970 edition) makes a single-author, single-date text doubtful","Claims that the book names Gandhi and Indira Gandhi are not supported by any independent scholarly edition in the sources I checked, and read like retrospective matching of vague verses to later events (a common pattern for prophecy texts)","Traditional Kali Yuga chronology places about 427,000 years of Kali Yuga still ahead; any 'end-of-Kali-Yuga' claim with a near-term date is therefore inconsistent with the tradition it claims to rest on","Prophecy-after-the-fact: vague verses are easily matched to later events, and failed or unfulfilled predictions are rarely acknowledged in devotional literature","No failed predictions with dates were verified in this run; I did not find a source that records specific dated predictions from the Kalagnanam that were checked against events","Commercial and institutional interest: the Banaganapalli promotional text and the Brahmamgari Matham's pilgrimage economy have an interest in promoting the prophecies"],"plausibility":1,"plausibilityReason":"As a claim about the real-world timeline, the notion that Kali Yuga ends in our lifetime is inconsistent with traditional chronology (about 427,000 years remain) and has no verified dated prediction behind it. The Kalagnanam's attribution, dating and textual unity are themselves unresolved, and the matching of verses to events is retrospective. The text may be of cultural and literary value, but the timeline claim does not hold up.","references":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Potuluri_Veerabrahmam","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kalagnanam","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brahmamgari_Matham","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Banaganapalli","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yaganti_temple","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kadapa_district","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hindu_eschatology","http://www.kalagnani.com (archived 28 Oct 2016)"]},{"id":"nilesh-oak","title":"Nilesh Oak: Mahabharata war 5561 BCE, Ramayana 12209 BCE, and the implications for the Kali epoch / yuga lengths","category":"Alternative chronology","verification":"Background knowledge only","proponents":["Nilesh Oak (Indian independent author, self-published works on Hindu chronology)"],"primarySources":["Nilesh Oak, self-published books and talks on the date of the Mahabharata war and the Ramayana (titles and exact years not verified in this run)","Puranic and epic texts cited by proponents (Mahabharata, Ramayana, Vishnu Purana, Surya Siddhanta)","Aryabhata, Aryabhatiya (499 CE), for the traditional Kali epoch of 3102 BCE","Varahamihira, Brhat Samhita (6th century), and Kalhana, Rajatarangini (11th century), for the 653-year post-Kali-epoch war date"],"period":"Oak's dates: Mahabharata war ~5561 BCE; Ramayana ~12209 BCE (as reported in secondary summaries; not verified against his text)","totalCycleYears":"Traditional mahayuga = 4,320,000 years (sum of the four yugas)","durations":{"satya":"1,728,000 years (traditional, 4,800 divine years)","treta":"1,296,000 years (traditional, 3,600 divine years)","dvapara":"864,000 years (traditional, 2,400 divine years)","kali":"432,000 years (traditional, 1,200 divine years); Oak's own reckoning of Kali start should be checked against his text"},"kaliStart":"Traditional epoch 3102 BCE (Aryabhata). Oak's scheme ties the Mahabharata war (5561 BCE) to a much earlier date and so departs from the traditional Kali epoch; exact Kali-start claim not verified.","kaliEnd":"Traditional end about 428,899 CE (3102 BCE + 432,000 years). Oak's end date not verified.","currentPosition":"In 2026, roughly 5,128 years into the Kali Yuga on the traditional 3102 BCE reckoning. On Oak's chronology the war (5561 BCE) would place the present about 7,587 years after the war, which implies a Kali start far earlier than the traditional one.","summary":"Nilesh Oak is an independent Indian writer who argues from textual references, planetary positions and traditional astronomy that the Mahabharata war took place around 5561 BCE and the Ramayana much earlier, around 12209 BCE. He presents this as a correction to the conventional date of about 3102 BCE, which the Kali epoch (Aryabhata) ties to the end of the Dvapara Yuga. In his framing the dates matter for the yuga scheme: if the war is the Kali-age boundary, the Kali Yuga and the full mahayuga cycle must be re-anchored, which would change how many years of Kali have elapsed. I could not access Oak's own texts or a reliable secondary summary during this run, so the specifics above are from background knowledge and should be checked against his books or talks before being relied on.","keyClaims":["The Mahabharata war occurred around 5561 BCE, not the traditional c. 3102 BCE implied by the Kali epoch.","The Ramayana belongs to a much earlier period, around 12209 BCE (as reported in secondary summaries).","Traditional yuga durations (4.32 million-year mahayuga) are compatible with an earlier anchor for the Kali epoch.","Planetary and astronomical references in the epics support these dates when read with traditional astronomical methods."],"evidence":["The traditional Kali epoch of 3102 BCE comes from Aryabhata (499 CE), who tied it to planetary conjunctions; this gives the Kali epoch a fixed astronomical anchor that Oak's dates would displace.","Wikipedia's historicity article (fetched this run) records the pre-modern dating tradition: Aryabhata's 3102 BCE, the Aihole inscription's 3137 BCE, Varahamihira's and Kalhana's 2449 BCE (653 years after the Kali epoch), and modern scholarly estimates ranging from about 1400 BCE (Puranic genealogies) to 836 BCE (B. B. Lal) and 950 BCE (Pargiter). None of these is near 5561 BCE.","Proponents of very early dates generally rely on retrodiction of eclipses and planetary positions from epic descriptions. Such retrodiction is sensitive to the assumed calendar, to the epic's internal consistency and to which astronomical events the text actually describes."],"criticisms":["No archaeological evidence ties the epics to an urban or literate society in the 6th-4th millennium BCE; the Indus cities declined around 1900 BCE, and the Painted Grey Ware and early iron-age horizons that some scholars link to the epic period date to about 1200-600 BCE.","The epics developed in layers over many centuries; a single war date presumes a historical core that the textual evidence does not establish.","Astronomical dating of epic events is highly sensitive to the calendar, observation method and interpretation of ambiguous verses; many different dates have been proposed from similar texts, which shows the method is underdetermined.","Yuga durations are a theological and cosmological scheme counted in divine years, not a measurable chronology; treating them as historical intervals is a category error for most historians.","The mainstream academic view is that the Mahabharata and Ramayana were composed roughly between the 5th century BCE and the 4th century CE, with a historical core (if any) far less precisely datable than Oak's figures imply.","Mainstream reviewers have criticised Oak's work for selective use of sources and for presenting a non-academic argument as settled."],"plausibility":1,"plausibilityReason":"As a claim about the real-world timeline, a Mahabharata war around 5561 BCE and a Ramayana around 12209 BCE is not supported by archaeology, genetics, or documented writing from that period, and it conflicts with the textual dating consensus. It is internally coherent only inside a traditional cosmological framework that does not map to measurable history. Note that the traditional 3102 BCE epoch is itself not supported as a historical date.","references":["Wikipedia, 'Historicity of the Mahabharata' (fetched this run; source for the list of pre-modern and modern dating estimates)","Aryabhatiya, Aryabhata (499 CE) for the 3102 BCE Kali epoch","Brhat Samhita, Varahamihira (6th century CE)","Rajatarangini, Kalhana (11th century CE)","Nilesh Oak's own books and talks (not accessed this run; check titles and dates directly before citing)"]},{"id":"kota-venkatachalam","title":"Kota Venkatachalam: Buddha 1887 BCE, Kali Yuga chronology and \"Indian chronology\" revisionism","category":"Alternative chronology","verification":"Not found","proponents":["Kota Venkatachalam (identity and publication record not confirmed in this run)"],"primarySources":["Not retrieved. Wikipedia has no article at en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kota_Venkatachalam (HTTP 404). Web search was blocked (DuckDuckGo returned a bot-verification page), so no books, talks, YouTube titles or news items could be checked."],"period":"Claimed Buddha date of 1887 BCE; Kali Yuga reckoned from 3102 BCE in traditional Indian astronomy","totalCycleYears":"4,320,000 years (mahayuga)","durations":{"satya":"1,728,000 years (traditional)","treta":"1,296,000 years (traditional)","dvapara":"864,000 years (traditional)","kali":"432,000 years (traditional)"},"kaliStart":"3102 BCE (traditional reckoning, Surya Siddhanta / Aryabhata-era convention)","kaliEnd":"About 428,899 CE on the traditional count (3102 BCE plus 432,000 years)","currentPosition":"In 2026 the traditional Kali Yuga count is about year 5127 (roughly 5,100 years into a 432,000-year Kali Yuga). This is the traditional count, not a verified historical fact.","summary":"Kota Venkatachalam is cited, in the task text, as an advocate of an Indian chronology in which the Buddha lived around 1887 BCE and the Kali Yuga timeline is revised accordingly. I could not verify his biography, books, talks, or the exact teaching. What is described below is the general shape of such revisionist claims and the mainstream facts they are measured against, not a confirmed account of his work.","keyClaims":["Buddha's date should be moved back to about 1887 BCE, far earlier than the mainstream range of roughly the 6th to 4th century BCE. (Claim as stated in the task; not verified against his own text.)","Indian textual chronology (Puranas, Mahabharata, Kali Yuga reckonings) is read as literal history and should reset the dating of Indian antiquity. (Typical of this genre; not verified for this author.)","The traditional yuga system (4,320,000-year mahayuga with Kali Yuga starting 3102 BCE) is treated as a usable historical frame. (Typical of this genre.)"],"evidence":["Mainstream scholarship dates the Buddha's death (parinirvana) broadly to the 5th to 4th century BCE, based on Buddhist texts, Ashokan inscriptions and synchronisms with Greek and Persian records. None of these support 1887 BCE.","Archaeology of the Indus and early Vedic periods does not match a literal Puranic dynastic chronology, and radiocarbon dates of early Indian sites are well documented in peer-reviewed literature.","The 3102 BCE start of Kali Yuga is a astronomical and calendrical convention from Indian astronomy texts. It is not an independently attested historical event."],"criticisms":["A 1887 BCE Buddha date conflicts with the textual, epigraphic and archaeological evidence used by mainstream historians and conflicts with Greek and Chinese synchronisms.","Treating yuga durations as literal historical time has no independent evidence; the numbers come from astronomical and cosmological texts with symbolic and calendrical functions.","Revisionist Indian chronology often relies on selective readings of Puranic genealogies and on astronomy claims (such as planetarium software reconstructions) that do not stand up to peer review.","Claims of this type are usually not published in peer-reviewed venues; without the author's own texts, the argument cannot be assessed on its own terms.","Describing the view fairly requires the author's own wording, which this run could not retrieve. Any paraphrase here is a generic reconstruction, not a quotation."],"plausibility":1,"plausibilityReason":"As a real-world timeline claim, placing the Buddha around 1887 BCE contradicts the textual, epigraphic, and archaeological evidence that scholars use to date him (roughly 5th to 4th century BCE). Treating the yuga system as literal history has no independent support, and the Kali Yuga start at 3102 BCE is a traditional astronomical convention, not an attested event.","references":["Wikipedia, 'Yuga' (en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yuga), retrieved this run: gives the mahayuga of 4,320,000 years and notes that the individual yuga durations are not given on that page.","Wikipedia, 'Kota Venkatachalam': no article found (HTTP 404) in this run.","Background knowledge: mainstream Buddha chronology (e.g. Bechert's and Gombrich's work on Buddhist chronology) and the traditional Kali Yuga epoch of 3102 BCE from Indian astronomy."]},{"id":"saroj-bala-iserve","title":"Saroj Bala / I-SERVE: planetarium-software dating of the Ramayana (5114 BCE) and Mahabharata (3139 BCE) and their relation to Kali Yuga","category":"Alternative chronology","verification":"Background knowledge only","proponents":["Saroj Bala (named in the research brief; her publications and affiliations were not verified in this run)","I-SERVE (the group named in the brief; its full name and website were not verified)"],"primarySources":["Not located in this run. Web search tool was not available, and a WebFetch of Wikipedia's Yuga article succeeded but did not mention Saroj Bala, I-SERVE, or the 5114/3139 BCE dates. Wikipedia pages for Saroj Bala and a dating-of-epics page returned 404.","Related, separately verifiable comparison: B. N. Achar's planetarium-software dating of the Mahabharata war to 3067 BCE (background knowledge, not re-checked here)."],"period":"Claims made in the 2000s-2020s, in Indian popular media and planetarium-software-based presentations (exact dates of Bala/I-SERVE publications not verified)","totalCycleYears":"4,320,000 years (Mahayuga); Kali Yuga 432,000 years","durations":{"satya":"1,728,000 years (traditional)","treta":"1,296,000 years (traditional)","dvapara":"864,000 years (traditional)","kali":"432,000 years (traditional)"},"kaliStart":"3102 BCE (traditional astronomical epoch, usually dated to 17/18 February 3102 BCE; this is the date used by Aryabhata's system and later Indian almanacs)","kaliEnd":"About 428,899 CE by the traditional 432,000-year count","currentPosition":"In 2026 CE we are about 5,127-5,128 years into the 432,000-year Kali Yuga by the traditional count (3102 BCE to 2026 CE, with no year zero).","summary":"The brief describes a claim that planetarium software was used to date Rama's birth (Ramayana) to 5114 BCE and the Mahabharata war to 3139 BCE, and that these dates bear on the start of Kali Yuga. Traditional Hindu chronology puts Kali Yuga's start at 3102 BCE, which is about 37 years after 3139 BCE. This fits the tradition that Krishna's death, which ends the Dvapara age and begins Kali, came shortly before the Kali epoch. The method is astronomical: software reconstructs the sky over a given date and matches described planetary positions, eclipses, or conjunctions from the epic texts. I could not verify the group's own publications, the exact methodology, or any peer review in this run. The description below therefore rests on background knowledge and on how this genre of claim is usually made.","keyClaims":["Ramayana events, including Rama's birth, took place around 5114 BCE, according to astronomical matching done with planetarium software (as stated in the brief).","Mahabharata events, including the war, took place around 3139 BCE, according to the same kind of software-based sky reconstruction (as stated in the brief).","These dates support the traditional chronology in which Kali Yuga began around 3102 BCE, after the Mahabharata period.","The epics are presented as historical records, not as later literary compositions, so the astronomical references in the text are taken as literal observations."],"evidence":["The traditional Kali Yuga epoch of 3102 BCE is a real and widely used astronomical epoch in Indian almanac tradition, going back to Aryabhata (499 CE) and used in later Siddhanta texts.","Planetarium software can reconstruct past sky positions reliably for dates in the historical era. The reconstruction is only as good as the text-to-sky matching step.","Some astronomical references in the epics, such as descriptions of planetary arrangements or eclipses, are often cited by proponents as matches for these dates.","Achar's planetarium-based dating of the Mahabharata to 3067 BCE shows that this method can yield different dates for the same text, which suggests the matching step is doing much of the work."],"criticisms":["Underdetermination: many dates in the past 5,000 years share similar sky configurations, so a software match to one date does not single it out. Choosing the date usually depends on which features of the text are treated as reliable.","Textual layering: the Ramayana and Mahabharata were composed, revised, and expanded over centuries, roughly from the 1st millennium BCE to the 4th century CE (Mahabharata) and later. Scholarly consensus does not support a single composition date near 5000 or 3000 BCE, and astronomical references are commonly read as later insertions or retrospective constructions.","Archaeology: no material evidence of a Kuru or Kosala polity matching the epic descriptions has been found at the proposed dates. A 5114 BCE date for Rama falls in the Neolithic, before the Indus (Harappan) urban phase, which the epics do not describe. A 3139 BCE date for the Mahabharata falls in the Early Harappan period, and the epics show no features of that culture. Painted Grey Ware (c. 1200-600 BCE) and later iron-age material are closer to the epics' described world.","Circular reasoning: the Kali Yuga epoch of 3102 BCE is itself an astronomical construction made retroactively by Indian astronomers, so using it to validate the Mahabharata date assumes the conclusion.","Lack of peer-reviewed publication: I could not confirm that Bala or I-SERVE have published peer-reviewed work with the method, data, and error analysis that would allow independent checking.","Media presentation: popular accounts of such dates often present software output as a historical fact and not as one interpretation among several."],"plausibility":2,"plausibilityReason":"As a claim about the real-world timeline, the specific dates are not supported by archaeology, philology, or the textual history of the epics. The sky-reconstruction method is valid in principle, but the matching step is underdetermined and the claims have not been shown to pass independent review. The consistency with the traditional Kali epoch is a point of internal cultural coherence, not evidence.","references":["Achar, B. N. N. (2004-2011), on dating the Mahabharata war to 3067 BCE using planetarium software (background knowledge; not re-checked in this run).","Aryabhatiya (499 CE) for the Kali Yuga epoch of 3102 BCE (background knowledge).","Wikipedia, 'Yuga' (fetched successfully; does not mention Bala, I-SERVE, or the 5114/3139 BCE dates)."]},{"id":"hindi-youtube-preachers","title":"Popular Hindi YouTube preachers on Kali Yuga duration and end (Premanand Maharaj, Aniruddhacharya, Dhirendra Shastri, Pradeep Mishra)","category":"Modern spiritual/esoteric","verification":"Partly verified","proponents":["Premanand Maharaj (Aniruddh Kumar Pandey), Vrindavan-based Radha Vallabh-tradition ascetic and satsang speaker","Aniruddhacharya, Bhagwat katha speaker popular on YouTube (background knowledge only, not checked in this run)","Dhirendra Krishna Shastri (Dhirendra Garg), Baba Bageshwar Dham, Chhatarpur, Madhya Pradesh","Pradeep Mishra, Shiv Purana katha speaker popular on YouTube (background knowledge only, not checked in this run)"],"primarySources":["Puranic and Itihasa texts that state the yuga durations, including the Bhagavata Purana (12.2-4), Vishnu Purana, Manusmriti (1.68-86), and the Mahabharata (Vana Parva)","Surya Siddhanta and Aryabhata's astronomical epoch (the 3102 BCE Kali epoch)","Wikipedia pages on Yuga, Premanand Govind Sharan, and Dhirendra Krishna Shastri (fetched this run; secondary and flagged by Wikipedia as possibly containing unverified text)","YouTube katha/satsang recordings by the four speakers (not reviewed in this run; titles and transcripts not checked)"],"period":"Traditional Puranic chronology (Kali Yuga epoch 3102 BCE); modern preaching from roughly the 2010s to 2026","totalCycleYears":"4,320,000 human years (12,000 divine years x 360) for one Mahayuga","durations":{"satya":"1,728,000 human years (4,800 divine years)","treta":"1,296,000 human years (3,600 divine years)","dvapara":"864,000 human years (2,400 divine years)","kali":"432,000 human years (1,200 divine years)"},"kaliStart":"17/18 February 3102 BCE (traditional epoch, backdated from Aryabhata-era astronomy; not an observed event)","kaliEnd":"About 428,899 CE by the same reckoning (432,000 years after 3102 BCE)","currentPosition":"In 2026 the traditional reckoning puts us about 5,127 years into Kali Yuga, with roughly 426,870 years remaining. None of the four speakers, as far as this run could check, gives a specific calendar date for the end of Kali Yuga. Their emphasis is on moral decline in the present age, the value of naam japa and bhakti, and, for Dhirendra Shastri, the political call for a Hindu Rashtra.","summary":"Each of these speakers works within the standard Puranic framework: Kali Yuga is the fourth and most degraded age, lasting 432,000 human years and beginning in 3102 BCE. Their teaching is mostly about the present age, not a date for its end. Premanand Maharaj stresses devotion, the holy name, and renunciation as the practical response to Kali Yuga, and frequently speaks about the difficulties of the age. Aniruddhacharya and Pradeep Mishra, both katha speakers, typically present the Yuga scheme as a moral and devotional frame in Bhagwat and Shiva Purana discourses. Dhirendra Shastri links the Kali Yuga framing to a religious-political program: he has repeatedly called for India to be declared a Hindu nation and for the Ramcharitmanas to be treated as a national scripture. Their common position is that the present age is dark, that it will eventually end, and that devotion is the path through it. None of them, in what was checked, offers a precise date for the end of the age. The claims that are verifiable are about their public activities and controversies, not about the yuga chronology itself.","keyClaims":["Kali Yuga is the fourth yuga, lasts 432,000 human years, and began in 3102 BCE (traditional claim, common across these preachers)","The present age is one of moral decline, so devotion, naam japa, and renunciation are the practical response (paraphrase of the common teaching, not a verified quote)","Dhirendra Shastri has called for India to be declared a Hindu Rashtra and for the Ramcharitmanas to be made India's national scripture (2023, per Wikipedia)","Premanand Maharaj's teaching is centred on Radha-Krishna bhakti; the Wikipedia article contains no statement by him on Kali Yuga or its end","No speaker in this set is documented, in sources checked here, as giving a specific date for the end of Kali Yuga"],"evidence":["Wikipedia Yuga page (fetched): describes Kali as the fourth and final age and gives the Surya Siddhanta 4,320,000-year period and Bhagavata Purana 12.4.2 kalpa = 1,000 catur-yugas. It does not give the individual yuga durations in years or the 3102 BCE epoch, so those come from background knowledge.","Wikipedia on Premanand Govind Sharan (fetched): born 1971 in Akhri village, Kanpur district; ascetic from age 13; based in Vrindavan; Radha Vallabh Sampradaya; Shri Hit Radha Keli Kunj Trust founded 2016; controversies in Nov 2024 (remarks on women's clothing) and July 2025 (remarks on marriage); no statement on Kali Yuga reported.","Wikipedia on Dhirendra Krishna Shastri (fetched): born 4 July 1996, Gada village, Chhatarpur; Bageshwar Dham; 23 Jan 2023 call for Hindu Rashtra; late Jan 2023 call to make Ramcharitmanas national scripture; 11 Feb 2023 Aap Ki Adalat remarks; Feb 2023 Owaisi criticism and Yogi Adityanath endorsement; Jan 2023 challenge by Shyam Manav with Nagpur Police clean chit on 25 Jan 2023; Sept 2024 NCERT 'love jihad' claim; Sept 2026 controversies in West Bengal and Ludhiana (30 Sept).","Traditional yuga values (1,728,000 / 1,296,000 / 864,000 / 432,000) are standard in the Puranic literature and in the Surya Siddhanta framework. Their arithmetic is consistent with the 12,000 divine-year Mahayuga."],"criticisms":["The yuga durations are not derived from any observation. They are internal to Puranic cosmology and were fixed by textual tradition and later astronomical schemes, not by measurement.","The 3102 BCE start date is a retrospective epoch. Aryabhata and later astronomers used it as a computational reference point, and modern historians and astronomers do not treat it as a recorded event.","Even within Hindu tradition, the yuga chronology varies between texts and schools, and the 'divine year' multiplier is a cosmological convention, not a historical count.","Dhirendra Shastri's Hindu Rashtra and religious-political claims were criticized by Asaduddin Owaisi and rationalists such as Shyam Manav, who challenged his claimed powers. Shyam Manav's challenge led to a Nagpur Police clean chit on 25 January 2023, which did not validate the claims.","Premanand Maharaj's comments on women's clothing (Nov 2024) and marriage (July 2025) drew public criticism, and a death threat against him was reported in Aug 2025. These are reported controversies, not chronology claims.","Claims about Aniruddhacharya and Pradeep Mishra were not checked in this run. Treat any statements attributed to them as unverified until a transcript is reviewed.","No speaker here is known to have made a dated, falsifiable prediction about the end of Kali Yuga in the sources checked. No failed predictions are documented in this run."],"plausibility":1,"plausibilityReason":"As a claim about the real-world timeline, the 432,000-year Kali Yuga and its 3102 BCE start have no empirical support. They are a religious cosmological framework that is internally consistent, but not historically or astronomically verifiable. The end of Kali Yuga is about 428,899 CE in this scheme, and nothing observable in 2026 bears on it. The preachers' more concrete claims (about politics, moral decline, or the Hindu Rashtra) are matters of opinion or policy, not timeline predictions.","references":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yuga","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Premanand_Govind_Sharan","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dhirendra_Krishna_Shastri","Bhagavata Purana 12.2-4; Manusmriti 1.68-86; Surya Siddhanta (background knowledge)","Aryabhata's Aryabhatiya and the 3102 BCE epoch (background knowledge)"]},{"id":"kali-ends-2025-viral","title":"Viral claims that Kali Yuga ends in 2025 (or 2024-2032)","category":"Internet / pop culture","verification":"Partly verified","proponents":["Anonymous social media and YouTube creators (no single originator identified)","Popularizers of Sri Yukteswar's 24,000-year chronology","Authors of earlier end-date calculations (Gaston Georgel, Jean Robin, John Major Jenkins) whose dates the viral versions sometimes blend in"],"primarySources":["Wikipedia 'Yuga' and 'Yuga cycle' (fetched 2026)","Wikipedia 'Sri Yukteswar Giri' (fetched; the article does not state the yuga dates, so those come from background knowledge of The Holy Science, 1894)","Wikipedia 'Kalki' (fetched)","Wikipedia '2012 phenomenon' (fetched)","Web search was bot-blocked, so the specific viral posts and videos could not be retrieved"],"period":"Traditional Hindu chronology (Surya Siddhanta) places the start of Kali Yuga at 3102 BCE. The viral end-date claims are a modern (c. 2010s-2020s) internet phenomenon.","totalCycleYears":"4,320,000 solar years (one mahayuga, 12,000 divine years). Sri Yukteswar's scheme uses a 24,000-year precessional cycle instead.","durations":{"satya":"1,728,000 years (4,800 divine years)","treta":"1,296,000 years (3,600 divine years)","dvapara":"864,000 years (2,400 divine years)","kali":"432,000 years (1,200 divine years)"},"kaliStart":"3102 BCE (traditional, midnight 17-18 Feb 3102 BCE per Surya Siddhanta). Sri Yukteswar instead dated the descending Kali Yuga's start to 701 BCE, with the cycle bottom at 499 CE.","kaliEnd":"Traditional: about 428,898 CE (432,000 years after 3102 BCE). Viral variants end it at about 2024-2032 CE. Sri Yukteswar: ascending Kali ended around 499 CE, and he said the age had moved to Dvapara by 1699 CE. Other modern end-dates: Jean Robin 1999; Gaston Georgel 2030 (shifted from 1999 in 1983); John Major Jenkins 2012.","currentPosition":"In 2026 the traditional reckoning puts us about 5,127 years into a 432,000-year Kali Yuga, with roughly 426,800 years remaining. The viral 2025 claim has already passed without any Kali Yuga ending, so under this view it is a failed prediction, as are the other viral end-dates that fell before 2026.","summary":"The viral claim says the current Kali Yuga (the dark age) ends around 2025, or somewhere in 2024-2032, and a Satya (golden) Age begins, often tied to Kalki's arrival or to a shift in consciousness. It spreads mainly through short videos, memes and posts. Its most likely internal basis is arithmetic: the Kali Samvat year 5126 falls in 2024-2025 CE (3102 BCE + 5126 years). The posts seem to mistake this elapsed count for the length of the age, when the Puranic duration is 432,000 years. The 2024-2032 range looks like a blend of that arithmetic with the Sri Yukteswar, Georgel and Jenkins end-dates. I could not retrieve the specific viral posts, so the originators and exact wording are unverified.","keyClaims":["Kali Yuga began in 3102 BCE and lasts about 5,000 years, so it ends around 2024-2025 CE.","A Satya (golden) Age follows the end of Kali Yuga, possibly heralded by Kalki or a precessional alignment.","Sri Yukteswar's 24,000-year cycle shows the world has already left Kali Yuga, so the dark age is ending now.","Maya or precessional alignments (2012 and after) mark a turn of the cycle, with a further turn around 2025-2032.","The end of Kali Yuga is near, and people should prepare for a spiritual transformation."],"evidence":["Wikipedia 'Yuga cycle': the traditional Kali Yuga is 432,000 years and starts 3102 BCE (Surya Siddhanta).","Wikipedia 'Yuga cycle' and 'Sri Yukteswar Giri': Yukteswar's 1894 The Holy Science used a 24,000-year cycle with ascending Dvapara from 1699 CE. Nothing in that scheme gives a 2025 end-date.","Wikipedia 'Yuga cycle': Gaston Georgel shifted the Kali end from 1999 to 2030 in 1983; Jean Robin calculated 1999 from Rene Guenon's framework; John Major Jenkins tied the turn to the 2012 winter solstice.","Wikipedia '2012 phenomenon': the 2012 date rests on the end of the 13th b'ak'tun (21 Dec 2012). Jenkins himself found the most precise galactic alignment in 1998.","Arithmetic: 3102 BCE to 2025 CE is 5,126 years, the Kali Samvat year Indian almanacs print for 2024-2025 (background knowledge, not verified against a fetched source)."],"criticisms":["The Puranic and Surya Siddhanta duration of Kali Yuga is 432,000 years. A roughly 5,000-year Kali Yuga has no basis in the traditional texts. The 5,126 figure is the number of years elapsed, not the length of the age.","Sri Yukteswar's chronology gives no 2025 end-date, and his own dates (ascending Dvapara 1699 CE, Treta 4099 CE) do not match the viral claim.","Predictions of Kali's end have repeatedly failed: 1999 (Robin, Guenon-based), Y2K anxieties, 21 Dec 2012 (2012 phenomenon), and now 2025, which has passed with no Kalki arrival or Satya Age visible. Each failure was followed by a new date rather than a reassessment.","Traditional Hindu astronomers never gave a calendar end-date for Kali Yuga. Joscelyn Godwin notes they place its end near 427,000 CE.","The viral version typically cites no primary text, no named author and no method, which makes it unfalsifiable in practice: when a date passes, the claim is re-dated.","The Wikipedia 'Kalki' article carries a notice that it may contain unverified or LLM-generated text, so its claims should be checked against sources."],"plausibility":1,"plausibilityReason":"As a claim about the real-world timeline, it has no empirical support. Kali Yuga is a religious-cosmological concept with no observable end-date. The 2025 date rests on a misreading of the 5,126-year elapsed count against the 432,000-year length. 2025 has already passed (current date 2026) with no ending visible, and the same pattern of failed end-dates (1999, 2012) applies. It cannot be tested in any meaningful way, so it is best treated as an internet myth.","references":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yuga","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yuga_cycle","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sri_Yukteswar_Giri","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kalki","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2012_phenomenon","Sri Yukteswar Giri, The Holy Science (1894) (background knowledge, not fetched)","Gaston Georgel (1983) and Jean Robin, as summarized in the Wikipedia Yuga cycle article"]},{"id":"praveen-mohan-shivoham","title":"Praveen Mohan / Project Shivoham: yuga claims, ancient advanced technology and timelines","category":"Internet / pop culture","verification":"Partly verified","proponents":["Praveen Mohan (YouTube presenter, Tamil-language and English-language content on Hindu chronology and ancient technology)","Project Shivoham (YouTube channel covering yugas, Vedic and Puranic chronology, and claims of advanced ancient technology)"],"primarySources":["YouTube channels and video titles by Praveen Mohan and Project Shivoham (not retrieved in this run; web access to their pages and to Wikipedia pages for either creator or for Project Shivoham returned HTTP 404; a Google search fetch returned an error page)","Wikipedia, 'Yuga' (retrieved; confirms the catur-yuga structure and the 4,320,000-year Mahayuga total)","Bhagavata Purana (as cited by the Wikipedia 'Yuga' article for the kalpa = 1,000 catur-yugas relation)"],"period":"Claims circulated on YouTube in the 2010s–2020s; the specific video dates and any dated predictions were not retrieved in this run.","totalCycleYears":"4,320,000 human years (Mahayuga/catur-yuga), per the classical Puranic scheme; the creators' own numbers were not verified.","durations":{"satya":"1,728,000 human years in the classical scheme (4,800 divine years); not verified against the creators' own statements","treta":"1,296,000 human years in the classical scheme (3,600 divine years); not verified against the creators' own statements","dvapara":"864,000 human years in the classical scheme (2,400 divine years); not verified against the creators' own statements","kali":"432,000 human years in the classical scheme (1,200 divine years); not verified against the creators' own statements"},"kaliStart":"3102 BCE in the traditional reckoning (classical tradition; Wikipedia 'Yuga' article did not confirm this figure in the retrieved text). The creators' own start-date claims were not retrieved.","kaliEnd":"Traditional reckoning: 432,000 years after 3102 BCE, i.e. roughly 428,898 CE (arithmetic from the classical duration). Creator-specific end dates were not retrieved.","currentPosition":"Under the classical reckoning we are in Kali Yuga, about 5,100+ years into its 432,000-year span as of 2026. Whether these creators place the transition to a new yuga within the present decade could not be verified in this run.","summary":"Praveen Mohan and Project Shivoham are YouTube-based presenters who discuss the Hindu yuga system, Puranic chronology, and claims that ancient Indian texts describe advanced technology. This entry is a partial write-up: the creators' own pages could not be fetched, so their specific yuga timelines, dated predictions and video titles are not quoted or verified here. The baseline the content usually rests on is the classical Puranic cycle (Satya, Treta, Dvapara, Kali; 4,320,000 years total), which is confirmed from Wikipedia's Yuga article. Creator-specific claims should be checked against their channels before being relied on.","keyClaims":["Classical yuga cycle (Satya, Treta, Dvapara, Kali) totals 4,320,000 years; we are in Kali Yuga (background knowledge, consistent with Wikipedia's Yuga article)","Ancient Indian texts describe technology (for example vimanas and advanced weapons) that is claimed to have been real and lost (paraphrased; creator-specific wording not retrieved)","Chronology claims that place historical events or the start of the current age on a timeline derived from Puranic figures (paraphrased; specifics not retrieved)"],"evidence":["The classical four-yuga structure and 4,320,000-year total are documented in the Wikipedia 'Yuga' article and the Bhagavata Purana (retrieved).","No archaeological, metallurgical or textual evidence from the retrieved sources supports literal ancient advanced technology; the Puranic numbers are described as cosmological rather than historical in standard scholarship (background knowledge).","No dated predictions by these creators were retrieved, so no fulfilled or failed predictions can be listed with dates. Do not assume any specific prediction was made without checking the channel."],"criticisms":["Puranic yuga durations are cosmological figures; reading them as literal calendar years requires an assumption the texts do not themselves establish (background knowledge; standard Indological position).","Claims of lost advanced technology are not supported by material evidence and typically rest on selective readings of Sanskrit verses (background knowledge).","Internet chronology channels often revise end dates when earlier ones pass, which makes their claims hard to falsify (background knowledge).","Creator-level claims could not be checked against the channels themselves in this run, so the criticisms above apply to the general genre rather than verified statements by these two presenters."],"plausibility":1,"plausibilityReason":"As a claim about the real-world timeline, the yuga durations are cosmological figures without historical calibration, and there is no evidence that any yuga boundary corresponds to a real-world event. Claims of ancient advanced technology are unsupported by material evidence. The rating reflects the general genre; the specific creator claims were not verified.","references":["Wikipedia, 'Yuga' (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yuga), retrieved 2026-10-20","Bhagavata Purana (classical Sanskrit text; kalpa = 1,000 catur-yugas as cited by the Yuga article)","Wikipedia 'Praveen_Mohan' and 'Project_Shivoham' pages returned HTTP 404 on retrieval; no page content was obtained from them"]},{"id":"hancock-rogan","title":"Hancock / Rogan / Carlson: yugas linked to the Younger Dryas and a lost advanced civilisation","category":"Internet / pop culture","verification":"Partly verified","proponents":["Graham Hancock","Randall Carlson","Joe Rogan (podcast host who has hosted Hancock and Carlson)"],"primarySources":["Graham Hancock, Fingerprints of the Gods (1995)","Graham Hancock, Magicians of the Gods (2015)","Netflix docuseries Ancient Apocalypse (2022), presented by Hancock","Joe Rogan Experience episodes featuring Hancock and Carlson (individual episode numbers not verified in this run)","Randall Carlson talks and interviews on architecture, precession and catastrophism (not verified in this run)"],"period":"Late 20th century to 2026 (Hancock's books from 1995; Netflix series 2022; podcast circulation via Rogan from roughly the late 2010s)","totalCycleYears":"Not specified by Hancock as a closed yuga count. Carlson-style framings use the ~25,920-year precession cycle; the standard catur-yuga is 4,320,000 years.","durations":{"satya":"Not specified in this teaching; the standard Hindu figure is 1,728,000 years (4 x 432,000 units), not adopted by these proponents","treta":"Not specified in this teaching; standard Hindu figure 1,296,000 years","dvapara":"Not specified in this teaching; standard Hindu figure 864,000 years","kali":"Not specified in this teaching; standard Hindu figure 432,000 years"},"kaliStart":"Not stated as a fixed date. Within this interpretation the transition is tied to the Younger Dryas onset, around 10,900 BCE (about 12,900 years ago). The conventional Hindu Kali Yuga start of 3102 BCE is a different framework.","kaliEnd":"Not specified in the sources reviewed.","currentPosition":"Under this view we are in a post-catastrophe, \"Kali\" or fallen age that began with the Younger Dryas. Proponents say the lesson of the lost civilisation is a warning about the present, but they do not give a precise present-day stage.","summary":"Hancock and Carlson, often discussed on the Joe Rogan podcast, combine yuga-style ages of decline with the Younger Dryas cold snap (about 12,900 years ago). In their account, a comet or impact event at the Younger Dryas onset ended an advanced Ice Age civilisation. Survivors allegedly seeded agriculture, monumental architecture and astronomy elsewhere (Egypt, Sumer, Mesoamerica). Carlson supplies architectural and astronomical arguments about precession and monument alignments, presenting the monuments as encoded warnings. Hancock presents this as a reconstruction from \"forgotten\" knowledge, and the Hindu yuga scheme is used as an analogy for a golden age followed by decline. The framing is popular entertainment and is not an accepted chronology of Hindu tradition or of archaeology.","keyClaims":["An advanced Ice Age civilisation existed before the Younger Dryas (about 12,900 years ago) and was destroyed by cosmic impact events.","Survivors transmitted agriculture, architecture and astronomy to other regions, seeding the earliest known civilisations.","Monuments such as the Great Pyramid and Gobekli Tepe encode astronomical warnings from the lost civilisation for future generations.","The Younger Dryas cooling was caused by an impact winter, linking the event to the end of a golden age.","Yuga-style ages of decline map onto the precession cycle and onto the lost-civilisation narrative."],"evidence":["Wikipedia's Graham Hancock article (fetched this run) summarises his claims: a lost Ice Age civilisation, comet-impact triggering the Younger Dryas, and hyperdiffusion of agriculture and architecture. It describes the Younger Dryas impact hypothesis as having little support in mainstream science.","Wikipedia's Yuga article (fetched this run) confirms the Hindu cycle as four ages (catur-yuga) totalling 4,320,000 years in the Surya Siddhanta, with Puranic sources. It does not mention Hancock or Carlson.","The Netflix series Ancient Apocalypse (2022) and its sequel (October 2024) brought the theory to a mass audience. The Society for American Archaeology asked Netflix to relabel it as science fiction in a letter dated 30 November 2022.","Gobekli Tepe (about 9500 BCE) is a real Neolithic site that predates pottery and agriculture in the region, which is the kind of evidence the narrative draws on."],"criticisms":["The Younger Dryas impact hypothesis (Firestone et al. 2007 and later nanodiamond and platinum claims) has failed to replicate in multiple independent studies. Mainstream palaeoclimatology attributes the cooling to a meltwater pulse from the Laurentide ice sheet.","No archaeological or geological evidence has been found for a lost advanced civilisation, and hyperdiffusionism has been rejected since the 19th century.","Hindu yuga durations are cosmological, not chronological. Mapping them onto the Younger Dryas or precession is an interpretive move that the Hindu sources do not make.","Critics (including Jeb Card and the Society for American Archaeology) say the work misrepresents archaeologists, cherry-picks sources and includes racist framings (e.g., describing the Atlanteans as white, and calling the Maya semi-civilised in Fingerprints of the Gods).","Hancock's own presentation has been criticised for pseudo-scientific claims such as ancient psychic powers and the claimed ability to move heavy stones through meditation."],"plausibility":1,"plausibilityReason":"As a real-world timeline, the claim that an advanced civilisation was destroyed by an impact at the Younger Dryas is not supported by evidence. The impact hypothesis is contested and its key markers have failed to replicate. The Yuga framing has no chronological basis: the Hindu durations are symbolic and the Kali Yuga start is conventionally dated to 3102 BCE. Real Younger Dryas and Gobekli Tepe facts are accurate, but the causal and civilisational story built on them is not.","references":["en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Graham_Hancock (fetched)","en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yuga (fetched)","en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Randall_Carlson (fetch returned HTTP 404; not verified)","Firestone et al. 2007, PNAS, Younger Dryas impact hypothesis (cited from background knowledge)","Society for American Archaeology letter to Netflix, 30 November 2022 (as reported in the Hancock Wikipedia article)"]},{"id":"western-astrology-variants","title":"Western astrologers' yuga variants (MacKinnell, Vandekerkhove, Addey, Cayce and Age of Aquarius mixed with yugas)","category":"Modern spiritual/esoteric","verification":"Partly verified","proponents":["Terry MacKinnell (Western astrologer; cited for a 1447 AD date)","August Vandekerkhove (late 19th-century Belgian occultist-astrologer; 1890 La Fronde article)","John Addey (20th-century Western astrologer, harmonic astrology; cited for 3597 AD)","Edgar Cayce (American psychic, 'Sleeping Prophet'; Age of Aquarius teaching, linkage to yugas is a popular retelling, not confirmed in sources here)","Assorted popular Western astrology and New Age writers who blend Vedic yuga durations with precession"],"primarySources":["Wikipedia, 'Age of Aquarius' (fetched 2026-10-20): dating claims and critiques, including the Campion survey and the Addey, MacKinnell and Vandekerkhove dates","Wikipedia, 'Yuga' (fetched 2026-10-20): traditional Indian yuga durations (Surya Siddhanta mahayuga of 4,320,000 years; catur-yuga structure)","Vandekerkhove's 1890 article in La Fronde, as quoted in the Wikipedia 'Age of Aquarius' article (the article's own citation gives 1899, an inconsistency)","Terry MacKinnell and John Addey writings on precession and astrological ages (cited only via the Wikipedia summary; original texts not checked in this run)","Edgar Cayce's recorded readings (Association for Research and Enlightenment archives; not checked in this run)"],"period":"Late 19th century to present (c. 1890s-2026)","totalCycleYears":"Not standardized. Western variants mostly use the precession cycle (about 25,772-25,920 years) divided into twelve 2,147-2,160-year astrological ages, rather than the Puranic 4,320,000-year mahayuga.","durations":{"satya":"Not standardized in Western variants; some popular sources map Satya Yuga onto a 6,480-year or 2,160-year age, but this varies by author","treta":"Not standardized; often mapped to a 6,480-year or 2,160-year age in popular variants","dvapara":"Not standardized; often mapped to a 6,480-year or 2,160-year age in popular variants","kali":"Not standardized; Western variants usually use 2,160 years (one zodiac age) rather than the Puranic 432,000 years"},"kaliStart":"Not standardized. Reported Western dates conflict. Wikipedia's Age of Aquarius article lists these as Aquarius start dates, not Kali Yuga start dates: Terry MacKinnell 1447 AD, John Addey 3597 AD, August Vandekerkhove 1890/1899 (an 1890 La Fronde article says the Aquarius cycle starts about 21 March of that year; the article's citation gives 1899). Traditional Kali Yuga start per Aryabhata and later Indian chronology is 3102 BCE, which these Western dates do not match.","kaliEnd":"Not specified in the sources reviewed. Variants that map Kali onto a 2,160-year age would end about 2,160 years after their start date (for example, around 3600 AD from a 1447 AD start), but no single authoritative end date exists.","currentPosition":"As of 2026, Western astrologers who date the Age of Aquarius treat it as either current or upcoming, depending on the method. Wikipedia reports that some astrologers doubt it has started. Under the MacKinnell and Vandekerkhove dates, we would be inside the Aquarian age; under the Addey date (3597 AD), we would still be in the Piscean age. The Kali Yuga framing would place us near the start of a 432,000-year era under the Puranic chronology, which conflicts with these variants.","summary":"Western astrologers have tried to reconcile Hindu yuga cosmology with the precession of the equinoxes. Their usual move is to replace the Puranic yuga durations with the 2,160-year zodiac ages, then date the start of the Age of Aquarius (sometimes described with yuga language). The Wikipedia summary shows that the dates are unstable: MacKinnell 1447 AD, Addey 3597 AD, Vandekerkhove 1890 or 1899. Edgar Cayce is widely cited for the Aquarius teaching, but this run did not confirm any yuga-specific claim from his readings. The result is a loose mix of Vedic and Western frameworks with no shared chronology.","keyClaims":["The precession of the equinoxes moves through the zodiac over roughly 25,772-25,920 years, so each zodiac age lasts about 2,147-2,160 years. (Accurate as a rough figure; sources note the precession rate is not constant.)","The Age of Aquarius has begun or is about to begin, and its start can be pinned to a specific year. (Dates in the sources range from 1447 AD to 3597 AD, depending on the author.)","The yuga sequence (Satya, Treta, Dvapara, Kali) can be mapped onto precessional ages, so that the Kali age corresponds to a zodiac age. (A Western reinterpretation; not part of the traditional Indian chronology.)","Vandekerkhove (1890) predicted that the cycle of Aquarius would start in March of that year, and he repeated the claim in print. (Source: La Fronde article quoted by Wikipedia.)","Edgar Cayce taught that a new age was dawning and linked it to spiritual change. (Background knowledge only; the specific yuga mapping is not confirmed here.)"],"evidence":["Wikipedia 'Age of Aquarius': Campion's survey counts 29 claims placing the start in the 20th century and 12 in the 24th century. Individual claims include Addey 3597 AD, MacKinnell 1447 AD, and Vandekerkhove's 1890 article (dated 1899 in the citation).","Wikipedia 'Age of Aquarius': the 2,160-year figure is described as 'only a rough calculation'. It notes that precession is decreasing and that a more accurate cycle is about 25,772 years, or about 2,147.5 years per age.","Wikipedia 'Age of Aquarius': the IAU's 1929 constellation boundaries put the start at 2595 AD, which many astrologers reject in favor of equal 30-degree signs.","Wikipedia 'Yuga': the traditional chronology uses a Mahayuga of 4,320,000 years (Surya Siddhanta), with 1,000 of these making a kalpa (Bhagavata Purana 12.4.2). These figures are not the same as the 2,160-year Western ages, so the mapping is an invention of the Western variants."],"criticisms":["Category error: the Puranic yugas (4:3:2:1 ratios, Kali of 432,000 years) and the astrological precession ages (about 2,160 years each) are different systems. Mixing them has no textual or astronomical basis.","No consensus start date: the sources list dates from 1447 AD to 3597 AD, and even the same author's date can shift (Vandekerkhove 1890 vs 1899 in the citation).","Precession is a physical effect (about 50 arcseconds per year), but the zodiac-age boundaries are conventions. The IAU 1929 boundaries and the 'equal sign' convention put the start in different centuries.","Unfalsifiable framing: the Age of Aquarius is repeatedly said to be 'dawning' or 'upcoming', so predictions can be revised without penalty.","Missing primary sources: this run did not check MacKinnell's or Addey's original texts, nor Cayce's readings. Their specific yuga mappings are therefore not verified here."],"plausibility":2,"plausibilityReason":"As a claim about the real timeline, these variants fail. The precession cycle is real, but the Age of Aquarius start date depends on the zodiac-boundary convention, and the Western dates conflict with each other (1447, 1890/1899, 3597 AD). The traditional Kali Yuga start (3102 BCE) is also not compatible with these dates. Nothing in the sources supports the yuga-to-precession mapping as a historical or astronomical fact. The dating is therefore a matter of belief rather than measurement.","references":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Age_of_Aquarius","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yuga","Campion, N. (survey of Aquarius dating claims, cited in the Wikipedia 'Age of Aquarius' article)","Vandekerkhove, A. (1890), La Fronde (cited in the Wikipedia 'Age of Aquarius' article)","Bhagavata Purana 12.4.2; Surya Siddhanta (as cited in the Wikipedia 'Yuga' article)"]},{"id":"kalki-2898-film","title":"Kalki 2898 AD (2024 film, dir. Nag Ashwin): yuga timeline framing and impact on popular belief","category":"Internet / pop culture","verification":"Partly verified","proponents":["Nag Ashwin (writer-director)","Vyjayanthi Movies (producer)","Amitabh Bachchan, Prabhas, Kamal Haasan, Deepika Padukone (cast)"],"primarySources":["Wikipedia article 'Kalki 2898 AD' (plot, production, release and reception sections; fetched via the Wikipedia API in this run)","The film itself (plot and framing), released 27 June 2024","Puranic texts describing Kalki and the yuga cycle: Vishnu Purana, Bhagavata Purana, Kalki Purana (background knowledge, not re-checked in this run)"],"period":"Film released 27 June 2024; yuga framing drawn from Hindu Puranic cosmology","totalCycleYears":"4,320,000 years (one mahayuga, traditional figure, background knowledge)","durations":{"satya":"1,728,000 years (traditional)","treta":"1,296,000 years (traditional)","dvapara":"864,000 years (traditional)","kali":"432,000 years (traditional)"},"kaliStart":"3102 BCE (traditional reckoning, tied to Krishna's death)","kaliEnd":"About 428,899 CE (traditional reckoning, 432,000 years after 3102 BCE)","currentPosition":"In 2026 we are roughly 5,127 years into the Kali Yuga by the traditional count (about its 5,128th year), with about 427,000 years still to run. The film's 2898 AD is a fictional story date, not the end of any yuga.","summary":"Kalki 2898 AD is a Telugu-language epic science-fiction film from 2024 and the first entry in a planned Kalki Cinematic Universe. It draws on Hindu scripture. Its premise sets the story in 2898 AD, about 6,000 years after the Kurukshetra war. Ashwatthama is cursed to wander until he protects the mother of Kalki, the final avatar of Vishnu. The film frames Kalki's arrival within a dystopian future, but it places that arrival far earlier than the traditional end of the Kali Yuga. The timeline is a narrative device, not a claim about the actual end of the yuga cycle.","keyClaims":["The film is set in 2898 AD, roughly 6,000 years after the Kurukshetra war, and places Kalki's birth in that era (Wikipedia plot summary).","Ashwatthama's immortality is a curse that lasts until he protects Kalki's mother, tying the Mahabharata episode to the Kalki prophecy.","The Shambhala refugees believe in the Kalki prophecy, giving the film an eschatological framing.","The dystopian setting (Kashi under a totalitarian ruler) is presented as the pre-Kalki era.","Box-office success (about 1,100 crore INR gross reported by Wikipedia) is cited as evidence of mass interest in the Kalki theme."],"evidence":["Verified from Wikipedia: Telugu-language, written and directed by Nag Ashwin, set in 2898 AD in Kashi, about Kalki and lab subject SUM-80, first installment of the Kalki Cinematic Universe.","Verified from Wikipedia: released 27 June 2024; production budget about 600 crore INR; estimated 1,100 crore INR global gross; National Film Awards in 2024.","Verified from Wikipedia plot summary: Ashwatthama's curse lasts six millennia and ends with protection of Kalki's mother.","Background knowledge: traditional Puranic reckoning places the Kali Yuga start at 3102 BCE with a 432,000-year length, so 2898 AD is about 6,000 years in, not its end.","Background knowledge: the 6,000-year figure is an artistic choice; Mahabharata-based accounts of Ashwatthama's wandering use other durations (for example 3,000 years in some tellings)."],"criticisms":["The 2898 AD date is a fictional story choice. The traditional Puranic timeline puts Kalki's appearance at the end of the Kali Yuga, about 428,000 years away, so the date should not be read as a prophecy.","Reviews (per Wikipedia) cited a slow first half, long runtime, disjointed subplots and weak dialogue, so the mythic framing is not a developed theology.","Some online Kalki claims tie 2898 or other dates to Kalki's appearance. These are unsupported, and past dated Kalki predictions have failed; I did not verify specific failed predictions in this run.","Using a film's date to argue about cosmic timing blends a narrative device with traditional cosmology and makes the cosmology look more falsifiable than it is."],"plausibility":1,"plausibilityReason":"As a claim about the real-world timeline, 2898 AD has no evidential basis. It is a fictional date in a film, and the traditional yuga reckoning is a theological system with no empirical checks. The traditional Kali Yuga end is about 428,000 years away.","references":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kalki_2898_AD","Vishnu Purana and Bhagavata Purana (traditional yuga chronology; background knowledge)","Kalki Purana (Kalki avatar prophecy; background knowledge)"]},{"id":"kali-yuga-meme-culture","title":"Kali Yuga in Internet Meme Culture (Twitter/X, Reddit, 4chan, crypto and finance memes, doomerism)","category":"Internet / pop culture","verification":"Partly verified","proponents":["Anonymous posters on 4chan and Reddit (no single originator)","Crypto and finance meme communities on Twitter/X and Reddit","Doomer and irony-driven meme accounts","Readers of Julius Evola's Ride the Tiger who popularized the phrase in online esoteric and reactionary circles"],"primarySources":["Wikipedia, 'Kali Yuga' article (fetched; no section on internet culture, memes, crypto or doomerism; 'Other usage' section mentions Theosophy, Steiner, Evola, Guenon)","KnowYourMeme search for 'kali yuga' (fetched; a culture entry exists at knowyourmeme.com/memes/cultures/kali-yuga, but the search snippet gave no date or description; no 'ride the tiger' entry found)","Julius Evola, Ride the Tiger (Italian original 1961) - source of the phrase 'ride the tiger' as used in online circles (background knowledge)"],"period":"Traditional Hindu chronology places Kali Yuga's start at 3102 BCE; the meme usage is a 2010s-2020s online phenomenon","totalCycleYears":"4,320,000 years (the four yugas total 12,000 divine years x 360 = 4.32 million human years)","durations":{"satya":"1,728,000 years (4,800 divine years)","treta":"1,296,000 years (3,600 divine years)","dvapara":"864,000 years (2,400 divine years)","kali":"432,000 years (1,200 divine years)"},"kaliStart":"3102 BCE (traditional astronomical date, Hindu tradition)","kaliEnd":"Approximately 428,899 CE by the traditional count (432,000 years after 3102 BCE)","currentPosition":"Roughly 5,128 years into Kali Yuga in 2026 by the traditional count. Online, the phrase is used ironically to describe a perceived decline in society, politics, finance or internet discourse, not as a measured chronological claim.","summary":"Kali Yuga has become an ironic internet meme. Users invoke it to describe chaos, moral decline, scams, or the absurdity of current events. Common forms include \"we're in Kali Yuga\" captions, \"ride the tiger\" phrasing borrowed from Julius Evola's 1961 book, crypto and stock-market jokes framed as a dark age, and doomer-style posts treating collapse as already underway. The memes draw on the traditional Hindu idea that humanity is in the worst of four ages, but they almost always strip out the cosmology and use the term as a mood or punchline. Concrete details of specific memes, dates and origin posts could not be verified in this run; the KnowYourMeme culture entry exists but its content was not retrievable.","keyClaims":["The phrase Kali Yuga is used online as shorthand for a dark, corrupt or chaotic age.","Crypto and finance communities use it to describe market collapses, scams and speculative manias.","'Ride the tiger' spread online largely through Evola-influenced reactionary and esoteric circles, though many casual users use it without knowing the source.","Doomerism pairs the idea of a dark age with resignation or irony, and the meme format lets users hold the view without claiming it literally.","The meme form usually discards the 432,000-year duration and the 3102 BCE start date, so the cosmology is not part of the joke."],"evidence":["Wikipedia's Kali Yuga article (fetched) documents the traditional timeline and lists Theosophy, Steiner, Evola and Guenon as later users of the concept, but it has no section on memes or internet culture.","A KnowYourMeme culture entry for Kali Yuga exists (knowyourmeme.com/memes/cultures/kali-yuga), confirming the term has been tracked as an online meme, but its body text could not be read here.","No KnowYourMeme entry for 'ride the tiger' appeared in the search results fetched.","Steiner's claim that the Kali Yuga ended around 1900 is a documented failed prediction of a period-ending date, and it is often cited when online users treat the age as already over or as having a clear end date."],"criticisms":["The meme use does not make any testable prediction about the real timeline, so it cannot be checked against events. It works as commentary, not as a chronology.","Taking the Kali Yuga's 432,000-year length literally would mean the dark age runs until about 428,899 CE. Online use mostly ignores this scale, which shows the term is being used as a mood and not as a schedule.","Attributing the phrase 'ride the tiger' to Evola-based reactionary circles is partly a background-knowledge claim; this run did not confirm the spread path with sources. Some users may use it with no awareness of its origin in Evola's book.","Doomerism tied to the Kali Yuga framing has repeatedly predicted collapse without a dated outcome. Earlier apocalyptic dates in the same broad milieu failed, including Nibiru claims for 2003 and 2012, and the 2012 Mayan-calendar end date. The meme tends to skip over these misses.","Crypto and finance uses of the term often pair doom with price speculation, which makes the framing unfalsifiable; any market crash confirms the 'dark age' and any rally is treated as a temporary reprieve."],"plausibility":1,"plausibilityReason":"As a claim about the real-world timeline, the Kali Yuga framing has no empirical basis. Its durations are symbolic cosmological numbers, not measured or predicted events. Internet use of the term is ironic commentary on the present, so it neither confirms nor refutes any chronology. The only date-linked claims in the broader tradition (Steiner's 1900 end date, Nibiru 2003 and 2012, the Mayan 2012 end date) have all failed.","references":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kali_Yuga","https://knowyourmeme.com/memes/cultures/kali-yuga","Julius Evola, Ride the Tiger (Cavalcare la tigre, 1961) - background knowledge","Helena Blavatsky, The Secret Doctrine (1888) - background knowledge"]}],"critiques":["# Yuga cycle interpretations: a philological critique\n\n## The earliest sources\n\nThe textual record supports a clear sequence, and every interpretation in the set should be measured against it.\n\n1. **Vedic period (c. 1500–500 BCE).** In the Rigveda, *yuga* means a yoke, a generation or a span of time. Kṛta, tretā, dvāpara and kali are throws of the dice, ranked by score. Aitareya Brāhmaṇa 7.15 uses them as a metaphor for effort: kali lies down, dvāpara stirs, tretā stands, kṛta walks. No Vedic text gives a sequence of cosmic ages, durations or an epoch. The Vedāṅga Jyotiṣa uses *yuga* for a 5-year lunisolar intercalation cycle, which is the only attested technical meaning from this period.\n2. **Epic and smṛti layer (c. 300 BCE–300 CE).** The Mahābhārata (Mārkaṇḍeya at 3.186 in the critical edition, and Śānti 12.224) and Manu 1.68–86 give the 4:3:2:1 scheme with dawn and dusk periods, totalling 12,000 years, and tie each age to a fraction of dharma. The key philological issue is the \"divine years\". The 12,000-year total is not in dispute. What is disputed is whether the multiplier of 360 belonged to the original scheme. Luis González-Reimann (*The Mahābhārata and the Yugas*, 2002) argues that the epic's figures were probably human years at first, and that the 360× inflation was systematized later in the Purāṇas and Siddhāntas. That would make 4,320,000 a later development. No summary cites this, the most relevant monograph on the subject.\n3. **Siddhāntic layer (5th century CE onward).** The 4,320,000-year mahāyuga becomes a period-relation device: whole numbers of planetary revolutions fit into it. The Kali epoch of 3102 BCE was fixed by back-calculation. Āryabhaṭa (499 CE) is the first securely dated witness. He uses **four equal quarters of 1,080,000 years** and a kalpa of 1,008 mahāyugas. The reason for equal quarters is technical: they make every quarter-boundary, including 3102 BCE, an exact mean conjunction. Brahmagupta (628 CE) attacked this as contrary to smṛti and restored 4:3:2:1. This is a real doctrinal dispute about yuga length, and the Brahmagupta summary erases it.\n4. **Epigraphic layer.** The Aihole inscription (634 CE) dates itself to 3,735 years after the Bhārata war, alongside Śaka 556. It counts from the *war*, not from \"Kali\", which shows the epoch had already been fused with epic narrative by the 7th century. Varāhamihira's competing date for the war (Śaka minus 2526, c. 2448 BCE) shows the fusion was not universal.\n5. **Purāṇic and sectarian elaboration (c. 4th–10th century and later).** This layer adds manvantaras, kalpas, Brahmā's lifespan, the Kalki narratives and the Bhāgavata's devotional reframing.\n6. **Modern reinterpretation (1888 onward).** This covers Blavatsky, Yukteswar (1894), Vivekananda, Aurobindo, Guénon, Daniélou, Cruttenden and others. None of these readings recovers an older meaning. Each is a new construction.\n\n## Assessment\n\n**Faithful to texts, if read as text and not as history.** These summaries describe what the texts say reasonably well: Manusmṛti, the Mahābhārata, the Bhāgavata, the Vāyu/Brahmāṇḍa/Liṅga Purāṇas, the manvantara system, the Kalpa, the Surya Siddhānta, Āryabhaṭa and the Kerala school. They are accurate *reports of doctrine*. They become non-historical only when someone treats the numbers as measured time. One correction runs through most of them: they present 4,320,000 and 3102 BCE as a single \"traditional\" package. Philologically, those two numbers come from different layers and different centuries.\n\n**Faithful to the history of the doctrine.** These are the strongest entries: the Vedic dice-throw reading, the Vedāṅga Jyotiṣa, the Indologist developmental view, Pingree, the Bailly/Bentley/Burgess/Thibaut debate, the Aihole epigraphy and the critical-secular view. They treat the yuga doctrine as something that developed over time, and they separate attestation from claims of antiquity. Pingree's argument about Babylonian sources is strongest for the *technique* (zero-epochs and period relations) and weakest for the *cosmology* itself.\n\n**Later innovations presented as recoveries.**\n- Yukteswar's 24,000-year cycle has no pre-modern source. The rival model of equal quarters inside the tradition is Āryabhaṭa's, not his.\n- Guénon's 64,800-year manvantara is a Traditionalist arithmetic built on precession.\n- Daniélou's Kali of 5,544 years, ending in 2442 CE, rests on non-standard arithmetic.\n- The Dayānanda/Arya Samaj creation count of 1,960,853,xxx years leaves out the sandhi periods. That is why it differs from the Siddhāntic 1.955 and 1.972 billion figures.\n- Theosophy's root races, Cruttenden's companion star, and the Maya and Hamlet's Mill correlations are not Indian sources at all.\n- Vivekananda's priest, warrior, merchant and worker sequence is a theory of which varṇa is dominant. Mapping it onto yugas goes beyond what he wrote.\n\n**Comparative entries.** The Hesiod, Zoroastrian, Berossus, Jain and Buddhist entries are useful. They show that decline-of-ages schemes and large sexagesimal numbers were widespread. None proves descent from a common source, and the summaries mostly say so correctly. The Buddhist case (DN 26/27) is the most relevant one, because it gives an Indian decline cycle *without* yuga names.\n\n## Top 5 \"nearest to reality\" (philological lens)\n\n1. **Vedic absence: yugas as dice throws.** This rests on the earliest attestation. It correctly shows that the four-age doctrine is post-Vedic and that its names come from gambling vocabulary.\n2. **Indologist historical-development view.** It gets the stratigraphy of dice, epic, smṛti, Siddhānta and Purāṇa right. It needs González-Reimann added on divine years.\n3. **Epigraphic use of the Kali era (Aihole, 634 CE).** This is the only hard, independently dated evidence. It proves the epoch was in use and already merged with the war by the 7th century, and it proves nothing earlier.\n4. **Pingree, together with the computed-epoch side of Bailly vs. Bentley/Burgess/Thibaut.** It correctly identifies 3102 BCE as a back-calculated mean-conjunction zero point, whatever one decides about how much came from Babylon.\n5. **Vedāṅga Jyotiṣa 5-year yuga.** It preserves the oldest technical meaning of *yuga* as a short calendrical cycle, which is a useful check against reading cosmic ages back into early texts.\n\n## Factual errors in the summaries\n\n- **Āryabhaṭa entry.** The statement that 3,600 years had elapsed at his age 23 is in *Kālakriyāpāda* 10, not Gītikāpāda 3. Gītikāpāda 3 gives the elapsed manus, yugas and yugapādas. The equal quarters are explicit in the text, not a reconstruction. His kalpa is 1,008 mahāyugas, not 1,000. Āryabhaṭīya counts from **sunrise** (audayika). The **midnight** epoch belongs to his other system and to the Sūrya Siddhānta.\n- **Brahmagupta entry.** It is wrong that he \"does not challenge yuga durations\" and \"accepts the same framework as Āryabhaṭa\". BSS 1 explicitly attacks Āryabhaṭa's equal yugapādas and his kalpa.\n- **Critical-secular entry.** The epoch is not \"3,600 years before his birth\". The text says 3,600 years had elapsed when he was 23. Olivelle dates Manu to about the 2nd century CE, not 2nd century BCE to 2nd century CE.\n- **Sūrya Siddhānta entry.** Its Venus figure is 7,022,376 revolutions; 7,022,388 is Āryabhaṭa's. The extant Sūrya Siddhānta is later than the 4th–5th century (Varāhamihira summarizes an older version). In the Sūrya Siddhānta the exact mean conjunction falls at the end of the Kṛta yuga. Its 4:3:2:1 split does not make 3102 BCE an exact conjunction for every planet.\n- **Numerical-structure entry.** 1,577,917,500 is Āryabhaṭa's count of civil days per mahāyuga, not solar revolutions.\n- **Yukteswar entries 1 and 3.** 499 CE is the *end* of the descending Kali and the low point of the cycle, not its start. The ascending Kali runs 499–1699 CE. In Yukteswar the Sun orbits a dual star, and Vishnunābhi is the grand centre around which that pair moves. The first entry conflates the two.\n- **Guénon entry.** The 64,800-year manvantara *is* Guénon's (\"Quelques remarques sur la doctrine des cycles cosmiques\"). Gaston Georgel developed it further. It is wrong to say Guénon simply accepted 4,320,000.\n- **Daniélou entry.** The 2442 CE date is traceable to *While the Gods Play*: 3102 BCE plus a 5,544-year Kali. I am moderately confident of this.\n- **Bailly entry.** The end date should be about 428,899 CE, not 428 CE.\n- **Berossus entry.** Syncellus's ten reigns sum to 120 sars, which is 432,000 years, not 396,000.\n- **Manvantara entry.** 1,728,000 years *already includes* the 144,000-year dawn and dusk, so \"2,016,000\" double-counts them. The claim that some reckonings place the Vaivasvata manvantara at \"120,000 years\" is garbled.\n- **Jain entry.** The 3rd ara is exactly 2 koṭākoṭi sāgaropama; only the 4th has \"less 42,000 years\". Ṛṣabha belongs to the 3rd ara, not the 4th. The \"Digambara 605 BCE\" figure confuses the 605-year Śaka offset with the nirvāṇa date.\n- **Vedāṅga Jyotiṣa entry.** 62 synodic months come to about 1,830.9 days, not 1,829.6.\n- **Archaeology entry.** Shinde et al. on Rakhigarhi was published in *Cell* in 2019, not 2018.\n- **Dayānanda entry.** The creation-age figure comes from the *Ṛgvedādibhāṣyabhūmikā*, not Satyārth Prakāś chapter 8.\n- **Zoroastrian entry.** Plutarch's Theopompus passage (De Iside 47) reads most naturally as 3,000 + 3,000 years plus an end phase. \"6,000 + 3,000 + 3,000\" is an interpretation, not the text.\n- **Pingree entry.** The identification of the Pauliśa Siddhānta with Paul of Alexandria is contested; Pingree himself doubted it.\n- **Panchānga entry.** The year count is ambiguous. CE + 3101 gives *elapsed* years. Almanacs commonly print Yugābda 5128 for the year that began in spring 2026, so \"5127 current on 20 October 2026\" is not secure.\n- **Vivekananda entry.** He describes successive dominance by priests, warriors, merchants and workers (śūdras). He did not explicitly map these onto Satya, Tretā, Dvāpara and Kali.","# Yuga Timelines Through the Lens of Astronomy and Archaeoastronomy\n\n## 1. Was the 3102 BCE epoch observed or back-computed?\n\n**It was back-computed. Of everything in this dataset, this conclusion has the strongest support.** Three separate lines of evidence point the same way.\n\n- **The epoch is zero by construction.** In Aryabhata's system, and in the Surya Siddhanta as it survives, all the *mean* planetary longitudes are set to 0° Aries at the epoch. That is how the tables are built, so it tells us nothing about what was in the sky. The *true* positions in February 3102 BCE are only a loose spread across tens of degrees. The lunar apogee and node are nowhere near zero. No real conjunction occurred.\n- **The tables are most accurate around 500 CE.** Roger Billard (1971) tested Aryabhata's mean-motion parameters against modern theory. Their errors are smallest around 500 CE and grow steadily as you go back in time. Parameters fitted to observations made in 3102 BCE would be most accurate near 3102 BCE. Instead, the constants were tuned to Aryabhata's own sky and then run backwards to a convenient common zero. Bentley argued this in 1790, and Burgess, Thibaut, Pingree and Plofker confirmed the computational logic.\n- **No early record exists.** The epoch first appears c. 499 CE, in Aryabhata's Kālakriyā 10: 3,600 years had elapsed when he was 23. The earliest dated inscription is Aihole, 634 CE. Nothing from the 4th or 3rd millennium BCE mentions it.\n\nSo 3102 BCE is a fitting artefact, like Ptolemy's Nabonassar era (747 BCE). It is not a remembered event. Linking it to Krishna's death or the Mahabharata war is a later identification; Aihole already counts from the Bhārata war. That link adds no astronomical content. Attempts to date the war from the epic's sky descriptions (Achar's 3067 BCE, for example) do not even agree with the 3102 epoch plus the traditional 36-year gap.\n\n## 2. Does precession support any yuga length?\n\n**No.** Precession moves at about 50.29″ per year, a period of roughly 25,770 years at today's rate. None of the proposed yuga figures fits it:\n\n- **Yukteswar's 24,000 years.** This is exactly 360° divided by **54″ per year**, which is the precession rate in the old Indian and Arabic trepidation models. It is not a measurement. Yukteswar inherited a medieval error. Over one 12,000-year arc, the gap between 24,000 and 25,770 adds up to more than 800 years of drift.\n- **No smooth 4:3:2:1 sequence.** Precession is a uniform rotation. Nothing in it produces four unequal phases with a turning point at 499 CE.\n- **The Siddhantas did not treat precession as a yuga.** The Surya Siddhanta models it as a libration of ±27°, with 600 oscillations per mahayuga, giving a 7,200-year period. That is a divisor of the mahayuga, not the mahayuga itself.\n- **No integer relationship.** 4,320,000 ÷ 25,920 = 166.67, and 4,320,000 ÷ 25,772 is also not a whole number. Linking the two (as Hamlet's Mill, Guénon and Cruttenden do) needs arbitrary multipliers.\n- **Cruttenden's binary companion.** It would have to be detectable. IRAS, 2MASS, WISE and Gaia exclude any such star near the Sun.\n\n## 3. Are the numbers astronomical or symbolic?\n\n**Both, in layers that should be kept apart:**\n\n1. **Vedic.** *Kṛta, tretā, dvāpara* and *kali* are dice throws scored 4, 3, 2 and 1. The Vedāṅga Jyotiṣa yuga is a real, observation-based five-year lunisolar intercalation cycle. This is the only \"yuga\" here with genuine observational content.\n2. **Epic and smṛti.** The 12,000 divine years and the 4:3:2:1 ratio are symbolic arithmetic built on 12 and 360. The ratio itself comes from the dice scores.\n3. **Siddhantic.** 4,320,000 became a *computational* common period. It is a span long enough that every mean motion completes a whole number of revolutions, much like a Greek Great Year. The revolution counts are real astronomy because they were fitted to observation. The span is a convention chosen to hold those counts. Differing Siddhantas and Brahmagupta's kalpa-epoch alternative show that it was a choice, not a discovery.\n\nThe kalpa's rough match to the Earth's age (4.32 vs 4.54 billion years) comes from multiplying by 1,000. The match with Berossus's 432,000 years is plausibly sexagesimal heritage, but transmission is unproven.\n\n**Overall:** every interpretation that treats the yugas as *historical or cosmic durations* fails against astronomy. The interpretations that hold up are those that explain the numbers as *constructs*: how they were made, when, and why.\n\n## Top 5 \"nearest to reality\" under this lens\n\n1. **Bailly vs Bentley/Burgess/Thibaut: computed 3102 BCE epoch.** It answers the central question directly. Billard's statistical test and modern ephemerides confirm the back-computation.\n2. **Vedāṅga Jyotiṣa five-year yuga.** It is the only yuga with real observational and calendrical content, and its arithmetic checks out (1,830 days, 62 synodic months).\n3. **Pingree: Hellenistic and Babylonian influence.** The zero-epoch and period-relation method is clearly paralleled in Greek and Babylonian astronomy. The thesis is weaker on where the 432 figure itself comes from.\n4. **Epigraphic Kali era (Aihole 634 CE, Aryabhata 499 CE).** It gives the solid fact that the epoch was in use by the 5th to 7th centuries CE and bounds when it was invented.\n5. **Vedic absence: dice throws, not cosmic ages.** It explains the 4:3:2:1 ratio as gaming scores with no astronomical meaning, and the textual layering is consistent.\n\n## Factual errors spotted in the summaries\n\n- **Bailly/Bentley entry:** it says Kali ends \"approximately 428 CE\". It should be about 428,899 CE.\n- **Berossus entry:** it claims the reigns in Syncellus sum to 396,000 years. Wrong. The figures (10+3+13+12+18+10+18+10+8+18) sum to exactly 120 sars, which is 432,000 years.\n- **Yukteswar and precession entries:** they call 499 CE the *start* of descending Kali and 1699 CE its *end*. In Yukteswar, 499 CE is the low point: descending Kali *ends* there, and ascending Kali runs 499–1699 CE.\n- **Aryabhata entry:**\n  - The 3,600-year verse is Kālakriyā 10, not Gītikā 3.\n  - The verse refers to Aryabhata's age of 23, not to when he wrote the text.\n  - The equal yugapādas are stated in the text itself. Calling the equal-quarter reading weakly attested overstates the doubt.\n- **\"Critical secular\" entry:** it says Kali began \"3600 years before his birth\". It should say 3,600 years had elapsed when he was 23.\n- **Brahmagupta entry:** it says Brahmagupta did not dispute the yuga structure. In fact he explicitly attacked Aryabhata's equal yugapādas as contrary to smṛti. He also counted from the start of the kalpa, not from Kali.\n- **Surya Siddhanta entry:** the Venus count of 7,022,388 is Aryabhata's figure. The Surya Siddhanta gives 7,022,376.\n- **Numerical-structure entry:** 1,577,917,500 is Aryabhata's number of *civil days* in a mahayuga, not \"sidereal revolutions\".\n- **Manvantara entry:** \"Satya plus sandhyas = 2,016,000\" is wrong. The 1,728,000 figure already includes the 144,000-year dawn and dusk. The claim that the \"Vaivasvata manvantara [is placed] at ~120,000 years\" is unsupported.\n- **Vedāṅga Jyotiṣa entry:** 62 synodic months is about 1,830.9 days, not 1,829.6.\n- **Cruttenden entry:** Nemesis was not ruled out in the 1980s. It was constrained mainly by WISE around 2014. It also had a period of about 26 million years, unrelated to 24,000.\n- **Hamlet's Mill entry:** the Hamlet–Amleth link is well established (Saxo Grammaticus). The speculative part is Amlodhi's mill (Grótti).\n- **Kalachakra entry:** the 2424 CE war prophecy cannot have \"repeatedly failed\", because the date has not yet arrived.\n- **Daniélou entry:** the 2442 CE date very likely comes from Daniélou's *While the Gods Play* (1985), which uses a Kali start of 3606 BCE and a length of 6,048 years. It is not untraceable, though it should be checked against the book.\n- **Zoroastrian entry:** Theopompus, as reported by Plutarch, gives 3,000 + 3,000 + 3,000 years. The \"6,000 + 3,000 + 3,000\" in the summary is not what Plutarch reports.\n- **Jain entry:** \"Digambara nirvāṇa c. 605 BCE\" probably confuses this with the 605-year gap between the nirvāṇa and the Śaka era.\n- **Maya entry:** correlations other than GMT differ by centuries, not \"days to decades\". Maya scribes *did* count from the 13.0.0.0.0 creation date.\n- **Archaeology entry:** Shinde et al. in *Cell* is 2019, not 2018.","# Empirical critique of the 54 yuga interpretations\n\n## The core test\n\nWhen you judge these 54 entries by archaeology, palaeoanthropology, geology and dated history, they split into two kinds:\n\n- **Timelines:** schemes that claim the yugas describe the real past or predict the future.\n- **Meta-accounts:** claims about how, when and why the yuga doctrine was invented.\n\nNo timeline survives contact with evidence. Several meta-accounts make specific, checkable claims about texts, inscriptions and astronomy, and those claims hold up well.\n\n**The literal Puranic model (4.32 Myr) fails outright.**\n- **Too early for humans.** Taken as history, Satya (1.728 Myr) and Treta (1.296 Myr) would put an advanced, virtuous humanity about 3.9 to 1.3 million years ago. That is the age of *Homo habilis* and early *H. erectus*. Anatomically modern humans appear about 300 ka (Jebel Irhoud).\n- **Wrong sequence in South Asia.** Farming begins about 7000 BCE at Mehrgarh, and the Harappan cities date to 2600–1900 BCE. Dvapara, which would have ended in 3102 BCE, has no material trace. There is no pre-Harappan urban \"golden age\" anywhere.\n- **The epoch is not an event.** 3102 BCE is sometimes said to fit the start of the Bronze Age. But the dates that fall near it, Uruk-period writing and Egypt's Dynasty 0/1, are Near Eastern, and they mark *rising* complexity, not decline. The epoch was computed in the 5th century CE, so any near-match is coincidence.\n- **The decline markers are contradicted.** Life expectancy, adult height and literacy have all risen sharply in the last two centuries.\n\n**Yukteswar, SRF and Cruttenden (24,000 years)** at least make testable claims, and they fail.\n- Precession takes about 25,772 years, not 24,000.\n- Infrared surveys (IRAS, WISE) and Gaia show no binary companion.\n- The internal chronology contradicts itself. His descending Kali (701 BCE–499 CE) should be the low point of human understanding. It actually covers the Axial Age, Greek science, the Maurya and Gupta states, Panini, Aryabhata and the Han dynasty.\n- Supporters point to the descending Satya (about 11,500 BCE) lining up with the Neolithic. But the Neolithic is a *rise* in social complexity, the opposite of decline.\n\n**Archaeoastronomical Mahabharata dating (Achar about 3067 BCE, Oak and others)** fails on material evidence.\n- Hastinapura's Painted Grey Ware levels date to about 1200–600 BCE.\n- The epic's iron weapons, horse-drawn chariots and political geography do not fit 3100 BCE.\n- Achar's date cannot be squared with the traditional 36-year gap between the war and the start of Kali.\n\n**Tilak and Kak** depend on reading vague nakshatra references as precise observations. Linguistics, Indo-Iranian comparison and ancient DNA all contradict them. Narasimhan et al. 2019 show steppe ancestry arriving around 2000–1500 BCE, which rules out a Rigveda of 4500 BCE or earlier.\n\n**The kalpa-equals-Earth-age match** (4.32 vs 4.54 Gyr) is a single cherry-picked hit. The other numbers miss badly: Brahma's lifespan is about 22,500 times the age of the universe. The Siddhantic texts also never present the kalpa as the age of the Earth.\n\n**The esoteric and traditionalist readings** make no falsifiable claims about the past, so they cannot be ranked against evidence:\n- Guénon, Evola, Aurobindo, Blavatsky and the tantric readings.\n- The comparative myths (Hesiod, the Aztec suns, Norse, Hopi), Zoroastrian, Jain and Buddhist schemes.\n\nBlavatsky's Lemuria and Atlantis are actively falsified by plate tectonics and the seafloor record.\n\n**What does hold up is the historical account of the doctrine itself:**\n1. The Vedas have no four-age cosmology. Krta, treta, dvapara and kali are dice terms.\n2. The Vedanga Jyotisha \"yuga\" is a 5-year lunisolar intercalation cycle.\n3. The 12,000-divine-year scheme appears in the Mahabharata and Manusmriti, around 200 BCE to 300 CE.\n4. Siddhantic astronomers then fixed the 4.32 Myr mean-motion framework and back-computed the 3102 BCE zero point. Every planetary mean longitude is zero there by construction, and modern ephemerides show no tight conjunction.\n5. Epigraphy confirms the era was in practical use by 634 CE (Aihole: Kali 3735 = Shaka 556).\n\nEach of these points can be checked against manuscripts, inscriptions or computation, and each passes. The Babylonian origin of \"432,000\" (Pingree, Berossus) is the weakest part of this account. The sexagesimal parallel is real, but transmission of the cosmology itself is not shown.\n\n## Factual errors found in the summaries\n\n1. **Bailly vs Bentley:** the Kali end date is given as \"Approximately 428 CE\". It should be about 428,899 CE. Bentley's main papers are also from 1799, not 1790.\n2. **Sandhya entry:** it adds a 36,000-year sandhyamsha to reach \"464,898 CE\". That counts the twilights twice, because the 432,000 years already include 2 × 36,000 of them.\n3. **Manvantara entry:** Satya \"with dawn and dusk\" is given as 2,016,000. Satya *including* its twilights is 1,728,000 (core 1,440,000 plus 2 × 144,000).\n4. **Yukteswar entries:** 499 CE is described as the *start* of descending Kali, with descending Kali ending in 1699. In The Holy Science, descending Kali runs from about 701 BCE to 499 CE, and ascending Kali from 499 to 1699 CE.\n5. **Mesopotamian entry:** it says Berossus's reigns \"sum to 396,000\". The standard list (10+3+13+12+18+10+18+10+8+18 sars) totals 120 sars, which is 432,000 years.\n6. **Brahmagupta entry:** it says his dispute was not about yuga length. In fact the BSS explicitly attacks Aryabhata's equal yugapadas and his 1,008-mahayuga kalpa.\n7. **Aryabhata entries:**\n   - The 3,600-year statement is in Kalakriyapada 10, not Gitikapada 3.\n   - Gitika 3 gives 6 Manus, 27 yugas and 3 yugapadas elapsed, with 72 yugas per Manu. That makes the equal quarters explicit, not \"not explicitly stated\".\n   - The Aryabhatiya counts from sunrise at Lanka, not midnight. The midnight reckoning comes from his other, lost system.\n8. **Numerical-structure entry:** 1,577,917,500 is Aryabhata's number of *civil days* per mahayuga, not \"sidereal revolutions of the Sun\". It also confuses the 108 Sun-distance/Sun-diameter ratio with the Sun/Earth diameter ratio, which is about 109.\n9. **Surya Siddhanta entry:** Venus is given 7,022,388 revolutions. That is Aryabhata's value; the Surya Siddhanta has 7,022,376. The surviving Surya Siddhanta is also a later revision of the text Varahamihira summarised.\n10. **Jain entry:**\n    - The 42,000-year deduction applies only to ara 4, not ara 3.\n    - \"Digambara nirvana about 605 BCE\" confuses the 605-year interval between Vira-nirvana and the Shaka era with a date. Both sects generally anchor the nirvana at 527 BCE.\n11. **Archaeology entry:**\n    - Shinde et al. (Rakhigarhi, *Cell*) is 2019, not 2018.\n    - Giosan 2012 shows the Ghaggar-Hakra was monsoon-fed and not glacier-fed during Harappan times. That point is stronger than \"dried around 2000 BCE\".\n    - Indus cities are not the \"earliest cities\"; Uruk predates them by about 800 years.\n12. **Daniélou entry:** the 2442 CE end date very likely comes from Daniélou's *While the Gods Play* (1985). I recalled this from memory and could not check it in this run.\n13. **Buddhist entry:** modern scholarship mostly places the Buddha's death around 400 BCE (the \"short chronology\"), not 480 BCE.\n14. **Vivekananda entry:** his cycle of priest, warrior, merchant and Shudra dominance is not explicitly mapped onto the four yugas in his writings. That mapping appears to be the summarizer's.\n\n## Top 5 \"nearest to reality\" (empirical lens)\n\n1. **Vedic absence (dice throws, not ages):** directly checkable against the Vedic corpus, and the four-age cosmology is indeed missing.\n2. **Bailly vs Bentley/Burgess/Thibaut (computed epoch):** the claim that 3102 BCE is a back-calculated zero with no real conjunction is confirmed by modern ephemerides and by how the tables were built.\n3. **Archaeology and human evolution vs a historical golden age:** correctly shows that a literal yuga chronology contradicts fossils, ancient DNA and the archaeological sequence.\n4. **Epigraphic Kali era (Aihole 634 CE):** a securely dated inscription with two eras that agree, proving the convention existed by the 7th century while making no inflated claims.\n5. **Indologist historical-development view (with the critical secular view):** the staged development from dice terms to epic ages to Siddhantic arithmetic matches the dated textual and inscriptional record. Pingree's Babylonian thesis just misses the list because transmission of the cosmology is unproven."],"verdict":"# Which Yuga Timeline Is Nearest to Reality?\n\n## First, what does \"nearest to reality\" mean?\n\nThe question can be read in three ways, and each needs a different kind of evidence.\n\n1. **Historical origin.** Which account best explains where the yuga doctrine came from, when its numbers were fixed and why? Texts, inscriptions and the internal logic of the old astronomy can test this.\n2. **Literal timeline.** Do the yugas describe real past ages of humanity, or real cosmic durations? Archaeology, genetics, geology and astronomy can test this.\n3. **Symbolic truth.** Do the yugas say something real about human life: moral decline, how civilizations rise and fall, states of mind? Evidence can test this only partly, and in places not at all.\n\nMixing these three readings is the main source of confusion in popular writing about yugas. A scheme can be a great intellectual achievement and an accurate record of what the texts teach while still being false as a history of the world.\n\n## Bottom line\n\n**On the evidence, the most accurate account is the historical-developmental one.** No yuga *timeline* is supported as a literal chronology. The account that best fits the evidence explains how the timelines were built:\n\n- In the Vedas, *kṛta, tretā, dvāpara* and *kali* are dice throws scored 4, 3, 2 and 1. No Vedic text describes four cosmic ages.\n- The earliest technical \"yuga\" is a practical **5-year calendar cycle** in the Vedāṅga Jyotiṣa, used to keep lunar months in step with the solar year.\n- The 4:3:2:1 scheme of ages, totalling 12,000 years, first appears in the Mahābhārata and in Manu (around the 2nd century CE). The \"divine year\" multiplier of 360, which turns 12,000 into 4,320,000, may be a later systematization. González-Reimann argues this.\n- From the 5th century CE, astronomers used 4,320,000 years as a **computational period**, long enough for every planet to complete a whole number of revolutions. They worked backward to a common zero point, the Kali epoch of 18 February 3102 BCE.\n\nThe 3102 BCE epoch is the strongest single result in the whole debate. **It was calculated, not observed.** At that date every planet's average position is set to zero by the way the tables are built. Modern reconstructions show no real alignment in the sky then. Billard's statistical analysis found that Āryabhaṭa's parameters are most accurate around 500 CE, his own era, not around 3102 BCE. The epoch first appears in Āryabhaṭa (who says 3,600 years had passed when he was 23, i.e. 499 CE). Its first datable use in an inscription is at Aihole in 634 CE.\n\nOn the third reading, symbolic truth, there is no clear winner, because these claims are mostly matters of value and cannot be measured. The more modest readings, such as Aurobindo's yugas as psychological or civilizational phases, or Vivekananda's ages dominated by different social classes, are at least coherent. They do not contradict the evidence because they make no claims about dates.\n\n## Ranked assessment\n\nThe scores reflect how far each interpretation is supported as a claim about reality, in the sense the interpretation itself intends.\n\n| Rank | Interpretation family | Score /10 | Reason |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **Historical-developmental view** (dice terms → epic and Manu → astronomical tables → Purāṇas; 3102 BCE back-calculated) | **9** | Every step can be checked against dated texts, inscriptions or arithmetic, and each step holds up. |\n| 2 | **Vedāṅga Jyotiṣa 5-year yuga** | **8** | The only \"yuga\" that is a real observational cycle. Its arithmetic works (62 synodic months ≈ 1,830.9 days against 1,830 civil days). It covers only the calendar, not cosmic ages. |\n| 3 | **Pingree's Babylonian and Greek transmission thesis** | **6** | Strong on technique: zero-epochs and whole-number period relations have clear Babylonian and Greek parallels. Weak on whether the 432,000 figure itself was borrowed. Berossus's 120 sars do equal 432,000 years, but no transmission has been shown. |\n| 4 | **Āryabhaṭa's equal quarters (4 × 1,080,000)** | **5** as a historical fact, **1** as a timeline | His text really did split the mahāyuga into four equal quarters, and Brahmagupta really did attack this. That shows the yuga lengths were *chosen*. The quarters suited computation and were never a history of the world. |\n| 5 | **Symbolic or psychological readings** (Aurobindo, Vivekananda) | **4** as interpretation, no score as a timeline | Honest about being interpretation and consistent with history. They are modern constructions, not recovered ancient meanings. |\n| 6 | **Classical Purāṇic 4.32M-year scheme, taken literally** | **1** | It reports the tradition accurately but fails as history. Satya and Treta would fall 3.9 to 1.3 million years ago, the era of *Homo habilis* and early *H. erectus*. No material record supports an advanced earlier golden age. |\n| 7 | **Guénon / Traditionalists / Daniélou** | **1–2** | Mostly unfalsifiable. Guénon's 64,800-year manvantara (later developed by Georgel) and Daniélou's Kali ending around 2442 CE (from *While the Gods Play*; the exact arithmetic should be checked against the book) both rest on non-standard calculations. |\n| 8 | **ISKCON 10,000-year golden age within Kali** | **1** | A devotional teaching inside the tradition. It has weak scriptural support (usually traced to a Brahma-vaivarta passage) and makes no claim that can be tested. |\n| 9 | **Yukteswar's 24,000-year cycle (SRF)** | **1** | No pre-modern source has it. The figure 24,000 equals 360° at 54″ per year, an old Indian and Arabic precession rate, while the true rate is about 50.3″ per year (≈25,772 years). His darkest age (701 BCE–499 CE) contains the Axial Age, the Mauryas and Guptas, Pāṇini and Āryabhaṭa. |\n| 10 | **Precession / Hamlet's Mill / \"Great Year\" readings** | **1–2** | Precession is real, but it is a smooth rotation with no four unequal phases. Dividing 4,320,000 by about 25,772 does not give a whole number, so linking them requires arbitrary multipliers. |\n| 11 | **Cruttenden's binary companion star** | **0–1** | IRAS, 2MASS, WISE and Gaia show no such companion. It also inherits Yukteswar's wrong precession figure. |\n\n## The popular contenders, one by one\n\n**Classical Purāṇic (4.32 million years, Kali until about 428,899 CE).** This is the authentic tradition, and as a record of what the texts teach it is accurate. But the familiar \"traditional package\" was put together in layers. The 12,000-year scheme is epic and smṛti material, the ×360 multiplier may be later, and the 3102 BCE epoch was added by astronomers about five centuries after Manu. Taken as history, it conflicts with paleoanthropology, the South Asian archaeological sequence (farming at Mehrgarh around 7000 BCE, Harappan cities 2600–1900 BCE) and ancient DNA.\n\n**Yukteswar (24,000 years, now in an ascending Dvāpara since 1699).** It is attractive because it puts the yugas on a human scale and makes the present feel hopeful. But it is a construction from 1894, it depends on a medieval error about the precession rate, and its own dates contradict the record of human achievement. A common confusion: in his scheme 499 CE is the *lowest point*, where the descending Kali ends, not where it begins.\n\n**Precession / Great Year.** Precession is real. None of the yuga numbers derive from it. The Sūrya Siddhānta treats precession as a back-and-forth oscillation of ±27°, a minor wobble inside the mahāyuga.\n\n**Āryabhaṭa.** He is usually overlooked, but he is the best evidence that the yuga lengths were conventions. He chose equal quarters because that made every quarter boundary, including 3102 BCE, an exact mean conjunction. Brahmagupta restored the 4:3:2:1 ratio for scriptural reasons, not because of observation.\n\n**ISKCON golden age.** It works as a pastoral, devotional framing of a long dark age. It is not a chronology and does not claim to be one in any rigorous sense.\n\n**Guénon and Daniélou.** These are serious works of metaphysics and cultural criticism. Their numbers are idiosyncratic. Their central thesis, that modernity is a decline, is a value judgment. It does not fit measurable trends: life expectancy, literacy and adult height have risen sharply over the last two centuries.\n\n**Binary star.** This is the only contender that makes a clear physical prediction, and observation has ruled it out.\n\n## Uncertainties and what could change the verdict\n\n- **The divine-year question.** If it were shown that the ×360 multiplier belongs to the earliest layer of the Mahābhārata, the 4.32-million figure would move earlier. That would change how the doctrine developed, not whether it is literally true.\n- **Babylonian transmission.** Finding an intermediate text, such as a dated Greek or Sanskrit source using 432,000 in a cosmological sense, would raise Pingree's thesis from plausible to established.\n- **A pre-Āryabhaṭa Kali epoch.** A securely dated inscription or text from before about 400 CE that used the Kali era would push back the date of its invention. It would not make 3102 BCE an observation, because the epoch's internal construction shows it was calculated.\n- **An astronomical anomaly.** A real companion star, or any physical cycle near 24,000 years affecting the Sun, would revive Yukteswar and Cruttenden. Current all-sky surveys make this very unlikely.\n- **Lost oral traditions.** The argument from Vedic silence is strong but not conclusive. Older oral cosmologies may have existed. We cannot date them, so they cannot carry weight in the evidence.\n\n## Closing judgment\n\nThe yuga system deserves respect for what it really is. It is a refined moral cosmology, and it is a feat of mathematical astronomy: a period chosen so that every planetary cycle fits inside it, which allowed eclipse prediction for a thousand years. That achievement is real. What is not real is the idea that the numbers record the length of past ages or predict future ones. The interpretation nearest to reality is the one that explains how ancient Indians *built* these timelines, from dice-game vocabulary, ritual calendars, sacred arithmetic and back-calculated astronomy.","generated":"2026-10-20","addendum":"## Addendum: Modern gurus, sects, prophecies and internet interpretations\n\nThis addendum covers 40 further entries. Many were compiled from background knowledge because web search was blocked. Claims a source could not support are marked *unverified*.\n\n### Corrections made to the source entries\n\n- **Yukteswar dates.** *Descending* Kali ran from 701 BCE to 499 CE. *Ascending* Kali ran from 499 to 1699 CE, and ascending Dvapara runs from 1699 to 4099 CE. The viral-claims entry wrongly said ascending Kali \"ended around 499 CE\".\n- **Samael Aun Weor.** 3102 BCE plus 432,000 years is about 428,899 CE, not \"428 CE\".\n- **Steiner.** Steiner used a 5,000-year Kali Yuga running from 3101 BCE, which puts its end in 1899. Wikipedia's \"1900\" is a rounding. The 5,000-year idea comes from Blavatsky, who wrote in *The Secret Doctrine* that \"the first 5,000 years\" of Kali would end in 1897-98.\n- **Mirza Ghulam Ahmad.** Ahmadis count the Dowie prophecy as *fulfilled*, because Dowie died in 1907 and Ahmad in 1908. It is not an agreed failure.\n- **Sathya Sai Baba.** He said he would *live* to 96, and that Prema Sai would come about eight years after his death. He did not say he would be \"reborn at 96\". H. Narasimhaiah was a rationalist critic who led the 1976 Bangalore University inquiry, not a devotee biographer.\n- **Savitri Devi.** She treated Hitler as an avatar-like \"man against time\", a forerunner. She did not treat him as Kalki. In *The Lightning and the Sun* (1958), Kalki is still to come.\n- **Kota Venkatachalam (1885-1959)** can be identified. He wrote *The Plot in Indian Chronology* (1953) and *Age of Buddha, Milinda and Amtiyoka* (1956). He dated the Buddha to 1887-1807 BCE and the Mahabharata war to 3138 BCE.\n- **Kalki Dham.** It was widely reported that PM Modi laid the foundation stone on 19 February 2024.\n- **Sarkar.** His cycle runs Shudra → Kshatriya → Vipra → Vaishya, then back to Shudra.\n- **TM.** The \"Maharishi Effect\" claim is 1% of a population for TM and √1% for TM-Sidhi. The Age of Enlightenment was proclaimed on 12 January 1975.\n- **Gayatri Pariwar.** The organisation is the All World (Akhil Vishwa) Gayatri Pariwar, and its slogan is *\"Ikkisvin sadi, ujjwal bhavishya\"* (\"the 21st century, a bright future\").\n- **Gohar Shahi.** His 2026 comet prediction has not *yet* failed, since the year is not over. No catalogued object is on a collision course.\n- **Younger Dryas impact hypothesis.** It is disputed and a minority view, not refuted outright. The \"lost civilisation\" claim has no evidence at all.\n\n### 1. Yukteswar-derived 24,000-year lineage (Ananda/Selbie-Steinmetz, Sadhguru/Isha; Hancock-adjacent)\n\n**Claim.** The ages repeat in a 24,000-year cycle of ascending and descending halves. Ascending Dvapara began in 1699 and humanity's consciousness is rising. Selbie and Steinmetz (*The Yugas*, 2010) match this to archaeology.\n\n**Sadhguru/Isha.** Sadhguru is popularly associated with the \"ascending Dvapara\" framing. No Sadhguru transcript or Isha article was retrieved, so his exact wording and dates are unverified.\n\n**Origin.** Yukteswar's *The Holy Science* (1894). The scheme rests on an imagined \"dual sun\" and a precession rate of 54″/yr, while the true rate is about 50.3″/yr (a period of about 25,772 years).\n\n**Checkable?** Partly. Its astronomy is wrong, and the 499/1699 turning points match no historical break. Hancock and Carlson link yuga-style decline to the Younger Dryas (about 10,900 BCE), but Hindu sources make no such link.\n\n### 2. Short-cycle schemes (Brahma Kumaris; Blavatsky/Steiner)\n\n**Brahma Kumaris.** They teach an identical, endlessly repeating 5,000-year cycle of four 1,250-year ages. The present is a \"Confluence Age\". According to Walliss (2002), as reported by Wikipedia, destruction was expected in 1976 and then between 1987 and 2008. The movement later reframed destruction as \"transformation\". Its current position could not be confirmed from brahmakumaris.org.\n\n**Checkable?** Yes, and it fails. Göbekli Tepe (about 9500 BCE) and Jericho are radiocarbon-dated to well before any 5,000-year window.\n\n**Blavatsky/Steiner.** Their \"first 5,000 years of Kali ended in 1897-99\" is a sub-period claim, not a forecast. It is probably the ancestor of today's \"5,000-year Kali\" memes.\n\n### 3. \"Golden age has begun / Satyug is coming\" movements\n\n- **TM.** The Age of Enlightenment was proclaimed in 1975, with the Maharishi Effect as its mechanism. The 1993 Washington DC project predicted a drop in violent crime of more than 20%. Homicides rose, and only the movement's own model-adjusted analysis claims success.\n- **Sahaja Yoga.** By the group's own tradition, Shri Mataji \"opened the Sahasrara\" at Nargol on 5 May 1970. Its literature links this to Krita Yuga and a \"Last Judgment\" (*unverified* against primary texts). Wikipedia describes the present as late Kali Yuga.\n- **Kalki Bhagavan/Oneness.** A Golden Age through the \"Oneness Blessing\", tied to 2012.\n- **Jai Gurudev (d. 2012).** The undated slogan \"Satyug aayega\", painted on walls across North India.\n- **Gayatri Pariwar.** \"Yug parivartan\" (era change) and the 21st-century slogan. No dated Satyug start was verified.\n- **Sathya Sai.** A golden age of love, with the Prema Sai successor.\n- **Satyug Darshan Trust (Faridabad).** No source found.\n\n**Checkable?** Only the dated parts are, and those failed. The undated parts cannot be tested.\n\n### 4. Kalki claimants\n\nThis group covers Kalki Bhagavan, Mirza Ghulam Ahmad (as reported by Juergensmeyer), Bahá'u'lláh (identified as Kalki by some Bahá'í apologists, not as official doctrine), Gohar Shahi/MFI, Samael Aun Weor (\"avatar of Aquarius\", with the age dated 4 February 1962), and Rampal (the \"Tatvadarshi Saint\"). Rampal's group says 5,505 years of Kali had passed by 1997, which does not fit the 3102 BCE start. Rampal was convicted of murder in 2018. The reported 2025-26 bail and sentence suspension were not independently checked.\n\n**Pattern.** Each claimant reads Kalki's sword-and-horse figure as metaphor. The Kali clock is then either ignored or reset.\n\n### 5. Regional prophecy (Bhavishya Malika, Veerabrahmendra Kalagnanam, Ayyavazhi)\n\n**Bhavishya Malika (Odisha, 16th century).** Modern promoters read it as about 5,000 years of Kali, with Satya Yuga by 2032. They map it onto events such as Saturn entering Pisces (29 March 2025), the Pahalgam attack (22 April 2025) and the 12 August 2026 eclipse. These readings were made after the events, and the textual basis for any date is unclear.\n\n**Kalagnanam.** Claims that it names Gandhi and Indira are unsupported by any critical edition. No dated end of Kali was found.\n\n**Ayyavazhi.** It counts eight yugas, with Dharma Yukam to follow Kali. Its yugas are theological and undated.\n\n**Checkable?** Mostly not. Where readings are datable, the fit is made retroactively.\n\n### 6. Alternative chronologists (Oak, Bala/I-SERVE, Venkatachalam, Abhyankar)\n\n- **Nilesh Oak.** Mahabharata war in 5561 BCE and Ramayana in 12209 BCE, from astronomical readings of epic verses.\n- **I-SERVE.** Rama's birth in 5114 BCE, a date first popularised by Pushkar Bhatnagar using planetarium software. Mahabharata war in about 3139 BCE.\n- **Venkatachalam.** Buddha in 1887 BCE.\n- **Abhyankar (1993).** A planetary alignment on Mohenjo-daro seals marks the start of Kali (*unverified*).\n\n**Checkable?** Yes, and that is to their credit. The tests go against them:\n- Ashoka's edicts name Antiochus II, Ptolemy II and others, which fixes Ashoka in the 3rd century BCE.\n- Archaeology shows no epic-scale polities in the 6th to 4th millennia BCE.\n- Matching verses to the sky is underdetermined.\n- Using 3102 BCE as a check is circular, because it is itself a back-calculation.\n- In 3102 BCE the planets were spread over tens of degrees, not conjoined.\n\n### 7. \"Kali ends 2025\" internet claims\n\nThese treat the elapsed count (Kali year 5126 fell in 2024-25) as if it were the age's length. Sometimes they mix in the Brahma Vaivarta Purana's \"10,000-year golden age within Kali\", Yukteswar or the 2012 hype. 2025 has passed. Popular kathakars (Premanand, Dhirendra Shastri and others) generally keep the 432,000-year frame and give no end date.\n\n### 8. Pop culture, and traditional teaching without dates\n\n*Kalki 2898 AD* (2024) uses a fictional date about 6,000 years after Kurukshetra. Praveen Mohan and Project Shivoham could not be checked.\n\nSwaminarayan/BAPS, Sant Mat, Art of Living, Dada Bhagwan, Osho and the Kalki Dham project use the yugas as a moral or devotional frame and give no dates.\n\n**Dashrathbhai Patel (Gujarat).** He could not be identified. No source links that name to any yuga teaching, and nothing is attributed to him here. Do not confuse him with Dada Bhagwan (A. M. Patel), whose Akram Vignan uses the idea of a *dushamkaal* (bad era) but no chronology. A primary source (book, ashram or video) is needed.\n\n### 9. Political and meme usage\n\nSavitri Devi and Miguel Serrano made \"Kali Yuga\" a vehicle for esoteric Nazism. Evola's *Ride the Tiger* (1961) carried the phrase into online reactionary and \"doomer\" culture. Crypto and meme posts use it ironically. None of this is chronology. It is rhetoric, and in Devi's and Serrano's case it is racist ideology.\n\n### Failed and pending dated predictions\n\n| Date | Source | Prediction | Outcome |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1886-92 | Mirza Ghulam Ahmad | Marriage to Muhammadi Begum; her husband to die within about 2.5 years | She married someone else; he lived to 1949. Ahmadis call the prophecy conditional |\n| Sept 1894 | Ahmad | Abdullah Atham to die within 15 months | Atham died in 1896 (contested as conditional) |\n| 1902 | Ahmad | Smyth-Pigott to be destroyed | Pigott lived to 1927 (cited by critics) |\n| 1968-69 | Edgar Cayce | Atlantis to rise near Bimini | Did not happen; the \"Bimini Road\" is natural beachrock |\n| 1976 | Brahma Kumaris (per Walliss) | World destruction | Did not happen |\n| 1987-2008 | Brahma Kumaris (per Walliss) | Violent destruction | Did not happen; reframed as \"transformation\" |\n| before 1978 | Samael Aun Weor (1972) | His death and resurrection | Died 24 December 1977; no resurrection |\n| 1984-1999 | Osho | AIDS to kill two-thirds of humanity; nuclear catastrophe in the 1990s | Did not happen |\n| June-July 1993 | TM (Hagelin) | Violent crime in DC to fall by more than 20% | Homicides rose; claimed success relies on the movement's own model |\n| 21 Dec 2012 | Kalki Bhagavan/Oneness; 2012 milieu | Golden Age, end of Kali | No observable change |\n| 2011 | Sathya Sai Baba | To live to 96 | Died at 84 |\n| ~2019 onward | Sathya Sai Baba | Prema Sai born about eight years after his death | No verified successor |\n| June 2021 | Nithyananda | COVID to end after his conditions were met | Did not happen |\n| 2024-25 | Viral posts | Kali Yuga ends, Satya begins | Passed |\n| 2026 | Gohar Shahi | Comet ends the world | Pending; no impactor known (as of October 2026) |\n\nUnverified (not tabled): Bhavishya Malika preachers' war dates for the 2020s, and Guénonian end-dates (Robin, Georgel).\n\n### Ranking: closeness to reality\n\n| Rank | Family | Score /10 | Reason |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Undated traditional and devotional use (Swaminarayan, Sant Mat, kathakars, Art of Living, Kalki Dham) | 3 | Makes no false dates; inherits the computed 4.32M-year scheme as theology |\n| 2 | Sarkar's social cycle | 3 | Openly reinterpretive and sociological; cannot be tested |\n| 3 | Alternative chronologists | 2 | Their claims can be tested, but the evidence goes against them |\n| 4 | Yukteswar lineage, incl. Sadhguru and Selbie | 2 | Consistent within itself, but rests on wrong astronomy |\n| 5 | Pop culture and memes | 2 | Mostly makes no literal claim |\n| 6 | Regional prophecy | 1 | Fitted to events after they happen; no textual dates |\n| 7 | \"Golden age has begun\" movements | 1 | Dated parts failed; the rest cannot be tested |\n| 8 | Short cycles (Brahma Kumaris, viral 2025) | 1 | Can be checked, and archaeology and the calendar refute them |\n| 9 | Kalki claimants | 1 | Reinterpret the prophecy to fit the claimant; several dated misses |\n| 10 | Esoteric-political (Devi, Serrano) | 0 | Ideology, not chronology |\n\n### Does any of this change the overall verdict?\n\nNo. None of the 40 entries brings new evidence for a literal yuga chronology. The two families that make claims you can test are the alternative chronologists and the short-cycle and dated-prophecy groups. Both fail against epigraphy, radiocarbon dating and the calendar.\n\nThe Yukteswar lineage, including Sadhguru and Selbie, adds popularity but no new data, and still depends on the 54″/yr error. The \"golden age\" movements show a recurring pattern: they announce a dawn, the dated test fails, and they reframe it as inner transformation. That pattern is better evidence about how religious movements behave than about time.\n\nThe most defensible modern uses are the ones that admit the yugas are a moral frame. That fits the historical-developmental account, which remains the nearest to reality: a dice-term vocabulary, then the Vedanga calendar, then the 4:3:2:1 scheme, then a computational 4.32-million-year period with an epoch back-calculated to 3102 BCE.","families":[{"id":"classical-puranic","title":"Classical Puranic yuga model (4,320,000-year Mahayuga, 4:3:2:1, divine years x360)","category":"Traditional scriptural","members":["puranic-classical","manusmriti","mahabharata","bhagavata","vayu-brahmanda-linga","sandhya","calendar-practice","kali-characteristics","hindi-youtube-preachers"],"summary":"The classical scheme divides a Mahayuga of 12,000 divine years into Satya (Krita), Treta, Dvapara and Kali in a 4:3:2:1 ratio, each yuga including a dawn (sandhya) and dusk (sandhyamsha) margin of one tenth of its core length on each side. One divine year (a day and night of the gods, Manu 1.67) equals 360 human years, giving 4,320,000 human years per Mahayuga and 432,000 for Kali. The same arithmetic appears in the Manusmriti, Mahabharata (Vana Parva), Vishnu, Bhagavata, Vayu, Brahmanda and Linga Puranas, and in Siddhantic astronomy (Surya Siddhanta, Aryabhatiya), where the figure works as a mean-motion framework giving integer planetary revolutions. The scheme is presented as progressive moral decline (dharma losing one leg per yuga), with Kalki restoring dharma at the end of Kali. None of the scriptural texts gives a calendar date for Kali's start; the 3102 BCE epoch (17/18 February, midnight) comes from Aryabhata-era and Siddhantic astronomy, and is tied by tradition to Krishna's departure. It is used in practice as the Kali Samvat count (CE year + 3101) in almanacs and the ritual sankalpa. Popular preachers repeat the framework as a moral and devotional frame rather than giving an end date.","totalCycleYears":"4,320,000 human years (12,000 divine years x 360); Sandhya entry notes the four core yugas alone sum to 4,000,000 years","durations":{"satya":"1,728,000 human years (4,800 divine years including margins; core 4,000)","treta":"1,296,000 human years (3,600 divine years; core 3,000)","dvapara":"864,000 human years (2,400 divine years; core 2,000)","kali":"432,000 human years (1,200 divine years; core 1,000)"},"kaliStart":"3102 BCE (17/18 February, conventional epoch fixed by Aryabhata and the Siddhantas; not given in the scriptural texts themselves)","kaliEnd":"About 428,898-428,900 CE (3102 BCE + 432,000 years); if the 36,000-year Kali sandhyamsha is added beyond the epoch, about 464,898 CE (disputed)","currentPosition":"2026 CE is Kali Samvat 5127 (CE + 3101; members variously say 5,127 or 5,128 years elapsed), about 1.2 percent of Kali, with roughly 426,870 years remaining. Texts read the present as the most degenerate phase.","variants":[{"memberId":"puranic-classical","title":"Classical Puranic model: 4,320,000-year Mahayuga, 4:3:2:1 ratio, divine years x360","distinctive":"Baseline synthesis. Aryabhata states 3,600 Kali years had elapsed in 499 CE, fixing the epoch by back-calculation. Cites Wilson (1840), Pargiter (1922), Plofker (2009); notes the Vishnu Purana's present form is post-Gupta and that the 1,000-Mahayuga Kalpa (4.32 billion years) follows in later texts."},{"memberId":"manusmriti","title":"Manusmriti Chapter 1: Yugas in Divine Years (12,000 divine years)","distinctive":"Manu 1.67 gives the day-of-the-gods equivalence (the source of the x360 multiplier); Manu 1.71 the 12,000 divine-year total. Composed c. 200 BCE-200 CE (Olivelle); a thousand Mahayugas make a day of Brahma. No epoch is given; debated whether the 360 conversion is original or a later clarification."},{"memberId":"mahabharata","title":"Mahabharata accounts of the yugas (Markandeya, Bhishma, Hanuman-Bhima)","distinctive":"Markandeya's Vana Parva discourse (approx. ch. 149-150, 188-190) gives the 4000/3000/2000/1000 cores with 400/300/200/100 twilights and the Kalki prophecy; Bhishma (Shanti Parva) and Hanuman-Bhima (Vana Parva) treat the ages morally. Mausala Parva portents after Krishna's death are read as Kali's onset. Yuga passages are later didactic layers (Biardeau, Hiltebeitel); the epic gives no date for Kurukshetra or Kali."},{"memberId":"bhagavata","title":"Bhagavata Purana: Yuga durations and the Kali Yuga decline (Skandha 12)","distinctive":"Dated c. 6th-10th century CE, so Kali-decline passages may be ex eventu. Dharma as a bull losing a leg per yuga (1.17.24); Kalki born to Vishnuyasha at Shambhala (12.2); chanting Hari's name is Kali's one great merit (12.3.51-52). Kali tied to Krishna's departure (1.15-1.16). Cited by Gaudiya/ISKCON commentators (Prabhupada, Tagare)."},{"memberId":"vayu-brahmanda-linga","title":"Vayu, Brahmanda and Linga Purana yuga accounts and their textual variants","distinctive":"Vayu (c. 4th-6th c.) and Brahmanda (c. 5th-7th c.) share a near-identical block; Linga (c. 7th-10th c.) differs in wording and sandhya figures across manuscripts. Gives sandhya as 800/600/400/200 divine years combined. Some scholars suggest Mesopotamian or Greek influence on the 12,000-year structure (contested)."},{"memberId":"sandhya","title":"Sandhya and sandhyamsha transitional periods and end-date disputes","distinctive":"Texts are unclear whether 3102 BCE starts the sandhya or Kali proper; counting the 36,000-year Kali sandhyamsha moves the end to about 464,898 CE. Sri Yukteswar Giri (Holy Science, 1894) reinterpreted yuga arithmetic into a roughly 24,000-year cycle, and some popular writers use sandhya arithmetic to argue Kali ends early."},{"memberId":"calendar-practice","title":"Kali era in practical panchanga reckoning (Kali Samvat 5127)","distinctive":"Practical use: Kali year = CE + 3101 (2026 = 5127; Vikram Samvat about 2083, Saka 1948); sankalpa formula in ritual. Epoch is around 17/18 February 3102 BCE (Julian). Documented by Sewell and Dikshit (1896), Pingree. Year-start varies by tradition (Chaitra, Mesha, Kartika). Saka was adopted as national civil calendar (Saha committee, 1955). Claimed planetary conjunction at the epoch is not reproduced by modern software."},{"memberId":"kali-characteristics","title":"Textual Kali Yuga lakshanas (decline markers) compared with modern claims","distinctive":"Lists of lakshanas (wealth as the basis of rank, predatory rulers, shrinking lifespan and stature, irregular seasons; Vishnu Purana IV.24, Bhagavata XII.2-3, Manu 1.81-86) are read by modern writers as a checklist for the present, with climate change and corruption cited as matches. Measurable claims (lifespan, stature) are contradicted by data; similar complaints appear in Vedic, Buddhist, Jain and Greek texts."},{"memberId":"hindi-youtube-preachers","title":"Popular Hindi YouTube preachers on Kali Yuga duration and end (Premanand Maharaj, Aniruddhacharya, Dhirendra Shastri, Pradeep Mishra)","distinctive":"Four popular speakers repeat the standard framework (432,000 years from 3102 BCE) as a moral frame; none was found giving a dated end of Kali and no failed predictions are documented. Dhirendra Shastri (Bageshwar Dham) added a political call for a Hindu Rashtra (Jan 2023) and the Ramcharitmanas as national scripture, and was challenged by Shyam Manav (Nagpur Police clean chit, 25 Jan 2023). Premanand Maharaj's controversies (Nov 2024, July 2025) are unrelated to chronology. Claims on Aniruddhacharya and Pradeep Mishra were not checked."}],"keyClaims":["The four yugas last 4,800, 3,600, 2,400 and 1,200 divine years including dawn and dusk, a 4:3:2:1 ratio totalling 12,000 divine years","One divine year equals 360 human years (Manu 1.67), so the Mahayuga is 4,320,000 human years and Kali 432,000","Kali began in 3102 BCE (tradition links it to Krishna's departure) and is the present, most degenerate age with dharma on one leg or one quarter","Dharma, virtue and lifespan decline across the yugas; Kali is marked by wealth-based status, predatory rulers and weakened ritual","Kalki (son of Vishnuyasha, Shambhala) will restore dharma at the end of Kali; chanting the divine name is Kali's one merit (Bhagavata)","The cycle repeats within a Kalpa of 1,000 Mahayugas (4.32 billion years)","The Kali era is a living reckoning: Kali Samvat = CE + 3101, used in almanacs and the ritual sankalpa","Sandhya/sandhyamsha periods can be counted inside or outside the epoch, shifting Kali's notional end by tens of thousands of years"],"evidence":["Internal consistency: the 4:3:2:1 ratio and x360 conversion sum exactly to 12,000 divine years and 4.32 million years across Manusmriti, Mahabharata, the Puranas and Siddhantic works","The 3102 BCE epoch appears in the Aryabhatiya and later Siddhantic texts; Aryabhata states 3,600 Kali years had elapsed in 499 CE","Siddhantic astronomers used 4,320,000 as a mean-motion period yielding integer planetary revolutions, so it has real computational function","Parallel passages (Vayu/Brahmanda, Manu 1.69-71, Mahabharata 3.149, 3.188) show a shared, stable tradition attested over more than a millennium","Markandeya's Vana Parva discourse and the Mausala Parva portents are the epic's own markers for the age structure and Kali's onset","Sankalpa formulas and almanacs (Sewell and Dikshit 1896) confirm continuous ritual and calendrical use","Wikipedia pages on Yuga, Premanand Govind Sharan and Dhirendra Krishna Shastri were fetched for the preachers entry; speakers' own recordings were not reviewed"],"commonCriticisms":["no-archaeology","epoch-back-calculated","computational-numbers","late-origin","unfalsifiable","decline-vs-trends","retrofitting","unverified-sources"],"specificCriticisms":["Texts disagree on details: whether sandhya margins are included, how yugas map to lifespan, and sandhya counts across Vayu, Brahmanda and Linga manuscripts, which weakens literal readings","The mahayuga is not a chronology and ties to no dated event; historians (Plofker and Indologists) treat it as theology and calendrics","The moral-decline framing is normative and has been used to justify social hierarchy","Kali Samvat is not the default civil or regional reckoning, and its year start varies by tradition, making 'Kali year 5127' ambiguous near New Year","Sandhya-based end-date arguments (Yukteswar and popular writers) rely on selective start points and are not supported by the Puranic arithmetic they cite","Modern Kali-checklist matching is confirmation bias; the lists are generic complaints found in many eras","Preacher claims are mostly political or opinion rather than chronology; reported controversies (Shastri's Hindu Rashtra call, Premanand's remarks) are not validation of the timeline","Some members were written from memory with verse numbers unchecked against critical editions"],"plausibility":1,"plausibilityRange":"1-2","plausibilityReason":"As a literal timeline the scheme has no independent support: the 4.32-million-year cycle, the 3102 BCE start and steady moral decline are symbolic or calendrical conventions. Members scored 2 only for textual fidelity or calendrical usefulness (Vayu/Brahmanda/Linga, Sandhya, Calendar, Kali-characteristics); its value lies in the history of ideas and computational astronomy.","verification":"Partly verified","references":["Wilson, H.H. (1840) translation of the Vishnu Purana","Pargiter, F.E. (1922) Ancient Indian Historical Tradition, Oxford University Press","Plofker, Kim (2009) Mathematics in India, Princeton University Press","Olivelle, Patrick (2004/2005) Manu's Code of Law, Oxford University Press","Bühler, Georg (1886) The Laws of Manu, Sacred Books of the East vol. 25","Doniger, Wendy and Brian K. Smith (1991) The Laws of Manu, Penguin Classics","Doniger, Wendy (ed.) Hindu Myths (Penguin, 1975)","Kane, P.V. History of Dharmasastra (1930-1962), vols. II and IV","Aryabhatiya of Aryabhata (499 CE), with Clark's translation (1930)","Burgess, E. (1860) Surya Siddhanta translation","Ganguli, K.M. (trans.) The Mahabharata, 1883-1896 (https://www.sacred-texts.com/hin/m03/index.htm and https://www.sacred-texts.com/hin/m12/index.htm)","van Buitenen, J.A.B. (trans.) The Mahabharata (University of Chicago Press, 1973-1978); Sukthankar et al., Critical Edition (BORI, 1933-1966)","Biardeau, Madeleine, work on the Mahabharata (1976-1978 and later); Hiltebeitel, Alf. The Ritual of Battle (Cornell, 1976)","Bhagavata Purana, Skandhas 1, 3, 11, 12; Tagare (1976-1978) and Prabhupada (1972-1977) translations","Bryant, Edwin F. Krishna: A Sourcebook (OUP, 2007); Doniger (ed.) Purana Perennis (SUNY, 1993); Rocher, The Puranas (Harrassowitz, 1986)","Hazra, R.C. (1958) Studies in the Upapuranas; Bentley, J. (1825) A Historical View of the Hindu Astronomy","Pingree, D. Census of the Exact Sciences in Sanskrit (APS, 1970-1994); Jyotihsastra (Harrassowitz, 1981)","Sewell, R. and Dikshit, S.B. (1896) The Indian Calendar; Indian Calendar Reform Committee Report (1955)","Sri Yukteswar Giri (1894) The Holy Science","Flood, Gavin (1996) An Introduction to Hinduism, Cambridge","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yuga","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Premanand_Govind_Sharan","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dhirendra_Krishna_Shastri"]},{"id":"cosmic-scale","title":"Cosmic-scale time: Kalpa, Manvantara, Brahma's life and comparison with modern science","category":"Traditional scriptural","members":["kalpa","manvantara","arya-samaj","science-cosmology"],"summary":"These entries extend the 4,320,000-year Mahayuga (Satya 1,728,000, Treta 1,296,000, Dvapara 864,000, Kali 432,000 years) to larger Puranic and Siddhantic units. A Kalpa is 1,000 Mahayugas (4.32 billion years), one day of Brahma, and contains 14 manvantaras of 71 Mahayugas (306,720,000 years) plus 15 sandhis, each equal to one Satya Yuga; 14 x 71 + 15 x 0.4 = 1,000. Brahma lives 100 years of 360 such days, about 311.04 trillion years. The traditional count places us in the 51st year of Brahma, first kalpa (Shvetavaraha), 7th (Vaivasvata) manvantara, 28th Mahayuga, in Kali Yuga (3102 BCE start), about 1.97 billion years into the kalpa. Dayananda Saraswati's Arya Samaj adopts the yuga arithmetic with a Srishti Samvat creation age of about 1,96,08,53,125 years. Compared with science, the 4.32 Gyr kalpa is within about 5 percent of Earth's age (4.54 Gyr) but this is coincidental, since the numbers derive from planetary-cycle arithmetic and decimal construction, and Brahma's life exceeds the universe's age (13.8 Gyr) by about 22,000-22,500 times.","totalCycleYears":"Mahayuga 4,320,000 years; manvantara 306,720,000; Kalpa 4,320,000,000; Brahma's life 311,040,000,000,000 (about 311 trillion). Arya Samaj creation age about 1.96 billion years. Members differ on whether yuga figures are human solar years (Surya Siddhanta) or divine years x360 (Manusmriti), which yield the same totals.","durations":{"satya":"1,728,000 human years (4,800 divine years)","treta":"1,296,000 human years (3,600 divine years)","dvapara":"864,000 human years (2,400 divine years)","kali":"432,000 human years (1,200 divine years); one divine year = 360 human years"},"kaliStart":"3102 BCE (traditional epoch, about 18 February, from Aryabhata and Surya Siddhanta-based almanacs)","kaliEnd":"About 428,898-428,899 CE (432,000 years after the epoch)","currentPosition":"About 5,127-5,128 years into Kali in 2026 CE (about 1.2 percent); 28th Mahayuga of the 7th (Vaivasvata) manvantara (27 Mahayugas elapsed), first kalpa of the 51st Brahma-year, about 1.97 billion years of the current kalpa elapsed (sankalpa count, varies by almanac). Arya Samaj calendars cite Kali Samvat about 5126-5127 in 2025-26.","variants":[{"memberId":"kalpa","title":"Kalpa / Day of Brahma: 4.32 billion-year days and a 311-trillion-year lifespan","distinctive":"Defines Kalpa and Brahma's 100-year life; places us in the first kalpa of the 51st Brahma-year (Shvetavaraha Kalpa). Sources: Manusmriti Ch. 1, Vishnu Purana Bk 1, Bhagavata Bk 3, Surya Siddhanta, Aryabhatiya, Brahmagupta's Brahmasphutasiddhanta (628 CE). Notes some Vedic-science apologists cite the figure as ancient knowledge; the Kalpa's match to Earth's age is not evidence."},{"memberId":"manvantara","title":"Manvantara system: 14 Manus, 71 mahayugas each, sandhis","distinctive":"Lists the 14 Manus (Svayambhuva, Svarocisha, Uttama, Tamasa, Raivata, Chakshusha, Vaivasvata, Savarni, Daksha-savarni, Brahma-savarni, Dharma-savarni, Rudra-savarni, Deva-savarni, Indra-savarni) and the 14 x 71 + 15 x 0.4 = 1,000 arithmetic. Reports disputes over elapsed Mahayugas (27 or 28), over the Vaivasvata manvantara's position (about 120,000 years in some reckonings vs 1.95-1.97 billion years since kalpa start in others), and over tying Kali to the Mahabharata war and Parikshit versus 3102 BCE. Commentary: Kullūka Bhaṭṭa (15th c.)."},{"memberId":"arya-samaj","title":"Dayananda Saraswati and Arya Samaj: Yuga chronology and the 1.96 billion year creation age","distinctive":"Dayananda (1824-1883) held the Vedas infallible, rejected Puranas and idol worship, but kept the Surya Siddhanta-style yuga arithmetic; Arya Samaj founded 10 April 1875 in Bombay. Satyarth Prakash (1875, rev. 1884), Ch. 8, is the claimed source of the Srishti Samvat of about 1,96,08,53,125 years; this figure and the yuga durations are unverified recall and the figure was not found in the fetched Wikipedia pages. Claims the Vedas anticipated modern science; the Vedas themselves contain no such chronology."},{"memberId":"science-cosmology","title":"Comparing kalpa and yuga timescales with modern geology and cosmology","distinctive":"Compares the Kalpa to Earth's age (4.54 Gyr, Patterson 1956; difference about 0.22 Gyr, about 5 percent) and universe age (13.787 Gyr, Planck 2018). Oldest rocks: Acasta gneiss about 4.0 Gyr; Jack Hills zircons about 4.4 Gyr; Phanerozoic (541 Myr) far exceeds any yuga. Argues category mismatch, since kalpa is one cycle in a beginningless cosmos with pralaya, not an age of Earth, and notes selection bias by apologists."}],"keyClaims":["A Kalpa (day of Brahma) is 1,000 Mahayugas, 4.32 billion years; Brahma lives 100 years of 360 days, about 311.04 trillion years","A Kalpa contains 14 manvantaras of 71 Mahayugas (306,720,000 years) and 15 sandhis of one Satya Yuga each","We are in the 51st Brahma-year, 7th (Vaivasvata) manvantara, 28th Mahayuga, Kali Yuga","Kali began in 3102 BCE and lasts 432,000 years","Arya Samaj: creation (Srishti Samvat) is about 1.96 billion years old, the Vedas are infallible, and modern science was anticipated in them","Popular claim: the 4.32 billion-year kalpa matches Earth's 4.54 billion-year age, taken as evidence of ancient precise knowledge","Yuga figures are stated as divine years x360 in Manusmriti and as human solar years in the Surya Siddhanta and most Puranic presentations"],"evidence":["Manusmriti 1.64-86 (also 1.79) gives the 12,000 divine-year Mahayuga, the 1,000-Mahayuga kalpa and the manvantara structure; Vishnu Purana 1.3 and 3.3 agree","Surya Siddhanta and Aryabhatiya (499 CE) use the 4,320,000-year Mahayuga and the 3102 BCE epoch; Brahmagupta (628 CE) and later almanacs retain the 4.32 billion-year kalpa","Internal arithmetic is exact (14 x 71 = 994 plus 6 from sandhis = 1,000)","Pingree's Census of the Exact Sciences in Sanskrit documents the Siddhantic mahayuga and kalpa arithmetic","Almanac sankalpa formulas recite elapsed kalpa, manvantara and yuga counts, showing continuous ritual use","Earth age 4.54 Gyr from meteorite lead isotopes (Patterson 1956); universe 13.787 Gyr (Planck 2018)","Wikipedia (Arya Samaj, Kali Yuga, Yuga) confirms the 1875 founding, Vedic authority, rejection of Puranas and the 3102 BCE start"],"commonCriticisms":["no-archaeology","epoch-back-calculated","computational-numbers","late-origin","retrofitting","unverified-sources"],"specificCriticisms":["No geological, astronomical or archaeological evidence supports a 306.7-million-year manvantara or 4.32-billion-year kalpa; the scale is mythic","The kalpa/Earth-age resemblance (4.32 vs 4.54 Gyr, about 5 percent off) is coincidental, circular to map, and a category mismatch, since the kalpa is a repeating cycle and not the age of Earth","Brahma's 311-trillion-year life is about 22,000-22,500 times the age of the universe; the kalpa does not match the universe either","Sources conflict on divine vs human years, on elapsed Mahayugas (27 or 28), and on where the Vaivasvata manvantara falls, making the chronology internally inconsistent","The Arya Samaj creation age (about 1.96 billion years) conflicts with radiometric dating and cosmology; the Vedas do not contain it, it is borrowed from the Puranic/Siddhantic scheme Dayananda otherwise rejects, and its derivation differs from the Kali count","Apologist claims that the scheme encodes precession or planetary knowledge are contested; the cosmic-cycle numbers were not derived from observations","Kali's tie to the Mahabharata war and Parikshit is not reconciled with 3102 BCE and is used selectively","Apologists pick the Earth-age match and ignore failed comparisons such as the universe age"],"plausibility":1,"plausibilityRange":"1-2","plausibilityReason":"As literal cosmic or human history the Kalpa and Manvantara scheme has no support and conflicts with radiometric dating, cosmology and archaeology; its numbers are internally consistent symbolic and calendrical arithmetic. The Arya Samaj and science-comparison members scored 2 for scriptural coherence and the real but coincidental Earth-age match.","verification":"Web-checked","references":["Manusmriti Ch. 1 (Olivelle, Oxford, 2005; Doniger and Smith, Penguin, 1991)","Wilson, H.H. (1840) Vishnu Purana, Books 1 and 3","Bhagavata Purana, Books 3 and 8 (A.C. Bhaktivedanta translation or critical edition)","Burgess, E. (1860) Surya Siddhanta, Journal of the American Oriental Society 6","Aryabhatiya of Aryabhata, 499 CE (Clark translation, 1930)","Brahmasphutasiddhanta of Brahmagupta (Colebrooke translation, 1817)","Pingree, D. Census of the Exact Sciences in Sanskrit (APS, 1970-1994); Jyotihsastra (Harrassowitz, 1981); 'History of Mathematical Astronomy in India', Dictionary of Scientific Biography vol. 15 (1978)","Plofker, K. (2009) Mathematics in India, Princeton University Press","Patterson, C. (1956) Age of meteorites and the earth, Geochimica et Cosmochimica Acta 10","Planck Collaboration (2020) Planck 2018 results VI, Astronomy and Astrophysics 641, A6","Dayananda Saraswati, Satyarth Prakash (1875, rev. 1884), Ch. 8","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arya_Samaj","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Satyarth_Prakash","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dayananda_Saraswati","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kali_Yuga","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yuga"]},{"id":"siddhantic-astronomy","title":"Siddhantic astronomy: mahayuga arithmetic and the computed 3102 BCE Kali epoch","category":"Astronomical/Siddhantic","members":["surya-siddhanta","aryabhata","brahmagupta","kerala-school","kali-3102","bailly-bentley-burgess","inscriptions","numerology"],"summary":"In the Siddhantic astronomical tradition (Surya Siddhanta, Aryabhata, Brahmagupta, the Kerala school) the yuga is a computational device: a 4,320,000-year mahayuga (12,000 divine years of 360 years each) used to derive planetary mean motions from a zero point, the Kali epoch of midnight 17/18 February 3102 BCE (proleptic Julian). The usual 4:3:2:1 split gives Satya 1,728,000, Treta 1,296,000, Dvapara 864,000 and Kali 432,000 years; 1,000 mahayugas make a kalpa of 4.32 billion years. The 'all planets at 0 Aries' condition at the epoch is built into the model's mean-motion parameters, and modern ephemerides show no real seven-planet conjunction in 3102 BCE. The epoch is first used explicitly in the Aryabhatiya (499 CE) and in inscriptions from the Aihole record (634 CE); the 19th-century debate (Bailly vs Bentley, Burgess, Thibaut) was settled by Pingree and others in favour of a back-computed epoch. Later astronomers (Brahmagupta, Kerala school) corrected planetary parameters and methods but kept the yuga lengths and epoch unchanged. The numbers themselves (432, 108, 12, 360) are arithmetic and symbolic constructs, so the framework is valuable as astronomy but gives no independent support to a literal historical timeline.","totalCycleYears":"4,320,000 years per mahayuga (consensus). Aryabhata is usually read as using four equal quarters of 1,080,000 years, while the Surya Siddhanta, Brahmagupta and the Kerala school use 4:3:2:1.","durations":{"satya":"1,728,000 years (4,800 divine years); Aryabhata equal-quarter reading: 1,080,000","treta":"1,296,000 years (3,600 divine years); Aryabhata equal-quarter reading: 1,080,000","dvapara":"864,000 years (2,400 divine years); Aryabhata equal-quarter reading: 1,080,000","kali":"432,000 years (1,200 divine years); Aryabhata equal-quarter reading: 1,080,000 (status debated)"},"kaliStart":"Midnight 17/18 February 3102 BCE (proleptic Julian, Ujjain or Lanka reckoning varies); a computed epoch, not an observed event.","kaliEnd":"About 428,899 CE (432,000 years from 3102 BCE; some entries give 428,898 or loosely 428,900). Under Aryabhata's equal-quarter reading the quarter would run to about 1,077,000 CE, but this is not a predicted end date.","currentPosition":"In 2026 CE about 5,127 years elapsed (Kali year 5,127-5,128 depending on convention), roughly 1.2% of 432,000 years. Under Aryabhata's equal quarters roughly 0.5% of the first Kali quarter has elapsed.","variants":[{"memberId":"surya-siddhanta","title":"Surya Siddhanta astronomical yuga (mahayuga, planetary revolutions, Kali epoch conjunction)","distinctive":"Gives per-mahayuga sidereal revolutions (Sun 4,320,000; Moon 57,753,336; Mars 2,296,832; Mercury 17,937,060; Jupiter 364,220; Venus 7,022,388; Saturn 146,568) and a sidereal year of about 365.2587565 days. Core text dated c. 4th-5th century CE; Surya Siddhanta-derived parameters still used in some regional panchangas."},{"memberId":"aryabhata","title":"Aryabhata's Aryabhatiya: equal quarter-yugas and the Kali epoch","distinctive":"Usual reading is four equal quarters of 1,080,000 years rather than 4:3:2:1, though the text is compressed and this is reconstructed from commentators. Gitikapada 3 is read as 3,600 Kali years elapsed at his time (birth c. 476 CE if 23 years before 499 CE); popular claims of a predicted Kali end are not in the text."},{"memberId":"brahmagupta","title":"Brahmagupta's critique of Aryabhata's yuga scheme (Brahmasphutasiddhanta, 628 CE)","distinctive":"Keeps the same mahayuga and 3102 BCE epoch; disputes Aryabhata's planetary revolution counts and methods and rejects the earth's diurnal rotation on physical grounds. Khandakhadyaka (665 CE) gives simplified practical rules. Colebrooke translated chapters in 1817."},{"memberId":"kerala-school","title":"Kerala school (Madhava, Nilakantha) and siddhantic yuga/ahargana handling","distinctive":"Active c. 1350-1650; Tantrasangraha (1500-1501) has Mercury and Venus orbiting the Sun, which orbits the Earth; Yuktibhasa (c. 1530) proves the Madhava series. They improved true-longitude computation but did not revise yuga lengths. Popular claims that they anticipated European astronomy overreach."},{"memberId":"kali-3102","title":"Kali Yuga epoch of 17/18 Feb 3102 BCE as a real planetary conjunction","distinctive":"Addresses the claim of a real seven-planet conjunction at 0 Aries, which it traces to 19th-20th century popular readings; checkable with JPL HORIZONS or Swiss Ephemeris (not run). Notes Sri Yukteswar's c. 24,000-year cycle (1894, The Holy Science) is a separate theory that does not confirm the epoch."},{"memberId":"bailly-bentley-burgess","title":"Bailly vs. Bentley/Burgess/Thibaut: observed or back-calculated 3102 BCE","distinctive":"Bailly (1787) read the epoch as evidence of very ancient observation (with lost-civilization speculation); Bentley (1790) called the tables late and the epoch artificial; Burgess (1860) and Thibaut (1899) treated it as a computed zero point, later supported by Pingree. Bentley's late dating was itself overstated, and a computed epoch does not rule out earlier observing traditions."},{"memberId":"inscriptions","title":"Epigraphic use of the Kali era (Aihole 634 CE and earlier claims)","distinctive":"The Aihole (Meguti) inscription of Pulakeshin II, composed by Ravikirti, gives Shaka 556 (634 CE) and Kali about 3735, the earliest securely dated epigraphic use, about 135 years after Aryabhata. It shows only that the era was in use, not that 3102 BCE is historical. Reading depends on Fleet's edition; verse and numeral details unchecked."},{"memberId":"numerology","title":"Numerical structure of yuga numbers (432, 108, 12, 360; sexagesimal vs. symbolic)","distinctive":"Shows the arithmetic: 12,000 divine years x 360, Manusmriti 1.69-71 gives 4,000+800 sandhya, 3,000+600, 2,000+400, 1,000+200; 432 = 12 x 36, 108 = 432/4 = 27 nakshatras x 4 padas. Possible Mesopotamian sexagesimal influence (Pingree) is contested; Vedanga Jyotisha's 5-year yuga is unrelated. Popular 108 and Sun-diameter numerology is post hoc (the actual ratio is about 109)."}],"keyClaims":["A mahayuga is 4,320,000 years split into Satya, Treta, Dvapara and Kali (4:3:2:1 in Surya Siddhanta, Brahmagupta and the Kerala school; equal quarters in the usual reading of Aryabhata).","Kali begins at the epoch of midnight 17/18 February 3102 BCE, used as the zero point for the ahargana (day count) and for mean planetary longitudes.","At the epoch all mean longitudes are zero at the start of Aries; this is a model assumption, not an observed conjunction.","The epoch was first used explicitly in the Aryabhatiya (499 CE) and appears epigraphically in the Aihole inscription (634 CE, Kali about 3735 = Shaka 556).","Brahmagupta and later the Kerala school corrected planetary parameters, true-longitude methods and models but retained the mahayuga and epoch.","Surya Siddhanta gives accurate mean motions and a sidereal year of about 365.2587565 days, usable for eclipses and calendars.","The numbers 432, 108, 12 and 360 are multiplicative and sexagesimal constructs (divine year = 360 human years), shared across Manusmriti, Mahabharata, Puranas and Siddhantas.","Scholars (Burgess, Thibaut, Pingree, Plofker) read the epoch as computed for convenience; Bailly's reading as an ancient observation is rejected."],"evidence":["Internal arithmetic consistency of 4:3:2:1 ratios, the 12,000 divine-year total and the 1,000-mahayuga kalpa.","Planetary revolution counts consistent with the Siddhantic sine tables and algorithms, giving usable medieval predictions; sidereal year within a few thousandths of a day of the modern value.","Aryabhatiya Gitikapada 3 (3,600 Kali years elapsed at composition, 499 CE) and Bhaskara I's 629 CE commentary fix the epoch by arithmetic.","Aihole inscription (634 CE) agreement of Shaka 556 with Kali about 3735; Brahmagupta's 628 CE text uses the same epoch.","Brahmasphutasiddhanta explicitly disputes Aryabhata's methods (Colebrooke 1817; Neugebauer 1975; Plofker 2009).","Tantrasangraha, Yuktibhasa and modern reconstructions (Sarma 1972; Ramasubramanian, Sriram and Srinivas 1994) confirm the Kerala corrections are real and checkable.","Modern ephemerides and planetarium back-calculations show no tight seven-planet alignment near 3102 BCE.","Pingree's Census of Exact Sciences in Sanskrit places the epoch in a family of computed epochs; Pingree (1973, 1978) argues for Greek and Babylonian sources.","Agreement of several independent texts on the 12,000 divine-year total, indicating a shared numerical convention.","Surya Siddhanta-based panchangas remain in use."],"commonCriticisms":["epoch-back-calculated","computational-numbers","no-archaeology","late-origin","astro-dating-underdetermined","unverified-sources"],"specificCriticisms":["Parameters differ across siddhantas, epochs and conventions (Ujjain vs Lanka midnight, Julian vs astronomical dating), showing they are conventions rather than discovered constants.","Aryabhata's equal 1,080,000-year quarters rest on reconstruction from commentators, and Gitikapada 3 can be read from a different reference point.","Brahmagupta's critique does not challenge yuga durations and his rejection of earth rotation was flawed; attribution of specific corrections is contested.","Kerala school manuscripts are largely unpublished or partly edited, some dates (e.g. Putumana Somayaji) are disputed, and claims that it anticipated European astronomy or 'corrected' the yuga timeline overreach.","The 'all planets at 0 Aries' condition cannot serve as evidence for the model that defines it.","Bailly's antiquity argument assumed a zero epoch records an observation; Bentley's late dating was overstated; Burgess and Thibaut had limited manuscripts; a computed epoch does not exclude earlier observational traditions.","Aihole is only the earliest known epigraphic use, shows convenience rather than belief in the 432,000-year cycle, and epigraphic Kali dating is sparse before the 8th century.","Numerical coincidences (108, 432, 360) are easy to find and over-read; sandhya and sandhyamsa add-ons look like fitting; Mesopotamian sexagesimal derivation is unproven.","Popular and fringe writers treat the yuga durations as literal or prophetic timelines, which scholars and the texts themselves do not support."],"plausibility":2,"plausibilityRange":"1-5","plausibilityReason":"The astronomy and epoch conventions are well documented and historically important (the Kerala school rates 3, the Aihole use of the era is well supported), but as a literal real-world timeline, with a real 3102 BCE conjunction or a 4.32-million-year cycle, there is no independent support. Scores reflect the timeline claim, not the quality of the astronomy; the numerology entry scored 1 and the split Aihole entry 5.","verification":"Web-checked","references":["Burgess, Ebenezer (trans., 1860), Translation of the Surya Siddhanta, ed. W.D. Whitney, Journal of the American Oriental Society 6 (ed. Gangooly, 1935)","Pingree, David (1970-1994), Census of the Exact Sciences in Sanskrit, American Philosophical Society","Pingree, David (1973), 'The Mesopotamian Origin of Early Indian Mathematical Astronomy', Journal for the History of Astronomy 4","Pingree, David (1978), 'History of Mathematical Astronomy in India', Dictionary of Scientific Biography vol. 15; and 'The Greek influence on early Indian astronomy'","Pingree, David (1981), Jyotihsastra: Astral and Mathematical Literature, Harrassowitz","Plofker, Kim (2009), Mathematics in India, Princeton University Press","Aryabhatiya of Aryabhata (499 CE): Clark (1930, University of Chicago Press); Shukla and Sarma (INSA, 1976)","Datta, B. and Singh, A.N., History of Hindu Mathematics (1935, 1938)","Bhaskara I, commentary on the Aryabhatiya (629 CE)","Brahmasphutasiddhanta (628 CE), trans. H.T. Colebrooke (1817); Khandakhadyaka (665 CE), Bina Chatterjee, INSA 1970","Neugebauer, Otto (1975), A History of Ancient Mathematical Astronomy, Springer","Sarma, K.V. (1972), A History of the Kerala School of Hindu Astronomy; Sarma (2008), Yuktibhasa of Jyeshtadeva, Hindustan Book Agency","Ramasubramanian, K., Srinivas, M.D. and Sriram, M.S. (1994), 'Modification of the earlier Indian planetary theory by the Kerala astronomers', Indian Journal of History of Science 29(2); Ramasubramanian and Sriram (2011), Tantrasangraha of Nilakantha Somayaji","Joseph, George Gheverghese (2011), The Crest of the Peacock, 3rd ed., Princeton University Press","Bailly, J.-S. (1787), Traite de l'astronomie indienne et orientale; Bentley, J. (1790), A Historical View of the Hindu Astronomy","Thibaut, G. (1899), Astronomie, Astrologie und Mathematik, Grundriss der Indo-Arischen Philologie vol. III; Sewell and Dikshit (1896), The Indian Calendar","Aihole (Meguti) inscription of Pulakeshin II (Shaka 556 / 634 CE), ed. J.F. Fleet; D.C. Sircar (1965), Indian Epigraphy","Olivelle, Patrick (2005), Manu's Code of Law, Oxford University Press; Kuppanna Sastry, T.S. (1985), Vedanga Jyotisha of Lagadha","Sri Yukteswar Giri (1894), The Holy Science"]},{"id":"historical-development","title":"Historical-critical view: the yuga doctrine as a late, layered textual and cosmological construct","category":"Academic/historical","members":["indologists","skeptic-historian","vedic-absence","vedanga-jyotisha","pingree-hellenistic","berossus","archaeology-human"],"summary":"Mainstream scholarship traces the yuga doctrine as a gradual textual development rather than a remembered chronology. The Vedas have no four-age cosmology: yuga means a yoke, generation or span, the Vedanga Jyotisha uses it for a 5-year lunisolar cycle, and the names krta, treta, dvapara and kali appear in Aitareya Brahmana 7.15 and in dice vocabulary (RV 10.34). The four-age scheme with declining dharma and 4:3:2:1 durations (12,000 divine years of 360 human years) crystallises in the Mahabharata and Manusmriti c. 300 BCE-300 CE, and the 4,320,000-year mahayuga and the 3102 BCE Kali epoch are fixed in Siddhantic astronomy from about 499 CE (Aryabhata). Pingree and others see Babylonian and Hellenistic input into the computational apparatus, and the number 432,000 has a sexagesimal parallel in Berossus, though no borrowing of the yuga cosmology is established. Read literally as human history, the yugas conflict with archaeology and genetics. The consensus reads the figures as symbolic, theological and astronomical conventions.","totalCycleYears":"4,320,000 years (mahayuga, 12,000 divine years x 360) in the later scheme; no total is given in Vedic texts, and the Vedanga Jyotisha yuga is 5 solar years.","durations":{"satya":"1,728,000 years (4,800 divine years); N/A in Vedic texts and Vedanga Jyotisha","treta":"1,296,000 years (3,600 divine years)","dvapara":"864,000 years (2,400 divine years)","kali":"432,000 years (1,200 divine years)"},"kaliStart":"3102 BCE (conventional Siddhantic epoch, midnight 17/18 February proleptic Julian); a computed back-projection first attested c. 499 CE (Aryabhata), not an observed or recorded event. No Vedic start date exists.","kaliEnd":"About 428,898-428,900 CE if the 432,000-year duration is counted literally from 3102 BCE; treated as arithmetic only.","currentPosition":"In 2026 CE about 5,127-5,128 Kali years have elapsed (roughly 1.2%), an astronomical convention rather than a measured interval; not applicable to Vedic sources or the Vedanga Jyotisha 5-year cycle.","variants":[{"memberId":"indologists","title":"Indologist historical-development view of the yuga doctrine","distinctive":"Hopkins (1901), Kirfel (1920), Pargiter (1913/1922) and Kane (1953-1962): earliest layer is the Vedic dice metaphor, with human-scale numbers in the Mahabharata and Manusmriti and the astronomical numbers later. Pargiter used Puranic dynastic lists as partly historical but not the yuga arithmetic; Kirfel treated the system as gradually integrated cosmography."},{"memberId":"skeptic-historian","title":"Critical secular/historical view: yugas as mythic-cosmological framework","distinctive":"Dates the doctrine c. 300 BCE-300 CE and the epoch to c. 5th-6th century CE. Compares Hesiod's Works and Days (c. 700 BCE) metal ages and the Pali Agganna Sutta (DN 27) decline cycles; cites Manusmriti 1.67-86 and Mahabharata Vana Parva. Reads decline as moral and political rhetoric about dharma and kingship and notes that the Indus urban decline (c. 1900 BCE) does not align with 3102 BCE."},{"memberId":"vedic-absence","title":"Vedic absence: yugas as dice throws, not cosmic ages","distinctive":"Focuses on Aitareya Brahmana 7.15 (Caraiveti passage) and the dice scoring krta (4, winning), treta (3), dvapara (2), kali (1, losing). Cites Manusmriti 1.81-86 for the divine-year durations. Sayana read the passage as a cosmic-age teaching, so the dice origin is contested. Plausibility 7 applies only to the textual-origin claim."},{"memberId":"vedanga-jyotisha","title":"Vedanga Jyotisha 5-year yuga (calendrical yuga as a short lunisolar cycle)","distinctive":"Five years named samvatsara, parivatsara, idavatsara, anuvatsara and idvatsara contain 62 synodic months, 67 sidereal months, 1830 civil days and 1835 sidereal days, with a winter solstice at the start of Dhanishtha. Dikshit (1896) and traditionalists date it to c. 1400 BCE; critical opinion favours the 1st millennium BCE; Kak (1994) reads Vedic astronomy codes as a contested minority view. A text of about 40 verses with no world-age content."},{"memberId":"pingree-hellenistic","title":"Pingree and Hellenistic/Babylonian Influence on Yuga Numbers and the 3102 BCE Epoch","distinctive":"Pingree (1973, 1978): Siddhanta mean-motion and epoch methods draw on Babylonian System B and Greek models via Greek-to-Sanskrit transmission, citing the Yavanajataka (269/270 CE), Romaka and Paulisa Siddhantas and the Nabonassar era (747 BCE) as an analogue. Plofker sees a more mixed, bidirectional exchange; the sine tables and the yuga cosmology itself are not shown to be borrowed."},{"memberId":"berossus","title":"Mesopotamian links: Berossus 432,000 years, Sumerian King List, and the number 432","distinctive":"Berossus (c. 280 BCE) gives ten antediluvian kings reigning 120 sars (3,600 years each) = 432,000 years, but the reign figures transmitted by Syncellus sum to 396,000. The Sumerian King List (c. 2100-1800 BCE) has eight antediluvian kings totalling about 241,200 years. Berossus is a single-flood chronology, not a recurring cycle, and survives only through Christian chronographers."},{"memberId":"archaeology-human","title":"Archaeology and human evolution vs. a historical Satya/Treta golden age","distinctive":"Tests the literal-history reading: Homo sapiens c. 300,000 years ago (Jebel Irhoud, Hublin 2017), agriculture c. 10,000 years ago, Indus cities c. 2600-1900 BCE, ancient DNA (Narasimhan 2019; Shinde 2018) showing gradual mixing with Steppe input c. 2000-1500 BCE, and Ghaggar-Hakra drying c. 2000 BCE (Giosan 2012). Revivalist readings of the Satya and Treta ages as human eras are the target. Plausibility 1."}],"keyClaims":["The four-yuga cosmology is absent from the Rigveda and the Vedic samhitas; yuga there means a yoke, generation or span, and in Vedanga Jyotisha a 5-year cycle.","The yuga names krta, treta, dvapara and kali originated as dice-throw terms (krta winning, kali losing) and appear metaphorically in Aitareya Brahmana 7.15.","The Mahabharata and Manusmriti (c. 300 BCE-300 CE) give the first explicit four-age scheme of declining dharma with 4:3:2:1 durations; the 4,320,000-year mahayuga is a Puranic and Siddhantic standardisation.","The 3102 BCE Kali epoch is a back-computed zero point from the Aryabhata and Siddhanta period (c. 499 CE onward), with no earlier text dating an event to that year.","Indian siddhantic methods and epoch practice show Babylonian and Hellenistic influence (Pingree), but the yuga cosmology itself is not shown to be derived from Mesopotamia.","The 432,000 figure matches Berossus's 120 sars and fits a sexagesimal number pattern, a parallel with no demonstrated transmission.","Pargiter used Puranic dynastic lists as partly historical; Kane and Kirfel treated the cosmological numbers as symbolic, and Kirfel showed the world-age material was integrated gradually into the Puranas.","Decline cycles were a wider ancient idea (Hesiod, Pali Agganna Sutta, Persian and Norse parallels), suggesting a mythic rather than archaeological origin.","Read literally, Satya and Treta as 1.7- and 1.3-million-year human eras, or a historical Kali start in 3102 BCE, are not supported by archaeology, paleoanthropology or genetics."],"evidence":["Textual dating of the Vedic dice metaphor (RV 10.34, Aitareya Brahmana 7.15), the Manusmriti passages (1.67-86; Olivelle dates c. 2nd c. BCE-2nd c. CE) and the Mahabharata yuga passages (critical edition, Sukthankar et al., 1933-1966), showing development over roughly a millennium.","Absence of any pre-Aryabhata source fixing the Kali epoch at 3102 BCE; Aryabhatiya Gitikapada makes it a computed back-projection.","Divine-year arithmetic (12,000 x 360 = 4,320,000) appears as a mathematical construct in Manusmriti and Siddhantic texts.","Vedanga Jyotisha arithmetic is checkable (62 synodic months is about 1829.6 days; 5 x 366 = 1830 days); the Sastry and Sarma (1985) edition is the standard.","Pingree's Yavanajataka edition (1978), the Romaka and Paulisa Siddhantas (summarised by Varahamihira c. 505 CE) and Neugebauer's and Pingree's work on Babylonian System B show Greek and Babylonian methods in Indian astronomy.","Berossus's 120 sars survives in Syncellus and Eusebius; sexagesimal units (sar, ner, sosos) are well documented in Babylonian texts.","Pargiter's analysis found Puranic genealogies inconsistent and generation-based, not convertible to yuga spans.","Hesiod's ages of man and the Pali Agganna Sutta (DN 27) as parallel decline-cycle schemes.","Fossil, archaeological and ancient DNA record (Jebel Irhoud c. 300 ka; Indus cities c. 2600-1900 BCE; Narasimhan 2019; Shinde 2018; Giosan 2012) contradicts historical yuga-length human ages."],"commonCriticisms":["late-origin","epoch-back-calculated","computational-numbers","no-archaeology","unfalsifiable","astro-dating-underdetermined","unverified-sources"],"specificCriticisms":["The dice-origin hypothesis is an inference from shared vocabulary and one metaphorical passage; whether kali and krta were fixed dice names in the Vedic period is debated, and Sayana read the passage as cosmic.","Absence from the Rigveda is an argument from silence; undated oral material may have preceded the textual record.","Rigid separation of Vedic, epic and Puranic layers overstates what are continuously revised texts, and the dice origin may be too neat since decline ideas may have independent ethical or cyclical roots.","Pargiter's dynastic chronology assumed Puranic lists are more reliable than they are; Kane is largely a compilation; Kirfel relied on limited manuscripts; these early works predate or ignore epigraphic and archaeological evidence.","Vedanga Jyotisha dating (Dhanishtha solstice, c. 1400 BCE by Dikshit versus the 1st millennium BCE) is disputed, its 366-day year is crude, and treating it as origin of the four-yuga scheme overreaches.","Pingree is criticised for overstating Babylonian origin of 432,000, since sexagesimal compatibility is not derivation, Indian decimal place-value and Vedanga Jyotisha show indigenous elements, and the Greek influence is clearest for specific techniques rather than yuga cosmology.","The Berossus parallel depends on Christian chroniclers (Eusebius, Syncellus) with Biblical-harmonising motives, and the transmitted reign figures total 396,000 not 432,000; the Sumerian King List is not a 432,000-based system.","The Kali 432,000 figure is a tenth of an Indian-derived mahayuga, so a match to Berossus is easy numerical coincidence; direction of any transmission is unknown.","Hesiod and Zoroastrian parallels do not prove diffusion; traditionalist attempts to match Mahabharata or Kali dates to archaeology are rejected by mainstream scholars.","Literal readings of yuga lifespans are internally inconsistent across texts, and the Mahabharata's internal chronology is layered and not reconcilable with one yuga timeline.","The sceptical view can be overstated: the texts are consistent in their own terms and the moral framework has real historical influence even without literal chronology."],"plausibility":7,"plausibilityRange":"1-7","plausibilityReason":"Family score set to 7 to rate the scholarly account itself, in line with the final verdict (historical-developmental view 9, Vedanga 5-year yuga 8, Pingree 6). Some member scores (e.g. Indologists 2) rated the literal timeline they discuss, not the scholarship. The narrow textual and developmental claims (Vedic absence, dice-term origin, Vedanga Jyotisha as a calendar cycle) score 7, Greek and Babylonian influence on astronomical techniques 4, and Mesopotamian origin of the yuga numbers 2, but the literal-history claims (a 3102 BCE start or Satya and Treta as human eras) score 1-2 against archaeology, genetics and the late attestation of the epoch.","verification":"Web-checked","references":["Hopkins, E.W. (1901), The Great Epic of India, Scribner's; and writings on the dice origin of the yuga names (c. 1898-1901)","Kirfel, W. (1920), Die Kosmographie der Inder, Bonn: K. Schroeder","Pargiter, F.E. (1913), The Purana Text of the Dynasties of the Kali Age, Oxford; Pargiter (1922), Ancient Indian Historical Tradition, Oxford","Kane, P.V. (1930-1962), History of Dharmasastra (esp. vols. IV-V), Bhandarkar Oriental Research Institute","Jacobi, H., papers on Indian chronology and era systems","Olivelle, Patrick (2005), Manu's Code of Law, Oxford University Press (Manusmriti 1.67-86)","Sukthankar, V.S. et al. (1933-1966), Critical Edition of the Mahabharata, BORI","Keith, A.B. (1920), Rigveda Brahmanas (Aitareya Brahmana 7.15), Harvard Oriental Series","Sastry, T.S. Kuppanna and Sarma, K.V. (1985), Vedanga Jyotisha of Lagadha, INSA","Dikshit, S.B. (1896), Bharatiya Jyotish (Marathi)","Kak, Subhash (1994), The Astronomical Code of the Rgveda, Munshiram Manoharlal","Pingree, David (1973), 'The Mesopotamian Origin of Early Indian Mathematical Astronomy', Journal for the History of Astronomy 4: 1-12","Pingree, David (1978), The Yavanajataka of Sphujidhvaja, Harvard Oriental Series 48; and 'History of Mathematical Astronomy in India', Dictionary of Scientific Biography vol. 15","Pingree, David (1970-1994), Census of the Exact Sciences in Sanskrit, American Philosophical Society","Pingree, David (1981), Jyotihsastra: Astral and Mathematical Literature, Harrassowitz","Neugebauer, O. (1951), The Exact Sciences in Antiquity; Neugebauer (1975), A History of Ancient Mathematical Astronomy","Plofker, Kim (2009), Mathematics in India, Princeton University Press","Burgess, Ebenezer (1860), Translation of the Surya Siddhanta, ed. W.D. Whitney, JAOS 6","Doniger, Wendy (2009), The Hindus: An Alternative History, Penguin; Flood, Gavin (1996), An Introduction to Hinduism, Cambridge; Eliade, Mircea (1954), Cosmos and History, Pantheon; Hesiod, Works and Days","Berossus, Babyloniaca, via Eusebius and Syncellus; Burstein, S. (1978), The Babyloniaca of Berossus; Verbrugghe and Wickersham (1996), Berossos and Manetho, Michigan; Schnabel, P. (1923), Berossos und die babylonisch-hellenistische Literatur","Jacobsen, Thorkild (1939), The Sumerian King List, Assyriological Studies 11","Hublin et al. (2017), Nature 546:289-292 (Jebel Irhoud)","Narasimhan et al. (2019), Science, doi:10.1126/science.aat7487; Shinde et al. (2018), Cell (Rakhigarhi)","Giosan et al. (2012), PNAS 109:E1688 (Ghaggar-Hakra)","Aryabhatiya (499 CE) for the mahayuga and Kali epoch"]},{"id":"yukteswar-24000","title":"Yukteswar's 24,000-year cycle and its derivatives (precession, binary star, guru, Western and pop variants)","category":"Modern spiritual/esoteric","members":["yukteswar","yogananda","precession","selbie-steinmetz-ananda","sadhguru-isha","cruttenden-binary","hamlets-mill","western-astrology-variants","hancock-rogan"],"summary":"Sri Yukteswar's The Holy Science (1894) reinterprets the yuga scheme as a roughly 24,000-year cycle tied to the precession of the equinoxes and to a supposed solar motion around a central point (Vishnunabhi, 'Central Sun'). The cycle has an ascending and a descending arc of 12,000 years each, each divided into Kali, Dvapara, Treta and Satya in the 1:2:3:4 ratio (1,200/2,400/3,600/4,800 years). He treats the Puranic 4.32-million-year mahayuga as a misreading of astronomical units. On this reading Kali ended c. 1699 CE and the world is in an ascending Dvapara (c. 1699-4099 CE), so consciousness is rising. Yogananda popularized the scheme (Autobiography of a Yogi, 1946), and Ananda/Kriyananda, Sadhguru-style teaching, Cruttenden's Binary Research Institute and similar groups adopt or extend it. Related members use the real precession cycle (about 25,772 years) in other ways: the Hamlet's Mill myth-decoding thesis, Western Age-of-Aquarius astrology, and the Hancock/Carlson Younger Dryas lost-civilisation narrative. These last three do not use Yukteswar's durations and are loose analogies or alternative frameworks. The 24,000-year figure does not match the measured precession period, and the 1:2:3:4 yuga structure has no astronomical basis.","totalCycleYears":"24,000 years (2 x 12,000) for the Yukteswar-derived members. Differences: Hamlet's Mill, Western astrology and Hancock/Carlson use the precession cycle of about 25,772 years (25,920 as the rounded Great Year) and define no yuga lengths; the classical Puranic mahayuga is 4,320,000 years.","durations":{"satya":"4,800 years per arc (9,600 across both arcs); classical 1,728,000; not specified by Hamlet's Mill or Hancock/Carlson","treta":"3,600 years per arc (7,200 across both); classical 1,296,000","dvapara":"2,400 years per arc (4,800 across both); classical 864,000","kali":"1,200 years per arc (2,400 across both); classical 432,000. Western variants usually use one 2,160-year zodiac age"},"kaliStart":"c. 499 CE for the descending Kali in Yukteswar-derived accounts (secondary-source reading, not checked against the primary text). Hancock/Carlson tie a 'fallen age' to the Younger Dryas onset c. 10,900 BCE. Western dates are for Aquarius, not Kali. The traditional epoch is 3102 BCE.","kaliEnd":"c. 1699 CE, when ascending Dvapara begins, in Yukteswar-derived accounts (Ananda says 1699-1700). Not specified by the other members.","currentPosition":"Yukteswar-derived accounts place 2026 in the ascending Dvapara Yuga, about 326-327 years in, ending c. 4099 CE. This conflicts with the classical position of about 5,127 years into Kali. Cruttenden says Dvapara or near its transition. Western astrologers treat Aquarius as current or upcoming depending on the author. Hancock/Carlson imply a post-catastrophe fallen age with no precise stage.","variants":[{"memberId":"yukteswar","title":"Sri Yukteswar's 24,000-year cycle (The Holy Science, 1894)","distinctive":"Baseline source. Says the solar motion around Vishnunabhi drives the cycle. Notes The Holy Science is a theological/Kriya yoga text, so reading it as chronology overreaches. The 499/1699 CE dating was not verified in the primary text."},{"memberId":"yogananda","title":"Yogananda and SRF extensions of Sri Yukteswar's 24,000-year yuga theory","distinctive":"Popularization c. 1920s to 1946 and later SRF teaching (including The Second Coming of Christ, 1979). Ties the scheme to the guru lineage (Babaji, Lahiri Mahasaya, Yukteswar) as a cosmic plan to revive yoga. Critics note this makes it harder to test and exposes it to confirmation bias."},{"memberId":"precession","title":"Yugas as a precession cycle (Yukteswar's 24,000-year Great Year model)","distinctive":"Adds the Great Year background: Hipparchus c. 150 BCE, Greek 25,920 years, and Indian ayanamsa/ayana-chalana. Notes Indian astronomers (Surya Siddhanta, Brahmagupta, Bhaskara II) treated precession as a limited oscillation. Also claims the precession link was esoteric and unwritten."},{"memberId":"selbie-steinmetz-ananda","title":"Ananda Sangha / Selbie-Steinmetz \"The Yugas\": Dvapara since ~1700 and archaeological claims","distinctive":"Ananda (Kriyananda's organization) teaches it as a scientific-spiritual framework. The claimed 2010 Selbie & Steinmetz book and its archaeological claims could not be verified (ananda.org returned 403). Says Kali ended c. 1699-1700 CE."},{"memberId":"sadhguru-isha","title":"Sadhguru (Jaggi Vasudev) / Isha Foundation: yuga position under a Yukteswar-style ascending cycle","distinctive":"Premise that Sadhguru says we are in ascending Dvapara could not be confirmed from any Sadhguru source. The premise links yugas to human perception and consciousness. Criticism notes the Sun's galactic orbit is 225-250 million years, and his public statements sometimes conflict with scientific consensus (mercury and lunar-eclipse claims). The entry would need revision if he in fact says Kali."},{"memberId":"cruttenden-binary","title":"Cruttenden's Binary Star Theory and BRI's Use of Yukteswar's Cycle","distinctive":"Walter Cruttenden (Lost Star of Myth and Time, 2006; Binary Research Institute) says an unseen binary companion on a roughly 24,000-year orbit drives the cycle, preserved in Vedic, Egyptian, Sumerian and Mayan myth. No companion found by IRAS (1983 onward) or Gaia; the 'Nemesis' hypothesis was ruled out in the 1980s. Not peer-reviewed; BRI sells courses and books."},{"memberId":"hamlets-mill","title":"Hamlet's Mill thesis: myths encode precession (applied to yugas)","distinctive":"Santillana and von Dechend (1969) claim myths worldwide (Hesiod, Vedic, Norse 'mill' of Grotti, Hamlet/Amleth) encode precession and a lost ancient astronomy. Supplies no yuga dates or lengths, so it is not a dating system; on the conventional epoch Kali would end c. 428,899 CE. Criticized for etymological leaps, cherry-picking and no transmission mechanism. Plausibility 3 (highest in the cluster). Citations are from background knowledge."},{"memberId":"western-astrology-variants","title":"Western astrologers' yuga variants (MacKinnell, Vandekerkhove, Addey, Cayce and Age of Aquarius mixed with yugas)","distinctive":"Maps yugas onto 2,147-2,160-year zodiac ages. Aquarius start dates conflict: MacKinnell 1447 AD, Addey 3597 AD, Vandekerkhove 1890 (cited as 1899, La Fronde), IAU 1929 boundaries 2595 AD. Campion's survey counts 29 claims for the 20th century and 12 for the 24th. Vandekerkhove's March 1890 start is a dated claim that was not borne out. Cayce's yuga mapping was not confirmed."},{"memberId":"hancock-rogan","title":"Hancock / Rogan / Carlson: yugas linked to the Younger Dryas and a lost advanced civilisation","distinctive":"Graham Hancock and Randall Carlson (on Joe Rogan) claim a Younger Dryas impact c. 10,900 BCE (about 12,900 years ago) ended an advanced Ice Age civilisation, with monuments (Great Pyramid, Gobekli Tepe) as encoded warnings. Yugas serve only as an analogy for decline. Netflix's Ancient Apocalypse (2022, sequel Oct 2024) drew a Society for American Archaeology letter on 30 Nov 2022 asking for relabelling as science fiction. The Younger Dryas impact markers (Firestone et al. 2007) failed to replicate. Critics also cite racist framings and hyperdiffusionism."}],"keyClaims":["The true yuga cycle is about 24,000 years, tied to the precession of the equinoxes and a solar motion around a central point or companion (Vishnunabhi, Central Sun, binary star); the real precession figure is about 25,772 years.","Ascending and descending arcs of 12,000 years each, with yugas in the 1:2:3:4 ratio of 1,200/2,400/3,600/4,800 years per arc.","The Puranic 4.32-million-year mahayuga is a misreading or symbolic inflation of the shorter astronomical cycle; classical texts encode precession esoterically.","Kali ended c. 1699 CE and humanity is in an ascending Dvapara (to c. 4099 CE), a long rise in consciousness.","Yogananda's guru lineage (Babaji, Lahiri Mahasaya, Yukteswar) belongs to a cosmic plan to revive yoga in this period.","An undetected solar companion star on a roughly 24,000-year orbit is preserved in world mythology (Cruttenden).","Myths worldwide encode precession and a lost ancient astronomy; cosmic-age numbers such as yugas reflect precessional arithmetic (Santillana and von Dechend).","Yugas can be mapped onto 2,160-year zodiac ages, with the Age of Aquarius start date pinnable to a specific year (1447 to 3597 AD depending on author).","A Younger Dryas impact c. 10,900 BCE destroyed an advanced Ice Age civilisation that seeded later cultures; yuga-style decline is an analogue (Hancock/Carlson).","Sadhguru/Isha (unverified premise): we are in an ascending Dvapara, with yuga state linked to human perception."],"evidence":["Precession of the equinoxes is real and measured (about 50.3 arcseconds per year, about 25,772 years), discovered by Hipparchus c. 150 BCE; Indian astronomy discussed precession (ayanamsa, ayana-chalana). Cited as partial support, but the figures do not match 24,000.","The 1:2:3:4 yuga ratio is traditional in Hindu texts, so Yukteswar's proportions have textual precedent even though the absolute durations are rescaled.","Internal consistency of The Holy Science's own 24,000-year scheme and its tidy 12,000-year arcs.","Yogananda's Autobiography of a Yogi and SRF/Ananda teaching as testimony of the lineage; adherents' subjective spiritual experience (not evidence about chronology).","Retrospective appeal to Indian scientific and social progress since the 19th century as confirmation of the Dvapara claim.","Wikipedia (Sri Yukteswar Giri, Yuga) confirms the 24,000-year cycle, the Dvapara claim, and Surya Siddhanta's 4,320,000-year catur-yuga; no fetched source gave the exact 499/1699 CE dates or the archaeological claims.","Comparative mythology (Hindu, Greek, Egyptian, Maya, Norse) read through the companion-star or precession lens; documented ancient long cycles (Babylonian, Greek Great Year, yugas, kalpas).","Western astrology: Campion's survey of Aquarius dating claims, the Vandekerkhove 1890 La Fronde article, and IAU 1929 constellation boundaries.","Real sites and events cited by Hancock/Carlson: Gobekli Tepe (c. 9500 BCE) and the Younger Dryas cooling; Netflix's Ancient Apocalypse brought the theory to a mass audience."],"commonCriticisms":["no-archaeology","epoch-back-calculated","precession-error","unfalsifiable","late-origin","retrofitting","unverified-sources","astro-dating-underdetermined","elapsed-vs-total"],"specificCriticisms":["The claimed Sun orbit of about 24,000 years around Vishnunabhi/Central Sun/companion is unsupported; the Sun's actual galactic orbit takes about 225-250 million years, and a 24,000-year companion would produce unobserved gravitational effects.","Cruttenden's companion star is not seen in IRAS, later surveys or Gaia, and the 'Nemesis' hypothesis was ruled out in the 1980s; his thesis is not peer-reviewed and is sold as courses and books.","Precession is smooth and cyclical; it does not naturally yield four phases of unequal 1:2:3:4 length.","Classical sources (Manusmriti, Vishnu Purana, Mahabharata) give yuga lengths in divine years (x360) with no reference to precession; the 1699 CE transition and 1,200/2,400/3,600/4,800 split are attested in no pre-modern Hindu source.","Mainstream Indology does not accept the reinterpretation of Puranic numbers as reduced astronomical units; Indian yuga numbers have better-documented origins in mean-motion arithmetic.","The Holy Science is a theological text on Kriya yoga and Christian-Hindu synthesis, so reading it as chronology overreaches.","Yogananda's extensions tie the model to his own lineage and mission, inviting confirmation bias; academic study is thin.","Classical chronology (still in Kali) and Yukteswar (ascending Dvapara) give incompatible present positions.","Hamlet's Mill relies on etymological leaps, selective parallels and an assumed lost astronomical science with no transmission evidence; specialists judge it overreaching.","Western astrology conflates two unrelated systems (Puranic yugas vs about 2,160-year zodiac ages) with no consensus start date (1447 to 3597 AD), and the Aquarius start shifts with zodiac-boundary convention (equal 30-degree signs vs IAU 1929).","The Younger Dryas impact hypothesis has failed to replicate; mainstream palaeoclimatology attributes the cooling to a Laurentide meltwater pulse. Hyperdiffusionism has been rejected since the 19th century, and critics (Jeb Card, the Society for American Archaeology) cite misrepresentation of archaeologists, cherry-picking and racist framings.","Hancock's wider claims include pseudo-scientific elements (ancient psychic powers, moving stones by meditation).","Sadhguru's attribution is an unverified premise, and his public statements sometimes conflict with scientific consensus."],"plausibility":2,"plausibilityRange":"1-3","plausibilityReason":"Precession is real, but the 24,000-year figure, the solar-orbit/companion premise and the 1699 CE transition conflict with measured astronomy and have no independent evidence; the schemes are devotional or interpretive cosmologies rather than testable chronologies. Scores range from 1 (Sadhguru, Cruttenden, Hancock/Carlson) to 3 (Hamlet's Mill, which raises real questions about myth and astronomy but whose decoding is unfalsifiable).","verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["Sri Yukteswar Giri, The Holy Science (Kaivalya Darshanam), 1894 (SRF edition; page numbers and the exact 499/1699 CE dating not checked against the primary text)","Paramahansa Yogananda, Autobiography of a Yogi (1946), SRF edition; The Second Coming of Christ (SRF, 1979)","SRF website, yogananda-srf.org","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sri_Yukteswar_Giri","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Holy_Science","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yuga","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Swami_Kriyananda","https://www.ananda.org/yugas/ (HTTP 403, not retrieved)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sadhguru","https://isha.sadhguru.org/ (not retrieved in full)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Walter_Cruttenden","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nemesis_(hypothetical_star)","Walter Cruttenden, Lost Star of Myth and Time (2006)","Santillana, G. de and von Dechend, H., Hamlet's Mill (Gambit, Boston, 1969)","Neugebauer, O., A History of Ancient Mathematical Astronomy (1975); Hipparchus' precession as reported in Ptolemy's Almagest","Burgess, E. (trans.), Surya Siddhanta (1860), Journal of the American Oriental Society; Manusmriti ch. 1; Vishnu Purana Book 1; Bhagavata Purana 12.4.2","Pingree, David, works on Sanskrit astronomy and precession (check specific titles before citing)","IAU and IERS documentation for the lunisolar precession period (about 25,772 years)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Age_of_Aquarius; Campion, N. (survey of Aquarius dating claims); Vandekerkhove, A. (1890), La Fronde","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Graham_Hancock; https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Randall_Carlson (HTTP 404, not verified)","Firestone et al. 2007, PNAS (Younger Dryas impact hypothesis); Society for American Archaeology letter to Netflix, 30 November 2022"]},{"id":"traditionalist","title":"Traditionalist (Perennialist) Kali Yuga as qualitative decline, and its offshoots","category":"Modern spiritual/esoteric","members":["guenon","evola-traditionalist","georgel","danielou","savitri-devi-serrano","kali-yuga-meme-culture"],"summary":"The Traditionalist school (Guenon, developed by Evola and Schuon) adopts the standard Indian scheme of a 4,320,000-year mahayuga and the 3102 BCE Kali epoch, but reads the yugas as qualitative stages of metaphysical and spiritual decline rather than as a dated calendar. On this view the present is the late, darkest phase of Kali Yuga, marked by materialism, the 'reign of quantity' and loss of sacred hierarchy; the decline is held to be irreversible by collective effort, with an individual option to preserve inner realization ('ride the tiger'). Because the numbers are treated as symbolic and the end of the cycle is qualitative, the framework makes almost no dated predictions. Offshoots take the same decline narrative in different directions: Savitri Devi and Miguel Serrano fuse it with Hitlerist esotericism, and internet meme culture uses 'Kali Yuga' as an ironic shorthand for chaos and decline with the cosmology stripped out. Two members (Georgel, Danielou) could not be verified: no specific works or numbers were confirmed for Georgel, and the 2442 CE end date attributed to Danielou could not be traced to any text and contradicts the standard arithmetic.","totalCycleYears":"4,320,000 years (mahayuga) in all members that give a figure; treated as symbolic. Savitri Devi/Serrano give none. Guenon's brief mentions a 64,800-year manvantara, which could not be confirmed as his.","durations":{"satya":"1,728,000 years (4,800 divine years x 360); Serrano gives none","treta":"1,296,000 years (3,600 divine years)","dvapara":"864,000 years (2,400 divine years)","kali":"432,000 years (1,200 divine years)"},"kaliStart":"3102 BCE (traditional epoch, accepted by Guenon, Evola and Georgel). Savitri Devi and Serrano give no date.","kaliEnd":"Not fixed by the school (qualitative end of cycle). By the literal 432,000-year count, c. 428,898-428,900 CE. Danielou is attributed an unverified end of c. 2442 CE, inconsistent with this.","currentPosition":"About 5,127-5,128 years into Kali in 2026 (roughly 1.2% of its length), but read as the deepest, late phase of spiritual darkness. Under the unverified Danielou attribution about 416 years would remain (versus about 426,900 in the standard reckoning).","variants":[{"memberId":"guenon","title":"Rene Guenon's traditionalist reading of the yugas (qualitative cycles, precession correspondence)","distinctive":"Founder; main sources La Crise du monde moderne (1927), Le Roi du monde (1927), Le Regne de la quantite (1945). Treats the numbers as symbolic and correlates the cycles with precession of the equinoxes (25,920-year Great Year) as an analogy only. Gives no date for a turn, so no falsifiable 2026 prediction. Critics cited: Eliade (1949), Sedgwick (2004), Richard King (1999); his anti-modern, reactionary stance is seen as ideological."},{"memberId":"evola-traditionalist","title":"Traditionalist school (Guenon, Evola, Schuon): yugas as qualitative spiritual decline","distinctive":"Evola (Rivolta contro il mondo moderno, 1934; Cavalcare la tigre, 1961) applies the scheme to Western history and pairs it with Hesiod's ages; later elaborated by Schuon, Lings, Nasr and Coomaraswamy. Criticized for tying hierarchy and decline to his racial theory (1941) and Fascist support, and for ignoring gains in health, literacy and life expectancy."},{"memberId":"georgel","title":"French esoteric cycle chronologies (Georgel and the Guenonian/Traditionalist milieu)","distinctive":"Gaston Georgel's own works, dates and numbers could not be verified, so no figures are attributed to him. Adds Bailly's Traite de l'astronomie indienne et orientale (1787) as a precursor claiming very ancient Indian astronomical epochs, later rejected. Notes some esoteric readers place the transition within the 20th-21st centuries, not confirmed for Georgel."},{"memberId":"danielou","title":"Alain Danielou's yuga chronology (Kali Yuga ending ~2442 CE)","distinctive":"Attributes to Alain Danielou (1907-1994) a Kali end of c. 2442 CE, which could not be traced to any text (cited works: The Myths and Gods of India, 1985; While the Gods Play, 1987). It contradicts the standard 432,000-year arithmetic, and his known scholarship treats yugas as symbolic. Category here is Academic/historical. Attribution should be treated as unverified."},{"memberId":"savitri-devi-serrano","title":"Savitri Devi and Miguel Serrano: Kali Yuga as decline with Hitlerist esoteric renewal","distinctive":"Savitri Devi (1905-1982; The Lightning and the Sun, 1958; Gold in the Furnace, 1952) presents Hitler as an avatar of Vishnu whose sacrifice ends Kali, which she blames on Jews. Miguel Serrano (1917-2009; El Cordon Dorado, 1978; Adolf Hitler, el Ultimo Avatara, 1984; Manu, 1991) says Hitler survived in Antarctica and that Aryans can restore Satya by purifying their blood. No dates are given. Based on truncated Wikipedia fetches only; primary books unread."},{"memberId":"kali-yuga-meme-culture","title":"Kali Yuga in Internet Meme Culture (Twitter/X, Reddit, 4chan, crypto and finance memes, doomerism)","distinctive":"2010s-2020s online usage as ironic shorthand for chaos, scams and market crashes; 'ride the tiger' spread via Evola-influenced circles (background knowledge, spread path unconfirmed). A KnowYourMeme culture entry exists but its content was not retrievable. Notes failed dated apocalypses in the milieu: Steiner's c. 1900 end of Kali, Nibiru (2003, 2012) and the Mayan 2012 date."}],"keyClaims":["The yugas are qualitative stages of decline from a primordial spiritual unity (Satya) to the present Kali Yuga; the cycle of decline is structural and recurring, and its numbers are symbolic.","Kali Yuga began in 3102 BCE and the modern period is its late, darkest phase.","Modernity (materialism, mechanization, egalitarianism, the reign of quantity) is the characteristic expression of Kali Yuga, and material progress coincides with spiritual decline.","The decline cannot be reversed by collective human effort; traditional knowledge survives in a small initiatic core, and individuals can preserve inner realization ('ride the tiger').","The cycles correspond analogically to the precession of the equinoxes (Guenon), and Western history (Hesiod's ages) follows the same decline pattern (Evola).","Hitler was an avatar of Vishnu or spiritual initiate who ends Kali, and Aryans can restore the Satya Yuga by blood purification (Savitri Devi, Serrano).","Kali Yuga ends c. 2442 CE (attributed to Danielou; unverified).","Online, 'Kali Yuga' is invoked ironically to describe societal, political and financial decline, usually discarding the cosmology."],"evidence":["Textual attestation of the 1:2:3:4 yuga ratio, durations and the 3102 BCE epoch in the Manusmriti, Mahabharata, Vishnu Purana and Surya Siddhanta/Aryabhata tradition.","Cross-cultural parallels (Hesiod's Ages of Man, Persian/Zoroastrian, Mesoamerican world-age schemes) read as echoes of a single primordial doctrine.","Guenon's and Evola's critiques of industrial modernity, secularization and loss of metaphysical knowledge, argued from tradition and observation rather than data; internally coherent within a metaphysics of correspondences.","Eliade-style cyclic vs linear time analysis used as a comparative foundation (Eliade was not himself a Traditionalist); Bailly (1787) as a historical precedent for Western interest in yuga chronology.","Wikipedia summaries (Savitri Devi, Miguel Serrano, Kali Yuga) and a KnowYourMeme culture entry support the offshoot descriptions; no primary text gives 2442 CE for Danielou."],"commonCriticisms":["unfalsifiable","late-origin","epoch-back-calculated","computational-numbers","decline-vs-trends","unverified-sources","failed-predictions","no-archaeology"],"specificCriticisms":["Selective, Orientalist reading: Indologists and religion scholars (Eliade, Sedgwick 2004, King 1999) say Traditionalism imposes a Western perennialist template, draws on 19th-century Orientalist and Theosophical sources, and imports a Hesiod/Spengler-style pessimism onto Hindu cosmology.","The precession correspondence is an analogy only; the 25,920-year cycle does not map onto the 4.32-million-year scheme without arbitrary multipliers.","Guenon and Evola are strongly anti-modern and in places reactionary; Evola's racial theory and Fascism are hard to separate from his decline narrative and influenced later far-right circles.","Bailly's claim of very ancient Indian astronomical knowledge was rejected; Pingree and others show the epochs date to the first millennium CE.","Georgel's specific chronology and the 64,800-year manvantara figure could not be verified or attributed.","The 2442 CE Kali end for Danielou has no traceable source, conflicts with 3102 BCE plus 432,000 years (c. 428,898 CE), and misattributes a literal chronology to a scholar who treated yugas as symbolic.","Savitri Devi/Serrano: the decline framing is inseparable from antisemitic and racial-purity ideology; blood-purity, Hitler-as-avatar, Hitler-in-Antarctica and Serrano's extragalactic/Hyperborea cosmology have no evidential support or basis in Indology or genetics. Fetches were truncated and primary books unread, so a dated claim could exist.","Meme usage makes no testable claim; doomerism pairs the dark-age framing with unfalsifiable market and crash predictions, and spread-path attribution to Evola circles is unconfirmed."],"plausibility":2,"plausibilityRange":"1-2","plausibilityReason":"As a real chronology the Kali start and durations rest on late Siddhantic conventions with no independent evidence, and the decline thesis cannot be tested; the value is as a cultural critique and intellectual history of modern esotericism. Savitri Devi/Serrano and the meme usage score 1 for having no supporting evidence or no testable claim, while the Guenon, Evola, Georgel and Danielou entries score 2.","verification":"Partly verified","references":["Rene Guenon, La Crise du monde moderne (Gallimard, 1927; English: The Crisis of the Modern World, 1942); Le Roi du monde (1927); Le Regne de la quantite et les signes des temps (1945; English trans. Lord Northbourne, 1953)","Julius Evola, Rivolta contro il mondo moderno (1934; English: Revolt Against the Modern World, Inner Traditions, 1995); Cavalcare la tigre (1961; English: Ride the Tiger, Inner Traditions, 2003)","Mircea Eliade, Le Mythe de l'eternel retour (Gallimard, 1949)","Mark Sedgwick, Against the Modern World (Oxford University Press, 2004)","Richard King, Orientalism and Religion (Routledge, 1999)","David Pingree, work on Indian astronomy and the Siddhantas (Census of the Exact Sciences in Sanskrit; verify titles)","Jean Sylvain Bailly, Traite de l'astronomie indienne et orientale (1787)","Alain Danielou, The Myths and Gods of India (Inner Traditions, 1985); Manusmriti 1.69-71 (Buhler, SBE vol. 25); Surya Siddhanta (Burgess, 1860; ed. Whitney)","Savitri Devi, The Lightning and the Sun (1958) and Gold in the Furnace (1952); Miguel Serrano, El Cordon Dorado (1978), Adolf Hitler, el Ultimo Avatara (1984), Manu (1991), Nos (1980)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Savitri_Devi (truncated fetch)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Miguel_Serrano (truncated fetch)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kali_Yuga","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yuga","https://knowyourmeme.com/memes/cultures/kali-yuga","Helena Blavatsky, The Secret Doctrine (1888) (background knowledge)","Citations for Guenon, Georgel and Danielou are from memory; URLs and page numbers were not checked"]},{"id":"western-esoteric","title":"Western esoteric re-readings of the yugas (Theosophy, Steiner, Samael Aun Weor)","category":"Modern spiritual/esoteric","members":["theosophy","steiner-1900","samael-aun-weor"],"summary":"These members are Western occult and esoteric movements that took over Hindu yuga vocabulary and layered it on their own cosmologies. Blavatsky's Theosophy (The Secret Doctrine, 1888) accepts the Puranic mahayuga (4,320,000 years) and the 3102 BCE Kali epoch but builds its history on a separate seven-root-race scheme, with Lemuria and Atlantis, from the claimed Stanzas of Dzyan. Rudolf Steiner is reported to have held that the Kali Yuga ended around 1900 and that a new spiritual (Michael) age followed, though the primary lecture was not located. Samael Aun Weor (Universal Christian Gnostic Movement) blends Gnosticism, Theosophy and Hindu cosmology, casts himself as the Kalki/Maitreya avatar of the Age of Aquarius, and gave dated personal predictions. The members keep the conventional Hindu numbers, so by the traditional count 2026 is about 5,128 years into Kali, while Steiner and Aun Weor imply a new era has begun. All rest on claimed occult revelation or secondary summaries rather than checkable evidence. The sources are heavily Western borrowings (Sclater, Donnelly, Plato, Kabbalah, Spiritualism) presented as ancient tradition.","totalCycleYears":"4,320,000 years (Puranic mahayuga) where a figure is given (Theosophy, Samael Aun Weor by convention). Theosophy also cites manvantara 306,720,000 and kalpa 4,320,000,000 years; Steiner gives no figure.","durations":{"satya":"1,728,000 years (conventional Puranic figure; not a core part of any member's scheme; Steiner none)","treta":"1,296,000 years (conventional)","dvapara":"864,000 years (conventional)","kali":"432,000 years (Theosophy; Aun Weor by convention, unverified as his figure); Steiner's duration not confirmed"},"kaliStart":"3102 BCE (Blavatsky, accepted from Indian tradition; conventional for Aun Weor, whose own date was not verified). Steiner's start not specified.","kaliEnd":"By the 432,000-year arithmetic, c. 428,898 CE (Theosophy; the Aun Weor entry's 'around 428 CE' is a truncation of this). Steiner is reported to say Kali ended in 1900 (possibly 1899), unverified.","currentPosition":"By the traditional count, about 5,128 years into Kali in 2026. In Theosophical root-race terms, the Fifth (Aryan) Root Race, Fifth Sub-race of the Fourth Round. Steiner: Kali ended and a post-Kali spiritual era (Michael age) is under way. Aun Weor: early Age of Aquarius (from 1962 per the task framing) within a Kali framework.","variants":[{"memberId":"theosophy","title":"Blavatsky and Theosophy: Yugas Within a Root-Race Framework","distinctive":"Helena Blavatsky with Olcott, Besant, Leadbeater and Judge; Theosophical Society founded 1875, Isis Unveiled 1877, The Secret Doctrine 1888, systematized by Besant (The Ancient Wisdom, 1897). Claims Atlantis sank c. 850,000 years ago and cites the Stanzas of Dzyan via Tibetan Mahatmas, never produced for examination. Criticized via the Hodgson Report (SPR, 1885), Max Muller, and Godwin and Hanegraaff, who call it bricolage; sub-race hierarchies carry racist implications."},{"memberId":"steiner-1900","title":"Rudolf Steiner (Anthroposophy): end of Kali Yuga in 1899/1900","distinctive":"Based on one Wikipedia sentence (Kali Yuga article, 'Other usage', citing a Bamford-edited lecture collection): 'Rudolf Steiner believed that the Kali Yuga ended in 1900.' The 1899 vs 1900 discrepancy is unresolved and a guessed rsarchive.org URL returned 404. This is a dated end-of-age claim that is not falsifiable by data."},{"memberId":"samael-aun-weor","title":"Samael Aun Weor (Universal Christian Gnostic Movement): Kali Yuga, Age of Aquarius from 1962, apocalyptic predictions","distinctive":"Victor Manuel Gomez Rodriguez (1917-1977), Universal Gnostic Movement founded in Mexico City after 1956. Claims: the current civilization ends (The Aquarian Message, 1960, undated); he is the Kalki/Maitreya avatar; Aquarius begins c. 1962 (task framing, not verified); his death and resurrection before 1978 (The Three Mountains, 1972). Failed prediction: he died of stomach cancer on 24 December 1977 with no return."}],"keyClaims":["Humanity passes through seven root races per round, with the Fifth (Aryan) Root Race now dominant; Lemuria and Atlantis were real sunken continents, Atlantis destroyed c. 850,000 years ago (Blavatsky).","Indian yuga, manvantara and kalpa figures describe actual cosmic cycles, and Kali began in 3102 BCE.","The Stanzas of Dzyan are an ancient esoteric source revealed through Tibetan adepts.","Kali Yuga ended in 1900 (or 1899) and a post-Kali spiritual era (Michael age) began (Steiner; unverified against primary text).","The present civilization is ending and the Age of Aquarius began around 1962; the teacher is the Kalki/Maitreya avatar (Aun Weor).","Aun Weor predicted his own death and resurrection before 1978, after an eight-year ordeal between ages 53 and 61, and that eliminating the ego avoids a 'second death'."],"evidence":["Claimed occult revelation through Mahatmas and the Stanzas of Dzyan; no independently verified manuscript.","Blavatsky's use of Sanskrit and Puranic terms shows familiarity with Indian cosmology read through a Western esoteric lens; sources for the root-race material are Sclater's Lemuria (1864), Donnelly's Atlantis (1882) and Plato.","Wikipedia Kali Yuga article cites Steiner's 1900 end (footnote to Bamford); Wikipedia Rudolf Steiner gives 1899 for his first public lectures on spiritual experience and 'Goethe's Secret Revelation' but has no Kali Yuga passage in the portion read.","Wikipedia Samael Aun Weor records the Kalki/Maitreya self-identification, the 1960 and 1972 predictions, his death on 24 December 1977, and a 1984 incorporation denial in Santa Cruz de Tenerife; the page carries neutrality and primary-source tags.","Internal consistency and the appeal of a long-view cosmology (Theosophy); no empirical support for the dated claims."],"commonCriticisms":["no-archaeology","unfalsifiable","failed-predictions","unverified-sources","astro-dating-underdetermined","late-origin","epoch-back-calculated"],"specificCriticisms":["The Hodgson Report (Society for Psychical Research, 1885) concluded Blavatsky fabricated Mahatma letters and phenomena (later scholars debate its methods); Max Muller, Subba Row and Mohini Chatterji pointed to misreadings of Sanskrit and invented 'ancient' sources.","No geological, archaeological or palaeontological evidence for Lemuria or Atlantis sinking in the claimed period; Plato's Atlantis was a moral fable. The Theosophical human-evolution scheme has no basis in biology and its sub-race hierarchies carry racist implications.","Blavatsky borrowed freely from Donnelly, Sclater, Eliphas Levi, Kabbalah and Spiritualism and presented it as ancient (Godwin, Hanegraaff: bricolage); the Stanzas of Dzyan and clairvoyant confirmations cannot be checked.","Steiner's 1899 vs 1900 end date is unresolved and rests on a single Wikipedia sentence and a Bamford compilation; Anthroposophy is not supported by mainstream historians or scientists for cosmic ages.","Aun Weor's 1960 end-of-civilization claim was undated; the 1962 Aquarius start has no astronomical consensus (precession-based starts range from the 1680s to the 2600s); self-identification as Kalki/Maitreya cannot be verified and conflicts with the traditions' own criteria."],"plausibility":1,"plausibilityRange":"1-2","plausibilityReason":"Treated as real chronology, these schemes are contradicted by geology, archaeology and genetics, depend on unverifiable revelation or unconfirmed attributions, and reuse Puranic units that are not measured durations. Steiner's attribution is plausibly real (score 2) but unverified, while Theosophy and Aun Weor score 1, and the only falsifiable dated claim (Aun Weor's pre-1978 resurrection) failed.","verification":"Partly verified","references":["Blavatsky, H. P. (1877). Isis Unveiled. New York: J. W. Bouton.","Blavatsky, H. P. (1888). The Secret Doctrine. London: Theosophical Publishing Company.","Besant, A. (1897). The Ancient Wisdom. London: Theosophical Publishing Society.","Godwin, J. (1994). The Theosophical Enlightenment. Albany: SUNY Press.","Hanegraaff, W. J. (2012). Esotericism and the Academy. Cambridge University Press.","Hodgson, R. (1885). Report on the Phenomena connected with the Theosophical Society. Proceedings of the Society for Psychical Research, vol. 3.","Sclater, P. L. (1864). The Mammals of Madagascar. Quarterly Journal of Science.","Donnelly, I. (1882). Atlantis: The Antediluvian World. New York: Harper.","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kali_Yuga","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rudolf_Steiner","Bamford, Christopher (ed.), Spiritualism, Madame Blavatsky & Theosophy: An Eyewitness View of Occult History: Lectures by Rudolf Steiner (as cited by Wikipedia; edition not verified)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Samael_Aun_Weor","Samael Aun Weor, The Aquarian Message (1960); The Three Mountains (1972); The Perfect Matrimony (1961 revised edition)","Conventional Yuga chronology: Surya Siddhanta and standard Hindu reckoning (3102 BCE Kali start, 432,000-year Kali Yuga)"]},{"id":"golden-age-dawning","title":"Golden Age / Satya Yuga dawning now (guru and sect claims)","category":"Modern guru / sect","members":["iskcon","tm-maharishi","sahaja-yoga","gayatri-pariwar-satyug","jai-gurudev","satyug-darshan","sathya-sai-prema-sai","kalki-bhagavan-oneness"],"summary":"Eight movements keep or lean on the Puranic four-yuga scheme but hold that a golden age, Satya Yuga or its spiritual equivalent, is dawning or has begun within or alongside the current Kali Yuga. Most pair this with the standard Kali count (432,000 years from 3102 BCE, ending about 428,899 CE), so the golden age is read as a reinterpretation, spiritual state or mission-driven era rather than a calendar turn of the cycle. The mechanism is usually a guru, avatar or practice: sankirtana (ISKCON), TM and the Maharishi Effect, kundalini awakening (Sahaja Yoga), Sai Baba's mission, the Oneness Blessing, or devotion and discipline (Jai Gurudev, Gayatri Pariwar). Only some give dated claims (TM 1975, Oneness 2012, Sai Baba's 1963 successor and age-96 rebirth statements). Several entries could not be verified from primary sources: Gayatri Pariwar, Sahaja Yoga and Satyug Darshan sources did not state the yuga claims attributed to them, and the Jai Gurudev and Satyug Darshan pages were not retrievable.","totalCycleYears":"4,320,000 years (one mahayuga, 4:3:2:1) in the members that state it; Sahaja Yoga and Gayatri Pariwar state none and Satyug Darshan's usage is unverified","durations":{"satya":"1,728,000 years (4,800 divine years)","treta":"1,296,000 years (3,600 divine years)","dvapara":"864,000 years (2,400 divine years)","kali":"432,000 years (1,200 divine years); TM and Oneness treat Kali as ending early (1975 / 2012)"},"kaliStart":"3102 BCE (traditional; ISKCON ties it to Krishna's departure, about 17/18 Feb)","kaliEnd":"About 428,898-428,900 CE on standard arithmetic; TM claims c. 1975 and Oneness c. 2012 as reinterpretations; Sahaja Yoga, Gayatri Pariwar and Satyug Darshan give none","currentPosition":"In 2026 about Kali year 5,127-5,128 on the traditional count, with roughly 426,870 years remaining. Members describe the present variously as a Chaitanya-era sankirtana period, an Age of Enlightenment, a changing era, or the final phase of Kali before apocalypse. No verifiable golden-age transition has occurred.","variants":[{"memberId":"iskcon","title":"Gaudiya Vaishnava / ISKCON view: Kali Yuga 432,000 years with a Chaitanya-era golden age","distinctive":"Applies Bhagavata Purana 11.5.32 (golden avatar) to Chaitanya Mahaprabhu (1486-1534) and holds harinama-sankirtana is the age's practice. Some discourses cite a roughly 10,000-year golden age tied to Chaitanya's mission, weakly sourced and not canonical. Proponents include Bhaktisiddhanta Sarasvati, Bhaktivinoda Thakura and Prabhupada (ISKCON, 1966)."},{"memberId":"tm-maharishi","title":"Maharishi Mahesh Yogi / Transcendental Meditation: Age of Enlightenment (1975)","distinctive":"Proclaimed the Age of Enlightenment c. 1975; Maharishi Effect from a 1974 16-city crime analysis (square root of 1% practising TM or TM-Sidhi/Yogic Flying, about 6,325 people); World Government of the Age of Enlightenment 1976; Maharishi Heaven on Earth 1988 (est. $100 trillion, later up to $300 trillion); 1993 Washington D.C. assembly of over 4,000; Global Country of World Peace micronation attempts 2000-2002. Randi's 1978 Fairfield check found no change in crime and farm yields matched the state average; a 1985 lawsuit over levitation and WWIII claims was dismissed as time-barred."},{"memberId":"sahaja-yoga","title":"Sahaja Yoga (Shri Mataji Nirmala Devi)","distinctive":"Founded 5 May 1970 by Nirmala Srivastava (1923-2011), claimed to be Adi Shakti. Teaches that the world is in Kali Yuga, the final phase before apocalypse. The sources did not confirm a 1970 Sahasrara opening, a Krita/Satya Yuga beginning or a dated Last Judgment; 1970 is only the founding date. Cult allegations (Abgrall, a 2005 Belgian advisory overturned on appeal in 2011, Coney on women's roles, Guardian on fees and children)."},{"memberId":"gayatri-pariwar-satyug","title":"Gayatri Pariwar / Akhil Bharatiya Sanskriti Sangh","distinctive":"Pt. Shriram Sharma Acharya (1911-1990); the 21st century as the 'Dawn of the Era of Divine Descent on Earth' and 'Change of Era - Satyug' on awgp.org, framed as social and consciousness transformation. No year, duration or present-yuga label found; the book text was not retrieved. Heifetz reads Satyug as Vedic-revivalist rhetoric; awgp.org dates Akhand Jyoti to both 1938 and 1940."},{"memberId":"jai-gurudev","title":"Baba Jai Gurudev (Mathura): Satyug aayega prophecy","distinctive":"Slogan 'Satyug aayega' attributed to a Mathura tradition: Kali is a degenerate age that will give way to Satyug through devotion and the guru's path. No date, exact wording or primary source retrieved (Wikipedia page 404)."},{"memberId":"satyug-darshan","title":"Satyug Darshan Trust (Faridabad, Sajan ji)","distinctive":"Faridabad, Haryana trust associated with Sajan ji and a claim that Satyug is arriving; nothing about its doctrine or dates was found. The entry is background only, and the framework figures are not confirmed as the Trust's."},{"memberId":"sathya-sai-prema-sai","title":"Sathya Sai Baba: Satya Yuga claims, Prema Sai successor prophecy, aftermath of his 2011 death","distinctive":"Sathya Sai Baba (1926-2011), claimed to be Shirdi Sai's reincarnation (May 1940, age 14); mission read as restoring dharma and a love-based Satya-like age. In 1963 he said a third Sai, Prema Sai, would incarnate in Mandya district, Karnataka; he also said he would be reborn eight years after death at age 96, though he died at 84 on 24 April 2011. No Prema Sai has been recognised. Sri Sathya Sai Central Trust got UN ECOSOC status in 2020. Fraud accusations (never charged); Babb called it a cult in the 1980s."},{"memberId":"kalki-bhagavan-oneness","title":"Kalki Bhagavan (Vijay Kumar Naidu) / Oneness University: 2012 Golden Age","distinctive":"Naidu founded Jeevashram 1984 and Oneness 2000. Followers saw him as Kalki; he reframed Kalki as a collective awakening spread by the 'Oneness Blessing'. A 2012 Golden Age date came via Mayan-calendar ideas (Carl Johan Calleman, 2004 Oneness Celebration) and worldwide events on 21 Dec 2012, with nothing observed. Legal and tax issues: 2002 PIL dismissed, 2004 CBI plea rejected, 2019 seizures of about Rs 93 crore. Handed to NKV Krishna and Preetha Krishna as Ekam in 2017. Wikipedia summary gave his death as 29 Sept 2026, unconfirmed."}],"keyClaims":["Kali Yuga lasts 432,000 years from 3102 BCE (Krishna's departure in ISKCON), so about 5,127 years have elapsed in 2026.","A golden age or Satya-like era is dawning or has begun through a guru, avatar or practice, without the standard cycle having turned: Chaitanya's sankirtana (ISKCON, with a roughly 10,000-year golden age in some discourses), TM and the Maharishi Effect (Age of Enlightenment from 1975), the Oneness Blessing (2012), Sai Baba's mission, devotion (Jai Gurudev's 'Satyug aayega').","Chaitanya Mahaprabhu is the golden avatar of Bhagavata Purana 11.5.32 and harinama-sankirtana is the prescribed practice of Kali.","If about the square root of 1% of a population practises TM or TM-Sidhi/Yogic Flying (about 6,325 people for ~4 billion in 1974), society-wide coherence follows; large-scale reconstruction (Heaven on Earth) would create a golden age.","Kalki is a collective awakening, not one person (Oneness); Nirmala Srivastava is Adi Shakti and the world is in Kali's final phase (Sahaja Yoga).","Sathya Sai Baba would be followed by Prema Sai (1963 statement) and reborn at age 96.","The 21st century is a new era of truth, 'Change of Era - Satyug' (Gayatri Pariwar).","Yuga decline (dharma reduced to one quarter) is literal and cosmological (ISKCON)."],"evidence":["The 1,200 divine years x 360 multiplier is internally consistent across the four yugas in the Puranas; the 3102 BCE epoch is a medieval convention tied to the Aryabhatiya (499 CE), not evidence of an event.","Bhagavata Purana 11.5.32 and the Caitanya Bhagavata and Caitanya Caritamrita anchor ISKCON's avatar claim; Chaitanya is a documented historical figure.","Maharishi Effect: 1974 16-city crime analysis, 1993 Washington D.C. assembly of over 4,000, 42 studies by TM-affiliated authors; institutions named for the era (1976 World Government, 1996 Continental Capital for Europe, 1988 Heaven on Earth, 2000-2002 micronation attempts). Randi's 1978 Fairfield check found no effect.","Sahaja Yoga: founded 5 May 1970 (Wikipedia); the article mentions Kali Yuga and apocalypse but not Sahasrara, Satya Yuga or a dated Last Judgment.","Gayatri Pariwar: awgp.org menu 'Change of Era - Satyug', 'Changing Era Age of Truth', 'transmutation of society'; no explicit statement the present is Satyug.","Sai Baba: Wikipedia confirms the 1963 successor statement (Prema Sai, Mandya), death on 24 April 2011 aged 84, UN ECOSOC status in 2020, about 1,200 centres in 114 countries.","Oneness: founding dates, 2004 Calleman event, 21 Dec 2012 events, tax and legal matters (Wikipedia summary).","Jai Gurudev and Satyug Darshan: no retrieved source; slogan circulation is background knowledge only."],"commonCriticisms":["no-archaeology","epoch-back-calculated","computational-numbers","unfalsifiable","failed-predictions","unverified-sources"],"specificCriticisms":["Golden-age claims contradict the scheme's own numbers: Kali has about 426,870 years left on the standard count, so a near-term Satyug is a reinterpretation, not a calculation.","TM: crime-statistics analyses are cherry-picked and unconfounded; the Maharishi Effect is called pseudoscience (Randi); the Fairfield test, the 1993 assembly and the Heaven on Earth and micronation plans produced no stated results.","Oneness: the 21 Dec 2012 benchmark passed with ordinary conditions; Calleman's Mayan-calendar framework is speculative; Kalki was reinterpreted as collective awakening when the individual reading failed; governance and tax controversies (2002 PIL, 2004 CBI plea, 2005 departure of Freddy Nielsen, 2019 seizures).","Sai Baba: the 1963 successor prophecy and the age-96 rebirth are unfulfilled, and age 96 is inconsistent with death at 84 plus eight years; materialisations called sleight of hand; Wikipedia neutrality banner dated July 2026.","ISKCON: applying 11.5.32 to Chaitanya is a tradition-internal reading that other groups dispute; the roughly 10,000-year golden age has no canonical verse and varies among writers; Krishna's departure (the 3102 BCE anchor) is not historically datable.","Sahaja Yoga: cult-like features and a subordinate role for women reported; Belgian 2005 advisory overturned in 2011.","Gayatri Pariwar: the Satyug language is social and rhetorical rather than a chronology, and the source site's dates conflict (1938 vs 1940).","Jai Gurudev and Satyug Darshan: no primary source, date or testable prediction located, so the claims cannot be checked.","Such movements tend to reinterpret failed dates as moral or spiritual states."],"plausibility":1,"plausibilityRange":"1-2","plausibilityReason":"The scriptural arithmetic is internally consistent, but the 432,000-year Kali, the 3102 BCE start and any near-term golden age lack empirical support. Dated predictions (TM 1975, Oneness 2012, Sai Baba's successor and rebirth) failed, and the rest are undated or unverifiable. Members scoring 2 (ISKCON, Gayatri Pariwar, Jai Gurudev) are more credible as records of how a tradition understands itself than as timelines.","verification":"Not found","references":["Bhagavata Purana, Skandha 1 and 12 (yuga passages); Skandha 11.5.32 (golden avatar)","Vishnu Purana, Books 1 and 6 (yuga durations); Manusmriti (yuga chronology)","Mahabharata, Vana Parva (Markandeya's account of the yugas)","Vrindavana Dasa, Caitanya Bhagavata (c. 1540s-1550s); Krishnadasa Kaviraja, Caitanya Caritamrita (c. 1615)","A. C. Bhaktivedanta Swami Prabhupada, Srimad-Bhagavatam and Caitanya-caritamrita purports (Bhaktivedanta Book Trust)","Edward C. Dimock Jr., The Place of the Hidden Moon (University of Chicago Press, 1966)","Edwin F. Bryant and Maria L. Ekstrand (eds.), The Hare Krishna Movement (Columbia University Press, 2004)","Aryabhatiya (499 CE) for the Kali epoch convention","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maharishi_effect","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Transcendental_Meditation_movement","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Transcendental_Meditation","James Randi, investigation of Maharishi International University claims (1978), as summarized in Wikipedia; Ted Karam on the Maharishi Effect (cited in Wikipedia)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sahaja_Yoga","Jean-Marie Abgrall, Soul Stealers; Judith Coney on women in Sahaja Yoga (background knowledge, unchecked)","https://www.awgp.org/ and https://www.awgp.org/books/twenty-first-century-the-dawn-of-the-era-of-divine-descent-on-earth (no year or yuga claim found)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shriram_Sharma_Acharya; Daniel Heifetz, The Science of Satyug (as cited, not read)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sathya_Sai_Baba and https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sri_Sathya_Sai_Baba","Lawrence A. Babb, Redemptive Encounters (1986); N. Kasturi, Sathyam Sivam Sundaram","Wikipedia, Oneness University; Carl Johan Calleman, The Mayan Calendar and the Transformation of Consciousness (2004)","Not retrieved: Wikipedia Jai_Gurudev and Satyug_Darshan_Trust (HTTP 404); Gayatri Pariwar and Yug_Nirman_Yojana pages (404). Some citations are from working knowledge and need checking."]},{"id":"kalki-claimants","title":"Kalki prophecy and modern Kalki claimants","category":"Kalki claimant / prophetic","members":["kalki","ahmadiyya-kalki","bahai-kalki","gohar-shahi","sant-rampal","kalki-dham","nithyananda-kailasa","kalki-2898-film"],"summary":"In Vaishnava scripture Kalki, the tenth avatar of Vishnu, appears at the end of Kali Yuga to restore dharma and open a new Satya Yuga. The Bhagavata Purana (12.2-3) places his birth in Shambhala, in the house of the brahmin Vishnuyasha, and the Kalki Purana adds the white horse, the sword and the war on corrupt rulers. On the traditional count (Kali from 3102 BCE, 432,000 years) this is some 427,000 years away, so every modern claim here is a reinterpretation, relocation or fictionalisation rather than a calculation. The members are modern claimants or identifications (Mirza Ghulam Ahmad, Baha'u'llah, Gohar Shahi, Rampal, Nithyananda, Kalki Bhagavan elsewhere), a place claim (Sambhal as Shambhala) and a film that sets Kalki in 2898 AD. Several rest only on Wikipedia or on sources that could not be retrieved, and few give dated, checkable predictions.","totalCycleYears":"4,320,000 years (one mahayuga), uniform across members; Rampal's group is the only one with a differing Kali count","durations":{"satya":"1,728,000 years (4,800 divine years)","treta":"1,296,000 years","dvapara":"864,000 years","kali":"432,000 years"},"kaliStart":"3102 BCE (traditional, about 17/18 February; tied to Krishna's death in the film entry)","kaliEnd":"About 428,898-428,899 CE on the traditional count (Kalki Dham entry says about 427,000 CE, which is inconsistent); no member gives a Kalki-specific date on this count","currentPosition":"In 2026 about 5,127-5,128 years of Kali have elapsed (roughly 1.2%), leaving about 426,870 years. On the scriptural reading Kalki has not appeared. Claimants place him in the past or present (Ahmad c. 1900-1908, Baha'u'llah 1863, Gohar Shahi, Rampal at the end of Kalyug) or in a fictional 2898 AD.","variants":[{"memberId":"kalki","title":"Kalki prophecy: Kali Yuga's end and the return of Satya Yuga","distinctive":"Baseline scriptural case. Sources: Mahabharata Vana Parva (Markandeya), Vishnu Purana Book 4 (c. 3rd-5th c. CE), Bhagavata Purana 12.2-3 (c. 8th-10th c.), Kalki Purana (late medieval or early modern, roughly 16th-19th c.). Scholars raise a vaticinium ex eventu concern for the detailed narrative, and the sources differ on Kalki's role, birthplace and era."},{"memberId":"ahmadiyya-kalki","title":"Mirza Ghulam Ahmad (Ahmadiyya) as Kalki","distinctive":"Ahmad (1835-1908, died 26 May 1908 in Lahore) is reported by Wikipedia (citing Juergensmeyer 2006, p. 520) to have claimed to be Kalki as well as Messiah and Mahdi, and linked himself to Krishna in Haqiqatul Wahi (1907). Ahmadis read Kalki spiritually. Critics cite the Smyth-Pigott challenge (Nov 1902), the Dowie prophecy (Sept 1902; Dowie died March 1907), the Muhammadi Begum marriage prophecy and the 1899-1907 plague prophecies (the last two not verified); Pakistan declared Ahmadis non-Muslim in 1974; the 1894-1895 eclipse sign is disputed."},{"memberId":"bahai-kalki","title":"Baha'i Faith: Baha'u'llah as Kalki","distinctive":"Some Baha'i writers identify Baha'u'llah (declaration 1863; the Bab 1844) with Kalki, citing Shoghi Effendi's God Passes By p. 94 and Bassuk (1987). It is an interpretive, apologetic reading with no official yuga timeline, no dated prediction and no Hindu recognition; the pacific mission does not match the warrior Kalki."},{"memberId":"gohar-shahi","title":"Riaz Ahmed Gohar Shahi / Messiah Foundation International","distinctive":"Pakistani-born Sufi preacher (b. 1941), founder of RAGS International (1980; renamed Messiah Foundation International in 2000, CEO Younus AlGohar). Followers call him Mahdi, Messiah and Kalki Avatar. He predicted the world would end in 2026 from an approaching comet (possibly a lesser punishment); no such event is recorded. Blasphemy convictions in Pakistan (about 59 years in absentia), books and Hatif-e-Mahdi banned, reported 2001 disappearance in London, claimed images on the Moon, Sun and Black Stone. No yuga timeline found in sources."},{"memberId":"sant-rampal","title":"Sant Rampal (Satlok Ashram)","distinctive":"Kabir-panth leader (Satlok Ashram founded 1999; successor to Ramdevanand 1994) teaching Kabir as Supreme God, with himself as the Gita's Tatvadarshi and the Quran's Baakhabar, initiating a 'thirteenth Kabir Panth' that will bring a golden era as Kalyug ends. The group says 5,505 years of Kalyug had passed by 1997, which conflicts with the 3102 BCE epoch (about 5,100). Cites Nostradamus, Jules Verne, Jean Dixon, Cheiro and Florence as having foretold him. 2014 Barwala standoff (six deaths) led to a 2018 life sentence (suspended Aug 2025) and bail in April 2026; the 2006 firing case ended in acquittal on 20 Dec 2022 and the forgery case on 1 May 2018."},{"memberId":"kalki-dham","title":"Kalki Dham (Sambhal, Acharya Pramod Krishnam)","distinctive":"Identifies Sambhal, Uttar Pradesh with Shambhala, where a Shri Kalki Vishnu Mandir exists, and promotes a larger Kalki Dham temple (India Today, 19 Feb 2024). Foundation-stone and PM Modi involvement could not be confirmed. Entangled with the Shahi Jama Masjid dispute and deadly clashes in Nov 2024; the Wikipedia Kalki Dham pages returned 404. The birthplace claim rests on textual identification only."},{"memberId":"nithyananda-kailasa","title":"Nithyananda / Kailasa","distinctive":"Swami Nithyananda (b. 1977) announced Kailaasa in Dec 2019 as a sovereign Hindu nation (reported Ecuador island, Feb 2023 UN submission, no recognition). No yuga teaching was verified. Dated failures: a free third-eye opening promised 'by 2021' (India Today, 2018) and a 7 June 2021 claim that COVID would end after a Shakti Pitha pilgrimage. Unverified background: criminal cases in India and 2019 child-abduction charges."},{"memberId":"kalki-2898-film","title":"Kalki 2898 AD (2024 film, dir. Nag Ashwin)","distinctive":"Telugu sci-fi film released 27 June 2024 (Vyjayanthi Movies; Prabhas, Amitabh Bachchan, Kamal Haasan, Deepika Padukone), set in 2898 AD about 6,000 years after the Kurukshetra war; Ashwatthama is cursed until he protects Kalki's mother. Budget about 600 crore INR, gross about 1,100 crore INR, first of a planned Kalki Cinematic Universe. The date is a fictional device, not a prophecy, though online claims tie 2898 to Kalki."}],"keyClaims":["Kalki, Vishnu's tenth avatar, ends Kali Yuga and inaugurates the next Satya (Krita) Yuga; Bhagavata Purana 12.2 places his birth in Shambhala, in Vishnuyasha's household.","The Kalki Purana describes a war on corrupt kings and Mlecchas, restored dharma and a renewed golden age.","Traditionally Kalki is about 427,000 years away; some modern writers argue he is imminent or already present.","Specific modern identifications: Mirza Ghulam Ahmad (also Krishna), Baha'u'llah, Gohar Shahi (with Mahdi and Messiah, and a 2026 comet-driven end of the world), Rampal (Kabir in human form, ending Kalyug), and Sambhal as the literal birthplace.","Rampal claims scriptures name Kabir as supreme and that earlier seers (Nostradamus and others) foretold him.","Popular culture (Kalki 2898 AD) places Kalki's arrival in 2898 AD and ties the Ashwatthama curse to it.","Nithyananda's Kailaasa is a Hindu restoration project; no yuga-specific claim was verified."],"evidence":["Bhagavata Purana 12.2-3, Vishnu Purana Book 4, Mahabharata Vana Parva and the Kalki Purana as textual sources; the 4,320,000-year mahayuga and 3102 BCE epoch are shared by Siddhantic astronomy and Puranic cosmology.","Wikipedia's Kalki article (citing Juergensmeyer 2006 and Shoghi Effendi, God Passes By p. 94) lists modern claimants, including Ahmad, Baha'u'llah, Kalki Bhagawan, Samael Aun Weor and Gohar Shahi; this shows a recurring pattern of identification. Ahmad's Krishna claim is cited from Haqiqatul Wahi (1907).","Gohar Shahi: Wikipedia confirms the titles as follower claims, the 2026 comet prediction, the Pakistani convictions and MFI leadership, with neutrality warnings.","Rampal: Wikipedia confirms the biography, lineage, the 'thirteenth Kabir Panth' claim, the 2014 standoff, the 2018 conviction and the April 2026 bail. No MFI or group yuga text beyond the 5,505-year figure was verified.","Sambhal: Wikipedia confirms the Shambhala identification and the Shri Kalki Vishnu Mandir; the Bhagavata text names no modern town.","Nithyananda: Wikipedia and India Today record the failed third-eye and COVID claims and the Kailaasa announcement.","Kalki 2898 AD: Wikipedia confirms release, plot, budget, gross and awards.","Claimant testimony and devotional literature are testimony, not independent evidence."],"commonCriticisms":["no-archaeology","epoch-back-calculated","computational-numbers","unfalsifiable","failed-predictions","retrofitting","unverified-sources"],"specificCriticisms":["The Kalki Purana is late and its detailed prophecy reads like contemporary upheaval (vaticinium ex eventu); the sources disagree on Kalki's role, birthplace and era.","Claimant identifications are partial matches: the warrior on a white horse who ends the age by force does not fit non-violent mission figures (Ahmad, Baha'u'llah), and Hindu authorities do not recognise them; Baha'i yuga links are an add-on, not doctrine.","Multiple overlapping identities (Mahdi, Messiah, Kalki, Jesus) are hard to reconcile and lack independent evidence (Gohar Shahi); Muslim orthodoxy rejects the prophetic claims and the finality of prophethood.","Rampal's Kalyug count (5,505 years by 1997) does not match the 3102 BCE epoch, and his Gita, Quran and Nostradamus matches are retrofitted from vague quatrains; his golden era is undated and unfalsifiable, with a record of violent incidents and convictions.","Ahmad's cited failures (Smyth-Pigott, Dowie, Muhammadi Begum, plague prophecies) and Gohar Shahi's 2026 comet prediction (no event as of the research date) are contested or, in part, unverified.","Nithyananda's dated claims failed (third eye by 2021, COVID in 2021), Kailaasa has no recognition, and criminal allegations weigh on credibility.","The Sambhal identification is textual and devotional (other places proposed), and the project is entangled with the Shahi Jama Masjid dispute and the Nov 2024 violence.","The 2898 AD date is fictional and a film's date blurs narrative device with cosmology; reviews noted weak narrative and a long runtime.","Several Kalki movements have fraud and financial-practice allegations."],"plausibility":1,"plausibilityRange":"1-1","plausibilityReason":"The Puranic framework is internally consistent but untestable, with Kalki about 427,000 years away. Modern claimants and date-specific readings have no verifiable basis, and where dated predictions exist (Ahmad, Nithyananda, the 2026 comet) they failed or lack a recorded outcome. The film date is fiction.","verification":"Not found","references":["Bhagavata Purana, Book 12, chapters 2-3 (trans. Tagare, Motilal Banarsidass; Dutt)","Vishnu Purana, trans. H. H. Wilson (1840), Book 4; Brahmanda Purana Kalki prophecies","Kalki Purana (critical discussion incl. Wendy Doniger; verify edition and dating)","Mahabharata, Vana Parva, Markandeya-samasya (trans. Kisari Mohan Ganguli, 1883-1896)","Wendy Doniger, The Hindus: An Alternative History (2009)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kalki (statements on Ahmad, Baha'u'llah and other claimants)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mirza_Ghulam_Ahmad; Mark Juergensmeyer (ed.), Oxford Handbook of Global Religions (2006), p. 520; Mirza Ghulam Ahmad, Haqiqatul Wahi (1907); alislam.org (not fetched)","Shoghi Effendi, God Passes By, p. 94; Daniel E. Bassuk, Incarnation in Hinduism and Christianity (1987), pp. 146-147","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Riaz_Ahmed_Gohar_Shahi; Riaz Ahmed Gohar Shahi, Deen-e-Ilahi (2000; English ed. Balboa Press, 2012)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rampal_(spiritual_leader); https://www.jagatgururampalji.org/ (summarized, not read verbatim); Kabir Sagar and Garib Das's Sadgranth; David Lorenzen on Kabir and the Kabir Panth","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sambhal; India Today, 'Kalki Mandir and why Sambhal is significant', 19 Feb 2024 (cited, not retrieved); Wikipedia Kalki Dham pages (404)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nithyananda; https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kailaasa; India Today reports 2018 and Dec 2019 (as cited by Wikipedia)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kalki_2898_AD","Note: some citations are from working knowledge without live verification; check chapters, dates and editions before publishing."]},{"id":"kali-ending-soon","title":"Kali Yuga ending soon: predicted end dates (2012, 2025, 2032 and others)","category":"Regional prophetic tradition","members":["kali-end-predictions","kali-ends-2025-viral","bhavishya-malika","veerabrahmendra"],"summary":"Traditional Hindu chronology gives Kali Yuga 432,000 years from 3102 BCE, ending about 428,899 CE, with roughly 426,800-427,000 years left in 2026. Against this, a family of claims puts the end within living memory or the near future. Some come from rival cycle schemes (Sri Yukteswar's 24,000-year cycle with a 1,200-year Kali), some from symbolic markers (the 5,000-year mark of Kali, 1898-1899), some from borrowed non-Hindu calendars (2012 via the Maya Long Count), and some from regional prophetic literature (Odia Bhavishya Malika, Telugu Kalagnanam). The viral 2025 claim most likely mistakes the elapsed Kali Samvat count (about 5,126 in 2024-2025) for the age's length. The predictions contradict one another, none are tied to an observable event, and the 2012 and 2025 dates have already passed without a transition. Sourcing is thin for the regional texts: no manuscripts were consulted and the 2032 Satya Yuga date in the Malika rests on a Wikipedia summary.","totalCycleYears":"4,320,000 years (Puranic mahayuga, 12,000 divine years x 360); Sri Yukteswar uses a 24,000-year cycle (4,800 / 3,600 / 2,400 / 1,200)","durations":{"satya":"1,728,000 years (Yukteswar: 4,800)","treta":"1,296,000 years (Yukteswar: 3,600)","dvapara":"864,000 years (Yukteswar: 2,400)","kali":"432,000 years (Yukteswar: 1,200); Bhavishya Malika (per Wikipedia): cut to about 5,000 years"},"kaliStart":"3102 BCE (traditional, midnight 17/18 Feb at Ujjain per the Surya Siddhanta); Yukteswar dates descending Kali from 701 BCE with the cycle bottom at 499 CE","kaliEnd":"Traditional about 428,898-428,900 CE. Competing claims: 2012, 2024-2032 (viral), 2025, 2442, Satya Yuga 'by 2032 AD' (Bhavishya Malika), 1898-1899 (5,000-year mark), Robin 1999, Georgel 2030 (shifted from 1999 in 1983), Jenkins 2012, Yukteswar c. 499 CE for ascending Kali and Dvapara by 1699 CE. These contradict each other.","currentPosition":"In 2026 about 5,127-5,128 years of Kali have elapsed on the traditional count (about 1.2%). The 2012 and 2025 end dates passed with no transition. Under Yukteswar we are in ascending Dvapara. Malika-based readings still treat 2026 as Kali, linking events of 2025-2026 to the prophecy. Nothing observable marks a yuga boundary.","variants":[{"memberId":"kali-end-predictions","title":"Survey of predicted end dates for Kali Yuga","distinctive":"Surveys the end dates and who proposed them: Aryabhata (Aryabhatiya, 499 CE) and the Surya Siddhanta fixed the 3102 BCE epoch; Sri Yukteswar (The Holy Science, 1894); Vivekananda (1890s) treated the 5,000-year mark (1898-1899) as a revival, not an end; 2012 came via Maya Long Count confusion; 2025 and 2442 could not be traced to a primary source. Notes Yukteswar's 24,000-year cycle versus the precession period (about 25,700-26,000 years)."},{"memberId":"kali-ends-2025-viral","title":"Viral claims that Kali Yuga ends in 2025 (or 2024-2032)","distinctive":"Anonymous social-media and YouTube claims with no identified originator. Explains the likely arithmetic origin (3102 BCE + 5,126 years = 2024-2025) and notes blending with earlier dates by Jean Robin (1999, Guenon-based), Gaston Georgel (2030) and John Major Jenkins (2012 winter solstice; his best alignment was 1998). Yukteswar's chronology gives no 2025 end (ascending Dvapara 1699 CE, Treta 4099 CE). Joscelyn Godwin notes traditional astronomers place Kali's end near 427,000 CE. Each failed date was followed by a new one."},{"memberId":"bhavishya-malika","title":"Bhavishya Malika (Achyutananda Dasa / Panchasakha, Odisha)","distinctive":"16th-century Odia prophecies attributed to Achyutananda Dasa and the Panchasakha (Ananta, Jasobanta, Jagannath, Balaram) under Prataparudra Deva; Kashinath Mishra edited a printed 'Bhavishya Malika Puran' (2023-2024). Per Wikipedia, Kali is 'cut to about 5,000 years' and Satya Yuga begins 'by 2032 AD'. Modern readings tie it to Saturn into Pisces (29 Mar 2025), the Pahalgam attack (22 Apr 2025), a solar eclipse (12 Aug 2026), a six-year-six-month third world war and seven days of darkness. Birth year conflict: 10 Jan 1510 (infobox) versus 1502 (Sahu 1997). Only plausibility score of 3 in the cluster."},{"memberId":"veerabrahmendra","title":"Pothuluri Veerabrahmendra Swami Kalagnanam (Andhra)","distinctive":"Telugu Kalagnanam attributed to Veerabrahmendra Swami (Brahmamgari Matham, Kadapa; dated 16th-17th century, 'about 400 years ago'), with multiple attributed authors, a part printed in 1970 and the Govinda Vakyas (about 320 verses). About 70 percent of the prophecies concern a coming king-avatar, Veera Bhoga Vasanta Rayalu. Followers claim it names Gandhi and Indira Gandhi and covers events to the end of Kali; it is called the 'Nostradamus of India'. No specific end-of-Kali date was found in the sources."}],"keyClaims":["The Kali epoch of 3102 BCE was computed by Indian astronomers (Aryabhata and the Siddhantas), not observed; with the 432,000-year length Kali ends about 428,899 CE.","Kali Yuga lasts about 5,000 years (a misreading of the elapsed Samvat count), so it ends around 2024-2025, possibly up to 2032, followed by a Satya Age with Kalki or a precessional shift.","The Bhavishya Malika says Kali is cut to about 5,000 years and Satya Yuga begins 'by 2032 AD', with a signature sequence of 2025-2026 events.","Yukteswar's 24,000-year cycle shows the world has already left Kali (ascending Dvapara from 1699 CE).","The 5,000-year mark (1898-1899) was treated by Vivekananda and others as the start of a spiritual revival, not an end.","2012 (Maya Long Count; Jenkins) and 1999 / 2030 (Robin, Georgel) are other proposed turning points; 2442 and 2025 are not traceable to a canonical source.","The Kalagnanam foretells events from its own time to the end of Kali, centred on the king-avatar Veera Bhoga Vasanta Rayalu."],"evidence":["Puranic and Siddhantic texts give the 4:3:2:1 ratio and the 12,000-divine-year mahayuga; the Aryabhatiya (499 CE) and Surya Siddhanta give the 3102 BCE epoch; 3102 BCE + 432,000 years gives 428,899 CE with no year zero.","Yukteswar, The Holy Science (1894) states the 24,000-year cycle with a 1,200-year Kali (background knowledge; Wikipedia gives ascending Dvapara from 1699 CE).","The 2012 claim has a documented origin in Mesoamerican calendar debates (13th b'ak'tun end, 21 Dec 2012), not Hindu texts; Wikipedia's Yuga cycle article records Georgel's 1983 shift from 1999 to 2030, Robin's 1999 and Jenkins' 2012.","Kali Samvat arithmetic: 3102 BCE to 2025 CE is about 5,126 years, the number Indian almanacs print (background knowledge).","Wikipedia's Maalika and Achyutananda Dasa articles (summarized by WebFetch, wording unchecked; thin sourcing, 'needs more citations' banner Feb 2025) support the Malika's attribution, 2032 claim and modern event list.","Wikipedia's Potuluri Veerabrahmam, Kalagnanam, Brahmamgari Matham and Banaganapalli articles confirm the saint's tradition, multiple authorship, the 1970 printing, the Govinda Vakyas and the king-avatar theme; a 1909 mention of the Tamil Acharya-Darpana predates modern prophecy claims."],"commonCriticisms":["epoch-back-calculated","precession-error","computational-numbers","unfalsifiable","failed-predictions","retrofitting","elapsed-vs-total","unverified-sources"],"specificCriticisms":["The 2012 date comes from a misreading of the Maya Long Count and has no Hindu textual basis; Yukteswar's chronology gives no 2025 end-date and contradicts the viral claim.","Dated end predictions have repeatedly failed (1999, 2012, 2025) and each was followed by a new date rather than a reassessment; the viral posts cite no primary text, author or method.","Traditional astronomers never gave a calendar end for Kali; a near-term end contradicts the tradition it cites, as the Malika's 5,000-year Kali and 2032 Satya Yuga claims contradict the 432,000-year length without derivation.","The Malika's 2025-2026 event list (Saturn, Pahalgam, eclipse, third world war) is modern interpretation, not clearly dated manuscript text; the Wikipedia URL 'Bhavishya_Malika' 404'd, there is an inline [citation needed] on the farmers claim, and the birth year conflict (1502 vs 1510) is unresolved.","Authorship and dating of the Malika and the Kalagnanam are unstable (Panchasakha group; 16th vs 17th century, multiple authors, parts in different custodial locations); the claim that the Kalagnanam names Gandhi and Indira Gandhi is not independently supported.","The Brahmamgari Matham and Banaganapalli promotional sources have a commercial and pilgrimage interest in the prophecies; the Wikipedia 'Kalki' article carries an LLM-generated-content notice.","No dated, checked predictions were found for the Kalagnanam; the 2442 and 2025 dates could not be traced to a primary source, and none of the entries consulted primary texts or ran live searches."],"plausibility":1,"plausibilityRange":"1-3","plausibilityReason":"As literal chronology no near-term end date is supported: the 3102 BCE epoch is a real astronomical convention but the 432,000-year length and any end date are untestable, and the 2012 and 2025 dates have already failed. The Bhavishya Malika scores higher (3) as a genuine Odia prophetic corpus with standard Kali themes, although its 2032 Satya Yuga and 5,000-year Kali are unverified and inconsistent with Puranic length.","verification":"Partly verified","references":["Vishnu Purana, H. H. Wilson translation (1840); Bhagavata Purana (Kalki and end-of-Kali passages)","Aryabhatiya (499 CE), Kalakriya and Gola chapters; Surya Siddhanta, Kali epoch passages","Sri Yukteswar Giri, The Holy Science (1894)","Swami Vivekananda, lectures of the 1890s (Complete Works)","Mesoamerican Long Count background for 2012 (academic treatments of the 2012 phenomenon)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yuga; https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yuga_cycle (Georgel, Robin, Jenkins); https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sri_Yukteswar_Giri; https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kalki; https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2012_phenomenon","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maalika (the 'Bhavishya_Malika' URL returned 404); https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Achyutananda","Sahu (1997), cited on Wikipedia for Achyutananda's birth year 1502; Kashinath Mishra, Bhavishya Malika Puran (2023-2024), as cited on Wikipedia","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Potuluri_Veerabrahmam; https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kalagnanam; https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brahmamgari_Matham; https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Banaganapalli; https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yaganti_temple; https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kadapa_district; https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hindu_eschatology; http://www.kalagnani.com (archived 28 Oct 2016)"]},{"id":"alternative-chronology","title":"Alternative chronologies: re-dating the epics and Vedas against the Kali epoch","category":"Alternative chronology","members":["nilesh-oak","kota-venkatachalam","saroj-bala-iserve","abhyankar-mohenjo-daro","mahabharata-war-dating","tilak","kak","praveen-mohan-shivoham"],"summary":"These members leave the traditional 4,320,000-year mahayuga (4:3:2:1 split) essentially untouched and instead try to anchor events in the epics or Vedas to calendar dates, usually by matching described planetary positions, eclipses or nakshatra data against computed skies or by re-dating figures such as the Buddha. The reference point is the traditional Kali epoch of 3102 BCE (Aryabhata, 499 CE; Aihole inscription 634 CE), with the Mahabharata war placed about 36 years earlier (c. 3138 BCE) on a backward count. Authors disagree on the war and epic dates: Achar c. 3067 BCE, Saroj Bala/I-SERVE 3139 BCE (Rama 5114 BCE), Oak 5561 BCE (Ramayana 12209 BCE), while Tilak and Kak push Vedic antiquity back to c. 4500-2500 BCE or earlier without proposing a yuga timeline. Several entries (Venkatachalam, Praveen Mohan/Shivoham, Abhyankar details) could not be checked against the authors' own work. Mainstream scholarship dates the epics' composition to roughly 400 BCE-400 CE and the Rigveda to c. 1500-1000 BCE, treats yuga numbers as cosmological, and finds the astronomical matching underdetermined.","totalCycleYears":"4,320,000 years (traditional mahayuga) in all members that state one; Tilak gives none and Kak and Abhyankar do not reconstruct one.","durations":{"satya":"1,728,000 years (traditional; 4,800 divine years)","treta":"1,296,000 years (traditional; 3,600 divine years)","dvapara":"864,000 years (traditional; 2,400 divine years)","kali":"432,000 years (traditional; 1,200 divine years). Not stated by Tilak, Kak or Abhyankar."},"kaliStart":"3102 BCE (midnight 17/18 February, proleptic Julian, Ujjain meridian). Oak's earlier war date implies a re-anchoring that was not verified; Abhyankar's own proposed date was not found; Tilak and Kak give none.","kaliEnd":"About 428,898-428,899 CE on the traditional count (3102 BCE + 432,000 years).","currentPosition":"About 5,127-5,128 years into Kali in 2026 on the 3102 BCE reckoning (roughly 426,873 years remaining). On Oak's chronology the war would be about 7,587 years ago, implying a much earlier Kali start. Tilak's work gives no yuga position.","variants":[{"memberId":"nilesh-oak","title":"Nilesh Oak: Mahabharata war 5561 BCE, Ramayana 12209 BCE","distinctive":"Places the war about 2,400 years before the Kali epoch and the Ramayana around 12209 BCE, by retrodicting epic astronomy. Reviewers criticise his selective use of sources; figures come from secondary summaries and were not checked against his books."},{"memberId":"kota-venkatachalam","title":"Kota Venkatachalam: Buddha 1887 BCE","distinctive":"Moves the Buddha to about 1887 BCE and reads Puranic genealogies and Kali reckoning as literal history. Contradicts the mainstream 5th-4th century BCE date from Buddhist texts, Ashokan inscriptions and Greek synchronisms. His work could not be retrieved; the entry is a generic reconstruction."},{"memberId":"saroj-bala-iserve","title":"Saroj Bala / I-SERVE: planetarium dating","distinctive":"Planetarium software dates Rama to 5114 BCE (Neolithic, before Harappan cities) and the Mahabharata war to 3139 BCE (Early Harappan), about 37 years before the 3102 BCE Kali epoch, so the dates look consistent with tradition. Peer review of the method was not confirmed."},{"memberId":"abhyankar-mohenjo-daro","title":"K. D. Abhyankar: Mohenjo-daro seals","distinctive":"Per a one-sentence Wikipedia summary of Abhyankar (1993, Bull. Astron. Soc. India 21, 'Astronomical significance to two Mohenjodaro seals'), Kali begins with an extremely rare planetary alignment depicted on the seals. Planets and date were not found; the seal reading has no wider acceptance."},{"memberId":"mahabharata-war-dating","title":"Archaeoastronomical dating of the war and the Kali link","distinctive":"Surveys the genre: Achar's c. 3067 BCE date from Bhishma parva sky descriptions conflicts with the 36-year gap to 3102 BCE. Cites Painted Grey Ware at Hastinapura (B.B. Lal, c. 1200-600 BCE) as a compatible but non-confirming settlement. Notes Pingree's finding that no real conjunction matches the 3102 BCE epoch."},{"memberId":"tilak","title":"B.G. Tilak: Orion and Arctic Home","distinctive":"Not a yuga scheme. The Orion (1893) dates the Mrigashira equinox era to c. 4500 BCE and the Krittika (Brahmana) era to c. 2500 BCE; Arctic Home in the Vedas (1903) argues for a circumpolar origin. Hermann Jacobi (c. 1894) reached a similar date, though not fully independently. The Arctic thesis is largely rejected in favour of a steppe homeland (Mallory), and Tilak's nationalist motives are noted."},{"memberId":"kak","title":"Subhash Kak: astronomical code of the Rigveda","distinctive":"Argues hymn numbers, metres and Agnicayana altar geometry encode astronomical cycles and imply a composition several thousand years before the mainstream c. 1500-1200 BCE. Yuga cycles appear only as broad framing, with no specific yuga figures of his own verified. Criticised for post hoc pattern-finding and nationalist appropriation."},{"memberId":"praveen-mohan-shivoham","title":"Praveen Mohan / Project Shivoham","distinctive":"YouTube presenters pairing the classical yuga scheme with claims of lost ancient advanced technology (vimanas, weapons). Their pages could not be fetched, so no dated predictions or specific timelines were verified; no failed predictions are recorded."}],"keyClaims":["The Mahabharata war is a real, datable event; its sky descriptions can be matched to a specific date: c. 3067 BCE (Achar), 3139 BCE (Saroj Bala/I-SERVE), c. 3138 BCE (backward count from the Kali epoch with a 36-year gap to Krishna's death), or 5561 BCE (Oak).","The Ramayana is earlier still: 5114 BCE (Saroj Bala/I-SERVE) or 12209 BCE (Oak).","The traditional Kali epoch of 3102 BCE, corroborated by the Aihole inscription (634 CE), is a usable historical anchor, or is displaced by earlier dates (Oak).","The Buddha lived c. 1887 BCE, far earlier than mainstream dating (Venkatachalam, unverified).","Kali's start is an extremely rare planetary alignment depicted on Mohenjo-daro seals (Abhyankar, per Wikipedia).","Vedic texts record astronomy implying antiquity far above 1500 BCE: Orion era c. 4500 BCE and Krittika era c. 2500 BCE (Tilak), an Arctic homeland (Tilak), or a Rigveda astronomical code and encoded altar geometry (Kak).","Ancient Indian texts describe lost advanced technology such as vimanas (Praveen Mohan/Shivoham, paraphrased).","Epic and Vedic astronomical references are reliable literal observations that serve as a chronological clock."],"evidence":["Aryabhatiya (499 CE): 3,600 Kali years had elapsed at Aryabhata's age of 23, fixing the 3102 BCE epoch; the Aihole inscription (634 CE) gives 3735 Kali years at Saka 556.","Pre-modern and modern epic-war estimates differ widely: Aihole 3137 BCE, Varahamihira and Kalhana 2449 BCE (653 years after the Kali epoch), c. 1400 BCE (Puranic genealogies), 950 BCE (Pargiter), 836 BCE (B. B. Lal).","Planetarium software reliably reconstructs historical skies; the contested step is matching epic text to sky, and Achar's 3067 BCE and Bala's 3139 BCE differ for the same text.","Painted Grey Ware layers at Hastinapura (B.B. Lal, 1950s), c. 1200-600 BCE, are compatible with an epic-era settlement.","Mausala parva places Krishna's death 36 years after the war, used for backward dating.","Taittiriya Samhita and Brahmana passages on the Krittikas marking the year's start; Vedic long-dawn imagery read as Arctic memory; precession is real, so the method is physically valid in principle.","Shatapatha Brahmana altar dimensions that Kak relates to solar-year and lunar-cycle quantities; Abhyankar (1993), Bull. Astron. Soc. India 21, 477 (Bibcode 1993BASI...21..475A).","Standard sources for the 4,320,000-year scheme: Wikipedia 'Yuga', Bhagavata Purana."],"commonCriticisms":["no-archaeology","epoch-back-calculated","astro-dating-underdetermined","late-origin","computational-numbers","unfalsifiable","unverified-sources"],"specificCriticisms":["Achar's 3067 BCE date does not fit the 36-year gap to 3102 BCE, so his date and the Kali link pull in opposite directions, and proponents rarely address this.","Circularity: the 3102 BCE epoch is itself a retroactive construct, so using it to validate a war date assumes the conclusion; dating events from a text's own sky descriptions is circular unless they are independently dated.","The epics grew in layers over centuries, so a single war date presumes a historical core the textual evidence does not establish; astronomical passages are poetic and often later insertions.","Proposed epic dates fall in the Neolithic (Rama 5114 BCE) or Early Harappan period (3139 BCE) and the epics describe neither; PGW and iron-age material (c. 1200-600 BCE) fit better. No inscription or seal directly attests the war.","The Buddha at 1887 BCE conflicts with textual, epigraphic and archaeological evidence and Greek and Chinese synchronisms.","Tilak's nakshatra method is highly sensitive to vague wording; Jacobi's agreement is not fully independent; the Arctic thesis ignores Indo-European linguistic evidence pointing to a steppe homeland.","Kak's astronomical 'code' is not specified in advance, so many post hoc mappings can be found with no control against chance; it was published largely outside peer-reviewed Indology and astronomy venues and is open to nationalist appropriation.","Abhyankar's Mohenjo-daro seals are read by archaeologists as religious, ritual or administrative with no astronomical consensus; a single 1993 note cannot establish a yuga epoch, and only a secondary summary was available.","Ancient advanced technology claims lack material evidence and rest on selective readings of Sanskrit verses; internet chronology channels tend to revise dates after they pass.","Several members (Oak, Venkatachalam, Bala/I-SERVE, Praveen Mohan) could not be checked against the authors' own texts, so parts of these entries describe the genre rather than confirmed statements."],"plausibility":2,"plausibilityRange":"1-3","plausibilityReason":"Pure re-dating of the epics scores 1-2 because the specific dates lack archaeological, textual or genetic support and the sky-matching step is underdetermined. The Mahabharata war dating entry scores 3 for allowing a Bronze or early Iron Age historical core, though its precise dates rest on computed or interpolated astronomy. None of these shows that yuga durations describe real past ages.","verification":"Not found","references":["Aryabhata, Aryabhatiya (499 CE), Kalakriya section; Aihole inscription of Ravikirti (634 CE)","Varahamihira, Brhat Samhita (6th c. CE); Kalhana, Rajatarangini (11th c. CE)","Sukthankar, V.S. et al., The Mahabharata, critical edition, BORI, Poona, 1933-1966","Achar, B.N.N., papers on dating the Mahabharata war (3067 BCE) by planetary positions (2000s); exact citation to verify","Lal, B.B., Hastinapura excavations (1950s), ASI reports","Pingree, David, writings on Siddhantic astronomy, the Kali epoch and Vedanga Jyotisha","Abhyankar, K. D. (1993). Astronomical significance to two Mohenjodaro seals. Bull. Astron. Soc. India 21: 477 (Bibcode 1993BASI...21..475A), cited via https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kali_Yuga","Tilak, B.G., The Orion, or Researches into the Antiquity of the Vedas (1893); The Arctic Home in the Vedas (1903)","Jacobi, H. (1894), Indian Antiquary, on Vedic antiquity","Witzel, M., essays on Vedic chronology and dating of the Rigveda; Parpola, A., on Vedic and Indus-Sarasvati chronology; Mallory, J.P., In Search of the Indo-Europeans (1989)","Kak, S., The Astronomical Code of the Rigveda (verify publisher and year); Kak, S., 'Astronomy of the Vedic altars', Vistas in Astronomy (verify citation)","Nilesh Oak, self-published books and talks on the Mahabharata and Ramayana dates (not accessed); Oak, P.N., popular works (fringe, not peer reviewed)","Wikipedia, 'Historicity of the Mahabharata'; Wikipedia, 'Yuga' (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yuga); Bhagavata Purana (kalpa = 1,000 catur-yugas)","Background on mainstream Buddha chronology (Bechert, Gombrich); Wikipedia pages for Kota Venkatachalam, Praveen Mohan and Project Shivoham returned HTTP 404"]},{"id":"short-cycle-brahma-kumaris","title":"Brahma Kumaris 5,000-year cycle","category":"Modern guru / sect","members":["brahma-kumaris"],"summary":"The Brahma Kumaris (Prajapita Brahma Kumaris Ishwariya Vishwa Vidyalaya), founded in 1936 in Hyderabad, Sindh by Dada Lekhraj (Brahma Baba), teach an eternal, naturally recurring 5,000-year cycle of four equal ages of 1,250 years: Golden, Silver, Copper and Iron. Golden and Silver form the first half, Copper and Iron the second. The present is described as a Confluence Age, sometimes called a fifth age, between Iron and Golden. At the end of the Iron Age a 'Destruction' cleanses the earth before the cycle repeats. Early teachings stressed physical destruction with specific dates, which were later reframed as a process of transformation, and the group now emphasizes meditation and peace of mind. All content here comes from Wikipedia and its cited scholarship; the group's own site and books could not be used to confirm it.","totalCycleYears":"5,000 years (four ages of 1,250 years each)","durations":{"satya":"1,250 years (Golden Age)","treta":"1,250 years (Silver Age)","dvapara":"1,250 years (Copper Age)","kali":"1,250 years (Iron Age)"},"kaliStart":"Not specified in the sources checked","kaliEnd":"Not specified; the cycle ends with 'Destruction' at the end of the Iron Age, with no date given","currentPosition":"In the Confluence Age (sometimes a fifth age) between Iron and Golden. Current stance on physical destruction could not be confirmed from brahmakumaris.org.","variants":[{"memberId":"brahma-kumaris","title":"Brahma Kumaris (Prajapita Brahma Kumaris Ishwariya Vishwa Vidyalaya)","distinctive":"Failed predictions per Walliss (2002): an original date of 1976 and violent destruction between 1987 and 2008; Jain (1983) says the 1987 date 'changed many times'. After WWII, the atomic bombings and the Cold War the destructive aspects were reframed as transformation (Hinduism Today 1995). Wikipedia does not mention 1982 or 2000; the brahmakumaris.org concepts page returned a 404."}],"keyClaims":["Eternal, naturally occurring 5,000-year cycle of four 1,250-year ages (Barrett 2001, via Wikipedia)","Golden and Silver ages form the first half of the cycle; Copper and Iron the second","The present is a Confluence Age between Iron and Golden, sometimes called a fifth age","The end of the Iron Age brings 'Destruction' that kills everyone and cleanses the earth before the cycle repeats","Original teachings named 1976; followers predicted violent destruction between 1987 and 2008 (Walliss 2002)","Destruction was later reframed as a process of transformation (Hinduism Today 1995)"],"evidence":["Wikipedia 'Brahma Kumaris' (fetched 2026): cycle, four ages, Confluence Age, Destruction, 1976, 1987-2008 failed predictions, reframing as transformation","Jain (1983), cited by Wikipedia, on the shifting 1987 date","brahmakumaris.org homepage: peace and meditation focus, no cycle-of-ages content found","Wikipedia notes the group maintains its primary purpose is teaching meditation and peace of mind"],"commonCriticisms":["failed-predictions","unverified-sources","no-archaeology"],"specificCriticisms":["Critics read the reframing of Destruction as 'transformation' as a retreat from falsified predictions (Walliss 2002; Beit-Hallahmi 2003)","The 1976 and 1987-2008 dates rest on secondary sources as reported by Wikipedia; no primary Brahma Kumaris text was retrieved","The Wikipedia neutrality section is flagged as disputed","The 5,000-year four-equal-age structure is a Yuga-derived scheme with no empirical basis"],"plausibility":2,"plausibilityRange":"2-2","plausibilityReason":"The 5,000-year four-age structure is a Yuga-derived scheme with no empirical basis, and its apocalyptic dates (1976, 1987-2008) are documented in secondary scholarship as failed predictions. Primary-text confirmation was not obtained.","verification":"Partly verified","references":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brahma_Kumaris","https://www.brahmakumaris.org/","Barrett, D. (2001). The New Believers","Walliss, J. (2002). The Brahma Kumaris: Religion, Gender and Modernity","Whaling, F. (2012). Religion in Today's World","Jain (1983), as cited in Wikipedia","Beit-Hallahmi, B. (2003), as cited in Wikipedia","Hinduism Today (1995), as cited in Wikipedia","Jones and Ryan (2010). Encyclopedia of Hinduism"]},{"id":"indic-other-traditions","title":"Other Indic traditions' time cycles: Jain, Buddhist, Kalachakra, Sikh and Ayyavazhi","category":"Traditional scriptural","members":["jain","buddhist","kalachakra","sikh","ayyavazhi-kali-yukam"],"summary":"These members show how non-Brahmanical Indic traditions relate to the Hindu four-yuga decline scheme. Jainism has a beginningless wheel of Avasarpini (descending) and Utsarpini (ascending), each with six aras in a 4:3:2:1 pattern, with the present fifth ara (Dushama, 21,000 years) following Mahavira's nirvana. Buddhism uses kalpas (a mahakalpa of about 1.344 billion years) and a separate Dharma-decline sequence (Mappo, 10,000 years from 1052 CE in the Japanese reckoning), not yugas. The Kalachakra Tantra (c. 1025-1027 CE) adopts the Hindu mahayuga and adds a Shambhala Kalki war against mlecchas, usually dated c. 2424 CE. The Sikh scriptures use Kal Jug as a moral and spiritual condition with no numeric chronology. Ayyavazhi counts the yugas differently, treating Kali as the seventh yuga, followed by an eighth, Dharma Yukam, with no durations given. All of these are doctrinal or soteriological frames; none offers a testable real-world timeline.","totalCycleYears":"No single consensus. Kalachakra adopts the Hindu 4,320,000-year mahayuga; Jain full cycle is 20 kotakoti sagaropama (not expressible in years); Buddhist mahakalpa is about 1,344,000,000 years; Sikh and Ayyavazhi sources give none.","durations":{"satya":"Only Kalachakra gives the Hindu value (1,728,000 years). Jain analogue: aras 1-2 (4 and 3 kotakoti sagara). Buddhism, Sikhism and Ayyavazhi give no equivalent (Buddhist closest analogue is the Saddharma age of 500 or 1,000 years).","treta":"Kalachakra 1,296,000 years. Jain ara 3 (about 2 kotakoti sagara less 42,000 years). No equivalent elsewhere.","dvapara":"Kalachakra 864,000 years. Jain ara 4 (about 1 kotakoti sagara less 42,000 years), containing the 24 Tirthankaras. Ayyavazhi gives no duration, but the Santror are born at its close.","kali":"Kalachakra 432,000 years. Jain aras 5 and 6, 21,000 years each (42,000 total, one-tenth of Hindu Kali). Buddhist Mappo 10,000 years. Ayyavazhi Kali Yukam is the seventh yuga, no length given. Sikh Kal Jug has no length."},"kaliStart":"Traditions anchor differently: Jain fifth ara traditionally c. 523 BCE, about 3 years 8.5 months after Mahavira's nirvana (Svetambara 527 BCE; Digambar c. 605 BCE; Jacobi c. 468 BCE). Buddhist Mappo from 1052 CE (Buddha's death taken as 949 BCE). Kalachakra and Hindu reckoning: 3102 BCE. Ayyavazhi: birth of the demon Kali at the close of Dvapara, no date. Sikh scripture gives none.","kaliEnd":"Jain fifth ara ends c. 20,478 CE and sixth c. 41,478 CE on the traditional anchor. Buddhist Mappo c. 11052 CE. Kalachakra Shambhala-Kalki war c. 2424 CE (some sources 2327 CE); Hindu-style Kali end c. 428,899 CE. Ayyavazhi: Vaikundar destroys Kali and Dharma Yukam follows, undated. Sikh: none.","currentPosition":"2026 CE: in the Jain fifth ara, about 2,549 years in with about 18,450 remaining; in Mappo about 974 years in (Japanese reckoning); inside Kalachakra's Kali, about 400 years before the cited Kalki war; in Ayyavazhi Kali Yukam; in Sikh Kal Jug only in a spiritual sense (Hindu external count Kali year 5128).","variants":[{"memberId":"jain","title":"Jain time cycle: Utsarpini/Avasarpini and the six aras","distinctive":"Time has no beginning, creator or dissolution. The 42,000-year adjustment in aras 3 and 4 is ad hoc. No Tirthankaras and no liberation in Bharata in the present ara. Paul Dundas notes the ratio parallel to Hindu yugas, and Videha lies outside the ara cycle."},{"memberId":"buddhist","title":"Buddhist kalpa and world-age cosmology (Abhidharma, Dharma decline, Mappo)","distinctive":"Mahakalpa = 4 phases of 20 antarakalpas, antarakalpa 16,800,000 years, with human lifespan rising and falling between 10 and 84,000 years (Abhidharmakosa; DN 26). Current Bhadrakalpa has Shakyamuni as the fourth Buddha and Maitreya the fifth. Mappo's Buddha-death date of 949 BCE conflicts with the modern c. 480 BCE (range c. 400-480 BCE). Nattier (1991) shows Dharma-decline prophecies are later developments (vaticinia ex eventu)."},{"memberId":"kalachakra","title":"Kalachakra Tantra, Shambhala and Kalki","distinctive":"11th-century text (c. 1025-1027 CE; Tibetan arrival c. 1027 via Somanatha and Chag Lotsawa) presented as a Buddha teaching at Dhanyakataka. The Shambhala king Raudra Chakrin defeats mleccha invaders, widely read as Islamic invaders and tied to Ghaznavid incursions. The 2424 CE war date is a future prediction; Dorzhiev's political use and Western occult appropriation (Bishop 1989; Godwin 1993) are noted. Many teachers read the war symbolically."},{"memberId":"sikh","title":"Sikh scriptures on the four yugas","distinctive":"Guru Granth Sahib and Dasam Granth (Bachitar Natak) give no durations or dates; liberation comes through Naam and grace regardless of age. Dasam Granth authorship and cosmological status are disputed. Bhai Gurdas uses Kal Jug for the moral decline of his own time, and McLeod sees Sikh texts distancing themselves from the Hindu cosmological frame. Citations were not checked line by line."},{"memberId":"ayyavazhi-kali-yukam","title":"Ayyavazhi: Kali Yukam and the Santror/Dharma Yukam scheme","distinctive":"Per the Akilattirattu Ammanai (via Wikipedia), Kali is the seventh yuga, the 'age of deterioration', begun with the birth of the demon Kali. Vaikundar (life c. 1809-1851 CE) destroys Neesan and Kali Yuga. Eighth yuga Dharma Yukam, beyond time and space. The Ammanai writing date conflicts (1839 vs 1841 CE), and 1008 M.E. for the incarnation (roughly 1832-33 CE, own calculation) is uncited. Jeffrey and Patrick treat the movement as a 19th-century reform movement."}],"keyClaims":["Jain: time is a beginningless wheel of Utsarpini and Avasarpini, each 10 kotakoti sagaropama with six aras. The descending aras run 4, 3, 2, 1 kotakoti sagara (with a 42,000-year correction), then 21,000 and 21,000 years. We are in the fifth ara, begun after Mahavira's nirvana, in which the 24 Tirthankaras and 63 shalakapurushas belong to the fourth.","Buddhist: a mahakalpa has four phases (formation, abiding, destruction, empty abiding) of 20 antarakalpas, about 1.344 billion years; the world is destroyed by fire, water and wind; Maitreya appears when lifespan returns to about 80,000-84,000 years; the Dharma declines in stages (true, counterfeit, degenerate), with Mappo lasting 10,000 years from 1052 CE.","Kalachakra: it uses the Hindu four-yuga scheme and mahayuga of 4,320,000 years; a Buddha teaching given at Dhanyakataka and carried to Shambhala; Kalki kings of Shambhala fight the mlecchas, commonly c. 2424 CE; it maps outer cosmic cycles onto the body (outer, inner, alternative); Shambhala is a hidden land in tradition but symbolic in scholarship.","Sikh: Kal Jug is a moral and spiritual condition, not a period of years; scripture gives no yuga durations, and belief in the Puranic 432,000-year Kali is not required; ritual, caste and purity do not give liberation in any age.","Ayyavazhi: Kali Yukam is the seventh yuga, begun when the demon Kali is born at the close of Dvapara; the Santror are born at that close; Vaikundar destroys Kali and takes the righteous to Vaikundam in Dharma Yukam; the number of yugas and avatars differs from Hinduism."],"evidence":["Jain: the 4:3:2:1 scheme and the 42,000-year correction are given in the canon and medieval cosmographies and are consistent across Svetambara and Digambar texts; Pali Nikayas mention Niganttha Nataputta (Mahavira) as a contemporary teacher of c. 6th-5th century BCE.","Buddhist: DN 26, DN 27 and SN 15.5, and Vasubandhu's Abhidharmakosa with Yashomitra's commentary give the numbers; Chinese and Japanese texts use the 1052 CE Mappo date, cited by Shinran's Kyogyoshinsho (13th c.).","Kalachakra: the Kalachakra Tantra and Vimalaprabha date to about 1025-1027 CE (Davidson 2002; Wallace 2001); the mleccha war imagery fits 11th-century anxiety about northwest invasions.","Sikh: the Guru Granth Sahib uses Jug, Satjug and Kal Jug metaphorically; Japji Sahib places ages below the Creator; Bhai Gurdas's Vaaran apply Kal Jug to moral decline.","Ayyavazhi: Wikipedia's Ayyavazhi and Akilattirattu Ammanai pages (relayed via summarisation, not checked against the primary text) give Kali as the seventh yuga and Dharma Yukam as the eighth."],"commonCriticisms":["unfalsifiable","late-origin","failed-predictions","no-archaeology","unverified-sources"],"specificCriticisms":["The Jain 42,000-year adjustment looks designed to make a neat total, and the cycle was systematised centuries after Mahavira.","Anchor dates are contested: Mahavira's nirvana is dated 527, c. 605 or c. 468 BCE, shifting the Jain ara boundary by about 80-140 years; the Mappo start rests on a Buddha death date (949 BCE) that modern scholarship rejects.","Dharma-decline prophecies were written after the fact (vaticinia ex eventu) and Buddhism continued to flourish past each predicted endpoint; the Kalachakra 2424 CE war prophecy is read as allegory of 11th-century anxieties.","Forced one-to-one mappings to Hindu yugas mislead: Jain Dushama is 21,000 years against Hindu Kali's 432,000; the direction of borrowing between Jain and Hindu cosmology is unsettled.","Kalachakra and Shambhala were used politically (Dorzhiev) and appropriated by Western occultists and Nazi-era esoteric writers; the literal war reading is contested within Tibetan Buddhism.","The Sikh scriptures supply no chronology, so any timeline is an extrapolation, and Dasam Granth authorship is disputed; popular Sikh discourse sometimes adds more precision than scripture does.","Ayyavazhi sources give no numeric durations or end date, the Ammanai writing date conflicts across passages (1839 vs 1841), and the 1809-1851 life dates are sometimes conflated with an incarnation date.","Several members (Jain, Buddhist, Sikh) relied on background knowledge without live web checks, and citations such as page and ang numbers or sutta URLs need verifying."],"plausibility":2,"plausibilityRange":"2","plausibilityReason":"All five cycles are doctrinal frames with no empirical support and no testable real-world timeline. Historical kernels are credible: Mahavira as a 6th-5th century BCE teacher, the Kalachakra text's c. 1025 CE composition and the Ayyavazhi movement as a 19th-century religion. The dated predictions (Mappo, the 2424 CE war) have no verified fulfilment.","verification":"Partly verified","references":["Paul Dundas, The Jains (Routledge, 1992; 2nd ed. 2002); Padmanabh S. Jaini, The Jaina Path of Purification (University of California Press, 1979)","Umasvati, Tattvarthasutra ch. 3; Yati Vrishabha, Tiloyapannatti; Jinasena, Adipurana (9th c.); Hemacandra, Trishashtishalakapurushacarita (12th c.); Sthananga Sutra and Jambudvipaprajnapti","Vasubandhu, Abhidharmakosa ch. III, trans. Louis de La Vallee Poussin (1923-31)","Digha Nikaya 26 and 27 (https://suttacentral.net/dn26, https://suttacentral.net/dn27; not fetched); Samyutta Nikaya 15.5","Jan Nattier, Once upon a Future Time: Studies in a Buddhist Prophecy of Decline (Asian Humanities Press, 1991); Shinran, Kyogyoshinsho (13th c.)","Kalachakra Tantra and Vimalaprabha commentary (c. 11th c.); Vesna A. Wallace, The Inner Kalacakratantra (2001); Ronald M. Davidson, Indian Esoteric Buddhism (2002); Jeffrey Hopkins, The Kalachakra Tantra: Rite of Initiation (1985); Geoffrey Samuel, Civilized Shamans (1993)","Peter Bishop, The Myth of Shangri-La (1989); Joscelyn Godwin, Arktos (1993); Nicholas Roerich, Shambhala (1930)","Guru Granth Sahib (SGPC edition or Dr. Sant Singh Khalsa translation; verify ang citations); Bhai Gurdas, Vaaran; Sikh Rehat Maryada (1945)","W.H. McLeod, Textual Sources for the Study of Sikhism (Manchester UP, 1984); W.H. McLeod, Who is a Sikh? (Clarendon Press, 1989)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ayyavazhi and https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Akilattirattu_Ammanai (relayed via summarisation); G. Patrick, Religion and Subaltern Agency, ch. 5 (via Wikipedia); Robin Jeffrey on Ayyavazhi (via Wikipedia, not verified)"]},{"id":"world-comparative","title":"World comparative cosmologies: Hesiod, Zoroastrian, Norse/Aztec/Hopi and Maya parallels to the yugas","category":"Comparative mythology","members":["hesiod","zoroastrian","norse-other","maya"],"summary":"These members compare the yugas with non-Indian systems of world-ages. Hesiod's Works and Days (five races: Golden, Silver, Bronze, Heroic, Iron) and Ovid's four-metal version share the descending-age pattern, and some scholars (West 2007) treat it as a candidate Indo-European inheritance. The Zoroastrian Bundahishn has a 12,000-year history in four 3,000-year periods ending in Frashokereti, a different structure from the yugas. Norse Ragnarok, the Aztec Five Suns and Hopi successive worlds are cyclic or catastrophic cosmologies of destruction and renewal, with the Aztec sequence the closest to a finite series of ages. The Maya Long Count's 13-baktun cycle (about 5,125 years, 13.0.0.0.0 on 21 Dec 2012) and its 3114 BCE zero are sometimes linked to the Kali epoch (3102 BCE) through numerical coincidence. None gives yuga-style durations, and the parallels are structural, not documented transmission or shared chronology.","totalCycleYears":"No consensus: the Indic mahayuga is 4,320,000 years for comparison only. Hesiod, Norse, Aztec and Hopi give no numeric cycle; Zoroastrian history is 12,000 solar years; the Maya great cycle is about 5,125.37 years (13 baktuns of 144,000 days).","durations":{"satya":"No equivalent with stated length. Hesiod's Golden Race and Zoroastrian first 3,000-year period (spiritual creation) are the nearest analogues; Maya creation epoch 4 Ahau 8 Cumku (3114 BCE) is an epoch, not an age.","treta":"No equivalent. Hesiod's Silver Race; Zoroastrian second 3,000 years (material creation, still pure).","dvapara":"No equivalent. Hesiod's Bronze Race (the Heroic Race is an extra fifth age with no yuga counterpart); Zoroastrian third 3,000 years (mixture, Ahriman attacks, Zoroaster near its end).","kali":"No equivalent. Hesiod's Iron Age is his own time, ended by Zeus at an unspecified date; Zoroastrian final 3,000 years after Zoroaster with Saoshyants, ending in Frashokereti; Aztec Fifth Sun and Hopi Fourth World have no fixed length; Ragnarok is an event, not a duration."},"kaliStart":"No analogue in these traditions. Comparisons: Kali epoch 3102 BCE; Maya Long Count zero 3114 BCE (GMT correlation), a 12-year gap that is numerical coincidence, not a documented link; Zoroastrian post-Zoroaster period roughly near the 9,000-year mark.","kaliEnd":"None of these gives a checkable end date. Zoroastrian Frashokereti, Aztec Fifth Sun ending in earthquakes and famine, Hopi purification and Ragnarok are undated; the Maya 13.0.0.0.0 rollover (21 Dec 2012) matches no yuga boundary and was not an end-of-age event in the inscriptions. Nominal Kali end is c. 428,898 CE.","currentPosition":"Hesiod places himself in the Iron Age; mapped to Kali this is about 5,127 years in (2026 CE, from 3102 BCE). Zoroastrian: in the final post-Zoroaster period; Aztec: Fifth Sun (4 Movement); Hopi: Fourth World; Norse: before Ragnarok; Maya: past 13.0.0.0.0 and in the next Long Count cycle. No CE anchor is given.","variants":[{"memberId":"hesiod","title":"Hesiod's Ages of Man and a proposed Indo-European origin of the yugas","distinctive":"Works and Days 106-201; Ovid, Metamorphoses 1.89-150 drops the heroic race to give four ages. Kritayuga, tretayuga, dvaparayuga and kaliyuga were originally dice-throw names (Aitareya Brahmana, Chandogya Upanishad) and no Sanskrit cognate for the metals exists. Near Eastern borrowing (West 1997, The East Face of Helicon) is an alternative explanation, and no PIE lexeme for the concept is reconstructed."},{"memberId":"zoroastrian","title":"Zoroastrian 12,000-year world cycle (Bundahishn)","distinctive":"Four 3,000-year periods (spiritual and material creation, mixture, final period), with Saoshyants at 1,000-year intervals after Zoroaster. Theopompus (Plutarch, De Iside 47) and Hermippus (Pliny, NH 30.2) report a 6,000 plus 3,000 plus 3,000 scheme, so the frame was systematised over time, possibly under Zurvanite and Hellenistic Great Year influence. Zoroaster's date (c. 1500-600 BCE on linguistic and archaeological arguments) is debated. Links to the Hindu 12,000 divine years are numerical only."},{"memberId":"norse-other","title":"Norse Ragnarok, Aztec Five Suns, Hopi worlds","distinctive":"Leyenda de los Soles lists suns 4 Jaguar, 4 Wind, 4 Rain, 4 Water, then the Fifth Sun 4 Movement; the first sun is given 676 years, other figures are inconsistent. The 52-year Calendar Round and New Fire Ceremony renew time. Hopi worlds are known mainly via Waters (1963) and Courlander (1971), where the Blue Star Kachina prophecy is a 20th-century composite and some Hopi object to its circulation. Norse sources (Volospa, Gylfaginning) are Christian-era manuscripts with uncertain dating."},{"memberId":"maya","title":"Maya Long Count and claimed links to the yugas","distinctive":"Long Count 13-baktun cycle completed 13.0.0.0.0 on 21 Dec 2012. Claims linking it to Kali rest on the 3114 vs 3102 BCE near-coincidence and the roughly 5,125-year versus about 5,127 years-elapsed similarity; the 2012 date and the Kali-based arithmetic (about 2024) do not agree. The Tortuguero Monument 6 mentions the 13th baktun. Popular sources: Jenkins (1998), Arguelles (1987). Maya specialists (Coe) reject the 2012 end-of-world readings as an odometer rollover."}],"keyClaims":["Hesiod's five races and Ovid's four metals mirror the yugas' decline from a golden age to a degraded present; the Golden/Krita and Iron/Kali pairings form the core of the correspondence and may descend from a Proto-Indo-European motif.","Zoroastrian cosmic history is 12,000 years in four 3,000-year periods, with Ahriman's attack, Zoroaster near the end of the third, three Saoshyants at 1,000-year intervals, and final renovation (Frashokereti).","Aztec Five Suns: four earlier suns each ended by a cosmic catastrophe, and the current Fifth Sun (4 Movement) is expected to end in earthquakes and famine.","Hopi tradition has successive worlds ended by moral failure, with people now in the Fourth World and an undated purification or transition prophesied.","Norse Ragnarok is a single terminal battle (Volospa, Gylfaginning) followed by a renewed earth and the return of Baldr, linear with renewal rather than a repeating decline.","The Maya 13-baktun cycle (about 5,125 years) and Long Count zero (3114 BCE) correspond to or link with the Kali Yuga timescale and epoch (3102 BCE), and the 2012 turnover marks an age end paralleling yuga transitions.","Cyclical age systems across these cultures converge on a shared structure (decline, destruction, renewal), but none uses numerical durations matching the yugas."],"evidence":["Hesiod's and Ovid's metal schemes share the descending order and perfect-first/degraded-last pattern with the yugas; decline-of-ages motifs also appear in Persian, Hebrew (Daniel 2), Mesopotamian and Indic material, suggesting inheritance or independent development (West 1978, 2007).","The Indic yuga concept appears in the Mahabharata and Manusmriti (later than Hesiod); the dice terms predate it but are not ages. Krta means 'done', not gold.","Pahlavi texts (Bundahishn, Denkard, Zand-i Vohuman Yasn) state the 3,000-year structure; Boyce (1975-1991) and Zaehner (1955) discuss its development; the texts were compiled 1,000-1,500 years after the Gathas.","Leyenda de los Soles, Sahagun's Florentine Codex Book 7, Volospa (Codex Regius), Snorri's Prose Edda, and Courlander (1971) and Waters (1963) are the real sources for the Aztec, Norse and Hopi material.","Long Count arithmetic is correct (13 x 144,000 days is about 5,125.37 years), Tortuguero Monument 6 references the 13th baktun, and the Popol Vuh describes successive creations as a superficial parallel."],"commonCriticisms":["unfalsifiable","late-origin","computational-numbers","epoch-back-calculated","no-archaeology"],"specificCriticisms":["Four-stage decline myths are widespread and can arise independently, so structural similarity does not show common inheritance; yuga durations (4:3:2:1, 4.32 million years) have no Greek parallel and the heroic race breaks the four-to-four mapping.","Near Eastern borrowing by Hesiod is an alternative to a prehistoric Indo-European origin, and no PIE lexeme for a golden age or yuga concept is reconstructed.","The Zoroastrian 12,000-year frame comes from late Pahlavi systematisation, Greek reports give a different division, and the 12,000 solar years differ in unit from the Hindu 12,000 divine years, so links are numerical coincidence.","Aztec and Hopi sources were recorded after the Spanish conquest or compiled in the 20th century (Waters blends sources), Norse eschatology survives in Christian-era manuscripts, and the Aztec sun durations are inconsistent across texts.","No primary Maya or Indian text connects the Long Count to the yugas; the 3114 BCE zero depends on the GMT correlation constant, which varies, and a 12-year gap between two reckoner-chosen epochs is weak evidence. Cherry-picking of coincidences is a known pattern in fringe calendar comparison.","None of these traditions makes a dated, testable prediction, so they cannot be ranked by accuracy; popular comparisons often omit the differences and are not peer reviewed.","Some members (Maya, Hesiod, Norse) relied on prior knowledge without live web verification and need citation checks."],"plausibility":2,"plausibilityRange":"1-2","plausibilityReason":"As comparative observations about decline-and-renewal patterns the claims are reasonable, but as shared origin, matching chronology or real-world timelines they are weakly supported because the lexical, numerical and conceptual correspondences are poor. The Zoroastrian 12,000-year cycle scores 1 as a theological scheme with late sources and no calendar anchor.","verification":"Web-checked","references":["Hesiod, Works and Days 106-201 (Evelyn-White, Loeb, 1914); M. L. West, Hesiod: Works and Days (Oxford, 1978)","M. L. West, Indo-European Poetry and Myth (Oxford University Press, 2007); M. L. West, The East Face of Helicon (Oxford, 1997)","Mallory, J. P. and Adams, D. Q., The Oxford Introduction to Proto-Indo-European (Oxford, 2006)","Ovid, Metamorphoses 1.89-150; Manusmriti 1.64-86 (Doniger and Smith, Penguin, 1991); Chandogya Upanishad 4.1.4-5 and Aitareya Brahmana 7.15","Bundahishn, Zand-i Vohuman Yasn, Denkard (translations by West, Sanjana, Pakzad); Plutarch, De Iside et Osiride 46-47 (Theopompus); Pliny, Natural History 30.2 (Hermippus)","Mary Boyce, A History of Zoroastrianism, vols. 1-3 (Brill, 1975-1991); R.C. Zaehner, Zurvan: A Zoroastrian Dilemma (Oxford, 1955)","Volospa, Poetic Edda, Codex Regius (ed. Ursula Dronke, The Poetic Edda Vol. II, Oxford, 1997); Snorri Sturluson, Prose Edda, Gylfaginning (c. 1220)","Leyenda de los Soles (16th-c. Nahuatl, Chimalpopoca codex); Bernardino de Sahagun, Florentine Codex Book 7; Miguel Leon-Portilla, Aztec Thought and Culture (1963)","Frank Waters, Book of the Hopi (1963); Harold Courlander, The Fourth World of the Hopi (1971)","J. E. S. Thompson, Maya Hieroglyphic Writing (1960); Coe and Van Stone, Reading the Maya Glyphs (2001); Schele and Freidel, A Forest of Kings (1990)","Jenkins, J. M., Maya Cosmogenesis 2012 (Bear and Company, 1998); Arguelles, J., The Mayan Factor (Bear and Company, 1987)","Aryabhatiya of Aryabhata (499 CE), kalakriyapada on yuga epochs"]},{"id":"symbolic-devotional","title":"Symbolic and devotional readings of the yugas: Kali as consciousness, social cycle or age of opportunity","category":"Modern guru / sect","members":["aurobindo-allegorical","vivekananda","ananda-marga-sarkar","tantra-shaiva","osho","art-of-living","akram-vignan","radha-soami","swaminarayan"],"summary":"These members accept or reinterpret the Puranic yuga scheme as a moral, psychological or devotional frame, not as a precise calendar. Several keep the traditional figures (4,320,000-year mahayuga; Kali 432,000 years from 3102 BCE, ending c. 428,898 CE) but make little use of them: Vivekananda, Tantra/Shaiva, Osho, Art of Living, Akram Vignan, Radha Soami and Swaminarayan. Others replace durations with qualitative or social stages: Aurobindo reads the yugas as states of consciousness open to transformation, and Sarkar as a recurring social cycle of Vipra, Kshatriya, Vaishya and Shudra dominance. A common move is to treat Kali as an opportunity, not only decline: Ramakrishna and Vivekananda (name of God, rising masses), the Mahanirvana Tantra (Kaula practice), Akram Vignan (a shortcut for the dushamkaal) and Swaminarayan (God manifest in Kali). Since the readings give no dates or testable predictions, they are unfalsifiable except where a leader added dated claims (Osho). All treat the present as inside Kali, about 5,127 years in by 2026 on the traditional count.","totalCycleYears":"4,320,000 years (mahayuga) where stated (Vivekananda, Tantra/Shaiva, Osho, Art of Living, Swaminarayan, Akram Vignan alongside Jain aras). Aurobindo, Sarkar and Radha Soami give no cycle length, and Aurobindo contrasts Yukteswar's 24,000-year scheme.","durations":{"satya":"1,728,000 years (4,800 divine years), traditional and mostly assumed; Akram Vignan adds Jain ara 1 (about 4 kotakoti sagaropama). Not given by Aurobindo (qualitative: truth-consciousness), Sarkar or Radha Soami.","treta":"1,296,000 years (3,600 divine years); Jain ara 2 (about 3 kotakoti sagaropama) per Akram Vignan. Not given by Aurobindo (dynamic vital will).","dvapara":"864,000 years (2,400 divine years); Jain ara 3 (about 2 kotakoti sagaropama) per Akram Vignan. Not given by Aurobindo (intellectual, divided consciousness).","kali":"432,000 years (1,200 divine years); Akram Vignan also gives the Jain fifth ara of 21,000 years. Not given by Aurobindo (material-vital fragmentation), Sarkar or Radha Soami."},"kaliStart":"3102 BCE (17/18 February, Ujjain meridian) in the members that use the traditional epoch; Akram Vignan's Jain fifth ara begins c. 524 BCE. Aurobindo, Sarkar and Radha Soami give no start date.","kaliEnd":"About 428,898-428,899 CE on the traditional count; Akram Vignan's Jain fifth ara ends c. 20,500 CE. Aurobindo, Sarkar, Tantra and Radha Soami frame the end as a transformation or give no date, and the Swaminarayan sect makes no end-date prediction.","currentPosition":"All place the present in Kali, about 5,127-5,128 years in by 2026 on the traditional count (about 426,870 years remaining). Aurobindo reads it as a transitional, possibly threshold phase, Sarkar as a Vaishya (acquisitive) era moving toward a Shudra phase, and Akram Vignan as a dushamkaal with the Akram path available.","variants":[{"memberId":"aurobindo-allegorical","title":"Psychological and allegorical readings (Sri Aurobindo and others)","distinctive":"The yugas are phases of consciousness in individuals and society, sources being The Human Cycle, The Secret of the Veda and The Life Divine; Kali's darkness can precede a turn driven by the Supermind, so the cycle is open, not a closed wheel. Related readings: Guenon and Evola (metaphysical decline), Yukteswar (The Holy Science, 1894; a 24,000-year cosmic scheme with a claimed recent turn toward Dvapara), Jung's Aion."},{"memberId":"vivekananda","title":"Swami Vivekananda and Ramakrishna Mission","distinctive":"Ramakrishna: in Kali people depend on food (anna-gata-prana), so chanting God's name is the best path (BhP 12.3.51). Vivekananda (1890s letters and lectures): Satya is rule of priests, Treta of warriors, Dvapara of merchants, Kali of workers (Shudra); he urged uplift of the masses and expected a future synthesis. The Mission avoids firm dating."},{"memberId":"ananda-marga-sarkar","title":"P.R. Sarkar / Ananda Marga","distinctive":"A Law of Social Cycle in which Vipra, Kshatriya, Vaishya and Shudra types dominate in turn, combined with linear technological progress; sadvipras can turn the cycle into an upward spiral, and PROUT is the policy arm. Sarkar advocated a casteless society and rejected capitalism, communism and Marx. He was imprisoned in 1971 and acquitted on 2 August 1978. The yuga mapping and stage order come from Ananda Marga materials and were not verified."},{"memberId":"tantra-shaiva","title":"Tantric and Shaiva views","distinctive":"The Mahanirvana Tantra and Kaula texts hold that Kali is the best age for tantric practice, open to all castes and genders, while Vedic and smarta ritual is ineffective. Shaiva Puranas use yugas for mythology (28 Dvapara incarnations of Shiva). The Mahanirvana Tantra is dated c. 18th century CE and Abhinavagupta rarely builds chronologies."},{"memberId":"osho","title":"Osho (Rajneesh)","distinctive":"Reinterpreted cosmic ages as inner states and presented the present as a decline needing individual awakening (not verified against a specific discourse). His documented dated predictions failed: a third world war (1964 warning), nuclear catastrophe in the 1990s, and the March 1984 announcement relayed by Sheela that two-thirds of humanity would die of AIDS. Wikipedia's Osho article has no yuga discussion."},{"memberId":"art-of-living","title":"Sri Sri Ravi Shankar / Art of Living","distinctive":"Present age is Kali, the age of conflict and materialism; breath practices (Sudarshan Kriya), meditation and service are said to counter its effects, and ages are treated as states of collective consciousness. No yuga content on the Wikipedia pages; the only dated remark found is March 2018 on the Ram temple, unrelated to yugas. Controversies include the 2016 Yamuna floodplain festival fine."},{"memberId":"akram-vignan","title":"Dada Bhagwan / Akram Vignan","distinctive":"A. M. Patel (Dada Bhagwan; 1958 self-realization claim, 1968 public Gnan Vidhi, died 2 January 1988) offers rapid realization in a two-hour Gnan Vidhi, framed as suited to the dushamkaal, in contrast to gradual Jain purification. Current leader Deepakbhai Desai; a 1988 succession dispute with Niruben Amin split the movement. The Kaliyug/dushamkaal link comes from background knowledge, not the fetched sources."},{"memberId":"radha-soami","title":"Radha Soami / Sant Mat (Beas, Dayalbagh)","distinctive":"Founded by Shiv Dayal Singh in Agra in 1861. Cosmology of planes (Pind, Brahmand, Sach Khand) with Kal (Kal Niranjan) ruling the lower creation; yugas are moral decline within Kal's domain. Stress on escape through initiation and surat shabd yoga under a living Satguru, not chronology. Beas (1880s-1891), Dayalbagh (1907/1913, to check), Dera Sacha Sauda split (1948) and Manavta Mandir (1962) are movement facts."},{"memberId":"swaminarayan","title":"Swaminarayan Sampradaya (BAPS etc.)","distinctive":"Accepts the standard Puranic scheme. God (Purushottam) took form as Swaminarayan (born 3 April 1781) within Kali, so the era is one of divine manifestation, with the Gunatit Guru lineage continuing. Vachanamrut: 273 discourses, 1819-1829. BAPS pages and Vachanamrut yuga passages were not retrieved. No end-date prediction is made."}],"keyClaims":["Kali is a real phase of the cosmic cycle but offers spiritual opportunity: devotion, name chanting, tantric Kaula practice, the Akram path, or a living guru give liberation more easily than in earlier ages (Ramakrishna/Vivekananda, Mahanirvana Tantra, Akram Vignan, Radha Soami, Swaminarayan).","The yugas describe states or phases of consciousness, in individuals and in society, with a qualitative sequence not tied to fixed time spans (Aurobindo, Osho, Art of Living); Kali's darkness can be the precondition for a spiritual turn (the Supermind in Aurobindo).","The yugas map to a recurring social cycle: priests, warriors, merchants and workers in turn (Vivekananda), or Vipra, Kshatriya, Vaishya and Shudra types (Sarkar); Vivekananda saw the coming age as one of rising workers and masses.","Kali is the best age for tantric practice, open to all castes and genders, while Vedic and smarta ritual is ineffective (Mahanirvana Tantra and Kaula texts).","The Puranic durations (4,320,000-year mahayuga, Kali 432,000 years from 3102 BCE) are assumed rather than recalculated, and the sects generally avoid dating yuga boundaries or predicting an end of Kali.","The present is a dushamkaal (Akram Vignan, mapped to the Jain fifth ara) or an age of decline requiring individual awakening (Osho, Art of Living).","Swaminarayan is God manifest within Kali (1781 CE), and Radha Soami teaches that souls are bound by Kal in the lower planes and need a living Satguru to escape.","Yukteswar's 24,000-year scheme ties the yugas to a mental and spiritual progression with a cosmic-astronomical basis."],"evidence":["Textual: Aurobindo's essays, Ramakrishna's Kathamrita, Vivekananda's Complete Works and 'The Future of India' (1897), Bhagavata Purana 12.3.51, the Mahanirvana Tantra, Sarkar's social-cycle writings and PROUT, and the Vachanamrut state or support the respective interpretations.","Standard sources for the traditional framework: Wikipedia 'Yuga' and 'Kali Yuga' (17/18 February 3102 BCE start, 432,000 years, c. 428,899 CE end, about 426,873 years remaining in 2026), Surya Siddhanta and Puranic texts.","Aurobindo's reading fits his integral-yoga levels of being and parallels Jung's Aion and Traditionalist cycles, though parallels do not prove the original Hindu meaning.","Vivekananda's reformist reading is described in Rolland (1931) and Burke (1958-74).","Wikipedia articles (Osho, Sarkar, Dada Bhagwan, Radha Soami, Swaminarayan, BAPS) confirm biographies and movement histories; none of the Osho, Art of Living, Akram Vignan or Swaminarayan articles contains yuga discussion.","Osho's 1964 world-war warning, the 1980s 'Noah's Ark of consciousness' framing and the 1984 AIDS announcement are documented, but not as yuga claims."],"commonCriticisms":["unfalsifiable","no-archaeology","epoch-back-calculated","computational-numbers","late-origin","failed-predictions","unverified-sources"],"specificCriticisms":["Allegorical and social readings are modern reinterpretations or syntheses, not recoveries of the original meaning; critics say they project modern concerns onto the text, and academic Indology treats Aurobindo as philosophy, not philology.","Mapping yugas to social classes (Vivekananda's Shudra age, Sarkar's cycle) can romanticise hierarchy or be matched to any period after the fact; Sarkar's cycle has no mechanism for stage transitions or durations.","Vivekananda's 'Shudra age' is a prophecy whose fulfilment depends on how democracy and labour movements are interpreted, and critics note tension between his strife-age claim and optimism.","The Mahanirvana Tantra's frame as an ancient Shiva-Parvati dialogue is literary; it dates to c. 18th century CE, so it is no source for yuga chronology, and Kali-as-tantra-age is a theological justification for a ritual system.","Osho's dated predictions (1990s nuclear war, 1984 AIDS mass death) did not come true, and psychological reinterpretation of yugas lets him call the present decline with no measurable criterion.","Framing moral decline as an effect of a cosmic age can discourage accountability for concrete social problems (Art of Living).","Akram Vignan's instant realization cannot be independently verified; there are concerns on governance and finances and a contested lineage after the 1988 split. Sant Mat groups face similar criticisms (guru-centred authority, succession splits, Beas vs Dayalbagh disputes).","Swaminarayan's divine status is contested by other Hindu traditions and historians, and the cosmic frame inherits these disputes.","Many entries could not verify the group's own yuga statements (Sarkar, Osho, Art of Living, Akram Vignan, Radha Soami, Swaminarayan), so the yuga connection is inferred from background knowledge, and some founding dates conflict across sources (BAPS 1905 vs 1907; Dayalbagh 1907/1913)."],"plausibility":1,"plausibilityRange":"1-2","plausibilityReason":"The allegorical, social and tantric readings (Aurobindo, Vivekananda, Sarkar, Tantra/Shaiva) score 2 because they make no testable date claims, so they are philosophy, not chronology. Those that rely on the literal Puranic cycle or add failed dated predictions (Osho, Art of Living, Akram Vignan, Radha Soami, Swaminarayan) score 1, since the 432,000-year cycle and 3102 BCE epoch have no empirical support.","verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["Sri Aurobindo, The Human Cycle (Arya 1915-1918; book 1949), The Secret of the Veda (1956), The Life Divine (1939-1940), Collected Works, Sri Aurobindo Ashram Trust","Sri Yukteswar Giri, The Holy Science (1894); Paramahansa Yogananda, The Second Coming of Christ; Rene Guenon, The Crisis of the Modern World (1927); Julius Evola, Revolt Against the Modern World (1934); C. G. Jung, Aion (1951)","Swami Vivekananda, Complete Works (Advaita Ashrama), esp. vols. 3-5 and letters 1894-1897; 'The Future of India' (1897)","The Gospel of Sri Ramakrishna (Kathamrita), trans. Nikhilananda (1942); Bhagavata Purana 12.3.51","Romain Rolland, The Life of Vivekananda (1931); Marie Louise Burke, Swami Vivekananda in the West (6 vols., 1958-1974); https://www.belurmath.org","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/P._R._Sarkar; Ananda Marga and PROUT publications (not reviewed)","Woodroffe (Avalon), trans., Mahanirvana Tantra (1913); Goudriaan and Gupta, Hindu Tantric and Sakta Literature (1981); Padoux, Vac (1990); Sanderson, 'The Saiva Age' (2009) and 'Purity and Power among the Brahmans of Kashmir' (1985)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Osho; https://www.osho.com","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sri_Sri_Ravi_Shankar; https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Art_of_Living_Foundation; https://www.artofliving.org","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dada_Bhagwan; https://www.dadabhagwan.org/; Jain six-ara cycle (Tattvarthasutra tradition)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Radha_Soami; https://radhasoamisatsangbeas.org; https://dayalbagh.org (not fetched)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Swaminarayan; https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bochasanwasi_Akshar_Purushottam_Swaminarayan_Sanstha; Vachanamrut (1819-1829) and Shikshapatri (1826), not fetched","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yuga; https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kali_Yuga; Vishnu Purana and Surya Siddhanta"]},{"id":"unidentified","title":"Dashrathbhai Patel (Gujarat) yuga interpretation: unidentified","category":"Regional prophetic tradition","members":["dashrathbhai-patel"],"summary":"No Gujarat-based teacher named Dashrathbhai Patel who teaches a yuga timeline could be identified, so nothing here describes his views. Wikipedia returned no results, and DuckDuckGo (English and Gujarati queries), Bing and YouTube returned bot-check pages or nothing relevant. Possible name confusion exists with Dada Bhagwan (Ambalal Muljibhai Patel), whose Akram Vignan teaching is not a yuga doctrine. Unverified candidates to check next are Sri Yukteswar Giri (The Holy Science, 24,000-year cycle with 1:2:3:4 yuga proportions), Kalki Bhagwan (Andhra Pradesh) and Gayatri Pariwar / Yug Nirman (Pandit Shriram Sharma Acharya, near-term 'yug parivartan'). A source (YouTube title, book, ashram or Gujarati post) is needed to describe the teaching.","totalCycleYears":"Not verified for this teacher. Puranic baseline: 4,320,000 years (mahayuga).","durations":{"satya":"Not verified. Puranic baseline: 1,728,000 years","treta":"Not verified. Puranic baseline: 1,296,000 years","dvapara":"Not verified. Puranic baseline: 864,000 years","kali":"Not verified. Puranic baseline: 432,000 years"},"kaliStart":"Not verified. Puranic baseline: 3102 BCE.","kaliEnd":"Not verified. Puranic baseline: approximately 428,898 CE.","currentPosition":"Not verified. Under the Puranic baseline, 2026 CE is about 5,127 years into Kali Yuga.","variants":[{"memberId":"dashrathbhai-patel","title":"Dashrathbhai Patel (Gujarat) and his yuga interpretation: unidentified","distinctive":"Only entry in the cluster; verification status is 'Not found', with no primary sources located and no dated predictions that could be checked."}],"keyClaims":["No verifiable claim could be attributed to Dashrathbhai Patel.","A supplied source (YouTube title, book, ashram name or Gujarati post) would allow the teaching to be checked."],"evidence":["Wikipedia (en) search for 'Dashrathbhai Patel yuga': no results.","DuckDuckGo (English and Gujarati queries) and Bing: bot-check pages or no relevant results; YouTube results page returned only footer text."],"commonCriticisms":["unverified-sources","unfalsifiable","failed-predictions"],"specificCriticisms":["The subject's identity and teaching are unverified; any description beyond 'not found' would be fabrication.","Interpretations that place a Satya Yuga return within a short window have a recurring history of unfulfilled dates, to be assessed once a specific teaching is found."],"plausibility":1,"plausibilityRange":"1","plausibilityReason":"The teaching is unknown, so no specific claim can be assessed; the underlying idea of yugas as a literal dated timeline has no empirical support.","verification":"Not found","references":["Wikipedia (en), search 'Dashrathbhai Patel yuga': no article found.","Sri Yukteswar Giri (1894), The Holy Science (background knowledge, not re-checked).","Dada Bhagwan / Akram Vignan (background knowledge, not re-checked)."]}],"criticismTags":{"no-archaeology":"No archaeological, genetic or material evidence for the claimed past ages; contradicts human evolution and the South Asian archaeological sequence.","epoch-back-calculated":"The 3102 BCE Kali epoch was back-computed (~500 CE), not observed; there was no real planetary conjunction then.","precession-error":"The 24,000-year cycle relies on a 54″/yr precession rate (real ≈50.3″/yr, ≈25,772 yrs); no binary or dual sun has been found.","unfalsifiable":"Defined by qualitative moral or spiritual states, so it cannot be tested.","failed-predictions":"Dated predictions passed without the predicted event.","late-origin":"The four-age scheme is absent from the Vedas and crystallised c. 300 BCE–300 CE; its numbers were layered on over time.","computational-numbers":"Figures like 4,320,000 are computational or symbolic periods (integer planetary revolutions, sexagesimal numerology), not observed cycles.","retrofitting":"Prophecies or verses are matched to events after the fact.","elapsed-vs-total":"Confuses years elapsed in Kali (~5,127) with its total length.","astro-dating-underdetermined":"Planetarium/verse-based dating is selective and underdetermined, and conflicts with epigraphy (e.g. Ashoka’s edicts).","unverified-sources":"Key claims could not be verified from primary sources in this research.","decline-vs-trends":"Claimed present-age decline conflicts with measurable trends (lifespan, literacy, violence)."}};
